{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:42:12.578Z","ordinal":7630,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0cb48b2b18d09e75016aaec96ba7ac87d2862dee3b6061e404","status":"completed","call_id":"call_rbQYFG5lq1sHawFeGxkJpPvT","name":"exec","input":"text(await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nimport pathlib,json\\nb=pathlib.Path('/Users/thomi/.codex/visualizations/2026/09/18/01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9/code-quality-audit');p=b/'coverage.json';a=json.loads(p.read_text());names={'.claude/agents/entire-search.md','.entire/agent-guide.md','AGENTS.md','CLAUDE.md','CONTRIBUTING.md','LICENSE','README.md','docs/limits.md','docs/proposal-queue-repair.md'}\\nfor x in a:\\n if x['path'] in names:x.update(review_status='source-inspected',checks=['Complete document read and comparison with previously inspected implementation.'],notes='README grammar typo; limits safety claims have known exceptions in F23/F26/F37/F54/F61/F62. No additional finding.')\\np.write_text(json.dumps(a,indent=2)+'\\\\n')\\nfor f in ['docs/operations.md','docs/manifest-loading-validation.md','docs/graph-identity-upgrades.md','docs/shared-fact-identity.md','docs/recall-evidence.md']:\\n print('\\\\nFILE:',f);print(pathlib.Path(f).read_text())\\nPY\",\"max_output_tokens\":11000}));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","create_time":1789839720.715712}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:42:12.675Z","ordinal":7631,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","session_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","root_turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","response_id":"resp_0cb48b2b18d09e75016aaec96861b887d296a55b190c72ef41","usage":{"input_tokens":197341,"cached_input_tokens":190848,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":341,"reasoning_output_tokens":36,"total_tokens":197682},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":75132241,"cached_input_tokens":71919232,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":174417,"reasoning_output_tokens":14794,"total_tokens":75306658},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":141017260,"cached_input_tokens":134491264,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":359216,"reasoning_output_tokens":36171,"total_tokens":141376476}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:42:12.714Z","ordinal":7632,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-0adff784-5ada-480c-b571-b9735c45c8f8","process_id":"63476","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","python3 - <<'PY'\nimport pathlib,json\nb=pathlib.Path('/Users/thomi/.codex/visualizations/2026/09/18/01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9/code-quality-audit');p=b/'coverage.json';a=json.loads(p.read_text());names={'.claude/agents/entire-search.md','.entire/agent-guide.md','AGENTS.md','CLAUDE.md','CONTRIBUTING.md','LICENSE','README.md','docs/limits.md','docs/proposal-queue-repair.md'}\nfor x in a:\n if x['path'] in names:x.update(review_status='source-inspected',checks=['Complete document read and comparison with previously inspected implementation.'],notes='README grammar typo; limits safety claims have known exceptions in F23/F26/F37/F54/F61/F62. No additional finding.')\np.write_text(json.dumps(a,indent=2)+'\\n')\nfor f in ['docs/operations.md','docs/manifest-loading-validation.md','docs/graph-identity-upgrades.md','docs/shared-fact-identity.md','docs/recall-evidence.md']:\n print('\\nFILE:',f);print(pathlib.Path(f).read_text())\nPY"],"cwd":"file:///Users/thomi/.codex/worktrees/5d33/entire-brain","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport pathlib,json\nb=pathlib.Path('/Users/thomi/.codex/visualizations/2026/09/18/01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9/code-quality-audit');p=b/'coverage.json';a=json.loads(p.read_text());names={'.claude/agents/entire-search.md','.entire/agent-guide.md','AGENTS.md','CLAUDE.md','CONTRIBUTING.md','LICENSE','README.md','docs/limits.md','docs/proposal-queue-repair.md'}\nfor x in a:\n if x['path'] in names:x.update(review_status='source-inspected',checks=['Complete document read and comparison with previously inspected implementation.'],notes='README grammar typo; limits safety claims have known exceptions in F23/F26/F37/F54/F61/F62. No additional finding.')\np.write_text(json.dumps(a,indent=2)+'\\n')\nfor f in ['docs/operations.md','docs/manifest-loading-validation.md','docs/graph-identity-upgrades.md','docs/shared-fact-identity.md','docs/recall-evidence.md']:\n print('\\nFILE:',f);print(pathlib.Path(f).read_text())\nPY"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"\nFILE: docs/operations.md\n# Operations\n\n`entire-brain` is a local Entire CLI plugin. It owns the persistent local brain,\nsemantic SQLite stores, bundle import/export, graph query surfaces, MCP stdio\ntools, and benchmark/evidence commands.\n\n## Full Install\n\n```sh\nscripts/install.sh\n```\n\nThe end-to-end installer, and the one the README documents. It has four effects\non the machine and no others: it builds and registers the `entire-graph`\nsemantic provider, builds and registers `entire-brain`, writes the default\nplugin configuration (`entire brain config init`), and runs `entire brain\ndoctor`. Both binaries are built inside their checkouts and linked into Entire's\nmanaged plugin directory; nothing is installed system-wide.\n\nBefore it builds anything it checks every prerequisite -- `git`, the `entire`\nCLI, a Go toolchain at least as new as the `go` directive in `go.mod`, and a C\ncompiler for `entire-graph`'s tree-sitter cgo bindings -- and reports all the\nmissing ones in one pass with the fix for each. `entire-brain` itself is a\npure-Go build; the cgo requirement is the provider's.\n\nNeither checkout has to have a particular name, and the two do not have to be\nsiblings. The provider is resolved by three routes, and the one taken is printed\nunder `==> entire-graph semantic provider`:\n\n| Route | Source | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR` | Used exactly as given. A value that is not an `entire-graph` checkout is a hard error, never a silent fallback. |\n| 2 | A checkout on this machine | Siblings of the brain checkout and of the main worktree when it is a linked one, the route-3 cache, and the usual source directories under `$HOME`. Accepted only when `cmd/entire-graph` and `scripts/install-local.sh` are both present, so a directory that merely has the name is skipped. |\n| 3 | A shallow clone | `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_REPO` (default the public `entireio/entire-graph`) into `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_CACHE` (default `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`). Later runs reuse it and refresh it with a shallow fetch, and keep going on the cached copy when the network is unavailable. |\n\n`ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` disables route 3, so an air-gapped machine fails with\nan explanation naming all three routes rather than blocking on a clone.\n\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` wraps this for a machine with nothing: it clones\n`entire-brain` when it is not already present -- falling back to `gh repo clone`\nfor the private-repo credentials -- and then execs `scripts/install.sh`. There is\ndeliberately no `curl | sh` form: `entireio/entire-brain` is private, so\n`raw.githubusercontent.com` serves 404 for it without a token; the source has to\nbe cloned regardless because the install is a source build; and cloning first\nputs the script that registers your plugins on disk at a reviewable commit\nbefore it runs.\n\n### After the install: onboard a repository\n\nThe installer is machine-scoped. It gives no repository a brain, and it is not\nthe last command a user runs. That is `entire brain setup`, once per repository:\n\n```sh\ncd /path/to/your/repo\nentire brain setup\n```\n\nOperationally the thing to know is that a default `setup` does two things beyond\nthe deterministic build, both of which spend tokens: it starts a detached fact\nbackfill (capped at 25 sessions per pass, `--no-backfill` to skip), and it\ninstalls **one machine-wide watcher as a persistent service, by default, with no\nprompt** — a launchd agent with `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive`, or a systemd user unit\nwith `Restart=always`. There is no prompt, but there is a pre-flight line: a run\nthat will register a watcher opens with it, before any work, naming the unit\nlabel and the path it will write and giving both `--no-daemon` and\n`entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`. `--no-daemon` skips it, `entire brain\nsetup --uninstall-daemon` removes it, and `entire brain setup --no-backfill\n--no-daemon` spends nothing and changes no service. See the reference's\n[step 3](reference.md#3-onboard-a-repository-entire-brain-setup) for the full\nphase table and the ordering against `entire enable`.\n\n## Local Install (brain only)\n\n```sh\nscripts/install-local.sh\n```\n\nThe script builds `./entire-brain`, installs it with `entire plugin install\n./entire-brain --force`, and prints `entire brain version`. It fails before\nwriting anything if the parent `entire` CLI is not on `PATH`. It does not touch\nthe provider, so `entire graph` has to be installed separately for the semantic\nlayer.\n\nThe default build is the pure-Go stack. To install the optional cgo vector stack:\n\n```sh\nBUILD_TAGS=\"brain_cgo sqlite_fts5\" scripts/install-local.sh\n```\n\n## Release Archives\n\n```sh\nscripts/release.sh\n```\n\nThe release script writes `dist/release-<version>/` with one `.tar.gz` archive per\ntarget and a `SHA256SUMS` manifest. `VERSION=<value>` overrides the version;\notherwise the script uses `git describe --tags --always --dirty`.\n\nDefault targets are:\n\n- `darwin/amd64`\n- `darwin/arm64`\n- `linux/amd64`\n- `linux/arm64`\n- `windows/amd64`\n\nSet `ENTIRE_RELEASE_TARGETS` to override that list:\n\n```sh\nENTIRE_RELEASE_TARGETS=\"linux/amd64\" scripts/release.sh\n```\n\nThe default release build uses the pure-Go fallback stack with `CGO_ENABLED=0`,\nwhich is the portable distribution path. To build cgo artifacts, set\n`BUILD_TAGS=\"brain_cgo sqlite_fts5\"` and provide the matching platform compiler.\nThe script records checksums for artifacts it successfully builds; it also signs\narchives when a local signing key is explicitly configured:\n\n- `COSIGN_KEY=<key-ref>` with `cosign` on `PATH` writes `<archive>.sig`.\n- `GPG_SIGNING_KEY=<key-id>` with `gpg` on `PATH` writes `<archive>.asc`.\n\nThe script does not publish artifacts.\n\n## Shared Baselines\n\nBrain bundle import/export is the policy-controlled shared baseline path for\nthis repository. A bundle carries the semantic index, seed, docs and facts by\ndefault; captured sessions and the history and patterns projections built from\nthem travel only under `--include-sessions`; session tombstones, privacy\ntransactions and local worker state are never bundled. Use:\n\n```sh\nentire brain bundle export --output /tmp/repo-brain.tar\nentire brain bundle import /tmp/repo-brain.tar --sha256 <sha256>\n```\n\nThe implementation validates checksums, schema compatibility, repo policy, path\ntraversal, symlink safety, size caps, and redaction before import. Import\nrefuses to overwrite a layer the destination brain already holds unless\n`--overwrite` is passed, and the refusal happens before anything is written.\nExport prints exactly which layers it wrote and which it left out. Shared\nbaselines remain explicit local files; there is no remote publishing or registry\ndiscovery.\n\n\nFILE: docs/manifest-loading-validation.md\n# Manifest loading validation\n\nImplementation base: `origin/main` at `4623fab909ebefdab35087dd682fde387917ec62`.\n\n## Implementation\n\nCanonical Brain manifests no longer use the auxiliary 16 MiB read ceiling or\nthe corresponding writer rejection. CLI loading, strict replacement checks,\nstatus/doctor health inspection, and forced-refresh validation share a dedicated\nfile decoder. Agent initialization's legacy/manual manifest fallback also decodes\nfrom its checked file without the setup-record ceiling.\n\nExisting file-type, path, descriptor-identity and alias checks remain in their\nrespective readers. Normal reads tolerate unknown fields; replacements reject\nfields they cannot round-trip. Schema versions 1–3 remain supported, and malformed,\ntruncated, trailing, and unsupported-version documents are rejected. Atomic\npublication remains in place. No storage migration is introduced.\n\nThe audit also covered refresh, status, doctor, MCP/agent surfaces, history\npublication, legacy identity publication, export, and bundle import. Archive\nvalidation and auxiliary state readers retain their existing independent rules.\n\n## Synthetic regression evidence\n\nAll fixture values are synthetic. Only sizes and aggregate structural counts\ncame from the verification helper.\n\n| Fixture | Bytes | Branches | Sessions | Warnings |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Session-heavy | 18,569,433 | 870 | 5,305 | 111 |\n| Warning-heavy | 17,220,291 | 389 | 2,633 | 6,238 |\n| Larger synthetic | 83,886,080 | 1,800 | 20,000 | 12,000 |\n\nFixtures retain the production duplication between top-level session aliases and\n`sources.sessions`. They use five or six source sections and distribute synthetic\nmetadata across session summaries to reach the target sizes. Round trips assert\nall decoded fields, including all records, remain equal.\n\nTests cover:\n\n- Production writer → normal reader, strict reader, and doctor health reader.\n- Production writer → coordinated Graph/Brain preview and instruction installation\n  through the no-setup-record fallback.\n- A 50,000-record retained seed-coverage manifest written and read back intact.\n- Failed serialization preserving the previous oversized manifest byte-for-byte.\n- Unknown-field compatibility, strict refusal to erase unknown data, and checked\n  forced-refresh validation on a synthetic oversized manifest.\n- Normal versions 1–3, missing/negative/newer versions, malformed/truncated JSON,\n  trailing JSON and trailing garbage.\n- Directory, symlink, hardlink and regular-file-to-FIFO-swap rejection, plus the\n  existing shared state-reader filesystem regression suite.\n- Unchanged limits for unrelated bounded reads and agent setup records.\n\nExecuted successfully:\n\n- `go test ./internal/cli ./internal/agentsetup -run 'Test.*(Manifest|Versioned|MemoryState|RepositoryBrain)' -count=1`\n  (CLI 11.698s; agent setup 0.679s).\n- `go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestBrainManifest' -count=1`\n  after adding cross-package installation and large unknown-field coverage (6.783s).\n- `go test ./...` passed (CLI 243.188s; all other packages passed or were cached).\n  The initial full run caught the new dedicated decoder missing from the\n  architecture-test registry; its strict-rewrite/tolerant-read contract is now\n  explicitly registered, and the full rerun passed.\n- Candidate binary build and `git diff --check`.\n\n## Memory and limitations\n\nDecoding from the file removes the arbitrary admission ceiling, not the memory\ncost. Go's decoder buffers the JSON value and materializes the manifest. Schema\nand body validation use multiple passes over the same descriptor; unknown fields\ncan add a tolerant retry. The writer still encodes the full document before atomic\nreplacement. Memory and CPU requirements grow with data size. No explicit resource\nbudget or recoverable out-of-memory guarantee is added. Other input ceilings and\nexisting seed projection policies are unchanged.\n\nRetained manifest loading requires neither a source checkout nor Git. Operations\nthat rebuild source-derived indexes still require their corresponding sources.\n\n## Sanitized manifest cohort validation\n\nValidation used locally built candidate binaries; installed binaries were not updated. Entire tracking remained enabled. Only opaque repository IDs and aggregate findings are reported. No paid inference, watcher installation, checkpoint repair, publication, source reset, or retained-data deletion was performed.\n\n### Existing manifests and normal readers\n\n- Initial status opened all 20 existing manifests unchanged; both original oversized manifests were verified before refresh.\n- Final status exit codes: {0: 20}. Doctor manifest checks: 20/20 OK. Final schemas: {3: 20}.\n- Overall doctor exit codes: {1: 2, 0: 18}; these are separate from manifest loading.\n- Coordinated candidate init-agents: {0: 20}; managed files changed: 0. The initial pass also succeeded on all 20 with zero changed files.\n\n| ID | Original manifest bytes | Final manifest bytes | Schema | Deterministic worktree refresh | Seconds | Status | Doctor manifest | Overall doctor |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| R01 | 18,569,433 | 14,039,589 | 3 | blocked by independent input limit | 327.01 | pass | pass | exit 1 |\n| R03 | 17,220,291 | 9,436,011 | 3 | completed | 1030.29 | pass | pass | exit 1 |\n| R02 | 9,251,020 | 9,250,985 | 3 | completed | 385.88 | pass | pass | exit 0 |\n\n### Refresh scope and remaining limitations\n\n- Deterministic processing used --agent none and --worktree, local-only/no-egress settings, and an explicitly disabled external embedder.\n- The first pass hit pre-existing dirty-worktree guards on 19 repositories. The two required large Brains and one already-running additional Brain were retried with --worktree. The other 17 full worktree refreshes were skipped once their regression read checks sufficed.\n- R01 completed session export and seed generation but history rebuilding encountered the unchanged 268,435,456-byte canonical-transcript limit (memory_input_too_large). The full refresh therefore remains incomplete; this is not a manifest-loading failure. No workaround that weakens that unrelated limit or truncates retained data was applied.\n- Post-refresh byte counts reflect normal production refresh writes. Support for the original larger inputs was established by opening their unchanged manifests first.\n\n### Other doctor findings\n\n- R01: memory_reconciliation.\n- R03: five persisted failures from the earlier setup run (seed, history, docs, semantic, coordinator). Doctor replays those historical records, including their old size-cap reasons; the current full refresh succeeded and the current manifest check is OK. The setup records were preserved, not cleared.\n\n### Checkpoint coverage warnings, separate from manifest failures\n\n- Remaining top-level checkpoint-scope warning entries: 500. Prior cohort baseline: 2,403.\n- Remaining top-level checkpoint-pointer warning entries: 5742. These counts classify top-level manifest warning entries by scope/pointer wording and do not count duplicated sources.sessions warnings again. They are not counts of unique missing checkpoints.\n\n| ID | Scope warnings | Pointer warnings |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| R01 | 53 | 0 |\n| R02 | 15 | 0 |\n| R03 | 34 | 0 |\n| R04 | 34 | 5361 |\n| R05 | 1 | 0 |\n| R09 | 1 | 0 |\n| R13 | 360 | 381 |\n| R14 | 1 | 0 |\n| R20 | 1 | 0 |\n\n### Retained-read probe\n\n- Both oversized Brains passed explicit-directory status with no Git executable on PATH and an unavailable ambient source root, from outside a repository. Actual source directories were left untouched, so this checks absent ambient source/Git rather than physically hiding the recorded source directory.\n- Real-cohort source code, transcript contents, identities, and raw diagnostics were kept out of the implementation report.\n\n\nFILE: docs/graph-identity-upgrades.md\n# Graph parser identity upgrades\n\nGraph's `identity_revision` is separate from its release version and its NDJSON\nschema version. It changes when a parser correction changes the IDs, names, or\nkinds of already indexed symbols. A missing field denotes the legacy rules.\nOrdinary provider releases need not change it.\n\nFor Graph trail 154 the revision is `js-ts-callable-scope-1`. JavaScript and\nTypeScript nested helpers become local functions qualified by their enclosing\ncallable; real methods no longer collide with phantom members. Anonymous\ncallables and callable class fields also have their bodies indexed correctly.\n\n## Upgrade order\n\n1. Install this Brain release before installing the Graph release carrying the\n   new identity revision. Keep the source repository and its Git objects available.\n2. Run `entire brain refresh` in each repository. Even if the source tree is\n   unchanged, a different provider identity revision rebuilds its semantic\n   snapshot and SQLite generation. Normal freshness checks report stale provider\n   identity until this happens. If the identity command is unavailable, refresh\n   warns and continues with source/artifact checks; it does not stamp identity as\n   verified or abort an otherwise usable warm refresh.\n3. Run `entire brain entities migrate` in each repository with existing entity\n   history. This carries the union of previously indexed commits across branches\n   into the current parser revision. Normal backfill also works, but follows its\n   own branch and budget; it does not automatically carry all older coverage.\n4. Check `entire brain entities history <current-qualified-name>`. A successful\n   migration retains the original commits and their checkpoint/session join.\n\n`entities migrate --graph-binary <entire-cli-wrapper>` selects a provider for\nmigration. The wrapper must support `graph version --json` and `graph diff`.\nRefresh's existing `--graph-binary` option likewise selects its provider.\nNormal status and history reads verify the provider installed as `entire`.\n\n## What migration changes\n\nMigration adds a separate namespace for the selected parser revision. It diffs\npreviously indexed commits missing from that namespace, using each commit's\nactual first parent and timestamp. Already present commits are skipped. A second\nmigration with no new input does no provider diff work and creates no commit.\n\nLegacy forward, reverse, alias and window keys remain byte-for-byte intact.\nRevisioned keys have the form\n`brain:entities-revision:<hex revision>:<hex legacy key>`. No global active-revision\nmarker is written and no old records are tombstoned. Readers and writers choose\nthe namespace from their local provider; older Brain versions ignore the new\nnamespace. Exchanging the added records therefore does not switch an older\npeer's parser or delete its history. An older peer can keep extending legacy\nhistory; another migration carries those newly indexed commits forward later.\nThe current MetaStore is local-only; tests exercise serialized record exchange,\nnot a hosted peer-sync deployment.\n\nSource Git commits, checkpoint trailers, session manifests, authored facts,\nretirements, and unrelated git-meta keys are untouched. Corrected current names\nretain the original commit/checkpoint/session join. Old parser spellings stay\navailable in the legacy namespace, without invented source-level rename aliases.\nSource coverage windows are copied only when the destination has no window;\nexisting destination windows stay intact. The query cache is keyed by both\nmetadata tip and parser revision, so switching or rolling back the provider\ncannot silently reuse the other parser's history cache.\n\nNormal backfill and unchanged-history ticks use namespace-specific cursor and\ncommit-key reads. They **do not scan historical delta documents**. Queries still\nmaterialize history when their derived cache is invalidated, as before; that\nrebuild also caches a warning if other revisions contain commits missing from\nthe selected revision. Offline history uses the last cached revision with a\nprovider-verification warning; `entities show` uses legacy history when both provider identity and a local\ncache are unavailable. Explicit backfill can also fall back to legacy history\nwith a warning, but rejects any diff that declares a different identity revision.\n\n## Publication and limits\n\nMigration captures the metadata tip and computes all diffs **without the shared\nwrite lock**. It then tries the lock without waiting, verifies the tip is still\nthe captured value, and publishes one atomic additive update. A concurrent fact,\nbackfill or metadata-import write causes publication to abort; rerun migration.\nNo concurrent work is overwritten and no partial migration is published.\n\nA failed/cancelled provider call, unavailable source commit, malformed or partial\ndiff envelope, wrong base/head, changing provider revision, or truncated delta\nalso aborts. Entity mapping follows the same rules as backfill: file-only changes\nand records without a resolvable name/path contribute no entity, and a missing\nkind is retained according to the existing contract. These do not abort migration;\nprovider warnings about partial results still do.\nMigration is capped at 20,000 pending commits and 200,000 new entity changes.\nAlready migrated commits do not count against the cap, so small incremental\nmigrations and no-op retries work on larger historical corpora. It never silently migrates a subset above those limits.\n\nThis removes the long lock around parser subprocesses, not all publication cost:\nMetaStore still materializes and serializes the final state under its write lock.\nThat cost scales with store size. Retaining revisions also consumes additional\nstorage, and contention can require recomputation on retry. No automatic pruning\nor cross-peer deletion protocol is introduced by this change.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe unit suite tests legacy documents with a global identity marker, unchanged\nticks without delta decoding, revision isolation, serialized exchange with a\nlegacy writer, idempotence, lock availability during parsing, concurrent-write\nrefusal, malformed provider output, coverage and unrelated-memory preservation.\nTo run the real provider upgrade:\n\n```sh\nBRAIN_TEST_GRAPH_BEFORE=/path/to/old/entire-graph \\\nBRAIN_TEST_GRAPH_AFTER=/path/to/new/entire-graph \\\n  go test -race ./internal/cli -run '^TestGraphIdentityUpgradeLive$' -count=1\n```\n\nThe live test creates an isolated two-commit JS/TS repository, indexes it with the\nold provider, switches binaries without editing source, refreshes the semantic\nsnapshot, runs the migration command, and checks corrected IDs, relations,\nhistory for both commits, checkpoint links, unchanged source HEAD, and reuse of\nthe now-current snapshot. It also switches back to the old provider and then\nforward again without changing metadata, verifying cache isolation and preserved\nlegacy history. The test is explicitly skipped if either binary is not\nsupplied; normal CI alone does not prove cross-version integration.\n\n\nFILE: docs/shared-fact-identity.md\n# Shared fact content identity\n\nShared fact-set sync verifies that each record's ID equals `RecordID(text,\npaths)` and that its stored paths already equal `NormalizePaths(paths)`. The\ncheck runs on local facts before sync publishes them and on shared heads read\nby sync, proposal resolution, and CLI proposal reconciliation.\n\nThis binds an ID to normalized text and canonical taxonomy paths. It prevents\na peer from replacing that content while retaining another statement's ID.\n`NormalizeText` ignores case and whitespace differences; the check does not\nprevent substitutions whose meaning depends on those differences.\n\n## What this does not authenticate\n\nThe check is not a record signature. Status, superseded-by, origin, kind,\nlocus, confidence, related IDs, branch, and provenance are outside the content\nID. A peer with write access to the shared head can still change those fields\nunder a valid ID, including retracting a statement or claiming a verified\nanchor. A valid content ID is not evidence that a lifecycle change or provenance\nclaim was authorized. Closing that trust gap requires an authenticated record\nand lifecycle protocol; changing this content check alone does not provide it.\n\nThese checks cover the shared fact-set transport. They do not add identity\nverification to the separate hosted Search/Get/MultiGet response interfaces.\nTreat hosted content and provenance as untrusted historical evidence.\n\n## Rejected heads and recovery\n\nA malformed local record is rejected before any request publishes it. Repair or\nregenerate that record through its trusted authoring source before retrying.\n\nAn invalid shared record stops the whole sync or resolution. The client does\nnot silently discard a record and publish a replacement head: doing that could\nturn corruption into loss of another member's facts, and content IDs cannot\nauthenticate the remaining lifecycle or provenance fields. Other members'\nlocal stores remain available.\n\nThere is currently no automatic quarantine or client-side repair command for a\nmalformed shared head. Recovery requires the hosted service operator to restore\nor repair the head from a trusted source through its storage administration,\nthen retry sync. Preserve the rejected version for diagnosis. This deliberate\nfail-closed policy trades shared-sync availability for avoiding an unreviewed\nrewrite; it does not prevent a malicious writer from causing denial of service.\n\n\nFILE: docs/recall-evidence.md\n# Deterministic evidence recall (experimental)\n\n`recall --evidence` returns original conversation blocks with source citations.\nIt uses lexical retrieval and makes no provider or embedding-model call. Ordinary\n`recall` continues to return distilled facts.\n\n```sh\nentire brain recall \"What approval does release require?\" --evidence --json\nentire brain recall \"What approval does release require?\" --evidence --branch main --k 20 --evidence-bytes 8192 --json\n```\n\nFor example, separate sessions might say \"Release requires owner approval\" and\n\"Release also requires security approval.\" If both blocks are retrieved and fit\nthe output budget, both appear verbatim with independent citations. Brain does\nnot infer that either statement replaces the other, combine them into a new\nfact, or establish that either statement is still current. The consuming agent\nmust interpret the evidence and acknowledge missing or conflicting context.\n\n## Retrieval and limits\n\n- Searches canonical sessions on the selected branch. Matching distilled facts\n  supply source-session handles only; their claims never replace source bytes.\n- `--k` limits candidate sessions (default 10, range 1–128). At most 128 eligible\n  sessions are scanned in manifest order, admitting at most 32 MiB of transcript\n  input through the hardened canonical reader. Larger corpora may be incomplete.\n- Lexical matching uses positive term coverage and simple plural matching. Each\n  matched session contributes from a ranked pool of up to 128 blocks, scanning\n  at most 4,096 blocks. Admission across sessions proceeds in rounds and gives\n  both user and assistant messages an opportunity. The global pool is at most\n  128 blocks. Its compact-text-and-metadata admission limit is the larger of\n  24 KiB and three times `--evidence-bytes` (up to 3 MiB). This preserves the\n  default candidate pool while allowing larger requests to retrieve larger\n  blocks and more context before output packing.\n- The heuristic candidate budget is distinct from the exact public output\n  budget. `input_truncated` and a warning report candidate limits, including\n  `--k`; the warning includes the active candidate byte limit. These limits do\n  not measure recall accuracy or guarantee corpus coverage. Larger output\n  budgets do not increase session or block count limits, or guarantee a full\n  response budget will be used.\n- Supports plain text, Claude/Pi messages, Codex user/assistant events and\n  response messages, simple role/content JSONL, and OpenCode message documents.\n  Structured hidden reasoning, tool output, system instructions and metadata\n  are not returned as conversation text. Plain text has no role classification.\n- Malformed JSON, invalid UTF-8, unavailable files, and sources with no supported\n  conversation fields yield warnings and `partial` or `unavailable` retrieval.\n  Known non-conversation records are ignored. Ambiguous session identities fail\n  closed rather than guessing which source supplied a fact anchor, including\n  conflicts later in the manifest than the source-read limits.\n\n## Citation contract\n\nEach evidence span includes `id`, `session_id`, `branch`, canonical Brain-relative\n`path`, `line`, `source_sha256`, `content_sha256`, `start_byte`, `end_byte`, and\nunchanged `text`. Structured sources also include a `json_pointer`; role and\ntimestamp are included when available.\n\nTo verify a citation:\n\n1. Read the canonical file at `path` and verify `source_sha256`.\n2. For JSONL, decode the record at one-based `line`. For a JSON document, decode\n   the document (`line` is 1). Resolve `json_pointer` to a string. For plain text,\n   the pointer is absent and the addressed content is the whole file.\n3. Verify the addressed content's UTF-8 bytes against `content_sha256`.\n4. Slice those bytes at `[start_byte, end_byte)` and compare them with `text`.\n\nOffsets count UTF-8 bytes, not characters. Blank-line boundaries preserve code\nfences, table rows, and original line endings. IDs bind the source version and\nrange; changing file bytes changes the ID. Hashes establish byte identity, not\nthe truth, authorship, or current applicability of a statement.\n\n## Output and privacy\n\n`--evidence-bytes` defaults to 8,192 (range 2–1,048,576). It bounds the compact Go\nJSON encoding of the **evidence array**, including citations, escaping, commas,\nand brackets. Enclosing metadata and pretty-print whitespace are outside this\nbudget, so it is not a full-response transport cap. Whole blocks that do not fit\nare skipped; later smaller blocks can still fit. `omitted_ids` and `truncated`\nreport those omissions. A budget of 2 returns `[]`.\nSerialization errors abort the buffered response; they are never treated as\nzero-byte spans or reported as budget omissions.\n\nThe response reports `schema_version: 1`, `selection_mode: deterministic`,\n`effective_engine: canonical_sessions_lexical_with_fact_anchors`, counts, byte\nusage, retrieval state, coverage scope, and warnings. Empty evidence does not\nprove that no answer exists.\n\nSession exclusions, source containment, and derived-state privacy guards apply.\nThe existing Brain privacy lock covers source inspection through buffered output\nso cleanup cannot commit between checking a source and emitting its bytes.\nThis command does not write facts, inferred relationships, or semantic vectors.\nIt works under `ENTIRE_BRAIN_NO_EGRESS=1`.\n\nSupported options are `--branch`, `--k`, `--evidence-bytes`, and `--json`.\n`--no-semantic` is harmless because this mode is already lexical. Explicit\nprovider, expansion, fact-filter, temporal-eligibility, and semantic-cache flags\nare rejected. `--evidence-bytes` requires `--evidence`.\n\nThis feature is CLI-only. It adds no MCP schema, Graph integration, model\nselector, temporal inference, or benchmark accuracy guarantee.\n\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"\nFILE: docs/operations.md\n# Operations\n\n`entire-brain` is a local Entire CLI plugin. It owns the persistent local brain,\nsemantic SQLite stores, bundle import/export, graph query surfaces, MCP stdio\ntools, and benchmark/evidence commands.\n\n## Full Install\n\n```sh\nscripts/install.sh\n```\n\nThe end-to-end installer, and the one the README documents. It has four effects\non the machine and no others: it builds and registers the `entire-graph`\nsemantic provider, builds and registers `entire-brain`, writes the default\nplugin configuration (`entire brain config init`), and runs `entire brain\ndoctor`. Both binaries are built inside their checkouts and linked into Entire's\nmanaged plugin directory; nothing is installed system-wide.\n\nBefore it builds anything it checks every prerequisite -- `git`, the `entire`\nCLI, a Go toolchain at least as new as the `go` directive in `go.mod`, and a C\ncompiler for `entire-graph`'s tree-sitter cgo bindings -- and reports all the\nmissing ones in one pass with the fix for each. `entire-brain` itself is a\npure-Go build; the cgo requirement is the provider's.\n\nNeither checkout has to have a particular name, and the two do not have to be\nsiblings. The provider is resolved by three routes, and the one taken is printed\nunder `==> entire-graph semantic provider`:\n\n| Route | Source | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR` | Used exactly as given. A value that is not an `entire-graph` checkout is a hard error, never a silent fallback. |\n| 2 | A checkout on this machine | Siblings of the brain checkout and of the main worktree when it is a linked one, the route-3 cache, and the usual source directories under `$HOME`. Accepted only when `cmd/entire-graph` and `scripts/install-local.sh` are both present, so a directory that merely has the name is skipped. |\n| 3 | A shallow clone | `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_REPO` (default the public `entireio/entire-graph`) into `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_CACHE` (default `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`). Later runs reuse it and refresh it with a shallow fetch, and keep going on the cached copy when the network is unavailable. |\n\n`ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` disables route 3, so an air-gapped machine fails with\nan explanation naming all three routes rather than blocking on a clone.\n\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` wraps this for a machine with nothing: it clones\n`entire-brain` when it is not already present -- falling back to `gh repo clone`\nfor the private-repo credentials -- and then execs `scripts/install.sh`. There is\ndeliberately no `curl | sh` form: `entireio/entire-brain` is private, so\n`raw.githubusercontent.com` serves 404 for it without a token; the source has to\nbe cloned regardless because the install is a source build; and cloning first\nputs the script that registers your plugins on disk at a reviewable commit\nbefore it runs.\n\n### After the install: onboard a repository\n\nThe installer is machine-scoped. It gives no repository a brain, and it is not\nthe last command a user runs. That is `entire brain setup`, once per repository:\n\n```sh\ncd /path/to/your/repo\nentire brain setup\n```\n\nOperationally the thing to know is that a default `setup` does two things beyond\nthe deterministic build, both of which spend tokens: it starts a detached fact\nbackfill (capped at 25 sessions per pass, `--no-backfill` to skip), and it\ninstalls **one machine-wide watcher as a persistent service, by default, with no\nprompt** — a launchd agent with `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive`, or a systemd user unit\nwith `Restart=always`. There is no prompt, but there is a pre-flight line: a run\nthat will register a watcher opens with it, before any work, naming the unit\nlabel and the path it will write and giving both `--no-daemon` and\n`entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`. `--no-daemon` skips it, `entire brain\nsetup --uninstall-daemon` removes it, and `entire brain setup --no-backfill\n--no-daemon` spends nothing and changes no service. See the reference's\n[step 3](reference.md#3-onboard-a-repository-entire-brain-setup) for the full\nphase table and the ordering against `entire enable`.\n\n## Local Install (brain only)\n\n```sh\nscripts/install-local.sh\n```\n\nThe script builds `./entire-brain`, installs it with `entire plugin install\n./entire-brain --force`, and prints `entire brain version`. It fails before\nwriting anything if the parent `entire` CLI is not on `PATH`. It does not touch\nthe provider, so `entire graph` has to be installed separately for the semantic\nlayer.\n\nThe default build is the pure-Go stack. To install the optional cgo vector stack:\n\n```sh\nBUILD_TAGS=\"brain_cgo sqlite_fts5\" scripts/install-local.sh\n```\n\n## Release Archives\n\n```sh\nscripts/release.sh\n```\n\nThe release script writes `dist/release-<version>/` with one `.tar.gz` archive per\ntarget and a `SHA256SUMS` manifest. `VERSION=<value>` overrides the version;\notherwise the script uses `git describe --tags --always --dirty`.\n\nDefault targets are:\n\n- `darwin/amd64`\n- `darwin/arm64`\n- `linux/amd64`\n- `linux/arm64`\n- `windows/amd64`\n\nSet `ENTIRE_RELEASE_TARGETS` to override that list:\n\n```sh\nENTIRE_RELEASE_TARGETS=\"linux/amd64\" scripts/release.sh\n```\n\nThe default release build uses the pure-Go fallback stack with `CGO_ENABLED=0`,\nwhich is the portable distribution path. To build cgo artifacts, set\n`BUILD_TAGS=\"brain_cgo sqlite_fts5\"` and provide the matching platform compiler.\nThe script records checksums for artifacts it successfully builds; it also signs\narchives when a local signing key is explicitly configured:\n\n- `COSIGN_KEY=<key-ref>` with `cosign` on `PATH` writes `<archive>.sig`.\n- `GPG_SIGNING_KEY=<key-id>` with `gpg` on `PATH` writes `<archive>.asc`.\n\nThe script does not publish artifacts.\n\n## Shared Baselines\n\nBrain bundle import/export is the policy-controlled shared baseline path for\nthis repository. A bundle carries the semantic index, seed, docs and facts by\ndefault; captured sessions and the history and patterns projections built from\nthem travel only under `--include-sessions`; session tombstones, privacy\ntransactions and local worker state are never bundled. Use:\n\n```sh\nentire brain bundle export --output /tmp/repo-brain.tar\nentire brain bundle import /tmp/repo-brain.tar --sha256 <sha256>\n```\n\nThe implementation validates checksums, schema compatibility, repo policy, path\ntraversal, symlink safety, size caps, and redaction before import. Import\nrefuses to overwrite a layer the destination brain already holds unless\n`--overwrite` is passed, and the refusal happens before anything is written.\nExport prints exactly which layers it wrote and which it left out. Shared\nbaselines remain explicit local files; there is no remote publishing or registry\ndiscovery.\n\n\nFILE: docs/manifest-loading-validation.md\n# Manifest loading validation\n\nImplementation base: `origin/main` at `4623fab909ebefdab35087dd682fde387917ec62`.\n\n## Implementation\n\nCanonical Brain manifests no longer use the auxiliary 16 MiB read ceiling or\nthe corresponding writer rejection. CLI loading, strict replacement checks,\nstatus/doctor health inspection, and forced-refresh validation share a dedicated\nfile decoder. Agent initialization's legacy/manual manifest fallback also decodes\nfrom its checked file without the setup-record ceiling.\n\nExisting file-type, path, descriptor-identity and alias checks remain in their\nrespective readers. Normal reads tolerate unknown fields; replacements reject\nfields they cannot round-trip. Schema versions 1–3 remain supported, and malformed,\ntruncated, trailing, and unsupported-version documents are rejected. Atomic\npublication remains in place. No storage migration is introduced.\n\nThe audit also covered refresh, status, doctor, MCP/agent surfaces, history\npublication, legacy identity publication, export, and bundle import. Archive\nvalidation and auxiliary state readers retain their existing independent rules.\n\n## Synthetic regression evidence\n\nAll fixture values are synthetic. Only sizes and aggregate structural counts\ncame from the verification helper.\n\n| Fixture | Bytes | Branches | Sessions | Warnings |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Session-heavy | 18,569,433 | 870 | 5,305 | 111 |\n| Warning-heavy | 17,220,291 | 389 | 2,633 | 6,238 |\n| Larger synthetic | 83,886,080 | 1,800 | 20,000 | 12,000 |\n\nFixtures retain the production duplication between top-level session aliases and\n`sources.sessions`. They use five or six source sections and distribute synthetic\nmetadata across session summaries to reach the target sizes. Round trips assert\nall decoded fields, including all records, remain equal.\n\nTests cover:\n\n- Production writer → normal reader, strict reader, and doctor health reader.\n- Production writer → coordinated Graph/Brain preview and instruction installation\n  through the no-setup-record fallback.\n- A 50,000-record retained seed-coverage manifest written and read back intact.\n- Failed serialization preserving the previous oversized manifest byte-for-byte.\n- Unknown-field compatibility, strict refusal to erase unknown data, and checked\n  forced-refresh validation on a synthetic oversized manifest.\n- Normal versions 1–3, missing/negative/newer versions, malformed/truncated JSON,\n  trailing JSON and trailing garbage.\n- Directory, symlink, hardlink and regular-file-to-FIFO-swap rejection, plus the\n  existing shared state-reader filesystem regression suite.\n- Unchanged limits for unrelated bounded reads and agent setup records.\n\nExecuted successfully:\n\n- `go test ./internal/cli ./internal/agentsetup -run 'Test.*(Manifest|Versioned|MemoryState|RepositoryBrain)' -count=1`\n  (CLI 11.698s; agent setup 0.679s).\n- `go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestBrainManifest' -count=1`\n  after adding cross-package installation and large unknown-field coverage (6.783s).\n- `go test ./...` passed (CLI 243.188s; all other packages passed or were cached).\n  The initial full run caught the new dedicated decoder missing from the\n  architecture-test registry; its strict-rewrite/tolerant-read contract is now\n  explicitly registered, and the full rerun passed.\n- Candidate binary build and `git diff --check`.\n\n## Memory and limitations\n\nDecoding from the file removes the arbitrary admission ceiling, not the memory\ncost. Go's decoder buffers the JSON value and materializes the manifest. Schema\nand body validation use multiple passes over the same descriptor; unknown fields\ncan add a tolerant retry. The writer still encodes the full document before atomic\nreplacement. Memory and CPU requirements grow with data size. No explicit resource\nbudget or recoverable out-of-memory guarantee is added. Other input ceilings and\nexisting seed projection policies are unchanged.\n\nRetained manifest loading requires neither a source checkout nor Git. Operations\nthat rebuild source-derived indexes still require their corresponding sources.\n\n## Sanitized manifest cohort validation\n\nValidation used locally built candidate binaries; installed binaries were not updated. Entire tracking remained enabled. Only opaque repository IDs and aggregate findings are reported. No paid inference, watcher installation, checkpoint repair, publication, source reset, or retained-data deletion was performed.\n\n### Existing manifests and normal readers\n\n- Initial status opened all 20 existing manifests unchanged; both original oversized manifests were verified before refresh.\n- Final status exit codes: {0: 20}. Doctor manifest checks: 20/20 OK. Final schemas: {3: 20}.\n- Overall doctor exit codes: {1: 2, 0: 18}; these are separate from manifest loading.\n- Coordinated candidate init-agents: {0: 20}; managed files changed: 0. The initial pass also succeeded on all 20 with zero changed files.\n\n| ID | Original manifest bytes | Final manifest bytes | Schema | Deterministic worktree refresh | Seconds | Status | Doctor manifest | Overall doctor |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| R01 | 18,569,433 | 14,039,589 | 3 | blocked by independent input limit | 327.01 | pass | pass | exit 1 |\n| R03 | 17,220,291 | 9,436,011 | 3 | completed | 1030.29 | pass | pass | exit 1 |\n| R02 | 9,251,020 | 9,250,985 | 3 | completed | 385.88 | pass | pass | exit 0 |\n\n### Refresh scope and remaining limitations\n\n- Deterministic processing used --agent none and --worktree, local-only/no-egress settings, and an explicitly disabled external embedder.\n- The first pass hit pre-existing dirty-worktree guards on 19 repositories. The two required large Brains and one already-running additional Brain were retried with --worktree. The other 17 full worktree refreshes were skipped once their regression read checks sufficed.\n- R01 completed session export and seed generation but history rebuilding encountered the unchanged 268,435,456-byte canonical-transcript limit (memory_input_too_large). The full refresh therefore remains incomplete; this is not a manifest-loading failure. No workaround that weakens that unrelated limit or truncates retained data was applied.\n- Post-refresh byte counts reflect normal production refresh writes. Support for the original larger inputs was established by opening their unchanged manifests first.\n\n### Other doctor findings\n\n- R01: memory_reconciliation.\n- R03: five persisted failures from the earlier setup run (seed, history, docs, semantic, coordinator). Doctor replays those historical records, including their old size-cap reasons; the current full refresh succeeded and the current manifest check is OK. The setup records were preserved, not cleared.\n\n### Checkpoint coverage warnings, separate from manifest failures\n\n- Remaining top-level checkpoint-scope warning entries: 500. Prior cohort baseline: 2,403.\n- Remaining top-level checkpoint-pointer warning entries: 5742. These counts classify top-level manifest warning entries by scope/pointer wording and do not count duplicated sources.sessions warnings again. They are not counts of unique missing checkpoints.\n\n| ID | Scope warnings | Pointer warnings |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| R01 | 53 | 0 |\n| R02 | 15 | 0 |\n| R03 | 34 | 0 |\n| R04 | 34 | 5361 |\n| R05 | 1 | 0 |\n| R09 | 1 | 0 |\n| R13 | 360 | 381 |\n| R14 | 1 | 0 |\n| R20 | 1 | 0 |\n\n### Retained-read probe\n\n- Both oversized Brains passed explicit-directory status with no Git executable on PATH and an unavailable ambient source root, from outside a repository. Actual source directories were left untouched, so this checks absent ambient source/Git rather than physically hiding the recorded source directory.\n- Real-cohort source code, transcript contents, identities, and raw diagnostics were kept out of the implementation report.\n\n\nFILE: docs/graph-identity-upgrades.md\n# Graph parser identity upgrades\n\nGraph's `identity_revision` is separate from its release version and its NDJSON\nschema version. It changes when a parser correction changes the IDs, names, or\nkinds of already indexed symbols. A missing field denotes the legacy rules.\nOrdinary provider releases need not change it.\n\nFor Graph trail 154 the revision is `js-ts-callable-scope-1`. JavaScript and\nTypeScript nested helpers become local functions qualified by their enclosing\ncallable; real methods no longer collide with phantom members. Anonymous\ncallables and callable class fields also have their bodies indexed correctly.\n\n## Upgrade order\n\n1. Install this Brain release before installing the Graph release carrying the\n   new identity revision. Keep the source repository and its Git objects available.\n2. Run `entire brain refresh` in each repository. Even if the source tree is\n   unchanged, a different provider identity revision rebuilds its semantic\n   snapshot and SQLite generation. Normal freshness checks report stale provider\n   identity until this happens. If the identity command is unavailable, refresh\n   warns and continues with source/artifact checks; it does not stamp identity as\n   verified or abort an otherwise usable warm refresh.\n3. Run `entire brain entities migrate` in each repository with existing entity\n   history. This carries the union of previously indexed commits across branches\n   into the current parser revision. Normal backfill also works, but follows its\n   own branch and budget; it does not automatically carry all older coverage.\n4. Check `entire brain entities history <current-qualified-name>`. A successful\n   migration retains the original commits and their checkpoint/session join.\n\n`entities migrate --graph-binary <entire-cli-wrapper>` selects a provider for\nmigration. The wrapper must support `graph version --json` and `graph diff`.\nRefresh's existing `--graph-binary` option likewise selects its provider.\nNormal status and history reads verify the provider installed as `entire`.\n\n## What migration changes\n\nMigration adds a separate namespace for the selected parser revision. It diffs\npreviously indexed commits missing from that namespace, using each commit's\nactual first parent and timestamp. Already present commits are skipped. A second\nmigration with no new input does no provider diff work and creates no commit.\n\nLegacy forward, reverse, alias and window keys remain byte-for-byte intact.\nRevisioned keys have the form\n`brain:entities-revision:<hex revision>:<hex legacy key>`. No global active-revision\nmarker is written and no old records are tombstoned. Readers and writers choose\nthe namespace from their local provider; older Brain versions ignore the new\nnamespace. Exchanging the added records therefore does not switch an older\npeer's parser or delete its history. An older peer can keep extending legacy\nhistory; another migration carries those newly indexed commits forward later.\nThe current MetaStore is local-only; tests exercise serialized record exchange,\nnot a hosted peer-sync deployment.\n\nSource Git commits, checkpoint trailers, session manifests, authored facts,\nretirements, and unrelated git-meta keys are untouched. Corrected current names\nretain the original commit/checkpoint/session join. Old parser spellings stay\navailable in the legacy namespace, without invented source-level rename aliases.\nSource coverage windows are copied only when the destination has no window;\nexisting destination windows stay intact. The query cache is keyed by both\nmetadata tip and parser revision, so switching or rolling back the provider\ncannot silently reuse the other parser's history cache.\n\nNormal backfill and unchanged-history ticks use namespace-specific cursor and\ncommit-key reads. They **do not scan historical delta documents**. Queries still\nmaterialize history when their derived cache is invalidated, as before; that\nrebuild also caches a warning if other revisions contain commits missing from\nthe selected revision. Offline history uses the last cached revision with a\nprovider-verification warning; `entities show` uses legacy history when both provider identity and a local\ncache are unavailable. Explicit backfill can also fall back to legacy history\nwith a warning, but rejects any diff that declares a different identity revision.\n\n## Publication and limits\n\nMigration captures the metadata tip and computes all diffs **without the shared\nwrite lock**. It then tries the lock without waiting, verifies the tip is still\nthe captured value, and publishes one atomic additive update. A concurrent fact,\nbackfill or metadata-import write causes publication to abort; rerun migration.\nNo concurrent work is overwritten and no partial migration is published.\n\nA failed/cancelled provider call, unavailable source commit, malformed or partial\ndiff envelope, wrong base/head, changing provider revision, or truncated delta\nalso aborts. Entity mapping follows the same rules as backfill: file-only changes\nand records without a resolvable name/path contribute no entity, and a missing\nkind is retained according to the existing contract. These do not abort migration;\nprovider warnings about partial results still do.\nMigration is capped at 20,000 pending commits and 200,000 new entity changes.\nAlready migrated commits do not count against the cap, so small incremental\nmigrations and no-op retries work on larger historical corpora. It never silently migrates a subset above those limits.\n\nThis removes the long lock around parser subprocesses, not all publication cost:\nMetaStore still materializes and serializes the final state under its write lock.\nThat cost scales with store size. Retaining revisions also consumes additional\nstorage, and contention can require recomputation on retry. No automatic pruning\nor cross-peer deletion protocol is introduced by this change.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe unit suite tests legacy documents with a global identity marker, unchanged\nticks without delta decoding, revision isolation, serialized exchange with a\nlegacy writer, idempotence, lock availability during parsing, concurrent-write\nrefusal, malformed provider output, coverage and unrelated-memory preservation.\nTo run the real provider upgrade:\n\n```sh\nBRAIN_TEST_GRAPH_BEFORE=/path/to/old/entire-graph \\\nBRAIN_TEST_GRAPH_AFTER=/path/to/new/entire-graph \\\n  go test -race ./internal/cli -run '^TestGraphIdentityUpgradeLive$' -count=1\n```\n\nThe live test creates an isolated two-commit JS/TS repository, indexes it with the\nold provider, switches binaries without editing source, refreshes the semantic\nsnapshot, runs the migration command, and checks corrected IDs, relations,\nhistory for both commits, checkpoint links, unchanged source HEAD, and reuse of\nthe now-current snapshot. It also switches back to the old provider and then\nforward again without changing metadata, verifying cache isolation and preserved\nlegacy history. The test is explicitly skipped if either binary is not\nsupplied; normal CI alone does not prove cross-version integration.\n\n\nFILE: docs/shared-fact-identity.md\n# Shared fact content identity\n\nShared fact-set sync verifies that each record's ID equals `RecordID(text,\npaths)` and that its stored paths already equal `NormalizePaths(paths)`. The\ncheck runs on local facts before sync publishes them and on shared heads read\nby sync, proposal resolution, and CLI proposal reconciliation.\n\nThis binds an ID to normalized text and canonical taxonomy paths. It prevents\na peer from replacing that content while retaining another statement's ID.\n`NormalizeText` ignores case and whitespace differences; the check does not\nprevent substitutions whose meaning depends on those differences.\n\n## What this does not authenticate\n\nThe check is not a record signature. Status, superseded-by, origin, kind,\nlocus, confidence, related IDs, branch, and provenance are outside the content\nID. A peer with write access to the shared head can still change those fields\nunder a valid ID, including retracting a statement or claiming a verified\nanchor. A valid content ID is not evidence that a lifecycle change or provenance\nclaim was authorized. Closing that trust gap requires an authenticated record\nand lifecycle protocol; changing this content check alone does not provide it.\n\nThese checks cover the shared fact-set transport. They do not add identity\nverification to the separate hosted Search/Get/MultiGet response interfaces.\nTreat hosted content and provenance as untrusted historical evidence.\n\n## Rejected heads and recovery\n\nA malformed local record is rejected before any request publishes it. Repair or\nregenerate that record through its trusted authoring source before retrying.\n\nAn invalid shared record stops the whole sync or resolution. The client does\nnot silently discard a record and publish a replacement head: doing that could\nturn corruption into loss of another member's facts, and content IDs cannot\nauthenticate the remaining lifecycle or provenance fields. Other members'\nlocal stores remain available.\n\nThere is currently no automatic quarantine or client-side repair command for a\nmalformed shared head. Recovery requires the hosted service operator to restore\nor repair the head from a trusted source through its storage administration,\nthen retry sync. Preserve the rejected version for diagnosis. This deliberate\nfail-closed policy trades shared-sync availability for avoiding an unreviewed\nrewrite; it does not prevent a malicious writer from causing denial of service.\n\n\nFILE: docs/recall-evidence.md\n# Deterministic evidence recall (experimental)\n\n`recall --evidence` returns original conversation blocks with source citations.\nIt uses lexical retrieval and makes no provider or embedding-model call. Ordinary\n`recall` continues to return distilled facts.\n\n```sh\nentire brain recall \"What approval does release require?\" --evidence --json\nentire brain recall \"What approval does release require?\" --evidence --branch main --k 20 --evidence-bytes 8192 --json\n```\n\nFor example, separate sessions might say \"Release requires owner approval\" and\n\"Release also requires security approval.\" If both blocks are retrieved and fit\nthe output budget, both appear verbatim with independent citations. Brain does\nnot infer that either statement replaces the other, combine them into a new\nfact, or establish that either statement is still current. The consuming agent\nmust interpret the evidence and acknowledge missing or conflicting context.\n\n## Retrieval and limits\n\n- Searches canonical sessions on the selected branch. Matching distilled facts\n  supply source-session handles only; their claims never replace source bytes.\n- `--k` limits candidate sessions (default 10, range 1–128). At most 128 eligible\n  sessions are scanned in manifest order, admitting at most 32 MiB of transcript\n  input through the hardened canonical reader. Larger corpora may be incomplete.\n- Lexical matching uses positive term coverage and simple plural matching. Each\n  matched session contributes from a ranked pool of up to 128 blocks, scanning\n  at most 4,096 blocks. Admission across sessions proceeds in rounds and gives\n  both user and assistant messages an opportunity. The global pool is at most\n  128 blocks. Its compact-text-and-metadata admission limit is the larger of\n  24 KiB and three times `--evidence-bytes` (up to 3 MiB). This preserves the\n  default candidate pool while allowing larger requests to retrieve larger\n  blocks and more context before output packing.\n- The heuristic candidate budget is distinct from the exact public output\n  budget. `input_truncated` and a warning report candidate limits, including\n  `--k`; the warning includes the active candidate byte limit. These limits do\n  not measure recall accuracy or guarantee corpus coverage. Larger output\n  budgets do not increase session or block count limits, or guarantee a full\n  response budget will be used.\n- Supports plain text, Claude/Pi messages, Codex user/assistant events and\n  response messages, simple role/content JSONL, and OpenCode message documents.\n  Structured hidden reasoning, tool output, system instructions and metadata\n  are not returned as conversation text. Plain text has no role classification.\n- Malformed JSON, invalid UTF-8, unavailable files, and sources with no supported\n  conversation fields yield warnings and `partial` or `unavailable` retrieval.\n  Known non-conversation records are ignored. Ambiguous session identities fail\n  closed rather than guessing which source supplied a fact anchor, including\n  conflicts later in the manifest than the source-read limits.\n\n## Citation contract\n\nEach evidence span includes `id`, `session_id`, `branch`, canonical Brain-relative\n`path`, `line`, `source_sha256`, `content_sha256`, `start_byte`, `end_byte`, and\nunchanged `text`. Structured sources also include a `json_pointer`; role and\ntimestamp are included when available.\n\nTo verify a citation:\n\n1. Read the canonical file at `path` and verify `source_sha256`.\n2. For JSONL, decode the record at one-based `line`. For a JSON document, decode\n   the document (`line` is 1). Resolve `json_pointer` to a string. For plain text,\n   the pointer is absent and the addressed content is the whole file.\n3. Verify the addressed content's UTF-8 bytes against `content_sha256`.\n4. Slice those bytes at `[start_byte, end_byte)` and compare them with `text`.\n\nOffsets count UTF-8 bytes, not characters. Blank-line boundaries preserve code\nfences, table rows, and original line endings. IDs bind the source version and\nrange; changing file bytes changes the ID. Hashes establish byte identity, not\nthe truth, authorship, or current applicability of a statement.\n\n## Output and privacy\n\n`--evidence-bytes` defaults to 8,192 (range 2–1,048,576). It bounds the compact Go\nJSON encoding of the **evidence array**, including citations, escaping, commas,\nand brackets. Enclosing metadata and pretty-print whitespace are outside this\nbudget, so it is not a full-response transport cap. Whole blocks that do not fit\nare skipped; later smaller blocks can still fit. `omitted_ids` and `truncated`\nreport those omissions. A budget of 2 returns `[]`.\nSerialization errors abort the buffered response; they are never treated as\nzero-byte spans or reported as budget omissions.\n\nThe response reports `schema_version: 1`, `selection_mode: deterministic`,\n`effective_engine: canonical_sessions_lexical_with_fact_anchors`, counts, byte\nusage, retrieval state, coverage scope, and warnings. Empty evidence does not\nprove that no answer exists.\n\nSession exclusions, source containment, and derived-state privacy guards apply.\nThe existing Brain privacy lock covers source inspection through buffered output\nso cleanup cannot commit between checking a source and emitting its bytes.\nThis command does not write facts, inferred relationships, or semantic vectors.\nIt works under `ENTIRE_BRAIN_NO_EGRESS=1`.\n\nSupported options are `--branch`, `--k`, `--evidence-bytes`, and `--json`.\n`--no-semantic` is harmless because this mode is already lexical. Explicit\nprovider, expansion, fact-filter, temporal-eligibility, and semantic-cache flags\nare rejected. `--evidence-bytes` requires `--evidence`.\n\nThis feature is CLI-only. It adds no MCP schema, Graph integration, model\nselector, temporal inference, or benchmark accuracy guarantee.\n\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2584},"formatted_output":"\nFILE: docs/operations.md\n# Operations\n\n`entire-brain` is a local Entire CLI plugin. It owns the persistent local brain,\nsemantic SQLite stores, bundle import/export, graph query surfaces, MCP stdio\ntools, and benchmark/evidence commands.\n\n## Full Install\n\n```sh\nscripts/install.sh\n```\n\nThe end-to-end installer, and the one the README documents. It has four effects\non the machine and no others: it builds and registers the `entire-graph`\nsemantic provider, builds and registers `entire-brain`, writes the default\nplugin configuration (`entire brain config init`), and runs `entire brain\ndoctor`. Both binaries are built inside their checkouts and linked into Entire's\nmanaged plugin directory; nothing is installed system-wide.\n\nBefore it builds anything it checks every prerequisite -- `git`, the `entire`\nCLI, a Go toolchain at least as new as the `go` directive in `go.mod`, and a C\ncompiler for `entire-graph`'s tree-sitter cgo bindings -- and reports all the\nmissing ones in one pass with the fix for each. `entire-brain` itself is a\npure-Go build; the cgo requirement is the provider's.\n\nNeither checkout has to have a particular name, and the two do not have to be\nsiblings. The provider is resolved by three routes, and the one taken is printed\nunder `==> entire-graph semantic provider`:\n\n| Route | Source | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR` | Used exactly as given. A value that is not an `entire-graph` checkout is a hard error, never a silent fallback. |\n| 2 | A checkout on this machine | Siblings of the brain checkout and of the main worktree when it is a linked one, the route-3 cache, and the usual source directories under `$HOME`. Accepted only when `cmd/entire-graph` and `scripts/install-local.sh` are both present, so a directory that merely has the name is skipped. |\n| 3 | A shallow clone | `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_REPO` (default the public `entireio/entire-graph`) into `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_CACHE` (default `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`). Later runs reuse it and refresh it with a shallow fetch, and keep going on the cached copy when the network is unavailable. |\n\n`ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` disables route 3, so an air-gapped machine fails with\nan explanation naming all three routes rather than blocking on a clone.\n\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` wraps this for a machine with nothing: it clones\n`entire-brain` when it is not already present -- falling back to `gh repo clone`\nfor the private-repo credentials -- and then execs `scripts/install.sh`. There is\ndeliberately no `curl | sh` form: `entireio/entire-brain` is private, so\n`raw.githubusercontent.com` serves 404 for it without a token; the source has to\nbe cloned regardless because the install is a source build; and cloning first\nputs the script that registers your plugins on disk at a reviewable commit\nbefore it runs.\n\n### After the install: onboard a repository\n\nThe installer is machine-scoped. It gives no repository a brain, and it is not\nthe last command a user runs. That is `entire brain setup`, once per repository:\n\n```sh\ncd /path/to/your/repo\nentire brain setup\n```\n\nOperationally the thing to know is that a default `setup` does two things beyond\nthe deterministic build, both of which spend tokens: it starts a detached fact\nbackfill (capped at 25 sessions per pass, `--no-backfill` to skip), and it\ninstalls **one machine-wide watcher as a persistent service, by default, with no\nprompt** — a launchd agent with `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive`, or a systemd user unit\nwith `Restart=always`. There is no prompt, but there is a pre-flight line: a run\nthat will register a watcher opens with it, before any work, naming the unit\nlabel and the path it will write and giving both `--no-daemon` and\n`entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`. `--no-daemon` skips it, `entire brain\nsetup --uninstall-daemon` removes it, and `entire brain setup --no-backfill\n--no-daemon` spends nothing and changes no service. See the reference's\n[step 3](reference.md#3-onboard-a-repository-entire-brain-setup) for the full\nphase table and the ordering against `entire enable`.\n\n## Local Install (brain only)\n\n```sh\nscripts/install-local.sh\n```\n\nThe script builds `./entire-brain`, installs it with `entire plugin install\n./entire-brain --force`, and prints `entire brain version`. It fails before\nwriting anything if the parent `entire` CLI is not on `PATH`. It does not touch\nthe provider, so `entire graph` has to be installed separately for the semantic\nlayer.\n\nThe default build is the pure-Go stack. To install the optional cgo vector stack:\n\n```sh\nBUILD_TAGS=\"brain_cgo sqlite_fts5\" scripts/install-local.sh\n```\n\n## Release Archives\n\n```sh\nscripts/release.sh\n```\n\nThe release script writes `dist/release-<version>/` with one `.tar.gz` archive per\ntarget and a `SHA256SUMS` manifest. `VERSION=<value>` overrides the version;\notherwise the script uses `git describe --tags --always --dirty`.\n\nDefault targets are:\n\n- `darwin/amd64`\n- `darwin/arm64`\n- `linux/amd64`\n- `linux/arm64`\n- `windows/amd64`\n\nSet `ENTIRE_RELEASE_TARGETS` to override that list:\n\n```sh\nENTIRE_RELEASE_TARGETS=\"linux/amd64\" scripts/release.sh\n```\n\nThe default release build uses the pure-Go fallback stack with `CGO_ENABLED=0`,\nwhich is the portable distribution path. To build cgo artifacts, set\n`BUILD_TAGS=\"brain_cgo sqlite_fts5\"` and provide the matching platform compiler.\nThe script records checksums for artifacts it successfully builds; it also signs\narchives when a local signing key is explicitly configured:\n\n- `COSIGN_KEY=<key-ref>` with `cosign` on `PATH` writes `<archive>.sig`.\n- `GPG_SIGNING_KEY=<key-id>` with `gpg` on `PATH` writes `<archive>.asc`.\n\nThe script does not publish artifacts.\n\n## Shared Baselines\n\nBrain bundle import/export is the policy-controlled shared baseline path for\nthis repository. A bundle carries the semantic index, seed, docs and facts by\ndefault; captured sessions and the history and patterns projections built from\nthem travel only under `--include-sessions`; session tombstones, privacy\ntransactions and local worker state are never bundled. Use:\n\n```sh\nentire brain bundle export --output /tmp/repo-brain.tar\nentire brain bundle import /tmp/repo-brain.tar --sha256 <sha256>\n```\n\nThe implementation validates checksums, schema compatibility, repo policy, path\ntraversal, symlink safety, size caps, and redaction before import. Import\nrefuses to overwrite a layer the destination brain already holds unless\n`--overwrite` is passed, and the refusal happens before anything is written.\nExport prints exactly which layers it wrote and which it left out. Shared\nbaselines remain explicit local files; there is no remote publishing or registry\ndiscovery.\n\n\nFILE: docs/manifest-loading-validation.md\n# Manifest loading validation\n\nImplementation base: `origin/main` at `4623fab909ebefdab35087dd682fde387917ec62`.\n\n## Implementation\n\nCanonical Brain manifests no longer use the auxiliary 16 MiB read ceiling or\nthe corresponding writer rejection. CLI loading, strict replacement checks,\nstatus/doctor health inspection, and forced-refresh validation share a dedicated\nfile decoder. Agent initialization's legacy/manual manifest fallback also decodes\nfrom its checked file without the setup-record ceiling.\n\nExisting file-type, path, descriptor-identity and alias checks remain in their\nrespective readers. Normal reads tolerate unknown fields; replacements reject\nfields they cannot round-trip. Schema versions 1–3 remain supported, and malformed,\ntruncated, trailing, and unsupported-version documents are rejected. Atomic\npublication remains in place. No storage migration is introduced.\n\nThe audit also covered refresh, status, doctor, MCP/agent surfaces, history\npublication, legacy identity publication, export, and bundle import. Archive\nvalidation and auxiliary state readers retain their existing independent rules.\n\n## Synthetic regression evidence\n\nAll fixture values are synthetic. Only sizes and aggregate structural counts\ncame from the verification helper.\n\n| Fixture | Bytes | Branches | Sessions | Warnings |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Session-heavy | 18,569,433 | 870 | 5,305 | 111 |\n| Warning-heavy | 17,220,291 | 389 | 2,633 | 6,238 |\n| Larger synthetic | 83,886,080 | 1,800 | 20,000 | 12,000 |\n\nFixtures retain the production duplication between top-level session aliases and\n`sources.sessions`. They use five or six source sections and distribute synthetic\nmetadata across session summaries to reach the target sizes. Round trips assert\nall decoded fields, including all records, remain equal.\n\nTests cover:\n\n- Production writer → normal reader, strict reader, and doctor health reader.\n- Production writer → coordinated Graph/Brain preview and instruction installation\n  through the no-setup-record fallback.\n- A 50,000-record retained seed-coverage manifest written and read back intact.\n- Failed serialization preserving the previous oversized manifest byte-for-byte.\n- Unknown-field compatibility, strict refusal to erase unknown data, and checked\n  forced-refresh validation on a synthetic oversized manifest.\n- Normal versions 1–3, missing/negative/newer versions, malformed/truncated JSON,\n  trailing JSON and trailing garbage.\n- Directory, symlink, hardlink and regular-file-to-FIFO-swap rejection, plus the\n  existing shared state-reader filesystem regression suite.\n- Unchanged limits for unrelated bounded reads and agent setup records.\n\nExecuted successfully:\n\n- `go test ./internal/cli ./internal/agentsetup -run 'Test.*(Manifest|Versioned|MemoryState|RepositoryBrain)' -count=1`\n  (CLI 11.698s; agent setup 0.679s).\n- `go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestBrainManifest' -count=1`\n  after adding cross-package installation and large unknown-field coverage (6.783s).\n- `go test ./...` passed (CLI 243.188s; all other packages passed or were cached).\n  The initial full run caught the new dedicated decoder missing from the\n  architecture-test registry; its strict-rewrite/tolerant-read contract is now\n  explicitly registered, and the full rerun passed.\n- Candidate binary build and `git diff --check`.\n\n## Memory and limitations\n\nDecoding from the file removes the arbitrary admission ceiling, not the memory\ncost. Go's decoder buffers the JSON value and materializes the manifest. Schema\nand body validation use multiple passes over the same descriptor; unknown fields\ncan add a tolerant retry. The writer still encodes the full document before atomic\nreplacement. Memory and CPU requirements grow with data size. No explicit resource\nbudget or recoverable out-of-memory guarantee is added. Other input ceilings and\nexisting seed projection policies are unchanged.\n\nRetained manifest loading requires neither a source checkout nor Git. Operations\nthat rebuild source-derived indexes still require their corresponding sources.\n\n## Sanitized manifest cohort validation\n\nValidation used locally built candidate binaries; installed binaries were not updated. Entire tracking remained enabled. Only opaque repository IDs and aggregate findings are reported. No paid inference, watcher installation, checkpoint repair, publication, source reset, or retained-data deletion was performed.\n\n### Existing manifests and normal readers\n\n- Initial status opened all 20 existing manifests unchanged; both original oversized manifests were verified before refresh.\n- Final status exit codes: {0: 20}. Doctor manifest checks: 20/20 OK. Final schemas: {3: 20}.\n- Overall doctor exit codes: {1: 2, 0: 18}; these are separate from manifest loading.\n- Coordinated candidate init-agents: {0: 20}; managed files changed: 0. The initial pass also succeeded on all 20 with zero changed files.\n\n| ID | Original manifest bytes | Final manifest bytes | Schema | Deterministic worktree refresh | Seconds | Status | Doctor manifest | Overall doctor |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| R01 | 18,569,433 | 14,039,589 | 3 | blocked by independent input limit | 327.01 | pass | pass | exit 1 |\n| R03 | 17,220,291 | 9,436,011 | 3 | completed | 1030.29 | pass | pass | exit 1 |\n| R02 | 9,251,020 | 9,250,985 | 3 | completed | 385.88 | pass | pass | exit 0 |\n\n### Refresh scope and remaining limitations\n\n- Deterministic processing used --agent none and --worktree, local-only/no-egress settings, and an explicitly disabled external embedder.\n- The first pass hit pre-existing dirty-worktree guards on 19 repositories. The two required large Brains and one already-running additional Brain were retried with --worktree. The other 17 full worktree refreshes were skipped once their regression read checks sufficed.\n- R01 completed session export and seed generation but history rebuilding encountered the unchanged 268,435,456-byte canonical-transcript limit (memory_input_too_large). The full refresh therefore remains incomplete; this is not a manifest-loading failure. No workaround that weakens that unrelated limit or truncates retained data was applied.\n- Post-refresh byte counts reflect normal production refresh writes. Support for the original larger inputs was established by opening their unchanged manifests first.\n\n### Other doctor findings\n\n- R01: memory_reconciliation.\n- R03: five persisted failures from the earlier setup run (seed, history, docs, semantic, coordinator). Doctor replays those historical records, including their old size-cap reasons; the current full refresh succeeded and the current manifest check is OK. The setup records were preserved, not cleared.\n\n### Checkpoint coverage warnings, separate from manifest failures\n\n- Remaining top-level checkpoint-scope warning entries: 500. Prior cohort baseline: 2,403.\n- Remaining top-level checkpoint-pointer warning entries: 5742. These counts classify top-level manifest warning entries by scope/pointer wording and do not count duplicated sources.sessions warnings again. They are not counts of unique missing checkpoints.\n\n| ID | Scope warnings | Pointer warnings |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| R01 | 53 | 0 |\n| R02 | 15 | 0 |\n| R03 | 34 | 0 |\n| R04 | 34 | 5361 |\n| R05 | 1 | 0 |\n| R09 | 1 | 0 |\n| R13 | 360 | 381 |\n| R14 | 1 | 0 |\n| R20 | 1 | 0 |\n\n### Retained-read probe\n\n- Both oversized Brains passed explicit-directory status with no Git executable on PATH and an unavailable ambient source root, from outside a repository. Actual source directories were left untouched, so this checks absent ambient source/Git rather than physically hiding the recorded source directory.\n- Real-cohort source code, transcript contents, identities, and raw diagnostics were kept out of the implementation report.\n\n\nFILE: docs/graph-identity-upgrades.md\n# Graph parser identity upgrades\n\nGraph's `identity_revision` is separate from its release version and its NDJSON\nschema version. It changes when a parser correction changes the IDs, names, or\nkinds of already indexed symbols. A missing field denotes the legacy rules.\nOrdinary provider releases need not change it.\n\nFor Graph trail 154 the revision is `js-ts-callable-scope-1`. JavaScript and\nTypeScript nested helpers become local functions qualified by their enclosing\ncallable; real methods no longer collide with phantom members. Anonymous\ncallables and callable class fields also have their bodies indexed correctly.\n\n## Upgrade order\n\n1. Install this Brain release before installing the Graph release carrying the\n   new identity revision. Keep the source repository and its Git objects available.\n2. Run `entire brain refresh` in each repository. Even if the source tree is\n   unchanged, a different provider identity revision rebuilds its semantic\n   snapshot and SQLite generation. Normal freshness checks report stale provider\n   identity until this happens. If the identity command is unavailable, refresh\n   warns and continues with source/artifact checks; it does not stamp identity as\n   verified or abort an otherwise usable warm refresh.\n3. Run `entire brain entities migrate` in each repository with existing entity\n   history. This carries the union of previously indexed commits across branches\n   into the current parser revision. Normal backfill also works, but follows its\n   own branch and budget; it does not automatically carry all older coverage.\n4. Check `entire brain entities history <current-qualified-name>`. A successful\n   migration retains the original commits and their checkpoint/session join.\n\n`entities migrate --graph-binary <entire-cli-wrapper>` selects a provider for\nmigration. The wrapper must support `graph version --json` and `graph diff`.\nRefresh's existing `--graph-binary` option likewise selects its provider.\nNormal status and history reads verify the provider installed as `entire`.\n\n## What migration changes\n\nMigration adds a separate namespace for the selected parser revision. It diffs\npreviously indexed commits missing from that namespace, using each commit's\nactual first parent and timestamp. Already present commits are skipped. A second\nmigration with no new input does no provider diff work and creates no commit.\n\nLegacy forward, reverse, alias and window keys remain byte-for-byte intact.\nRevisioned keys have the form\n`brain:entities-revision:<hex revision>:<hex legacy key>`. No global active-revision\nmarker is written and no old records are tombstoned. Readers and writers choose\nthe namespace from their local provider; older Brain versions ignore the new\nnamespace. Exchanging the added records therefore does not switch an older\npeer's parser or delete its history. An older peer can keep extending legacy\nhistory; another migration carries those newly indexed commits forward later.\nThe current MetaStore is local-only; tests exercise serialized record exchange,\nnot a hosted peer-sync deployment.\n\nSource Git commits, checkpoint trailers, session manifests, authored facts,\nretirements, and unrelated git-meta keys are untouched. Corrected current names\nretain the original commit/checkpoint/session join. Old parser spellings stay\navailable in the legacy namespace, without invented source-level rename aliases.\nSource coverage windows are copied only when the destination has no window;\nexisting destination windows stay intact. The query cache is keyed by both\nmetadata tip and parser revision, so switching or rolling back the provider\ncannot silently reuse the other parser's history cache.\n\nNormal backfill and unchanged-history ticks use namespace-specific cursor and\ncommit-key reads. They **do not scan historical delta documents**. Queries still\nmaterialize history when their derived cache is invalidated, as before; that\nrebuild also caches a warning if other revisions contain commits missing from\nthe selected revision. Offline history uses the last cached revision with a\nprovider-verification warning; `entities show` uses legacy history when both provider identity and a local\ncache are unavailable. Explicit backfill can also fall back to legacy history\nwith a warning, but rejects any diff that declares a different identity revision.\n\n## Publication and limits\n\nMigration captures the metadata tip and computes all diffs **without the shared\nwrite lock**. It then tries the lock without waiting, verifies the tip is still\nthe captured value, and publishes one atomic additive update. A concurrent fact,\nbackfill or metadata-import write causes publication to abort; rerun migration.\nNo concurrent work is overwritten and no partial migration is published.\n\nA failed/cancelled provider call, unavailable source commit, malformed or partial\ndiff envelope, wrong base/head, changing provider revision, or truncated delta\nalso aborts. Entity mapping follows the same rules as backfill: file-only changes\nand records without a resolvable name/path contribute no entity, and a missing\nkind is retained according to the existing contract. These do not abort migration;\nprovider warnings about partial results still do.\nMigration is capped at 20,000 pending commits and 200,000 new entity changes.\nAlready migrated commits do not count against the cap, so small incremental\nmigrations and no-op retries work on larger historical corpora. It never silently migrates a subset above those limits.\n\nThis removes the long lock around parser subprocesses, not all publication cost:\nMetaStore still materializes and serializes the final state under its write lock.\nThat cost scales with store size. Retaining revisions also consumes additional\nstorage, and contention can require recomputation on retry. No automatic pruning\nor cross-peer deletion protocol is introduced by this change.\n\n## Verification\n\nThe unit suite tests legacy documents with a global identity marker, unchanged\nticks without delta decoding, revision isolation, serialized exchange with a\nlegacy writer, idempotence, lock availability during parsing, concurrent-write\nrefusal, malformed provider output, coverage and unrelated-memory preservation.\nTo run the real provider upgrade:\n\n```sh\nBRAIN_TEST_GRAPH_BEFORE=/path/to/old/entire-graph \\\nBRAIN_TEST_GRAPH_AFTER=/path/to/new/entire-graph \\\n  go test -race ./internal/cli -run '^TestGraphIdentityUpgradeLive$' -count=1\n```\n\nThe live test creates an isolated two-commit JS/TS repository, indexes it with the\nold provider, switches binaries without editing source, refreshes the semantic\nsnapshot, runs the migration command, and checks corrected IDs, relations,\nhistory for both commits, checkpoint links, unchanged source HEAD, and reuse of\nthe now-current snapshot. It also switches back to the old provider and then\nforward again without changing metadata, verifying cache isolation and preserved\nlegacy history. The test is explicitly skipped if either binary is not\nsupplied; normal CI alone does not prove cross-version integration.\n\n\nFILE: docs/shared-fact-identity.md\n# Shared fact content identity\n\nShared fact-set sync verifies that each record's ID equals `RecordID(text,\npaths)` and that its stored paths already equal `NormalizePaths(paths)`. The\ncheck runs on local facts before sync publishes them and on shared heads read\nby sync, proposal resolution, and CLI proposal reconciliation.\n\nThis binds an ID to normalized text and canonical taxonomy paths. It prevents\na peer from replacing that content while retaining another statement's ID.\n`NormalizeText` ignores case and whitespace differences; the check does not\nprevent substitutions whose meaning depends on those differences.\n\n## What this does not authenticate\n\nThe check is not a record signature. Status, superseded-by, origin, kind,\nlocus, confidence, related IDs, branch, and provenance are outside the content\nID. A peer with write access to the shared head can still change those fields\nunder a valid ID, including retracting a statement or claiming a verified\nanchor. A valid content ID is not evidence that a lifecycle change or provenance\nclaim was authorized. Closing that trust gap requires an authenticated record\nand lifecycle protocol; changing this content check alone does not provide it.\n\nThese checks cover the shared fact-set transport. They do not add identity\nverification to the separate hosted Search/Get/MultiGet response interfaces.\nTreat hosted content and provenance as untrusted historical evidence.\n\n## Rejected heads and recovery\n\nA malformed local record is rejected before any request publishes it. Repair or\nregenerate that record through its trusted authoring source before retrying.\n\nAn invalid shared record stops the whole sync or resolution. The client does\nnot silently discard a record and publish a replacement head: doing that could\nturn corruption into loss of another member's facts, and content IDs cannot\nauthenticate the remaining lifecycle or provenance fields. Other members'\nlocal stores remain available.\n\nThere is currently no automatic quarantine or client-side repair command for a\nmalformed shared head. Recovery requires the hosted service operator to restore\nor repair the head from a trusted source through its storage administration,\nthen retry sync. Preserve the rejected version for diagnosis. This deliberate\nfail-closed policy trades shared-sync availability for avoiding an unreviewed\nrewrite; it does not prevent a malicious writer from causing denial of service.\n\n\nFILE: docs/recall-evidence.md\n# Deterministic evidence recall (experimental)\n\n`recall --evidence` returns original conversation blocks with source citations.\nIt uses lexical retrieval and makes no provider or embedding-model call. Ordinary\n`recall` continues to return distilled facts.\n\n```sh\nentire brain recall \"What approval does release require?\" --evidence --json\nentire brain recall \"What approval does release require?\" --evidence --branch main --k 20 --evidence-bytes 8192 --json\n```\n\nFor example, separate sessions might say \"Release requires owner approval\" and\n\"Release also requires security approval.\" If both blocks are retrieved and fit\nthe output budget, both appear verbatim with independent citations. Brain does\nnot infer that either statement replaces the other, combine them into a new\nfact, or establish that either statement is still current. The consuming agent\nmust interpret the evidence and acknowledge missing or conflicting context.\n\n## Retrieval and limits\n\n- Searches canonical sessions on the selected branch. Matching distilled facts\n  supply source-session handles only; their claims never replace source bytes.\n- `--k` limits candidate sessions (default 10, range 1–128). At most 128 eligible\n  sessions are scanned in manifest order, admitting at most 32 MiB of transcript\n  input through the hardened canonical reader. Larger corpora may be incomplete.\n- Lexical matching uses positive term coverage and simple plural matching. Each\n  matched session contributes from a ranked pool of up to 128 blocks, scanning\n  at most 4,096 blocks. Admission across sessions proceeds in rounds and gives\n  both user and assistant messages an opportunity. The global pool is at most\n  128 blocks. Its compact-text-and-metadata admission limit is the larger of\n  24 KiB and three times `--evidence-bytes` (up to 3 MiB). This preserves the\n  default candidate pool while allowing larger requests to retrieve larger\n  blocks and more context before output packing.\n- The heuristic candidate budget is distinct from the exact public output\n  budget. `input_truncated` and a warning report candidate limits, including\n  `--k`; the warning includes the active candidate byte limit. These limits do\n  not measure recall accuracy or guarantee corpus coverage. Larger output\n  budgets do not increase session or block count limits, or guarantee a full\n  response budget will be used.\n- Supports plain text, Claude/Pi messages, Codex user/assistant events and\n  response messages, simple role/content JSONL, and OpenCode message documents.\n  Structured hidden reasoning, tool output, system instructions and metadata\n  are not returned as conversation text. Plain text has no role classification.\n- Malformed JSON, invalid UTF-8, unavailable files, and sources with no supported\n  conversation fields yield warnings and `partial` or `unavailable` retrieval.\n  Known non-conversation records are ignored. Ambiguous session identities fail\n  closed rather than guessing which source supplied a fact anchor, including\n  conflicts later in the manifest than the source-read limits.\n\n## Citation contract\n\nEach evidence span includes `id`, `session_id`, `branch`, canonical Brain-relative\n`path`, `line`, `source_sha256`, `content_sha256`, `start_byte`, `end_byte`, and\nunchanged `text`. Structured sources also include a `json_pointer`; role and\ntimestamp are included when available.\n\nTo verify a citation:\n\n1. Read the canonical file at `path` and verify `source_sha256`.\n2. For JSONL, decode the record at one-based `line`. For a JSON document, decode\n   the document (`line` is 1). Resolve `json_pointer` to a string. For plain text,\n   the pointer is absent and the addressed content is the whole file.\n3. Verify the addressed content's UTF-8 bytes against `content_sha256`.\n4. Slice those bytes at `[start_byte, end_byte)` and compare them with `text`.\n\nOffsets count UTF-8 bytes, not characters. Blank-line boundaries preserve code\nfences, table rows, and original line endings. IDs bind the source version and\nrange; changing file bytes changes the ID. Hashes establish byte identity, not\nthe truth, authorship, or current applicability of a statement.\n\n## Output and privacy\n\n`--evidence-bytes` defaults to 8,192 (range 2–1,048,576). It bounds the compact Go\nJSON encoding of the **evidence array**, including citations, escaping, commas,\nand brackets. Enclosing metadata and pretty-print whitespace are outside this\nbudget, so it is not a full-response transport cap. Whole blocks that do not fit\nare skipped; later smaller blocks can still fit. `omitted_ids` and `truncated`\nreport those omissions. A budget of 2 returns `[]`.\nSerialization errors abort the buffered response; they are never treated as\nzero-byte spans or reported as budget omissions.\n\nThe response reports `schema_version: 1`, `selection_mode: deterministic`,\n`effective_engine: canonical_sessions_lexical_with_fact_anchors`, counts, byte\nusage, retrieval state, coverage scope, and warnings. Empty evidence does not\nprove that no answer exists.\n\nSession exclusions, source containment, and derived-state privacy guards apply.\nThe existing Brain privacy lock covers source inspection through buffered output\nso cleanup cannot commit between checking a source and emitting its bytes.\nThis command does not write facts, inferred relationships, or semantic vectors.\nIt works under `ENTIRE_BRAIN_NO_EGRESS=1`.\n\nSupported options are `--branch`, `--k`, `--evidence-bytes`, and `--json`.\n`--no-semantic` is harmless because this mode is already lexical. Explicit\nprovider, expansion, fact-filter, temporal-eligibility, and semantic-cache flags\nare rejected. `--evidence-bytes` requires `--evidence`.\n\nThis feature is CLI-only. It adds no MCP schema, Graph integration, model\nselector, temporal inference, or benchmark accuracy guarantee.\n\n"},"started_at_ms":1789839732714,"completed_at_ms":1789839732714}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:42:12.766Z","ordinal":7633,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0bac2-f01d-7253-99dd-58a6dda97210","call_id":"call_rbQYFG5lq1sHawFeGxkJpPvT","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.2 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"f070b0\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000002584,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":7469,\"output\":\"\\nFILE: docs/operations.md\\n# Operations\\n\\n`entire-brain` is a local Entire CLI plugin. It owns the persistent local brain,\\nsemantic SQLite stores, bundle import/export, graph query surfaces, MCP stdio\\ntools, and benchmark/evidence commands.\\n\\n## Full Install\\n\\n```sh\\nscripts/install.sh\\n```\\n\\nThe end-to-end installer, and the one the README documents. It has four effects\\non the machine and no others: it builds and registers the `entire-graph`\\nsemantic provider, builds and registers `entire-brain`, writes the default\\nplugin configuration (`entire brain config init`), and runs `entire brain\\ndoctor`. Both binaries are built inside their checkouts and linked into Entire's\\nmanaged plugin directory; nothing is installed system-wide.\\n\\nBefore it builds anything it checks every prerequisite -- `git`, the `entire`\\nCLI, a Go toolchain at least as new as the `go` directive in `go.mod`, and a C\\ncompiler for `entire-graph`'s tree-sitter cgo bindings -- and reports all the\\nmissing ones in one pass with the fix for each. `entire-brain` itself is a\\npure-Go build; the cgo requirement is the provider's.\\n\\nNeither checkout has to have a particular name, and the two do not have to be\\nsiblings. The provider is resolved by three routes, and the one taken is printed\\nunder `==> entire-graph semantic provider`:\\n\\n| Route | Source | Notes |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1 | `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR` | Used exactly as given. A value that is not an `entire-graph` checkout is a hard error, never a silent fallback. |\\n| 2 | A checkout on this machine | Siblings of the brain checkout and of the main worktree when it is a linked one, the route-3 cache, and the usual source directories under `$HOME`. Accepted only when `cmd/entire-graph` and `scripts/install-local.sh` are both present, so a directory that merely has the name is skipped. |\\n| 3 | A shallow clone | `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_REPO` (default the public `entireio/entire-graph`) into `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_CACHE` (default `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`). Later runs reuse it and refresh it with a shallow fetch, and keep going on the cached copy when the network is unavailable. |\\n\\n`ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` disables route 3, so an air-gapped machine fails with\\nan explanation naming all three routes rather than blocking on a clone.\\n\\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` wraps this for a machine with nothing: it clones\\n`entire-brain` when it is not already present -- falling back to `gh repo clone`\\nfor the private-repo credentials -- and then execs `scripts/install.sh`. There is\\ndeliberately no `curl | sh` form: `entireio/entire-brain` is private, so\\n`raw.githubusercontent.com` serves 404 for it without a token; the source has to\\nbe cloned regardless because the install is a source build; and cloning first\\nputs the script that registers your plugins on disk at a reviewable commit\\nbefore it runs.\\n\\n### After the install: onboard a repository\\n\\nThe installer is machine-scoped. It gives no repository a brain, and it is not\\nthe last command a user runs. That is `entire brain setup`, once per repository:\\n\\n```sh\\ncd /path/to/your/repo\\nentire brain setup\\n```\\n\\nOperationally the thing to know is that a default `setup` does two things beyond\\nthe deterministic build, both of which spend tokens: it starts a detached fact\\nbackfill (capped at 25 sessions per pass, `--no-backfill` to skip), and it\\ninstalls **one machine-wide watcher as a persistent service, by default, with no\\nprompt** — a launchd agent with `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive`, or a systemd user unit\\nwith `Restart=always`. There is no prompt, but there is a pre-flight line: a run\\nthat will register a watcher opens with it, before any work, naming the unit\\nlabel and the path it will write and giving both `--no-daemon` and\\n`entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`. `--no-daemon` skips it, `entire brain\\nsetup --uninstall-daemon` removes it, and `entire brain setup --no-backfill\\n--no-daemon` spends nothing and changes no service. See the reference's\\n[step 3](reference.md#3-onboard-a-repository-entire-brain-setup) for the full\\nphase table and the ordering against `entire enable`.\\n\\n## Local Install (brain only)\\n\\n```sh\\nscripts/install-local.sh\\n```\\n\\nThe script builds `./entire-brain`, installs it with `entire plugin install\\n./entire-brain --force`, and prints `entire brain version`. It fails before\\nwriting anything if the parent `entire` CLI is not on `PATH`. It does not touch\\nthe provider, so `entire graph` has to be installed separately for the semantic\\nlayer.\\n\\nThe default build is the pure-Go stack. To install the optional cgo vector stack:\\n\\n```sh\\nBUILD_TAGS=\\\"brain_cgo sqlite_fts5\\\" scripts/install-local.sh\\n```\\n\\n## Release Archives\\n\\n```sh\\nscripts/release.sh\\n```\\n\\nThe release script writes `dist/release-<version>/` with one `.tar.gz` archive per\\ntarget and a `SHA256SUMS` manifest. `VERSION=<value>` overrides the version;\\notherwise the script uses `git describe --tags --always --dirty`.\\n\\nDefault targets are:\\n\\n- `darwin/amd64`\\n- `darwin/arm64`\\n- `linux/amd64`\\n- `linux/arm64`\\n- `windows/amd64`\\n\\nSet `ENTIRE_RELEASE_TARGETS` to override that list:\\n\\n```sh\\nENTIRE_RELEASE_TARGETS=\\\"linux/amd64\\\" scripts/release.sh\\n```\\n\\nThe default release build uses the pure-Go fallback stack with `CGO_ENABLED=0`,\\nwhich is the portable distribution path. To build cgo artifacts, set\\n`BUILD_TAGS=\\\"brain_cgo sqlite_fts5\\\"` and provide the matching platform compiler.\\nThe script records checksums for artifacts it successfully builds; it also signs\\narchives when a local signing key is explicitly configured:\\n\\n- `COSIGN_KEY=<key-ref>` with `cosign` on `PATH` writes `<archive>.sig`.\\n- `GPG_SIGNING_KEY=<key-id>` with `gpg` on `PATH` writes `<archive>.asc`.\\n\\nThe script does not publish artifacts.\\n\\n## Shared Baselines\\n\\nBrain bundle import/export is the policy-controlled shared baseline path for\\nthis repository. A bundle carries the semantic index, seed, docs and facts by\\ndefault; captured sessions and the history and patterns projections built from\\nthem travel only under `--include-sessions`; session tombstones, privacy\\ntransactions and local worker state are never bundled. Use:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain bundle export --output /tmp/repo-brain.tar\\nentire brain bundle import /tmp/repo-brain.tar --sha256 <sha256>\\n```\\n\\nThe implementation validates checksums, schema compatibility, repo policy, path\\ntraversal, symlink safety, size caps, and redaction before import. Import\\nrefuses to overwrite a layer the destination brain already holds unless\\n`--overwrite` is passed, and the refusal happens before anything is written.\\nExport prints exactly which layers it wrote and which it left out. Shared\\nbaselines remain explicit local files; there is no remote publishing or registry\\ndiscovery.\\n\\n\\nFILE: docs/manifest-loading-validation.md\\n# Manifest loading validation\\n\\nImplementation base: `origin/main` at `4623fab909ebefdab35087dd682fde387917ec62`.\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\nCanonical Brain manifests no longer use the auxiliary 16 MiB read ceiling or\\nthe corresponding writer rejection. CLI loading, strict replacement checks,\\nstatus/doctor health inspection, and forced-refresh validation share a dedicated\\nfile decoder. Agent initialization's legacy/manual manifest fallback also decodes\\nfrom its checked file without the setup-record ceiling.\\n\\nExisting file-type, path, descriptor-identity and alias checks remain in their\\nrespective readers. Normal reads tolerate unknown fields; replacements reject\\nfields they cannot round-trip. Schema versions 1–3 remain supported, and malformed,\\ntruncated, trailing, and unsupported-version documents are rejected. Atomic\\npublication remains in place. No storage migration is introduced.\\n\\nThe audit also covered refresh, status, doctor, MCP/agent surfaces, history\\npublication, legacy identity publication, export, and bundle import. Archive\\nvalidation and auxiliary state readers retain their existing independent rules.\\n\\n## Synthetic regression evidence\\n\\nAll fixture values are synthetic. Only sizes and aggregate structural counts\\ncame from the verification helper.\\n\\n| Fixture | Bytes | Branches | Sessions | Warnings |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| Session-heavy | 18,569,433 | 870 | 5,305 | 111 |\\n| Warning-heavy | 17,220,291 | 389 | 2,633 | 6,238 |\\n| Larger synthetic | 83,886,080 | 1,800 | 20,000 | 12,000 |\\n\\nFixtures retain the production duplication between top-level session aliases and\\n`sources.sessions`. They use five or six source sections and distribute synthetic\\nmetadata across session summaries to reach the target sizes. Round trips assert\\nall decoded fields, including all records, remain equal.\\n\\nTests cover:\\n\\n- Production writer → normal reader, strict reader, and doctor health reader.\\n- Production writer → coordinated Graph/Brain preview and instruction installation\\n  through the no-setup-record fallback.\\n- A 50,000-record retained seed-coverage manifest written and read back intact.\\n- Failed serialization preserving the previous oversized manifest byte-for-byte.\\n- Unknown-field compatibility, strict refusal to erase unknown data, and checked\\n  forced-refresh validation on a synthetic oversized manifest.\\n- Normal versions 1–3, missing/negative/newer versions, malformed/truncated JSON,\\n  trailing JSON and trailing garbage.\\n- Directory, symlink, hardlink and regular-file-to-FIFO-swap rejection, plus the\\n  existing shared state-reader filesystem regression suite.\\n- Unchanged limits for unrelated bounded reads and agent setup records.\\n\\nExecuted successfully:\\n\\n- `go test ./internal/cli ./internal/agentsetup -run 'Test.*(Manifest|Versioned|MemoryState|RepositoryBrain)' -count=1`\\n  (CLI 11.698s; agent setup 0.679s).\\n- `go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestBrainManifest' -count=1`\\n  after adding cross-package installation and large unknown-field coverage (6.783s).\\n- `go test ./...` passed (CLI 243.188s; all other packages passed or were cached).\\n  The initial full run caught the new dedicated decoder missing from the\\n  architecture-test registry; its strict-rewrite/tolerant-read contract is now\\n  explicitly registered, and the full rerun passed.\\n- Candidate binary build and `git diff --check`.\\n\\n## Memory and limitations\\n\\nDecoding from the file removes the arbitrary admission ceiling, not the memory\\ncost. Go's decoder buffers the JSON value and materializes the manifest. Schema\\nand body validation use multiple passes over the same descriptor; unknown fields\\ncan add a tolerant retry. The writer still encodes the full document before atomic\\nreplacement. Memory and CPU requirements grow with data size. No explicit resource\\nbudget or recoverable out-of-memory guarantee is added. Other input ceilings and\\nexisting seed projection policies are unchanged.\\n\\nRetained manifest loading requires neither a source checkout nor Git. Operations\\nthat rebuild source-derived indexes still require their corresponding sources.\\n\\n## Sanitized manifest cohort validation\\n\\nValidation used locally built candidate binaries; installed binaries were not updated. Entire tracking remained enabled. Only opaque repository IDs and aggregate findings are reported. No paid inference, watcher installation, checkpoint repair, publication, source reset, or retained-data deletion was performed.\\n\\n### Existing manifests and normal readers\\n\\n- Initial status opened all 20 existing manifests unchanged; both original oversized manifests were verified before refresh.\\n- Final status exit codes: {0: 20}. Doctor manifest checks: 20/20 OK. Final schemas: {3: 20}.\\n- Overall doctor exit codes: {1: 2, 0: 18}; these are separate from manifest loading.\\n- Coordinated candidate init-agents: {0: 20}; managed files changed: 0. The initial pass also succeeded on all 20 with zero changed files.\\n\\n| ID | Original manifest bytes | Final manifest bytes | Schema | Deterministic worktree refresh | Seconds | Status | Doctor manifest | Overall doctor |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---|---:|---|---|---|\\n| R01 | 18,569,433 | 14,039,589 | 3 | blocked by independent input limit | 327.01 | pass | pass | exit 1 |\\n| R03 | 17,220,291 | 9,436,011 | 3 | completed | 1030.29 | pass | pass | exit 1 |\\n| R02 | 9,251,020 | 9,250,985 | 3 | completed | 385.88 | pass | pass | exit 0 |\\n\\n### Refresh scope and remaining limitations\\n\\n- Deterministic processing used --agent none and --worktree, local-only/no-egress settings, and an explicitly disabled external embedder.\\n- The first pass hit pre-existing dirty-worktree guards on 19 repositories. The two required large Brains and one already-running additional Brain were retried with --worktree. The other 17 full worktree refreshes were skipped once their regression read checks sufficed.\\n- R01 completed session export and seed generation but history rebuilding encountered the unchanged 268,435,456-byte canonical-transcript limit (memory_input_too_large). The full refresh therefore remains incomplete; this is not a manifest-loading failure. No workaround that weakens that unrelated limit or truncates retained data was applied.\\n- Post-refresh byte counts reflect normal production refresh writes. Support for the original larger inputs was established by opening their unchanged manifests first.\\n\\n### Other doctor findings\\n\\n- R01: memory_reconciliation.\\n- R03: five persisted failures from the earlier setup run (seed, history, docs, semantic, coordinator). Doctor replays those historical records, including their old size-cap reasons; the current full refresh succeeded and the current manifest check is OK. The setup records were preserved, not cleared.\\n\\n### Checkpoint coverage warnings, separate from manifest failures\\n\\n- Remaining top-level checkpoint-scope warning entries: 500. Prior cohort baseline: 2,403.\\n- Remaining top-level checkpoint-pointer warning entries: 5742. These counts classify top-level manifest warning entries by scope/pointer wording and do not count duplicated sources.sessions warnings again. They are not counts of unique missing checkpoints.\\n\\n| ID | Scope warnings | Pointer warnings |\\n|---|---:|---:|\\n| R01 | 53 | 0 |\\n| R02 | 15 | 0 |\\n| R03 | 34 | 0 |\\n| R04 | 34 | 5361 |\\n| R05 | 1 | 0 |\\n| R09 | 1 | 0 |\\n| R13 | 360 | 381 |\\n| R14 | 1 | 0 |\\n| R20 | 1 | 0 |\\n\\n### Retained-read probe\\n\\n- Both oversized Brains passed explicit-directory status with no Git executable on PATH and an unavailable ambient source root, from outside a repository. Actual source directories were left untouched, so this checks absent ambient source/Git rather than physically hiding the recorded source directory.\\n- Real-cohort source code, transcript contents, identities, and raw diagnostics were kept out of the implementation report.\\n\\n\\nFILE: docs/graph-identity-upgrades.md\\n# Graph parser identity upgrades\\n\\nGraph's `identity_revision` is separate from its release version and its NDJSON\\nschema version. It changes when a parser correction changes the IDs, names, or\\nkinds of already indexed symbols. A missing field denotes the legacy rules.\\nOrdinary provider releases need not change it.\\n\\nFor Graph trail 154 the revision is `js-ts-callable-scope-1`. JavaScript and\\nTypeScript nested helpers become local functions qualified by their enclosing\\ncallable; real methods no longer collide with phantom members. Anonymous\\ncallables and callable class fields also have their bodies indexed correctly.\\n\\n## Upgrade order\\n\\n1. Install this Brain release before installing the Graph release carrying the\\n   new identity revision. Keep the source repository and its Git objects available.\\n2. Run `entire brain refresh` in each repository. Even if the source tree is\\n   unchanged, a different provider identity revision rebuilds its semantic\\n   snapshot and SQLite generation. Normal freshness checks report stale provider\\n   identity until this happens. If the identity command is unavailable, refresh\\n   warns and continues with source/artifact checks; it does not stamp identity as\\n   verified or abort an otherwise usable warm refresh.\\n3. Run `entire brain entities migrate` in each repository with existing entity\\n   history. This carries the union of previously indexed commits across branches\\n   into the current parser revision. Normal backfill also works, but follows its\\n   own branch and budget; it does not automatically carry all older coverage.\\n4. Check `entire brain entities history <current-qualified-name>`. A successful\\n   migration retains the original commits and their checkpoint/session join.\\n\\n`entities migrate --graph-binary <entire-cli-wrapper>` selects a provider for\\nmigration. The wrapper must support `graph version --json` and `graph diff`.\\nRefresh's existing `--graph-binary` option likewise selects its provider.\\nNormal status and history reads verify the provider installed as `entire`.\\n\\n## What migration changes\\n\\nMigration adds a separate namespace for the selected parser revision. It diffs\\npreviously indexed commits missing from that namespace, using each commit's\\nactual first parent and timestamp. Already present commits are skipped. A second\\nmigration with no new input does no provider diff work and creates no commit.\\n\\nLegacy forward, reverse, alias and window keys remain byte-for-byte intact.\\nRevisioned keys have the form\\n`brain:entities-revision:<hex revision>:<hex legacy key>`. No global active-revision\\nmarker is written and no old records are tombstoned. Readers and writers choose\\nthe namespace from their local provider; older Brain versions ignore the new\\nnamespace. Exchanging the added records therefore does not switch an older\\npeer's parser or delete its history. An older peer can keep extending legacy\\nhistory; another migration carries those newly indexed commits forward later.\\nThe current MetaStore is local-only; tests exercise serialized record exchange,\\nnot a hosted peer-sync deployment.\\n\\nSource Git commits, checkpoint trailers, session manifests, authored facts,\\nretirements, and unrelated git-meta keys are untouched. Corrected current names\\nretain the original commit/checkpoint/session join. Old parser spellings stay\\navailable in the legacy namespace, without invented source-level rename aliases.\\nSource coverage windows are copied only when the destination has no window;\\nexisting destination windows stay intact. The query cache is keyed by both\\nmetadata tip and parser revision, so switching or rolling back the provider\\ncannot silently reuse the other parser's history cache.\\n\\nNormal backfill and unchanged-history ticks use namespace-specific cursor and\\ncommit-key reads. They **do not scan historical delta documents**. Queries still\\nmaterialize history when their derived cache is invalidated, as before; that\\nrebuild also caches a warning if other revisions contain commits missing from\\nthe selected revision. Offline history uses the last cached revision with a\\nprovider-verification warning; `entities show` uses legacy history when both provider identity and a local\\ncache are unavailable. Explicit backfill can also fall back to legacy history\\nwith a warning, but rejects any diff that declares a different identity revision.\\n\\n## Publication and limits\\n\\nMigration captures the metadata tip and computes all diffs **without the shared\\nwrite lock**. It then tries the lock without waiting, verifies the tip is still\\nthe captured value, and publishes one atomic additive update. A concurrent fact,\\nbackfill or metadata-import write causes publication to abort; rerun migration.\\nNo concurrent work is overwritten and no partial migration is published.\\n\\nA failed/cancelled provider call, unavailable source commit, malformed or partial\\ndiff envelope, wrong base/head, changing provider revision, or truncated delta\\nalso aborts. Entity mapping follows the same rules as backfill: file-only changes\\nand records without a resolvable name/path contribute no entity, and a missing\\nkind is retained according to the existing contract. These do not abort migration;\\nprovider warnings about partial results still do.\\nMigration is capped at 20,000 pending commits and 200,000 new entity changes.\\nAlready migrated commits do not count against the cap, so small incremental\\nmigrations and no-op retries work on larger historical corpora. It never silently migrates a subset above those limits.\\n\\nThis removes the long lock around parser subprocesses, not all publication cost:\\nMetaStore still materializes and serializes the final state under its write lock.\\nThat cost scales with store size. Retaining revisions also consumes additional\\nstorage, and contention can require recomputation on retry. No automatic pruning\\nor cross-peer deletion protocol is introduced by this change.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\nThe unit suite tests legacy documents with a global identity marker, unchanged\\nticks without delta decoding, revision isolation, serialized exchange with a\\nlegacy writer, idempotence, lock availability during parsing, concurrent-write\\nrefusal, malformed provider output, coverage and unrelated-memory preservation.\\nTo run the real provider upgrade:\\n\\n```sh\\nBRAIN_TEST_GRAPH_BEFORE=/path/to/old/entire-graph \\\\\\nBRAIN_TEST_GRAPH_AFTER=/path/to/new/entire-graph \\\\\\n  go test -race ./internal/cli -run '^TestGraphIdentityUpgradeLive$' -count=1\\n```\\n\\nThe live test creates an isolated two-commit JS/TS repository, indexes it with the\\nold provider, switches binaries without editing source, refreshes the semantic\\nsnapshot, runs the migration command, and checks corrected IDs, relations,\\nhistory for both commits, checkpoint links, unchanged source HEAD, and reuse of\\nthe now-current snapshot. It also switches back to the old provider and then\\nforward again without changing metadata, verifying cache isolation and preserved\\nlegacy history. The test is explicitly skipped if either binary is not\\nsupplied; normal CI alone does not prove cross-version integration.\\n\\n\\nFILE: docs/shared-fact-identity.md\\n# Shared fact content identity\\n\\nShared fact-set sync verifies that each record's ID equals `RecordID(text,\\npaths)` and that its stored paths already equal `NormalizePaths(paths)`. The\\ncheck runs on local facts before sync publishes them and on shared heads read\\nby sync, proposal resolution, and CLI proposal reconciliation.\\n\\nThis binds an ID to normalized text and canonical taxonomy paths. It prevents\\na peer from replacing that content while retaining another statement's ID.\\n`NormalizeText` ignores case and whitespace differences; the check does not\\nprevent substitutions whose meaning depends on those differences.\\n\\n## What this does not authenticate\\n\\nThe check is not a record signature. Status, superseded-by, origin, kind,\\nlocus, confidence, related IDs, branch, and provenance are outside the content\\nID. A peer with write access to the shared head can still change those fields\\nunder a valid ID, including retracting a statement or claiming a verified\\nanchor. A valid content ID is not evidence that a lifecycle change or provenance\\nclaim was authorized. Closing that trust gap requires an authenticated record\\nand lifecycle protocol; changing this content check alone does not provide it.\\n\\nThese checks cover the shared fact-set transport. They do not add identity\\nverification to the separate hosted Search/Get/MultiGet response interfaces.\\nTreat hosted content and provenance as untrusted historical evidence.\\n\\n## Rejected heads and recovery\\n\\nA malformed local record is rejected before any request publishes it. Repair or\\nregenerate that record through its trusted authoring source before retrying.\\n\\nAn invalid shared record stops the whole sync or resolution. The client does\\nnot silently discard a record and publish a replacement head: doing that could\\nturn corruption into loss of another member's facts, and content IDs cannot\\nauthenticate the remaining lifecycle or provenance fields. Other members'\\nlocal stores remain available.\\n\\nThere is currently no automatic quarantine or client-side repair command for a\\nmalformed shared head. Recovery requires the hosted service operator to restore\\nor repair the head from a trusted source through its storage administration,\\nthen retry sync. Preserve the rejected version for diagnosis. This deliberate\\nfail-closed policy trades shared-sync availability for avoiding an unreviewed\\nrewrite; it does not prevent a malicious writer from causing denial of service.\\n\\n\\nFILE: docs/recall-evidence.md\\n# Deterministic evidence recall (experimental)\\n\\n`recall --evidence` returns original conversation blocks with source citations.\\nIt uses lexical retrieval and makes no provider or embedding-model call. Ordinary\\n`recall` continues to return distilled facts.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain recall \\\"What approval does release require?\\\" --evidence --json\\nentire brain recall \\\"What approval does release require?\\\" --evidence --branch main --k 20 --evidence-bytes 8192 --json\\n```\\n\\nFor example, separate sessions might say \\\"Release requires owner approval\\\" and\\n\\\"Release also requires security approval.\\\" If both blocks are retrieved and fit\\nthe output budget, both appear verbatim with independent citations. Brain does\\nnot infer that either statement replaces the other, combine them into a new\\nfact, or establish that either statement is still current. The consuming agent\\nmust interpret the evidence and acknowledge missing or conflicting context.\\n\\n## Retrieval and limits\\n\\n- Searches canonical sessions on the selected branch. Matching distilled facts\\n  supply source-session handles only; their claims never replace source bytes.\\n- `--k` limits candidate sessions (default 10, range 1–128). At most 128 eligible\\n  sessions are scanned in manifest order, admitting at most 32 MiB of transcript\\n  input through the hardened canonical reader. Larger corpora may be incomplete.\\n- Lexical matching uses positive term coverage and simple plural matching. Each\\n  matched session contributes from a ranked pool of up to 128 blocks, scanning\\n  at most 4,096 blocks. Admission across sessions proceeds in rounds and gives\\n  both user and assistant messages an opportunity. The global pool is at most\\n  128 blocks. Its compact-text-and-metadata admission limit is the larger of\\n  24 KiB and three times `--evidence-bytes` (up to 3 MiB). This preserves the\\n  default candidate pool while allowing larger requests to retrieve larger\\n  blocks and more context before output packing.\\n- The heuristic candidate budget is distinct from the exact public output\\n  budget. `input_truncated` and a warning report candidate limits, including\\n  `--k`; the warning includes the active candidate byte limit. These limits do\\n  not measure recall accuracy or guarantee corpus coverage. Larger output\\n  budgets do not increase session or block count limits, or guarantee a full\\n  response budget will be used.\\n- Supports plain text, Claude/Pi messages, Codex user/assistant events and\\n  response messages, simple role/content JSONL, and OpenCode message documents.\\n  Structured hidden reasoning, tool output, system instructions and metadata\\n  are not returned as conversation text. Plain text has no role classification.\\n- Malformed JSON, invalid UTF-8, unavailable files, and sources with no supported\\n  conversation fields yield warnings and `partial` or `unavailable` retrieval.\\n  Known non-conversation records are ignored. Ambiguous session identities fail\\n  closed rather than guessing which source supplied a fact anchor, including\\n  conflicts later in the manifest than the source-read limits.\\n\\n## Citation contract\\n\\nEach evidence span includes `id`, `session_id`, `branch`, canonical Brain-relative\\n`path`, `line`, `source_sha256`, `content_sha256`, `start_byte`, `end_byte`, and\\nunchanged `text`. Structured sources also include a `json_pointer`; role and\\ntimestamp are included when available.\\n\\nTo verify a citation:\\n\\n1. Read the canonical file at `path` and verify `source_sha256`.\\n2. For JSONL, decode the record at one-based `line`. For a JSON document, decode\\n   the document (`line` is 1). Resolve `json_pointer` to a string. For plain text,\\n   the pointer is absent and the addressed content is the whole file.\\n3. Verify the addressed content's UTF-8 bytes against `content_sha256`.\\n4. Slice those bytes at `[start_byte, end_byte)` and compare them with `text`.\\n\\nOffsets count UTF-8 bytes, not characters. Blank-line boundaries preserve code\\nfences, table rows, and original line endings. IDs bind the source version and\\nrange; changing file bytes changes the ID. Hashes establish byte identity, not\\nthe truth, authorship, or current applicability of a statement.\\n\\n## Output and privacy\\n\\n`--evidence-bytes` defaults to 8,192 (range 2–1,048,576). It bounds the compact Go\\nJSON encoding of the **evidence array**, including citations, escaping, commas,\\nand brackets. Enclosing metadata and pretty-print whitespace are outside this\\nbudget, so it is not a full-response transport cap. Whole blocks that do not fit\\nare skipped; later smaller blocks can still fit. `omitted_ids` and `truncated`\\nreport those omissions. A budget of 2 returns `[]`.\\nSerialization errors abort the buffered response; they are never treated as\\nzero-byte spans or reported as budget omissions.\\n\\nThe response reports `schema_version: 1`, `selection_mode: deterministic`,\\n`effective_engine: canonical_sessions_lexical_with_fact_anchors`, counts, byte\\nusage, retrieval state, coverage scope, and warnings. Empty evidence does not\\nprove that no answer exists.\\n\\nSession exclusions, source containment, and derived-state privacy guards apply.\\nThe existing Brain privacy lock covers source inspection through buffered output\\nso cleanup cannot commit between checking a source and emitting its bytes.\\nThis command does not write facts, inferred relationships, or semantic vectors.\\nIt works under `ENTIRE_BRAIN_NO_EGRESS=1`.\\n\\nSupported options are `--branch`, `--k`, `--evidence-bytes`, and `--json`.\\n`--no-semantic` is harmless because this mode is already lexical. Explicit\\nprovider, expansion, fact-filter, temporal-eligibility, and semantic-cache flags\\nare rejected. `--evidence-bytes` requires `--evidence`.\\n\\nThis feature is CLI-only. It adds no MCP schema, Graph integration, model\\nselector, temporal inference, or benchmark accuracy guarantee.\\n\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","create_time":1789839732.765933}}}
