{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:42:21.875Z","ordinal":7637,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0cb48b2b18d09e75016aaec97c637087d2ae9ab256da19973f","status":"completed","call_id":"call_so6alqMsU8Ho4QJMhn3qahPb","name":"exec","input":"text(await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '1,360p' CHANGELOG.md\",\"max_output_tokens\":10000}));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","create_time":1789839733.243421}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:42:21.963Z","ordinal":7638,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-9cc7216c-59d1-467a-ab90-2f7ff326d631","process_id":"91864","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,360p' CHANGELOG.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/thomi/.codex/worktrees/5d33/entire-brain","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,360p' CHANGELOG.md","name":"CHANGELOG.md","path":"CHANGELOG.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to `entire-brain` are recorded here. The format follows\n[Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).\n\n## [Unreleased]\n\n### Added\n\n- `init-agents` installs a shared Brain operating guide and preserves existing\n  AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md text with managed blocks.\n- `capabilities` reports compiled features and retrieval requirements without\n  requiring a repository, model, or initialized Brain.\n\n\n### Changed\n\n- Renamed `guide` to `agent-guide` (the old name remains an alias) and grouped\n  the agent setup commands together in top-level help.\n\n- Unified retrieval under `query`: hybrid by default, with mutually exclusive\n  `--keyword` and `--semantic` modes. Query text can be positional or passed\n  with `--query`, with flags before or after it. Workspace queries support the\n  same forms. `search` and `vsearch` remain hidden compatibility commands.\n- MCP `brain_query` now accepts `keyword` and `semantic` booleans; existing\n  retrieval tools remain compatible. Updated CLI help, docs, and agent guidance.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- A repository reached through a symlinked path no longer ends up with two\n  brains and then locked out of both. A local repo key was the hash of whatever\n  path string the caller supplied, so one checkout could have two identities:\n  a relative invocation resolves symlinks (git and the `getcwd` syscall both\n  report the physical root), while an absolute `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` kept the\n  caller's spelling — and `mcp --print-config` baked in the UNRESOLVED path,\n  because `os.Getwd` honours `$PWD`. On macOS `/tmp` is a symlink to\n  `/private/tmp`, so this was the default for any repository created under\n  `/tmp`, not an edge case. Once both stores existed, every repo-scoped command\n  refused with `repo_identity_conflict`, including `brain_delete_project` — the\n  one call that could have cleared them — so the MCP surface had no recovery\n  path at all.\n  - A local repo key is now derived from the FULLY symlink-resolved root, so it\n    is a property of the directory rather than of the route taken to it. Every\n    spelling collapses onto one key.\n  - A brain already stored under an older key spelling is adopted **in place**:\n    nothing moves on disk, so no existing store is orphaned and there is no\n    half-finished migration to recover from. Repositories on paths with no\n    symlinked ancestor keep byte-identical keys.\n  - An existing root whose symlinks cannot be resolved is now reported instead\n    of silently keyed on its lexical spelling, which would mint the second key\n    this change exists to prevent. A root that does not exist yet still keeps\n    its spelling: workspace manifests use those paths as durable hints.\n  - `mcp --print-config` emits the canonical spelling, so a config-file launch\n    and a `cd`-and-run launch of the same repository agree.\n  - `brain_delete_project` resolves storage through the conflict-tolerant path\n    and erases every store the repository resolves to, so it can clear a\n    conflict rather than be blocked by one. It now removes the per-repo head\n    store alongside the brain; leaving it behind left a cursor pointing at a\n    deleted brain, and left the duplicate identity standing.\n\n### Added\n\n- `entire brain repo-identity` reports every brain store a repository resolves\n  to, and `--keep <repo-key>` resolves a `repo_identity_conflict` by keeping one\n  store and RENAMING the rest to a dated sibling — nothing is deleted, so a\n  wrong choice is undone with `mv`. The kept history is moved onto the canonical\n  key so every spelling of the repository finds it. The conflict message now\n  names this command; it used to end with \"move one brain/head store aside and\n  retry\", an instruction to run `mv` by hand with nothing behind it.\n- `bundle export` carried one layer of seven and said nothing about the other\n  six. The exporter hardcoded `Sources: &brainSources{Semantic: …}` and wrote\n  only the `semantic/` subtree, so an export/import round trip into a fresh\n  store destroyed every authored fact — in the one command whose job is moving a\n  brain without losing it. It was never a scope decision: the code was written\n  the day `semantic` was a brain's only source, and `history`, `facts`, `docs`\n  and `patterns` all arrived afterwards. A bundle now carries the semantic\n  index, seed, docs and facts by default; captured sessions and the history and\n  patterns projections built from them travel under `--include-sessions`;\n  session tombstones, privacy transactions and local worker state are never\n  bundled and are refused on import. `bundle import` restores every layer it\n  receives with its manifest source, and refuses — before writing anything — to\n  overwrite a layer the destination already holds unless `--overwrite` is\n  passed. Export prints what it included and what it omitted. The bundle\n  manifest also stops claiming `generated_at: 0001-01-01T00:00:00Z`, keeps\n  `branches`, and records `brain_version`, the build that produced the artifact.\n- `privacy exclude` and `privacy purge` could not run at all on Windows for any\n  session id containing a colon. Both write a durable transaction record before\n  anything else, and its filename was built with `url.PathEscape`, which is\n  specified against URL path segments and therefore deliberately leaves a colon\n  alone — legal in a URL, an alternate data stream on NTFS. The first write\n  failed with `ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER` (\"The parameter is incorrect\"). Captured\n  session ids are UUIDs and were never affected, which is how it survived: every\n  fixture in the suite used a hyphenated id. Ids an operator types are affected,\n  and this tool's own vocabulary is full of colons (`conversation-session:…`,\n  `fact:…`, `history:…`). `memoryJobRel` already hashes job ids with the comment\n  \"Job IDs contain a colon, which is not a valid Windows filename\"; this path\n  simply never got the same treatment. The colon and the reserved DOS device\n  names are now escaped, and nothing else is, so every id whose characters were\n  already safe — every UUID, and so every real captured session — keeps a\n  byte-identical filename and no existing record is renamed.\n- `privacy exclude` reported cleanup it had not performed. Excluding a session\n  id that no captured session answers to is a legitimate pre-emptive privacy\n  operation and still succeeds, but it printed \"removed or rebuilt its derived\n  projections\" for a session that had produced none, and `privacy list` then\n  rendered the tombstone exactly like a completed exclusion forever. Exclude now\n  reports the real inventory the way `purge` already did, names a tombstone that\n  matched nothing, and `privacy list` marks it `UNMATCHED` (`unmatched` in\n  `--json`).\n- `doctor` called an unreadable semantic store a warning, so the default\n  `--fail-on error` gate exited 0 on a brain whose SQLite store was not a\n  database. The `semantic` check is a mixed one — its axes include\n  host-environment reasons like a missing `entire` binary — so it is now split\n  along the scope line #239 introduced: the artifact axes (snapshot, store)\n  become a brain-scoped `error` when unreadable, and the provider axis becomes a\n  separate environment-scoped finding, reported at full severity and never\n  gated. Staleness and a never-built index stay warnings.\n- `facts proposals --json` printed twelve lines of human help to stdout and\n  exited 0 — the only `--json` violation among the 92 nodes that advertise the\n  flag. It now emits the standard JSON error envelope naming the missing verb\n  and exits nonzero. Without `--json` the help is unchanged.\n- `refresh index` exited 1 whenever an index existed, so a scripted refresh loop\n  containing the documented step could be green exactly once. An already-current\n  index is now a no-op that succeeds and says so; a stale or unreadable one is\n  rebuilt; `--force` still rebuilds unconditionally; real failures still exit\n  nonzero.\n- `--version`, `-v` were \"unknown flag\". `Options.Version` was set but the\n  `cobra.Command`'s own `Version` field never was, so cobra's\n  `InitDefaultVersionFlag` no-opped. Both now print the same string as\n  `entire-brain version`.\n\n### Changed\n\n- Every printed repair command resolves in the dispatch mode that printed it.\n  The memory health and error surfaces named `entire memory repair` and\n  `entire memory migrate` — commands NEITHER mode can run, because `memory` is a\n  subcommand of this binary and not a verb of the host CLI. They now carry the\n  spelling `setupCommandPrefix` resolves, the same as every other printer.\n- `doctor` and `config` are visible in `--help` again. Both were hidden in June\n  2026 as plugin plumbing; since then `doctor` became the documented\n  verification step with an exit-code contract (`--fail-on`) that CI is meant to\n  gate on, `scripts/install.sh` runs both and tells the reader to re-run\n  `doctor`, and a user who did that and then grepped `--help` found nothing.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **The v0.3.0 fact-integrity cross-check compared counts, so a corruption that\n  preserved the count was invisible.** Overwriting one line of `facts.ndjson`\n  with a copy of another leaves the store parseable, the line count unchanged\n  and the manifest's number correct — and the fact whose line was taken is gone.\n  `get fact:<id>` answered \"not found\" while `doctor` said `facts: ok`, `status`\n  raised no warning, and `search` went back to volunteering that \"the answer may\n  genuinely not be in the brain\" — the exact false statement #242 was written to\n  eliminate, reached by a route the count could not see. The check now also\n  compares IDENTITY: within a branch an id names exactly one fact (every writer\n  keys by id before it writes), so a branch that yields the same id twice was\n  not written by this program and the repeat is standing where another fact used\n  to be. The four existing modes keep their own words; this is a fifth,\n  `duplicated`, because a substitution is not a shortfall — nothing is short,\n  something was replaced. The comparison is scoped to a branch and never to the\n  brain: a fact id is derived from text and paths only, so `facts promote`\n  carrying a fact between branches stores one id twice on purpose, and a\n  brain-wide id set would have reported every promote as data loss. A duplicate\n  that was appended rather than written over used to land in `stale`, whose\n  advice — \"the manifest is behind the store — run `entire brain refresh`\" —\n  would have re-declared the collided store and silenced the brain about it; it\n  now lands in `duplicated`, which names the re-distill.\n- **A missing `git` was reported as a missing repository.** `git rev-parse\n  --show-toplevel` fails for both reasons and the four repo-ness gates (`setup`,\n  `path`, `refresh`, `workspace add`) read every failure as the second. On a\n  machine with no git, a perfectly good repository was reported as \"not a git\n  repository\" and the remedy offered was `git init` — which needs the program\n  that is missing. The two are now told apart by a `git --version` probe, which\n  answers on git's presence alone and so cannot be confounded by the state of\n  the directory, and each gets its own message and its own followable remedy.\n- `docs/getting-started.md` advertised two install methods that do not exist: a\n  \"prebuilt release archive\" (the tagged releases carry no binaries and no\n  source archive) and `go install` (which the same file later admits is\n  unavailable). It now describes only the source install that works, and says\n  plainly that the Go toolchain is therefore not optional.\n- `scripts/install.sh`'s Entire CLI prerequisite said \"install the Entire CLI\",\n  which is the restatement of the problem, not a fix. It now gives the same\n  grade of answer the Go check already gave: the Homebrew cask, the install\n  script, and the repository.\n- `CHANGELOG.md` had exactly one heading, `## [Unreleased]`, covering work that\n  shipped in `v0.3.0` — no version section had ever been cut at any tag. The\n  `0.3.0` and `0.2.0` sections below are that history, recorded from the\n  published release notes.\n\n## [0.3.0] - 2026-09-13\n\n219 commits since `v0.2.0`, 62 merged PRs. Cut after an adversarial campaign of\nroughly 400 hostile invocations across nine attack families: zero panics, zero\nstack traces, zero escapes outside the store, no lost updates under 12\nconcurrent writers, and `bundle import` rejecting traversal, absolute paths,\nsymlinks, hardlinks, device nodes and a 600 MB decompression bomb. What the\ncampaign found instead was commands that reported success while something was\nwrong, which is most of what follows.\n\n### Changed\n\n- The documented journey now ends where a user actually ends up: a brain\n  onboarded in *their* repository. It used to stop at the install. `README.md`\n  said \"That is the whole install\" and the next thing a reader was given was\n  `entire brain refresh --agent none`; `entire brain setup` appeared only as an\n  aside two sections later, and `docs/getting-started.md` — a guide whose whole\n  job is the journey — never mentioned `setup` at all in 762 lines. The install\n  is machine-scoped and gives no repository a brain, so a reader finished it\n  with working plugins and nothing to show for them.\n  - `README.md` opens `## Install` with the two commands the whole journey is\n    (install once per machine, `entire brain setup` once per repository), renames\n    step 1 to \"Install the plugins on this machine\", and promotes `setup` to\n    **step 3**, the next thing a reader does. The old step 3 (`refresh`) is now\n    step 3b, described as the manual path rather than the default one.\n  - A new **step 3a** documents what `setup` genuinely requires — a local path\n    that is a git repository, checked before any side effect — versus what it\n    degrades around: `entire enable`, captured sessions, the `entire graph`\n    provider, a service manager, an agent CLI. It states the real order\n    (`entire enable` then `setup`) and that the order is a quality decision, not\n    a correctness one, and it names what a repository new to Entire actually\n    produces: an all-green run, with `Brain ready (degraded)` explained as\n    exit-0 information and each remaining cause — an uncommitted worktree, a\n    missing `entire graph` provider, an unreadable checkpoint inventory — given\n    with its fix.\n  - Step 3 says plainly that the watcher is installed as a **persistent\n    launchd/systemd service, by default, with no prompt**, names the\n    `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive` and `Restart=always` that make it survive reboot,\n    and gives `--no-daemon` and `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`.\n  - `docs/getting-started.md` gains \"Onboard a repository: `entire brain setup`\"\n    in place of \"First run\", with the phase table, the spend labels, the\n    prerequisite/ordering section, and `refresh` kept as the by-hand path.\n  - `docs/operations.md` gains an \"After the install: onboard a repository\"\n    section, because the installer it documents is not the last command a user\n    runs.\n  - `scripts/install.sh`'s closing banner said `Next: build a brain: entire\n    brain refresh --agent none` — the same truncation in the terminal. It now\n    says the machine is set up, no repository has a brain yet, and gives\n    `cd /path/to/your/repo && entire brain setup` with its token-spend caveat.\n- Every command `setup` and `status` print back is now spelled the way the\n  reader reached this binary. The brain ships as a kubectl-style external\n  command of the Entire CLI, so a reader who types `entire brain setup` never\n  types the binary's own name — and on a managed install they cannot: the\n  binary lives in `<xdg_data>/entire/plugins/bin`, a directory the Entire CLI\n  prepends to `PATH` inside its own process and nowhere else. Everything was\n  printed as `entire-brain …` regardless, so the closing **Next** block handed a\n  plugin reader three commands their shell cannot resolve. The signal is the\n  host's: its plugin dispatcher sets `ENTIRE_CLI_VERSION` unconditionally on\n  every plugin launch (`os.Args[0]` is no signal — the dispatcher execs the\n  resolved path). Absent it the standalone name stays, which is the honest\n  answer for a reader who may not have the host CLI at all. The prefix is\n  resolved once per run and threaded to every printer, so the summary's Next\n  block, every skip and failure line, all three hints, the `status` verdict and\n  `setup --help` move together.\n- `setup` says what it will register with the machine **before** it does it. A\n  run that will install the watcher now opens, ahead of any work, with one line\n  naming the unit and its path, saying plainly that it is a persistent service\n  that starts again at every login, and giving both `--no-daemon` and\n  `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon` on the same screen. Previously the\n  only mention arrived mid-summary, after a persistent launchd agent had been\n  registered in the user's real `~/Library/LaunchAgents` and started, and\n  `--uninstall-daemon` appeared only in `setup --help`. The default is\n  deliberately unchanged — whether the watcher installs unasked is a product\n  decision — but the consequence is now stated first. A re-run that finds the\n  service already there says it *keeps* it instead of claiming a second\n  install; `--no-daemon` announces nothing it will not do; a platform with no\n  service manager has nothing to announce.\n- Installing from git is one command that works with nothing arranged in\n  advance. `scripts/install.sh` no longer requires `entire-graph` to be a\n  sibling checkout with that exact name — the documented constraint that made\n  the install path fail for anyone who cloned one repository, cloned under a\n  different directory name, or ran from a worktree. It now resolves the provider\n  by three routes and prints the one it took: `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR` when set (an\n  override that is not an `entire-graph` checkout is a hard error, not a silent\n  fallback); any checkout already on the machine — siblings of this checkout and\n  of the main worktree when this is a linked one, plus the usual source\n  directories under `$HOME`, accepted only when `cmd/entire-graph` is really\n  there rather than only the directory name; otherwise a shallow clone of the\n  public `entireio/entire-graph` into\n  `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`, reused and refreshed\n  by later runs. When no route can work it fails naming all three and how to\n  satisfy each. `ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` disables the clone for an air-gapped\n  machine, and `ENTIRE_GRAPH_REPO`/`ENTIRE_GRAPH_CACHE` redirect it.\n- `scripts/install.sh` checks the prerequisites before it builds anything and\n  reports all the missing ones at once with the fix for each — `git`, the\n  `entire` CLI, a Go toolchain at least as new as the `go` directive in `go.mod`\n  (tolerating an older `go` when `GOTOOLCHAIN` may fetch the newer one), and a C\n  compiler for `entire-graph`'s tree-sitter cgo bindings. Previously the first\n  two were separate late failures and a missing C compiler surfaced minutes into\n  a build.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- First-run onboarding. Every item below made a brand-new user's very first\n  `entire-brain setup` report a failure it did not have to:\n  - `session export failed (... parse checkpoint list json: invalid character\n    'U' after top-level value)`. The `U` is the host `entire` CLI's\n    `Update available!` banner, which released versions print on the SAME stream\n    as `--json` output. The whole-buffer `json.Unmarshal` is now a decode of the\n    first JSON value that tolerates trailing output. Because the host caches its\n    version check for 24h, this failed on the first-ever run and silently healed\n    on the second.\n  - A missing `entire-graph` plugin reported only\n    `provider_doctor_failed: semantic provider no-egress status is not\n    verified`. The provider doctor's actual error is now surfaced, shortened to\n    one line, and names the install step when the plugin is simply absent.\n  - `entire-brain doctor` — the command `setup` tells you to run when a\n    component fails — printed nothing about the repository unless\n    `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` was set, which only the host sets. It now infers the\n    repository from the working directory, and says so when it cannot.\n  - A pristine clone graded itself `freshness: degraded` because the host CLI's\n    own `.entire/logs/entire.log` made the live-state check see a dirty tree.\n    That check now applies the same ignore policy every other reader already\n    applied.\n  - `setup` in a directory that is not a git repository failed four components\n    with a raw `fatal: not a git repository`, registered a workspace, installed\n    the watcher, and exited 0. It now refuses up front, before any side effect.\n  - `--uninstall-daemon` appeared only in `setup --help`. `setup` and `status`\n    now name it, and `setup` says plainly that a persistent service was\n    installed and where its unit lives.\n  - The watcher logged `budget=0` — the value a setup-installed watcher always\n    has — while `--max-sessions`, the cap that actually binds it, was not logged\n    at all.\n  - `status` hard-coded five component names, so a component `setup` reported as\n    FAILED (`entities`, say) was missing from `status` entirely.\n  - `x semantic index: repo key mismatch` on **every** repository created with\n    `git init` — and, less visibly, on every non-GitHub origin. `entire-graph`\n    recognises `github.com` remotes and otherwise falls back to\n    `local/<basename>`; the brain recognises many hosts by slug and, with no\n    usable remote, derives `local/<basename>-<sha256(abs path)[:12]>`. So the\n    two tools spelled the same repository differently, and the hint blamed the\n    reader's repository (\"this repo has no git remote; add one … or upgrade the\n    entire CLI\") for a disagreement between two of ours — advice that fired on\n    repositories which already had the remote it asked for, and that no upgrade\n    of either tool would have satisfied. The brain now recognises the provider's\n    own spelling of the repository it just asked the provider to index and\n    stores the snapshot under its own key. **A fresh `git init` repository\n    reaches an all-green `setup` with no user action.** This is not a weaker\n    crossover guard: the snapshot's commit and tree are still validated against\n    this repository's HEAD, and a snapshot naming a genuinely different\n    repository is still refused — with a hint that now says to rebuild the index\n    (`refresh index --force`) rather than to change the repository. Bundle\n    import and stored-snapshot read-back still compare keys exactly.\n  - A dirty worktree had no remedy. `entire enable` writes `.entire/settings.json`\n    and the agent's `.claude/settings.json` and does not commit them, so the very\n    next `setup` — the documented next step — found a dirty worktree and refused\n    to seed or index it, naming `--worktree`, a flag `setup` does not accept.\n    Both refusals now share one error code and `setup` attaches the remedy:\n    commit or stash, and why the tree is dirty when the reader edited nothing.\n  - A repository with no sessions yet read as a fault: \"Entire returned no\n    readable checkpoint IDs from an incomplete persistent-store inventory\".\n    True, and useless. The detail survives for anyone debugging a real fault; the\n    hint now says what the state means and points at `entire checkpoint list`.\n    A routed discovery that genuinely failed keeps its own detail.\n  - `setup` did not build the durable history projection and then told the user\n    to run `brief`, so the first `brief` after a first-ever `setup` dropped its\n    transcript half and printed \"history index missing; run `entire brain\n    refresh`\" — setup's own recommended next command reporting a gap in setup's\n    own output. `setup` and a watch tick now say which behaviour each wants, and\n    `refresh` skips the projection when its target directory does not exist\n    (the state a run where every deterministic source failed used to leave,\n    surfacing as a raw `lstat <brainDir>: no such file or directory`).\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to `entire-brain` are recorded here. The format follows\n[Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).\n\n## [Unreleased]\n\n### Added\n\n- `init-agents` installs a shared Brain operating guide and preserves existing\n  AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md text with managed blocks.\n- `capabilities` reports compiled features and retrieval requirements without\n  requiring a repository, model, or initialized Brain.\n\n\n### Changed\n\n- Renamed `guide` to `agent-guide` (the old name remains an alias) and grouped\n  the agent setup commands together in top-level help.\n\n- Unified retrieval under `query`: hybrid by default, with mutually exclusive\n  `--keyword` and `--semantic` modes. Query text can be positional or passed\n  with `--query`, with flags before or after it. Workspace queries support the\n  same forms. `search` and `vsearch` remain hidden compatibility commands.\n- MCP `brain_query` now accepts `keyword` and `semantic` booleans; existing\n  retrieval tools remain compatible. Updated CLI help, docs, and agent guidance.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- A repository reached through a symlinked path no longer ends up with two\n  brains and then locked out of both. A local repo key was the hash of whatever\n  path string the caller supplied, so one checkout could have two identities:\n  a relative invocation resolves symlinks (git and the `getcwd` syscall both\n  report the physical root), while an absolute `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` kept the\n  caller's spelling — and `mcp --print-config` baked in the UNRESOLVED path,\n  because `os.Getwd` honours `$PWD`. On macOS `/tmp` is a symlink to\n  `/private/tmp`, so this was the default for any repository created under\n  `/tmp`, not an edge case. Once both stores existed, every repo-scoped command\n  refused with `repo_identity_conflict`, including `brain_delete_project` — the\n  one call that could have cleared them — so the MCP surface had no recovery\n  path at all.\n  - A local repo key is now derived from the FULLY symlink-resolved root, so it\n    is a property of the directory rather than of the route taken to it. Every\n    spelling collapses onto one key.\n  - A brain already stored under an older key spelling is adopted **in place**:\n    nothing moves on disk, so no existing store is orphaned and there is no\n    half-finished migration to recover from. Repositories on paths with no\n    symlinked ancestor keep byte-identical keys.\n  - An existing root whose symlinks cannot be resolved is now reported instead\n    of silently keyed on its lexical spelling, which would mint the second key\n    this change exists to prevent. A root that does not exist yet still keeps\n    its spelling: workspace manifests use those paths as durable hints.\n  - `mcp --print-config` emits the canonical spelling, so a config-file launch\n    and a `cd`-and-run launch of the same repository agree.\n  - `brain_delete_project` resolves storage through the conflict-tolerant path\n    and erases every store the repository resolves to, so it can clear a\n    conflict rather than be blocked by one. It now removes the per-repo head\n    store alongside the brain; leaving it behind left a cursor pointing at a\n    deleted brain, and left the duplicate identity standing.\n\n### Added\n\n- `entire brain repo-identity` reports every brain store a repository resolves\n  to, and `--keep <repo-key>` resolves a `repo_identity_conflict` by keeping one\n  store and RENAMING the rest to a dated sibling — nothing is deleted, so a\n  wrong choice is undone with `mv`. The kept history is moved onto the canonical\n  key so every spelling of the repository finds it. The conflict message now\n  names this command; it used to end with \"move one brain/head store aside and\n  retry\", an instruction to run `mv` by hand with nothing behind it.\n- `bundle export` carried one layer of seven and said nothing about the other\n  six. The exporter hardcoded `Sources: &brainSources{Semantic: …}` and wrote\n  only the `semantic/` subtree, so an export/import round trip into a fresh\n  store destroyed every authored fact — in the one command whose job is moving a\n  brain without losing it. It was never a scope decision: the code was written\n  the day `semantic` was a brain's only source, and `history`, `facts`, `docs`\n  and `patterns` all arrived afterwards. A bundle now carries the semantic\n  index, seed, docs and facts by default; captured sessions and the history and\n  patterns projections built from them travel under `--include-sessions`;\n  session tombstones, privacy transactions and local worker state are never\n  bundled and are refused on import. `bundle import` restores every layer it\n  receives with its manifest source, and refuses — before writing anything — to\n  overwrite a layer the destination already holds unless `--overwrite` is\n  passed. Export prints what it included and what it omitted. The bundle\n  manifest also stops claiming `generated_at: 0001-01-01T00:00:00Z`, keeps\n  `branches`, and records `brain_version`, the build that produced the artifact.\n- `privacy exclude` and `privacy purge` could not run at all on Windows for any\n  session id containing a colon. Both write a durable transaction record before\n  anything else, and its filename was built with `url.PathEscape`, which is\n  specified against URL path segments and therefore deliberately leaves a colon\n  alone — legal in a URL, an alternate data stream on NTFS. The first write\n  failed with `ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER` (\"The parameter is incorrect\"). Captured\n  session ids are UUIDs and were never affected, which is how it survived: every\n  fixture in the suite used a hyphenated id. Ids an operator types are affected,\n  and this tool's own vocabulary is full of colons (`conversation-session:…`,\n  `fact:…`, `history:…`). `memoryJobRel` already hashes job ids with the comment\n  \"Job IDs contain a colon, which is not a valid Windows filename\"; this path\n  simply never got the same treatment. The colon and the reserved DOS device\n  names are now escaped, and nothing else is, so every id whose characters were\n  already safe — every UUID, and so every real captured session — keeps a\n  byte-identical filename and no existing record is renamed.\n- `privacy exclude` reported cleanup it had not performed. Excluding a session\n  id that no captured session answers to is a legitimate pre-emptive privacy\n  operation and still succeeds, but it printed \"removed or rebuilt its derived\n  projections\" for a session that had produced none, and `privacy list` then\n  rendered the tombstone exactly like a completed exclusion forever. Exclude now\n  reports the real inventory the way `purge` already did, names a tombstone that\n  matched nothing, and `privacy list` marks it `UNMATCHED` (`unmatched` in\n  `--json`).\n- `doctor` called an unreadable semantic store a warning, so the default\n  `--fail-on error` gate exited 0 on a brain whose SQLite store was not a\n  database. The `semantic` check is a mixed one — its axes include\n  host-environment reasons like a missing `entire` binary — so it is now split\n  along the scope line #239 introduced: the artifact axes (snapshot, store)\n  become a brain-scoped `error` when unreadable, and the provider axis becomes a\n  separate environment-scoped finding, reported at full severity and never\n  gated. Staleness and a never-built index stay warnings.\n- `facts proposals --json` printed twelve lines of human help to stdout and\n  exited 0 — the only `--json` violation among the 92 nodes that advertise the\n  flag. It now emits the standard JSON error envelope naming the missing verb\n  and exits nonzero. Without `--json` the help is unchanged.\n- `refresh index` exited 1 whenever an index existed, so a scripted refresh loop\n  containing the documented step could be green exactly once. An already-current\n  index is now a no-op that succeeds and says so; a stale or unreadable one is\n  rebuilt; `--force` still rebuilds unconditionally; real failures still exit\n  nonzero.\n- `--version`, `-v` were \"unknown flag\". `Options.Version` was set but the\n  `cobra.Command`'s own `Version` field never was, so cobra's\n  `InitDefaultVersionFlag` no-opped. Both now print the same string as\n  `entire-brain version`.\n\n### Changed\n\n- Every printed repair command resolves in the dispatch mode that printed it.\n  The memory health and error surfaces named `entire memory repair` and\n  `entire memory migrate` — commands NEITHER mode can run, because `memory` is a\n  subcommand of this binary and not a verb of the host CLI. They now carry the\n  spelling `setupCommandPrefix` resolves, the same as every other printer.\n- `doctor` and `config` are visible in `--help` again. Both were hidden in June\n  2026 as plugin plumbing; since then `doctor` became the documented\n  verification step with an exit-code contract (`--fail-on`) that CI is meant to\n  gate on, `scripts/install.sh` runs both and tells the reader to re-run\n  `doctor`, and a user who did that and then grepped `--help` found nothing.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **The v0.3.0 fact-integrity cross-check compared counts, so a corruption that\n  preserved the count was invisible.** Overwriting one line of `facts.ndjson`\n  with a copy of another leaves the store parseable, the line count unchanged\n  and the manifest's number correct — and the fact whose line was taken is gone.\n  `get fact:<id>` answered \"not found\" while `doctor` said `facts: ok`, `status`\n  raised no warning, and `search` went back to volunteering that \"the answer may\n  genuinely not be in the brain\" — the exact false statement #242 was written to\n  eliminate, reached by a route the count could not see. The check now also\n  compares IDENTITY: within a branch an id names exactly one fact (every writer\n  keys by id before it writes), so a branch that yields the same id twice was\n  not written by this program and the repeat is standing where another fact used\n  to be. The four existing modes keep their own words; this is a fifth,\n  `duplicated`, because a substitution is not a shortfall — nothing is short,\n  something was replaced. The comparison is scoped to a branch and never to the\n  brain: a fact id is derived from text and paths only, so `facts promote`\n  carrying a fact between branches stores one id twice on purpose, and a\n  brain-wide id set would have reported every promote as data loss. A duplicate\n  that was appended rather than written over used to land in `stale`, whose\n  advice — \"the manifest is behind the store — run `entire brain refresh`\" —\n  would have re-declared the collided store and silenced the brain about it; it\n  now lands in `duplicated`, which names the re-distill.\n- **A missing `git` was reported as a missing repository.** `git rev-parse\n  --show-toplevel` fails for both reasons and the four repo-ness gates (`setup`,\n  `path`, `refresh`, `workspace add`) read every failure as the second. On a\n  machine with no git, a perfectly good repository was reported as \"not a git\n  repository\" and the remedy offered was `git init` — which needs the program\n  that is missing. The two are now told apart by a `git --version` probe, which\n  answers on git's presence alone and so cannot be confounded by the state of\n  the directory, and each gets its own message and its own followable remedy.\n- `docs/getting-started.md` advertised two install methods that do not exist: a\n  \"prebuilt release archive\" (the tagged releases carry no binaries and no\n  source archive) and `go install` (which the same file later admits is\n  unavailable). It now describes only the source install that works, and says\n  plainly that the Go toolchain is therefore not optional.\n- `scripts/install.sh`'s Entire CLI prerequisite said \"install the Entire CLI\",\n  which is the restatement of the problem, not a fix. It now gives the same\n  grade of answer the Go check already gave: the Homebrew cask, the install\n  script, and the repository.\n- `CHANGELOG.md` had exactly one heading, `## [Unreleased]`, covering work that\n  shipped in `v0.3.0` — no version section had ever been cut at any tag. The\n  `0.3.0` and `0.2.0` sections below are that history, recorded from the\n  published release notes.\n\n## [0.3.0] - 2026-09-13\n\n219 commits since `v0.2.0`, 62 merged PRs. Cut after an adversarial campaign of\nroughly 400 hostile invocations across nine attack families: zero panics, zero\nstack traces, zero escapes outside the store, no lost updates under 12\nconcurrent writers, and `bundle import` rejecting traversal, absolute paths,\nsymlinks, hardlinks, device nodes and a 600 MB decompression bomb. What the\ncampaign found instead was commands that reported success while something was\nwrong, which is most of what follows.\n\n### Changed\n\n- The documented journey now ends where a user actually ends up: a brain\n  onboarded in *their* repository. It used to stop at the install. `README.md`\n  said \"That is the whole install\" and the next thing a reader was given was\n  `entire brain refresh --agent none`; `entire brain setup` appeared only as an\n  aside two sections later, and `docs/getting-started.md` — a guide whose whole\n  job is the journey — never mentioned `setup` at all in 762 lines. The install\n  is machine-scoped and gives no repository a brain, so a reader finished it\n  with working plugins and nothing to show for them.\n  - `README.md` opens `## Install` with the two commands the whole journey is\n    (install once per machine, `entire brain setup` once per repository), renames\n    step 1 to \"Install the plugins on this machine\", and promotes `setup` to\n    **step 3**, the next thing a reader does. The old step 3 (`refresh`) is now\n    step 3b, described as the manual path rather than the default one.\n  - A new **step 3a** documents what `setup` genuinely requires — a local path\n    that is a git repository, checked before any side effect — versus what it\n    degrades around: `entire enable`, captured sessions, the `entire graph`\n    provider, a service manager, an agent CLI. It states the real order\n    (`entire enable` then `setup`) and that the order is a quality decision, not\n    a correctness one, and it names what a repository new to Entire actually\n    produces: an all-green run, with `Brain ready (degraded)` explained as\n    exit-0 information and each remaining cause — an uncommitted worktree, a\n    missing `entire graph` provider, an unreadable checkpoint inventory — given\n    with its fix.\n  - Step 3 says plainly that the watcher is installed as a **persistent\n    launchd/systemd service, by default, with no prompt**, names the\n    `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive` and `Restart=always` that make it survive reboot,\n    and gives `--no-daemon` and `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`.\n  - `docs/getting-started.md` gains \"Onboard a repository: `entire brain setup`\"\n    in place of \"First run\", with the phase table, the spend labels, the\n    prerequisite/ordering section, and `refresh` kept as the by-hand path.\n  - `docs/operations.md` gains an \"After the install: onboard a repository\"\n    section, because the installer it documents is not the last command a user\n    runs.\n  - `scripts/install.sh`'s closing banner said `Next: build a brain: entire\n    brain refresh --agent none` — the same truncation in the terminal. It now\n    says the machine is set up, no repository has a brain yet, and gives\n    `cd /path/to/your/repo && entire brain setup` with its token-spend caveat.\n- Every command `setup` and `status` print back is now spelled the way the\n  reader reached this binary. The brain ships as a kubectl-style external\n  command of the Entire CLI, so a reader who types `entire brain setup` never\n  types the binary's own name — and on a managed install they cannot: the\n  binary lives in `<xdg_data>/entire/plugins/bin`, a directory the Entire CLI\n  prepends to `PATH` inside its own process and nowhere else. Everything was\n  printed as `entire-brain …` regardless, so the closing **Next** block handed a\n  plugin reader three commands their shell cannot resolve. The signal is the\n  host's: its plugin dispatcher sets `ENTIRE_CLI_VERSION` unconditionally on\n  every plugin launch (`os.Args[0]` is no signal — the dispatcher execs the\n  resolved path). Absent it the standalone name stays, which is the honest\n  answer for a reader who may not have the host CLI at all. The prefix is\n  resolved once per run and threaded to every printer, so the summary's Next\n  block, every skip and failure line, all three hints, the `status` verdict and\n  `setup --help` move together.\n- `setup` says what it will register with the machine **before** it does it. A\n  run that will install the watcher now opens, ahead of any work, with one line\n  naming the unit and its path, saying plainly that it is a persistent service\n  that starts again at every login, and giving both `--no-daemon` and\n  `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon` on the same screen. Previously the\n  only mention arrived mid-summary, after a persistent launchd agent had been\n  registered in the user's real `~/Library/LaunchAgents` and started, and\n  `--uninstall-daemon` appeared only in `setup --help`. The default is\n  deliberately unchanged — whether the watcher installs unasked is a product\n  decision — but the consequence is now stated first. A re-run that finds the\n  service already there says it *keeps* it instead of claiming a second\n  install; `--no-daemon` announces nothing it will not do; a platform with no\n  service manager has nothing to announce.\n- Installing from git is one command that works with nothing arranged in\n  advance. `scripts/install.sh` no longer requires `entire-graph` to be a\n  sibling checkout with that exact name — the documented constraint that made\n  the install path fail for anyone who cloned one repository, cloned under a\n  different directory name, or ran from a worktree. It now resolves the provider\n  by three routes and prints the one it took: `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR` when set (an\n  override that is not an `entire-graph` checkout is a hard error, not a silent\n  fallback); any checkout already on the machine — siblings of this checkout and\n  of the main worktree when this is a linked one, plus the usual source\n  directories under `$HOME`, accepted only when `cmd/entire-graph` is really\n  there rather than only the directory name; otherwise a shallow clone of the\n  public `entireio/entire-graph` into\n  `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`, reused and refreshed\n  by later runs. When no route can work it fails naming all three and how to\n  satisfy each. `ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` disables the clone for an air-gapped\n  machine, and `ENTIRE_GRAPH_REPO`/`ENTIRE_GRAPH_CACHE` redirect it.\n- `scripts/install.sh` checks the prerequisites before it builds anything and\n  reports all the missing ones at once with the fix for each — `git`, the\n  `entire` CLI, a Go toolchain at least as new as the `go` directive in `go.mod`\n  (tolerating an older `go` when `GOTOOLCHAIN` may fetch the newer one), and a C\n  compiler for `entire-graph`'s tree-sitter cgo bindings. Previously the first\n  two were separate late failures and a missing C compiler surfaced minutes into\n  a build.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- First-run onboarding. Every item below made a brand-new user's very first\n  `entire-brain setup` report a failure it did not have to:\n  - `session export failed (... parse checkpoint list json: invalid character\n    'U' after top-level value)`. The `U` is the host `entire` CLI's\n    `Update available!` banner, which released versions print on the SAME stream\n    as `--json` output. The whole-buffer `json.Unmarshal` is now a decode of the\n    first JSON value that tolerates trailing output. Because the host caches its\n    version check for 24h, this failed on the first-ever run and silently healed\n    on the second.\n  - A missing `entire-graph` plugin reported only\n    `provider_doctor_failed: semantic provider no-egress status is not\n    verified`. The provider doctor's actual error is now surfaced, shortened to\n    one line, and names the install step when the plugin is simply absent.\n  - `entire-brain doctor` — the command `setup` tells you to run when a\n    component fails — printed nothing about the repository unless\n    `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` was set, which only the host sets. It now infers the\n    repository from the working directory, and says so when it cannot.\n  - A pristine clone graded itself `freshness: degraded` because the host CLI's\n    own `.entire/logs/entire.log` made the live-state check see a dirty tree.\n    That check now applies the same ignore policy every other reader already\n    applied.\n  - `setup` in a directory that is not a git repository failed four components\n    with a raw `fatal: not a git repository`, registered a workspace, installed\n    the watcher, and exited 0. It now refuses up front, before any side effect.\n  - `--uninstall-daemon` appeared only in `setup --help`. `setup` and `status`\n    now name it, and `setup` says plainly that a persistent service was\n    installed and where its unit lives.\n  - The watcher logged `budget=0` — the value a setup-installed watcher always\n    has — while `--max-sessions`, the cap that actually binds it, was not logged\n    at all.\n  - `status` hard-coded five component names, so a component `setup` reported as\n    FAILED (`entities`, say) was missing from `status` entirely.\n  - `x semantic index: repo key mismatch` on **every** repository created with\n    `git init` — and, less visibly, on every non-GitHub origin. `entire-graph`\n    recognises `github.com` remotes and otherwise falls back to\n    `local/<basename>`; the brain recognises many hosts by slug and, with no\n    usable remote, derives `local/<basename>-<sha256(abs path)[:12]>`. So the\n    two tools spelled the same repository differently, and the hint blamed the\n    reader's repository (\"this repo has no git remote; add one … or upgrade the\n    entire CLI\") for a disagreement between two of ours — advice that fired on\n    repositories which already had the remote it asked for, and that no upgrade\n    of either tool would have satisfied. The brain now recognises the provider's\n    own spelling of the repository it just asked the provider to index and\n    stores the snapshot under its own key. **A fresh `git init` repository\n    reaches an all-green `setup` with no user action.** This is not a weaker\n    crossover guard: the snapshot's commit and tree are still validated against\n    this repository's HEAD, and a snapshot naming a genuinely different\n    repository is still refused — with a hint that now says to rebuild the index\n    (`refresh index --force`) rather than to change the repository. Bundle\n    import and stored-snapshot read-back still compare keys exactly.\n  - A dirty worktree had no remedy. `entire enable` writes `.entire/settings.json`\n    and the agent's `.claude/settings.json` and does not commit them, so the very\n    next `setup` — the documented next step — found a dirty worktree and refused\n    to seed or index it, naming `--worktree`, a flag `setup` does not accept.\n    Both refusals now share one error code and `setup` attaches the remedy:\n    commit or stash, and why the tree is dirty when the reader edited nothing.\n  - A repository with no sessions yet read as a fault: \"Entire returned no\n    readable checkpoint IDs from an incomplete persistent-store inventory\".\n    True, and useless. The detail survives for anyone debugging a real fault; the\n    hint now says what the state means and points at `entire checkpoint list`.\n    A routed discovery that genuinely failed keeps its own detail.\n  - `setup` did not build the durable history projection and then told the user\n    to run `brief`, so the first `brief` after a first-ever `setup` dropped its\n    transcript half and printed \"history index missing; run `entire brain\n    refresh`\" — setup's own recommended next command reporting a gap in setup's\n    own output. `setup` and a watch tick now say which behaviour each wants, and\n    `refresh` skips the projection when its target directory does not exist\n    (the state a run where every deterministic source failed used to leave,\n    surfacing as a raw `lstat <brainDir>: no such file or directory`).\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2334},"formatted_output":"# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to `entire-brain` are recorded here. The format follows\n[Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).\n\n## [Unreleased]\n\n### Added\n\n- `init-agents` installs a shared Brain operating guide and preserves existing\n  AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md text with managed blocks.\n- `capabilities` reports compiled features and retrieval requirements without\n  requiring a repository, model, or initialized Brain.\n\n\n### Changed\n\n- Renamed `guide` to `agent-guide` (the old name remains an alias) and grouped\n  the agent setup commands together in top-level help.\n\n- Unified retrieval under `query`: hybrid by default, with mutually exclusive\n  `--keyword` and `--semantic` modes. Query text can be positional or passed\n  with `--query`, with flags before or after it. Workspace queries support the\n  same forms. `search` and `vsearch` remain hidden compatibility commands.\n- MCP `brain_query` now accepts `keyword` and `semantic` booleans; existing\n  retrieval tools remain compatible. Updated CLI help, docs, and agent guidance.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- A repository reached through a symlinked path no longer ends up with two\n  brains and then locked out of both. A local repo key was the hash of whatever\n  path string the caller supplied, so one checkout could have two identities:\n  a relative invocation resolves symlinks (git and the `getcwd` syscall both\n  report the physical root), while an absolute `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` kept the\n  caller's spelling — and `mcp --print-config` baked in the UNRESOLVED path,\n  because `os.Getwd` honours `$PWD`. On macOS `/tmp` is a symlink to\n  `/private/tmp`, so this was the default for any repository created under\n  `/tmp`, not an edge case. Once both stores existed, every repo-scoped command\n  refused with `repo_identity_conflict`, including `brain_delete_project` — the\n  one call that could have cleared them — so the MCP surface had no recovery\n  path at all.\n  - A local repo key is now derived from the FULLY symlink-resolved root, so it\n    is a property of the directory rather than of the route taken to it. Every\n    spelling collapses onto one key.\n  - A brain already stored under an older key spelling is adopted **in place**:\n    nothing moves on disk, so no existing store is orphaned and there is no\n    half-finished migration to recover from. Repositories on paths with no\n    symlinked ancestor keep byte-identical keys.\n  - An existing root whose symlinks cannot be resolved is now reported instead\n    of silently keyed on its lexical spelling, which would mint the second key\n    this change exists to prevent. A root that does not exist yet still keeps\n    its spelling: workspace manifests use those paths as durable hints.\n  - `mcp --print-config` emits the canonical spelling, so a config-file launch\n    and a `cd`-and-run launch of the same repository agree.\n  - `brain_delete_project` resolves storage through the conflict-tolerant path\n    and erases every store the repository resolves to, so it can clear a\n    conflict rather than be blocked by one. It now removes the per-repo head\n    store alongside the brain; leaving it behind left a cursor pointing at a\n    deleted brain, and left the duplicate identity standing.\n\n### Added\n\n- `entire brain repo-identity` reports every brain store a repository resolves\n  to, and `--keep <repo-key>` resolves a `repo_identity_conflict` by keeping one\n  store and RENAMING the rest to a dated sibling — nothing is deleted, so a\n  wrong choice is undone with `mv`. The kept history is moved onto the canonical\n  key so every spelling of the repository finds it. The conflict message now\n  names this command; it used to end with \"move one brain/head store aside and\n  retry\", an instruction to run `mv` by hand with nothing behind it.\n- `bundle export` carried one layer of seven and said nothing about the other\n  six. The exporter hardcoded `Sources: &brainSources{Semantic: …}` and wrote\n  only the `semantic/` subtree, so an export/import round trip into a fresh\n  store destroyed every authored fact — in the one command whose job is moving a\n  brain without losing it. It was never a scope decision: the code was written\n  the day `semantic` was a brain's only source, and `history`, `facts`, `docs`\n  and `patterns` all arrived afterwards. A bundle now carries the semantic\n  index, seed, docs and facts by default; captured sessions and the history and\n  patterns projections built from them travel under `--include-sessions`;\n  session tombstones, privacy transactions and local worker state are never\n  bundled and are refused on import. `bundle import` restores every layer it\n  receives with its manifest source, and refuses — before writing anything — to\n  overwrite a layer the destination already holds unless `--overwrite` is\n  passed. Export prints what it included and what it omitted. The bundle\n  manifest also stops claiming `generated_at: 0001-01-01T00:00:00Z`, keeps\n  `branches`, and records `brain_version`, the build that produced the artifact.\n- `privacy exclude` and `privacy purge` could not run at all on Windows for any\n  session id containing a colon. Both write a durable transaction record before\n  anything else, and its filename was built with `url.PathEscape`, which is\n  specified against URL path segments and therefore deliberately leaves a colon\n  alone — legal in a URL, an alternate data stream on NTFS. The first write\n  failed with `ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER` (\"The parameter is incorrect\"). Captured\n  session ids are UUIDs and were never affected, which is how it survived: every\n  fixture in the suite used a hyphenated id. Ids an operator types are affected,\n  and this tool's own vocabulary is full of colons (`conversation-session:…`,\n  `fact:…`, `history:…`). `memoryJobRel` already hashes job ids with the comment\n  \"Job IDs contain a colon, which is not a valid Windows filename\"; this path\n  simply never got the same treatment. The colon and the reserved DOS device\n  names are now escaped, and nothing else is, so every id whose characters were\n  already safe — every UUID, and so every real captured session — keeps a\n  byte-identical filename and no existing record is renamed.\n- `privacy exclude` reported cleanup it had not performed. Excluding a session\n  id that no captured session answers to is a legitimate pre-emptive privacy\n  operation and still succeeds, but it printed \"removed or rebuilt its derived\n  projections\" for a session that had produced none, and `privacy list` then\n  rendered the tombstone exactly like a completed exclusion forever. Exclude now\n  reports the real inventory the way `purge` already did, names a tombstone that\n  matched nothing, and `privacy list` marks it `UNMATCHED` (`unmatched` in\n  `--json`).\n- `doctor` called an unreadable semantic store a warning, so the default\n  `--fail-on error` gate exited 0 on a brain whose SQLite store was not a\n  database. The `semantic` check is a mixed one — its axes include\n  host-environment reasons like a missing `entire` binary — so it is now split\n  along the scope line #239 introduced: the artifact axes (snapshot, store)\n  become a brain-scoped `error` when unreadable, and the provider axis becomes a\n  separate environment-scoped finding, reported at full severity and never\n  gated. Staleness and a never-built index stay warnings.\n- `facts proposals --json` printed twelve lines of human help to stdout and\n  exited 0 — the only `--json` violation among the 92 nodes that advertise the\n  flag. It now emits the standard JSON error envelope naming the missing verb\n  and exits nonzero. Without `--json` the help is unchanged.\n- `refresh index` exited 1 whenever an index existed, so a scripted refresh loop\n  containing the documented step could be green exactly once. An already-current\n  index is now a no-op that succeeds and says so; a stale or unreadable one is\n  rebuilt; `--force` still rebuilds unconditionally; real failures still exit\n  nonzero.\n- `--version`, `-v` were \"unknown flag\". `Options.Version` was set but the\n  `cobra.Command`'s own `Version` field never was, so cobra's\n  `InitDefaultVersionFlag` no-opped. Both now print the same string as\n  `entire-brain version`.\n\n### Changed\n\n- Every printed repair command resolves in the dispatch mode that printed it.\n  The memory health and error surfaces named `entire memory repair` and\n  `entire memory migrate` — commands NEITHER mode can run, because `memory` is a\n  subcommand of this binary and not a verb of the host CLI. They now carry the\n  spelling `setupCommandPrefix` resolves, the same as every other printer.\n- `doctor` and `config` are visible in `--help` again. Both were hidden in June\n  2026 as plugin plumbing; since then `doctor` became the documented\n  verification step with an exit-code contract (`--fail-on`) that CI is meant to\n  gate on, `scripts/install.sh` runs both and tells the reader to re-run\n  `doctor`, and a user who did that and then grepped `--help` found nothing.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **The v0.3.0 fact-integrity cross-check compared counts, so a corruption that\n  preserved the count was invisible.** Overwriting one line of `facts.ndjson`\n  with a copy of another leaves the store parseable, the line count unchanged\n  and the manifest's number correct — and the fact whose line was taken is gone.\n  `get fact:<id>` answered \"not found\" while `doctor` said `facts: ok`, `status`\n  raised no warning, and `search` went back to volunteering that \"the answer may\n  genuinely not be in the brain\" — the exact false statement #242 was written to\n  eliminate, reached by a route the count could not see. The check now also\n  compares IDENTITY: within a branch an id names exactly one fact (every writer\n  keys by id before it writes), so a branch that yields the same id twice was\n  not written by this program and the repeat is standing where another fact used\n  to be. The four existing modes keep their own words; this is a fifth,\n  `duplicated`, because a substitution is not a shortfall — nothing is short,\n  something was replaced. The comparison is scoped to a branch and never to the\n  brain: a fact id is derived from text and paths only, so `facts promote`\n  carrying a fact between branches stores one id twice on purpose, and a\n  brain-wide id set would have reported every promote as data loss. A duplicate\n  that was appended rather than written over used to land in `stale`, whose\n  advice — \"the manifest is behind the store — run `entire brain refresh`\" —\n  would have re-declared the collided store and silenced the brain about it; it\n  now lands in `duplicated`, which names the re-distill.\n- **A missing `git` was reported as a missing repository.** `git rev-parse\n  --show-toplevel` fails for both reasons and the four repo-ness gates (`setup`,\n  `path`, `refresh`, `workspace add`) read every failure as the second. On a\n  machine with no git, a perfectly good repository was reported as \"not a git\n  repository\" and the remedy offered was `git init` — which needs the program\n  that is missing. The two are now told apart by a `git --version` probe, which\n  answers on git's presence alone and so cannot be confounded by the state of\n  the directory, and each gets its own message and its own followable remedy.\n- `docs/getting-started.md` advertised two install methods that do not exist: a\n  \"prebuilt release archive\" (the tagged releases carry no binaries and no\n  source archive) and `go install` (which the same file later admits is\n  unavailable). It now describes only the source install that works, and says\n  plainly that the Go toolchain is therefore not optional.\n- `scripts/install.sh`'s Entire CLI prerequisite said \"install the Entire CLI\",\n  which is the restatement of the problem, not a fix. It now gives the same\n  grade of answer the Go check already gave: the Homebrew cask, the install\n  script, and the repository.\n- `CHANGELOG.md` had exactly one heading, `## [Unreleased]`, covering work that\n  shipped in `v0.3.0` — no version section had ever been cut at any tag. The\n  `0.3.0` and `0.2.0` sections below are that history, recorded from the\n  published release notes.\n\n## [0.3.0] - 2026-09-13\n\n219 commits since `v0.2.0`, 62 merged PRs. Cut after an adversarial campaign of\nroughly 400 hostile invocations across nine attack families: zero panics, zero\nstack traces, zero escapes outside the store, no lost updates under 12\nconcurrent writers, and `bundle import` rejecting traversal, absolute paths,\nsymlinks, hardlinks, device nodes and a 600 MB decompression bomb. What the\ncampaign found instead was commands that reported success while something was\nwrong, which is most of what follows.\n\n### Changed\n\n- The documented journey now ends where a user actually ends up: a brain\n  onboarded in *their* repository. It used to stop at the install. `README.md`\n  said \"That is the whole install\" and the next thing a reader was given was\n  `entire brain refresh --agent none`; `entire brain setup` appeared only as an\n  aside two sections later, and `docs/getting-started.md` — a guide whose whole\n  job is the journey — never mentioned `setup` at all in 762 lines. The install\n  is machine-scoped and gives no repository a brain, so a reader finished it\n  with working plugins and nothing to show for them.\n  - `README.md` opens `## Install` with the two commands the whole journey is\n    (install once per machine, `entire brain setup` once per repository), renames\n    step 1 to \"Install the plugins on this machine\", and promotes `setup` to\n    **step 3**, the next thing a reader does. The old step 3 (`refresh`) is now\n    step 3b, described as the manual path rather than the default one.\n  - A new **step 3a** documents what `setup` genuinely requires — a local path\n    that is a git repository, checked before any side effect — versus what it\n    degrades around: `entire enable`, captured sessions, the `entire graph`\n    provider, a service manager, an agent CLI. It states the real order\n    (`entire enable` then `setup`) and that the order is a quality decision, not\n    a correctness one, and it names what a repository new to Entire actually\n    produces: an all-green run, with `Brain ready (degraded)` explained as\n    exit-0 information and each remaining cause — an uncommitted worktree, a\n    missing `entire graph` provider, an unreadable checkpoint inventory — given\n    with its fix.\n  - Step 3 says plainly that the watcher is installed as a **persistent\n    launchd/systemd service, by default, with no prompt**, names the\n    `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive` and `Restart=always` that make it survive reboot,\n    and gives `--no-daemon` and `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`.\n  - `docs/getting-started.md` gains \"Onboard a repository: `entire brain setup`\"\n    in place of \"First run\", with the phase table, the spend labels, the\n    prerequisite/ordering section, and `refresh` kept as the by-hand path.\n  - `docs/operations.md` gains an \"After the install: onboard a repository\"\n    section, because the installer it documents is not the last command a user\n    runs.\n  - `scripts/install.sh`'s closing banner said `Next: build a brain: entire\n    brain refresh --agent none` — the same truncation in the terminal. It now\n    says the machine is set up, no repository has a brain yet, and gives\n    `cd /path/to/your/repo && entire brain setup` with its token-spend caveat.\n- Every command `setup` and `status` print back is now spelled the way the\n  reader reached this binary. The brain ships as a kubectl-style external\n  command of the Entire CLI, so a reader who types `entire brain setup` never\n  types the binary's own name — and on a managed install they cannot: the\n  binary lives in `<xdg_data>/entire/plugins/bin`, a directory the Entire CLI\n  prepends to `PATH` inside its own process and nowhere else. Everything was\n  printed as `entire-brain …` regardless, so the closing **Next** block handed a\n  plugin reader three commands their shell cannot resolve. The signal is the\n  host's: its plugin dispatcher sets `ENTIRE_CLI_VERSION` unconditionally on\n  every plugin launch (`os.Args[0]` is no signal — the dispatcher execs the\n  resolved path). Absent it the standalone name stays, which is the honest\n  answer for a reader who may not have the host CLI at all. The prefix is\n  resolved once per run and threaded to every printer, so the summary's Next\n  block, every skip and failure line, all three hints, the `status` verdict and\n  `setup --help` move together.\n- `setup` says what it will register with the machine **before** it does it. A\n  run that will install the watcher now opens, ahead of any work, with one line\n  naming the unit and its path, saying plainly that it is a persistent service\n  that starts again at every login, and giving both `--no-daemon` and\n  `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon` on the same screen. Previously the\n  only mention arrived mid-summary, after a persistent launchd agent had been\n  registered in the user's real `~/Library/LaunchAgents` and started, and\n  `--uninstall-daemon` appeared only in `setup --help`. The default is\n  deliberately unchanged — whether the watcher installs unasked is a product\n  decision — but the consequence is now stated first. A re-run that finds the\n  service already there says it *keeps* it instead of claiming a second\n  install; `--no-daemon` announces nothing it will not do; a platform with no\n  service manager has nothing to announce.\n- Installing from git is one command that works with nothing arranged in\n  advance. `scripts/install.sh` no longer requires `entire-graph` to be a\n  sibling checkout with that exact name — the documented constraint that made\n  the install path fail for anyone who cloned one repository, cloned under a\n  different directory name, or ran from a worktree. It now resolves the provider\n  by three routes and prints the one it took: `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR` when set (an\n  override that is not an `entire-graph` checkout is a hard error, not a silent\n  fallback); any checkout already on the machine — siblings of this checkout and\n  of the main worktree when this is a linked one, plus the usual source\n  directories under `$HOME`, accepted only when `cmd/entire-graph` is really\n  there rather than only the directory name; otherwise a shallow clone of the\n  public `entireio/entire-graph` into\n  `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`, reused and refreshed\n  by later runs. When no route can work it fails naming all three and how to\n  satisfy each. `ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` disables the clone for an air-gapped\n  machine, and `ENTIRE_GRAPH_REPO`/`ENTIRE_GRAPH_CACHE` redirect it.\n- `scripts/install.sh` checks the prerequisites before it builds anything and\n  reports all the missing ones at once with the fix for each — `git`, the\n  `entire` CLI, a Go toolchain at least as new as the `go` directive in `go.mod`\n  (tolerating an older `go` when `GOTOOLCHAIN` may fetch the newer one), and a C\n  compiler for `entire-graph`'s tree-sitter cgo bindings. Previously the first\n  two were separate late failures and a missing C compiler surfaced minutes into\n  a build.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- First-run onboarding. Every item below made a brand-new user's very first\n  `entire-brain setup` report a failure it did not have to:\n  - `session export failed (... parse checkpoint list json: invalid character\n    'U' after top-level value)`. The `U` is the host `entire` CLI's\n    `Update available!` banner, which released versions print on the SAME stream\n    as `--json` output. The whole-buffer `json.Unmarshal` is now a decode of the\n    first JSON value that tolerates trailing output. Because the host caches its\n    version check for 24h, this failed on the first-ever run and silently healed\n    on the second.\n  - A missing `entire-graph` plugin reported only\n    `provider_doctor_failed: semantic provider no-egress status is not\n    verified`. The provider doctor's actual error is now surfaced, shortened to\n    one line, and names the install step when the plugin is simply absent.\n  - `entire-brain doctor` — the command `setup` tells you to run when a\n    component fails — printed nothing about the repository unless\n    `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` was set, which only the host sets. It now infers the\n    repository from the working directory, and says so when it cannot.\n  - A pristine clone graded itself `freshness: degraded` because the host CLI's\n    own `.entire/logs/entire.log` made the live-state check see a dirty tree.\n    That check now applies the same ignore policy every other reader already\n    applied.\n  - `setup` in a directory that is not a git repository failed four components\n    with a raw `fatal: not a git repository`, registered a workspace, installed\n    the watcher, and exited 0. It now refuses up front, before any side effect.\n  - `--uninstall-daemon` appeared only in `setup --help`. `setup` and `status`\n    now name it, and `setup` says plainly that a persistent service was\n    installed and where its unit lives.\n  - The watcher logged `budget=0` — the value a setup-installed watcher always\n    has — while `--max-sessions`, the cap that actually binds it, was not logged\n    at all.\n  - `status` hard-coded five component names, so a component `setup` reported as\n    FAILED (`entities`, say) was missing from `status` entirely.\n  - `x semantic index: repo key mismatch` on **every** repository created with\n    `git init` — and, less visibly, on every non-GitHub origin. `entire-graph`\n    recognises `github.com` remotes and otherwise falls back to\n    `local/<basename>`; the brain recognises many hosts by slug and, with no\n    usable remote, derives `local/<basename>-<sha256(abs path)[:12]>`. So the\n    two tools spelled the same repository differently, and the hint blamed the\n    reader's repository (\"this repo has no git remote; add one … or upgrade the\n    entire CLI\") for a disagreement between two of ours — advice that fired on\n    repositories which already had the remote it asked for, and that no upgrade\n    of either tool would have satisfied. The brain now recognises the provider's\n    own spelling of the repository it just asked the provider to index and\n    stores the snapshot under its own key. **A fresh `git init` repository\n    reaches an all-green `setup` with no user action.** This is not a weaker\n    crossover guard: the snapshot's commit and tree are still validated against\n    this repository's HEAD, and a snapshot naming a genuinely different\n    repository is still refused — with a hint that now says to rebuild the index\n    (`refresh index --force`) rather than to change the repository. Bundle\n    import and stored-snapshot read-back still compare keys exactly.\n  - A dirty worktree had no remedy. `entire enable` writes `.entire/settings.json`\n    and the agent's `.claude/settings.json` and does not commit them, so the very\n    next `setup` — the documented next step — found a dirty worktree and refused\n    to seed or index it, naming `--worktree`, a flag `setup` does not accept.\n    Both refusals now share one error code and `setup` attaches the remedy:\n    commit or stash, and why the tree is dirty when the reader edited nothing.\n  - A repository with no sessions yet read as a fault: \"Entire returned no\n    readable checkpoint IDs from an incomplete persistent-store inventory\".\n    True, and useless. The detail survives for anyone debugging a real fault; the\n    hint now says what the state means and points at `entire checkpoint list`.\n    A routed discovery that genuinely failed keeps its own detail.\n  - `setup` did not build the durable history projection and then told the user\n    to run `brief`, so the first `brief` after a first-ever `setup` dropped its\n    transcript half and printed \"history index missing; run `entire brain\n    refresh`\" — setup's own recommended next command reporting a gap in setup's\n    own output. `setup` and a watch tick now say which behaviour each wants, and\n    `refresh` skips the projection when its target directory does not exist\n    (the state a run where every deterministic source failed used to leave,\n    surfacing as a raw `lstat <brainDir>: no such file or directory`).\n"},"started_at_ms":1789839741963,"completed_at_ms":1789839741963}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:42:21.988Z","ordinal":7639,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","session_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","root_turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","response_id":"resp_0cb48b2b18d09e75016aaec9751abc87d293a14037450e5cc8","usage":{"input_tokens":204571,"cached_input_tokens":197248,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":62,"reasoning_output_tokens":22,"total_tokens":204633},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":75336812,"cached_input_tokens":72116480,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":174479,"reasoning_output_tokens":14816,"total_tokens":75511291},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":141221831,"cached_input_tokens":134688512,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":359278,"reasoning_output_tokens":36193,"total_tokens":141581109}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:42:22.020Z","ordinal":7640,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0bac3-1444-7ed0-b207-4e83d4c0cb95","call_id":"call_so6alqMsU8Ho4QJMhn3qahPb","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"07c3db\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000002334,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":6054,\"output\":\"# Changelog\\n\\nAll notable changes to `entire-brain` are recorded here. The format follows\\n[Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/).\\n\\n## [Unreleased]\\n\\n### Added\\n\\n- `init-agents` installs a shared Brain operating guide and preserves existing\\n  AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md text with managed blocks.\\n- `capabilities` reports compiled features and retrieval requirements without\\n  requiring a repository, model, or initialized Brain.\\n\\n\\n### Changed\\n\\n- Renamed `guide` to `agent-guide` (the old name remains an alias) and grouped\\n  the agent setup commands together in top-level help.\\n\\n- Unified retrieval under `query`: hybrid by default, with mutually exclusive\\n  `--keyword` and `--semantic` modes. Query text can be positional or passed\\n  with `--query`, with flags before or after it. Workspace queries support the\\n  same forms. `search` and `vsearch` remain hidden compatibility commands.\\n- MCP `brain_query` now accepts `keyword` and `semantic` booleans; existing\\n  retrieval tools remain compatible. Updated CLI help, docs, and agent guidance.\\n\\n### Fixed\\n\\n- A repository reached through a symlinked path no longer ends up with two\\n  brains and then locked out of both. A local repo key was the hash of whatever\\n  path string the caller supplied, so one checkout could have two identities:\\n  a relative invocation resolves symlinks (git and the `getcwd` syscall both\\n  report the physical root), while an absolute `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` kept the\\n  caller's spelling — and `mcp --print-config` baked in the UNRESOLVED path,\\n  because `os.Getwd` honours `$PWD`. On macOS `/tmp` is a symlink to\\n  `/private/tmp`, so this was the default for any repository created under\\n  `/tmp`, not an edge case. Once both stores existed, every repo-scoped command\\n  refused with `repo_identity_conflict`, including `brain_delete_project` — the\\n  one call that could have cleared them — so the MCP surface had no recovery\\n  path at all.\\n  - A local repo key is now derived from the FULLY symlink-resolved root, so it\\n    is a property of the directory rather than of the route taken to it. Every\\n    spelling collapses onto one key.\\n  - A brain already stored under an older key spelling is adopted **in place**:\\n    nothing moves on disk, so no existing store is orphaned and there is no\\n    half-finished migration to recover from. Repositories on paths with no\\n    symlinked ancestor keep byte-identical keys.\\n  - An existing root whose symlinks cannot be resolved is now reported instead\\n    of silently keyed on its lexical spelling, which would mint the second key\\n    this change exists to prevent. A root that does not exist yet still keeps\\n    its spelling: workspace manifests use those paths as durable hints.\\n  - `mcp --print-config` emits the canonical spelling, so a config-file launch\\n    and a `cd`-and-run launch of the same repository agree.\\n  - `brain_delete_project` resolves storage through the conflict-tolerant path\\n    and erases every store the repository resolves to, so it can clear a\\n    conflict rather than be blocked by one. It now removes the per-repo head\\n    store alongside the brain; leaving it behind left a cursor pointing at a\\n    deleted brain, and left the duplicate identity standing.\\n\\n### Added\\n\\n- `entire brain repo-identity` reports every brain store a repository resolves\\n  to, and `--keep <repo-key>` resolves a `repo_identity_conflict` by keeping one\\n  store and RENAMING the rest to a dated sibling — nothing is deleted, so a\\n  wrong choice is undone with `mv`. The kept history is moved onto the canonical\\n  key so every spelling of the repository finds it. The conflict message now\\n  names this command; it used to end with \\\"move one brain/head store aside and\\n  retry\\\", an instruction to run `mv` by hand with nothing behind it.\\n- `bundle export` carried one layer of seven and said nothing about the other\\n  six. The exporter hardcoded `Sources: &brainSources{Semantic: …}` and wrote\\n  only the `semantic/` subtree, so an export/import round trip into a fresh\\n  store destroyed every authored fact — in the one command whose job is moving a\\n  brain without losing it. It was never a scope decision: the code was written\\n  the day `semantic` was a brain's only source, and `history`, `facts`, `docs`\\n  and `patterns` all arrived afterwards. A bundle now carries the semantic\\n  index, seed, docs and facts by default; captured sessions and the history and\\n  patterns projections built from them travel under `--include-sessions`;\\n  session tombstones, privacy transactions and local worker state are never\\n  bundled and are refused on import. `bundle import` restores every layer it\\n  receives with its manifest source, and refuses — before writing anything — to\\n  overwrite a layer the destination already holds unless `--overwrite` is\\n  passed. Export prints what it included and what it omitted. The bundle\\n  manifest also stops claiming `generated_at: 0001-01-01T00:00:00Z`, keeps\\n  `branches`, and records `brain_version`, the build that produced the artifact.\\n- `privacy exclude` and `privacy purge` could not run at all on Windows for any\\n  session id containing a colon. Both write a durable transaction record before\\n  anything else, and its filename was built with `url.PathEscape`, which is\\n  specified against URL path segments and therefore deliberately leaves a colon\\n  alone — legal in a URL, an alternate data stream on NTFS. The first write\\n  failed with `ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER` (\\\"The parameter is incorrect\\\"). Captured\\n  session ids are UUIDs and were never affected, which is how it survived: every\\n  fixture in the suite used a hyphenated id. Ids an operator types are affected,\\n  and this tool's own vocabulary is full of colons (`conversation-session:…`,\\n  `fact:…`, `history:…`). `memoryJobRel` already hashes job ids with the comment\\n  \\\"Job IDs contain a colon, which is not a valid Windows filename\\\"; this path\\n  simply never got the same treatment. The colon and the reserved DOS device\\n  names are now escaped, and nothing else is, so every id whose characters were\\n  already safe — every UUID, and so every real captured session — keeps a\\n  byte-identical filename and no existing record is renamed.\\n- `privacy exclude` reported cleanup it had not performed. Excluding a session\\n  id that no captured session answers to is a legitimate pre-emptive privacy\\n  operation and still succeeds, but it printed \\\"removed or rebuilt its derived\\n  projections\\\" for a session that had produced none, and `privacy list` then\\n  rendered the tombstone exactly like a completed exclusion forever. Exclude now\\n  reports the real inventory the way `purge` already did, names a tombstone that\\n  matched nothing, and `privacy list` marks it `UNMATCHED` (`unmatched` in\\n  `--json`).\\n- `doctor` called an unreadable semantic store a warning, so the default\\n  `--fail-on error` gate exited 0 on a brain whose SQLite store was not a\\n  database. The `semantic` check is a mixed one — its axes include\\n  host-environment reasons like a missing `entire` binary — so it is now split\\n  along the scope line #239 introduced: the artifact axes (snapshot, store)\\n  become a brain-scoped `error` when unreadable, and the provider axis becomes a\\n  separate environment-scoped finding, reported at full severity and never\\n  gated. Staleness and a never-built index stay warnings.\\n- `facts proposals --json` printed twelve lines of human help to stdout and\\n  exited 0 — the only `--json` violation among the 92 nodes that advertise the\\n  flag. It now emits the standard JSON error envelope naming the missing verb\\n  and exits nonzero. Without `--json` the help is unchanged.\\n- `refresh index` exited 1 whenever an index existed, so a scripted refresh loop\\n  containing the documented step could be green exactly once. An already-current\\n  index is now a no-op that succeeds and says so; a stale or unreadable one is\\n  rebuilt; `--force` still rebuilds unconditionally; real failures still exit\\n  nonzero.\\n- `--version`, `-v` were \\\"unknown flag\\\". `Options.Version` was set but the\\n  `cobra.Command`'s own `Version` field never was, so cobra's\\n  `InitDefaultVersionFlag` no-opped. Both now print the same string as\\n  `entire-brain version`.\\n\\n### Changed\\n\\n- Every printed repair command resolves in the dispatch mode that printed it.\\n  The memory health and error surfaces named `entire memory repair` and\\n  `entire memory migrate` — commands NEITHER mode can run, because `memory` is a\\n  subcommand of this binary and not a verb of the host CLI. They now carry the\\n  spelling `setupCommandPrefix` resolves, the same as every other printer.\\n- `doctor` and `config` are visible in `--help` again. Both were hidden in June\\n  2026 as plugin plumbing; since then `doctor` became the documented\\n  verification step with an exit-code contract (`--fail-on`) that CI is meant to\\n  gate on, `scripts/install.sh` runs both and tells the reader to re-run\\n  `doctor`, and a user who did that and then grepped `--help` found nothing.\\n\\n### Fixed\\n\\n- **The v0.3.0 fact-integrity cross-check compared counts, so a corruption that\\n  preserved the count was invisible.** Overwriting one line of `facts.ndjson`\\n  with a copy of another leaves the store parseable, the line count unchanged\\n  and the manifest's number correct — and the fact whose line was taken is gone.\\n  `get fact:<id>` answered \\\"not found\\\" while `doctor` said `facts: ok`, `status`\\n  raised no warning, and `search` went back to volunteering that \\\"the answer may\\n  genuinely not be in the brain\\\" — the exact false statement #242 was written to\\n  eliminate, reached by a route the count could not see. The check now also\\n  compares IDENTITY: within a branch an id names exactly one fact (every writer\\n  keys by id before it writes), so a branch that yields the same id twice was\\n  not written by this program and the repeat is standing where another fact used\\n  to be. The four existing modes keep their own words; this is a fifth,\\n  `duplicated`, because a substitution is not a shortfall — nothing is short,\\n  something was replaced. The comparison is scoped to a branch and never to the\\n  brain: a fact id is derived from text and paths only, so `facts promote`\\n  carrying a fact between branches stores one id twice on purpose, and a\\n  brain-wide id set would have reported every promote as data loss. A duplicate\\n  that was appended rather than written over used to land in `stale`, whose\\n  advice — \\\"the manifest is behind the store — run `entire brain refresh`\\\" —\\n  would have re-declared the collided store and silenced the brain about it; it\\n  now lands in `duplicated`, which names the re-distill.\\n- **A missing `git` was reported as a missing repository.** `git rev-parse\\n  --show-toplevel` fails for both reasons and the four repo-ness gates (`setup`,\\n  `path`, `refresh`, `workspace add`) read every failure as the second. On a\\n  machine with no git, a perfectly good repository was reported as \\\"not a git\\n  repository\\\" and the remedy offered was `git init` — which needs the program\\n  that is missing. The two are now told apart by a `git --version` probe, which\\n  answers on git's presence alone and so cannot be confounded by the state of\\n  the directory, and each gets its own message and its own followable remedy.\\n- `docs/getting-started.md` advertised two install methods that do not exist: a\\n  \\\"prebuilt release archive\\\" (the tagged releases carry no binaries and no\\n  source archive) and `go install` (which the same file later admits is\\n  unavailable). It now describes only the source install that works, and says\\n  plainly that the Go toolchain is therefore not optional.\\n- `scripts/install.sh`'s Entire CLI prerequisite said \\\"install the Entire CLI\\\",\\n  which is the restatement of the problem, not a fix. It now gives the same\\n  grade of answer the Go check already gave: the Homebrew cask, the install\\n  script, and the repository.\\n- `CHANGELOG.md` had exactly one heading, `## [Unreleased]`, covering work that\\n  shipped in `v0.3.0` — no version section had ever been cut at any tag. The\\n  `0.3.0` and `0.2.0` sections below are that history, recorded from the\\n  published release notes.\\n\\n## [0.3.0] - 2026-09-13\\n\\n219 commits since `v0.2.0`, 62 merged PRs. Cut after an adversarial campaign of\\nroughly 400 hostile invocations across nine attack families: zero panics, zero\\nstack traces, zero escapes outside the store, no lost updates under 12\\nconcurrent writers, and `bundle import` rejecting traversal, absolute paths,\\nsymlinks, hardlinks, device nodes and a 600 MB decompression bomb. What the\\ncampaign found instead was commands that reported success while something was\\nwrong, which is most of what follows.\\n\\n### Changed\\n\\n- The documented journey now ends where a user actually ends up: a brain\\n  onboarded in *their* repository. It used to stop at the install. `README.md`\\n  said \\\"That is the whole install\\\" and the next thing a reader was given was\\n  `entire brain refresh --agent none`; `entire brain setup` appeared only as an\\n  aside two sections later, and `docs/getting-started.md` — a guide whose whole\\n  job is the journey — never mentioned `setup` at all in 762 lines. The install\\n  is machine-scoped and gives no repository a brain, so a reader finished it\\n  with working plugins and nothing to show for them.\\n  - `README.md` opens `## Install` with the two commands the whole journey is\\n    (install once per machine, `entire brain setup` once per repository), renames\\n    step 1 to \\\"Install the plugins on this machine\\\", and promotes `setup` to\\n    **step 3**, the next thing a reader does. The old step 3 (`refresh`) is now\\n    step 3b, described as the manual path rather than the default one.\\n  - A new **step 3a** documents what `setup` genuinely requires — a local path\\n    that is a git repository, checked before any side effect — versus what it\\n    degrades around: `entire enable`, captured sessions, the `entire graph`\\n    provider, a service manager, an agent CLI. It states the real order\\n    (`entire enable` then `setup`) and that the order is a quality decision, not\\n    a correctness one, and it names what a repository new to Entire actually\\n    produces: an all-green run, with `Brain ready (degraded)` explained as\\n    exit-0 information and each remaining cause — an uncommitted worktree, a\\n    missing `entire graph` provider, an unreadable checkpoint inventory — given\\n    with its fix.\\n  - Step 3 says plainly that the watcher is installed as a **persistent\\n    launchd/systemd service, by default, with no prompt**, names the\\n    `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive` and `Restart=always` that make it survive reboot,\\n    and gives `--no-daemon` and `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`.\\n  - `docs/getting-started.md` gains \\\"Onboard a repository: `entire brain setup`\\\"\\n    in place of \\\"First run\\\", with the phase table, the spend labels, the\\n    prerequisite/ordering section, and `refresh` kept as the by-hand path.\\n  - `docs/operations.md` gains an \\\"After the install: onboard a repository\\\"\\n    section, because the installer it documents is not the last command a user\\n    runs.\\n  - `scripts/install.sh`'s closing banner said `Next: build a brain: entire\\n    brain refresh --agent none` — the same truncation in the terminal. It now\\n    says the machine is set up, no repository has a brain yet, and gives\\n    `cd /path/to/your/repo && entire brain setup` with its token-spend caveat.\\n- Every command `setup` and `status` print back is now spelled the way the\\n  reader reached this binary. The brain ships as a kubectl-style external\\n  command of the Entire CLI, so a reader who types `entire brain setup` never\\n  types the binary's own name — and on a managed install they cannot: the\\n  binary lives in `<xdg_data>/entire/plugins/bin`, a directory the Entire CLI\\n  prepends to `PATH` inside its own process and nowhere else. Everything was\\n  printed as `entire-brain …` regardless, so the closing **Next** block handed a\\n  plugin reader three commands their shell cannot resolve. The signal is the\\n  host's: its plugin dispatcher sets `ENTIRE_CLI_VERSION` unconditionally on\\n  every plugin launch (`os.Args[0]` is no signal — the dispatcher execs the\\n  resolved path). Absent it the standalone name stays, which is the honest\\n  answer for a reader who may not have the host CLI at all. The prefix is\\n  resolved once per run and threaded to every printer, so the summary's Next\\n  block, every skip and failure line, all three hints, the `status` verdict and\\n  `setup --help` move together.\\n- `setup` says what it will register with the machine **before** it does it. A\\n  run that will install the watcher now opens, ahead of any work, with one line\\n  naming the unit and its path, saying plainly that it is a persistent service\\n  that starts again at every login, and giving both `--no-daemon` and\\n  `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon` on the same screen. Previously the\\n  only mention arrived mid-summary, after a persistent launchd agent had been\\n  registered in the user's real `~/Library/LaunchAgents` and started, and\\n  `--uninstall-daemon` appeared only in `setup --help`. The default is\\n  deliberately unchanged — whether the watcher installs unasked is a product\\n  decision — but the consequence is now stated first. A re-run that finds the\\n  service already there says it *keeps* it instead of claiming a second\\n  install; `--no-daemon` announces nothing it will not do; a platform with no\\n  service manager has nothing to announce.\\n- Installing from git is one command that works with nothing arranged in\\n  advance. `scripts/install.sh` no longer requires `entire-graph` to be a\\n  sibling checkout with that exact name — the documented constraint that made\\n  the install path fail for anyone who cloned one repository, cloned under a\\n  different directory name, or ran from a worktree. It now resolves the provider\\n  by three routes and prints the one it took: `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR` when set (an\\n  override that is not an `entire-graph` checkout is a hard error, not a silent\\n  fallback); any checkout already on the machine — siblings of this checkout and\\n  of the main worktree when this is a linked one, plus the usual source\\n  directories under `$HOME`, accepted only when `cmd/entire-graph` is really\\n  there rather than only the directory name; otherwise a shallow clone of the\\n  public `entireio/entire-graph` into\\n  `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`, reused and refreshed\\n  by later runs. When no route can work it fails naming all three and how to\\n  satisfy each. `ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` disables the clone for an air-gapped\\n  machine, and `ENTIRE_GRAPH_REPO`/`ENTIRE_GRAPH_CACHE` redirect it.\\n- `scripts/install.sh` checks the prerequisites before it builds anything and\\n  reports all the missing ones at once with the fix for each — `git`, the\\n  `entire` CLI, a Go toolchain at least as new as the `go` directive in `go.mod`\\n  (tolerating an older `go` when `GOTOOLCHAIN` may fetch the newer one), and a C\\n  compiler for `entire-graph`'s tree-sitter cgo bindings. Previously the first\\n  two were separate late failures and a missing C compiler surfaced minutes into\\n  a build.\\n\\n### Fixed\\n\\n- First-run onboarding. Every item below made a brand-new user's very first\\n  `entire-brain setup` report a failure it did not have to:\\n  - `session export failed (... parse checkpoint list json: invalid character\\n    'U' after top-level value)`. The `U` is the host `entire` CLI's\\n    `Update available!` banner, which released versions print on the SAME stream\\n    as `--json` output. The whole-buffer `json.Unmarshal` is now a decode of the\\n    first JSON value that tolerates trailing output. Because the host caches its\\n    version check for 24h, this failed on the first-ever run and silently healed\\n    on the second.\\n  - A missing `entire-graph` plugin reported only\\n    `provider_doctor_failed: semantic provider no-egress status is not\\n    verified`. The provider doctor's actual error is now surfaced, shortened to\\n    one line, and names the install step when the plugin is simply absent.\\n  - `entire-brain doctor` — the command `setup` tells you to run when a\\n    component fails — printed nothing about the repository unless\\n    `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` was set, which only the host sets. It now infers the\\n    repository from the working directory, and says so when it cannot.\\n  - A pristine clone graded itself `freshness: degraded` because the host CLI's\\n    own `.entire/logs/entire.log` made the live-state check see a dirty tree.\\n    That check now applies the same ignore policy every other reader already\\n    applied.\\n  - `setup` in a directory that is not a git repository failed four components\\n    with a raw `fatal: not a git repository`, registered a workspace, installed\\n    the watcher, and exited 0. It now refuses up front, before any side effect.\\n  - `--uninstall-daemon` appeared only in `setup --help`. `setup` and `status`\\n    now name it, and `setup` says plainly that a persistent service was\\n    installed and where its unit lives.\\n  - The watcher logged `budget=0` — the value a setup-installed watcher always\\n    has — while `--max-sessions`, the cap that actually binds it, was not logged\\n    at all.\\n  - `status` hard-coded five component names, so a component `setup` reported as\\n    FAILED (`entities`, say) was missing from `status` entirely.\\n  - `x semantic index: repo key mismatch` on **every** repository created with\\n    `git init` — and, less visibly, on every non-GitHub origin. `entire-graph`\\n    recognises `github.com` remotes and otherwise falls back to\\n    `local/<basename>`; the brain recognises many hosts by slug and, with no\\n    usable remote, derives `local/<basename>-<sha256(abs path)[:12]>`. So the\\n    two tools spelled the same repository differently, and the hint blamed the\\n    reader's repository (\\\"this repo has no git remote; add one … or upgrade the\\n    entire CLI\\\") for a disagreement between two of ours — advice that fired on\\n    repositories which already had the remote it asked for, and that no upgrade\\n    of either tool would have satisfied. The brain now recognises the provider's\\n    own spelling of the repository it just asked the provider to index and\\n    stores the snapshot under its own key. **A fresh `git init` repository\\n    reaches an all-green `setup` with no user action.** This is not a weaker\\n    crossover guard: the snapshot's commit and tree are still validated against\\n    this repository's HEAD, and a snapshot naming a genuinely different\\n    repository is still refused — with a hint that now says to rebuild the index\\n    (`refresh index --force`) rather than to change the repository. Bundle\\n    import and stored-snapshot read-back still compare keys exactly.\\n  - A dirty worktree had no remedy. `entire enable` writes `.entire/settings.json`\\n    and the agent's `.claude/settings.json` and does not commit them, so the very\\n    next `setup` — the documented next step — found a dirty worktree and refused\\n    to seed or index it, naming `--worktree`, a flag `setup` does not accept.\\n    Both refusals now share one error code and `setup` attaches the remedy:\\n    commit or stash, and why the tree is dirty when the reader edited nothing.\\n  - A repository with no sessions yet read as a fault: \\\"Entire returned no\\n    readable checkpoint IDs from an incomplete persistent-store inventory\\\".\\n    True, and useless. The detail survives for anyone debugging a real fault; the\\n    hint now says what the state means and points at `entire checkpoint list`.\\n    A routed discovery that genuinely failed keeps its own detail.\\n  - `setup` did not build the durable history projection and then told the user\\n    to run `brief`, so the first `brief` after a first-ever `setup` dropped its\\n    transcript half and printed \\\"history index missing; run `entire brain\\n    refresh`\\\" — setup's own recommended next command reporting a gap in setup's\\n    own output. `setup` and a watch tick now say which behaviour each wants, and\\n    `refresh` skips the projection when its target directory does not exist\\n    (the state a run where every deterministic source failed used to leave,\\n    surfacing as a raw `lstat <brainDir>: no such file or directory`).\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","create_time":1789839742.020094}}}
