{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:43:04.000Z","ordinal":7671,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0cb48b2b18d09e75016aaec9a7147087d29079d1c327c5593a","status":"completed","call_id":"call_a6NRq7gZSps1dkPiVEStwday","name":"exec","input":"text(await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '731,1100p' docs/reference.md\",\"max_output_tokens\":10000}));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","create_time":1789839780.37253}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:43:04.092Z","ordinal":7672,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-741c2bcc-a1f1-46e7-a66c-5c5e599c9383","process_id":"66208","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '731,1100p' docs/reference.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/thomi/.codex/worktrees/5d33/entire-brain","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '731,1100p' docs/reference.md","name":"reference.md","path":"docs/reference.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"entire brain watch --distill --distill-every 24h --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low\n```\n\n`--distill-every` plus the persisted watch cursor are the durable spend guard:\none gated agent run per repo per window, across restarts. `--max-sessions` caps\nhow many not-yet-distilled sessions ONE gated pass processes (0 = no cap), so\nthe frequency guard and the volume guard are separate knobs. `--budget` is a\ndifferent, weaker thing — it counts gated runs for the life of the **process**\nand never resets, so on a long-lived daemon `--budget 1` means one run *ever*,\nnot one per window. Use it only for a bounded foreground run.\n\nThe watcher keeps memory fresh in two tiers, like a brain: on every tick it\nruns the cheap **short-term** path (`entire brain refresh delta`; incremental\ncheckpoint export plus an overlay index of only the transcripts that changed;\nseconds even on very large brains), so an in-flight session's earlier turns\nand parallel terminals' work are searchable near-real-time. The change-gated\nfull refresh is **consolidation**: it absorbs the short-term overlay into\nlong-term memory (full index, FTS, vectors) and clears the buffer. Between\nchanges it is additionally throttled by `--consolidate-every` (default 30m);\nthe throttle is skipped whenever the delta was incomplete (a failed scan or a\nfull buffer), so partial short-term coverage always consolidates promptly. You\ncan run `entire brain refresh delta` by hand any time you want immediate\nrecall of just-captured work.\n\nIf you also want generated seed summaries, run refresh with an agent instead of\nthe deterministic seed path (`entire brain refresh --agent auto --seed-model\ngpt-5.4-mini --seed-effort low`). Seed synthesis orients an agent on the\nrepository; durable facts are branch-scoped retained knowledge — the two are\nseparate.\n\n## How Agents Use The Brain\n\nAgents rely on a capture layer plus four consumption surfaces. The capture layer\nproduces the raw material; the consumption surfaces are how an agent actually\nqueries or receives brain context. Which surface matters depends on the agent\nclient and how the repository is configured.\n\n### Capture layer: Entire hooks produce the raw material\n\n`entire-cli` installs lifecycle hooks for agents such as Claude Code, Codex,\nGemini CLI, OpenCode, Cursor, Factory AI Droid, Copilot CLI, and Pi. Those hooks\nstart and stop sessions, capture prompts and transcripts, track changed files,\nrecord subagent work where the agent exposes it, and attach checkpoint metadata\nto commits. This is background infrastructure: the agent does not remember to\nsave a transcript, and the human does not paste context into the brain.\n\nIf an agent is not seeing Entire context at all, check the base Entire\nintegration first, not the brain (`entire status`, `entire agent`).\n\n### 1. MCP is the preferred agent tool surface\n\nFor agents that support MCP, register `entire brain mcp` as a local stdio MCP\nserver. It opens no network listener and wraps the local brain's JSON contracts\nas tools. Once MCP is available, an agent should call MCP tools instead of\nshelling out to the CLI. The normal first call is `brain_brief`, then targeted\nfollow-ups:\n\n- `brain_brief` for task-shaped context, history hits, likely files, and tests\n- `brain_status` for a compact freshness/coverage preflight; set\n  `details: true` for the full status JSON contract\n- `brain_refresh` for a bounded seed/docs refresh when retrieval freshness is\n  unsafe. Its seed and docs include the current worktree by default, it never\n  exports checkpoint sessions, and it is capped at 60 seconds; set\n  `semantic: true` only for small repositories and use\n  `brain_index_repository` as the separate long-running semantic step for large\n  repositories. `worktree` covers seed and docs only -- the semantic index is\n  always built from committed HEAD, so a dirty semantic snapshot needs\n  `brain_index_repository` with `worktree: true`. Set `worktree: false` only\n  when seed and docs must come from committed\n  HEAD; use `entire brain refresh sessions` from the CLI for checkpoint history\n- retrieval tools such as `brain_query` and `brain_get` for facts, docs, history\n- semantic tools such as `brain_code`, `brain_context`, `brain_impact`, and\n  `brain_tests` for code navigation and validation planning\n- `brain_entity_history` for \"which checkpoints and sessions changed this\n  function/class\", answered from the persisted entity index rather than by\n  re-reading history (build it once with `entire brain entities backfill`)\n- review, workspace, and pattern tools such as `brain_regressions`,\n  `brain_workspace_graph`, `brain_workspace_review`, and `brain_patterns` when\n  the task calls for them\n\nSome MCP tools write local state (for example `brain_ingest_traces` persists\nruntime edges as `RUNTIME_TRACE` facts); pattern MCP tools are read-only. The\nMCP and CLI surfaces are intentionally close but not perfect mirrors — prefer the\nnative surface your agent client has, then translate only when needed. See\n[docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md](docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md) for the full tool surface.\n\n### 2. Intake instructions are the file-based fallback\n\nWhen MCP is not available, an agent can use the brain through the intake\ninstructions in `templates/`. The templates tell the agent to locate the brain\nwith `entire brain path \"$PWD\"`, then read staged context instead of blindly\nopening everything: `README.md` and `manifest.json` in the brain directory, seed\nfiles under `seed/agent/`, `seed/history-gaps.md` when present, and only\ntask-relevant transcripts selected from the manifest. Treat gaps as uncertainty\nrather than inventing rationale.\n\n### 3. Direct CLI is a compatibility surface\n\nFor clients without MCP, direct `entire brain ... --json` calls are the\ncompatibility surface (humans and scripts use the same commands). Treat it like a\ntool API, not an invitation to run a long command tour:\n\nThe provider naming migration is an intentional compatibility break. Direct CLI\nautomation must use `entire graph`, `--graph-binary`, `--skip-graph`,\n`--graph-timeout`, and `--graph-inactivity-timeout`. MCP server deployments use\n`ENTIRE_BRAIN_GRAPH_BINARY` for the trusted provider-binary override. Aliases for\nprior provider-facing names are not supported.\n\n```sh\nentire brain status --json\nentire brain brief \"<task>\" --json\n# then the smallest next question:\nentire brain query \"<decision or prior-work question>\" --json\nentire brain inspect code \"<symbol or concept>\" --json\nentire brain inspect tests \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect regressions \"<task or invariant>\" --location-only --json\n```\n\n`status` without `--json` prints a SHORT human report: the brain's identity,\nthe onboarding block (fact-backfill progress, watcher health, instant-phase\ncomponents) and a one-line health verdict. `status --verbose` prints the full\ntext report — coverage histograms, freshness axes, blind spots, live state —\nwith repeated warnings collapsed to one line per reason; `entire brain doctor`\nlists the skipped files in full. `status --json` is unchanged by either flag.\n\n`status --json` preserves the full status contract. Check\n`semantic.freshness.severity` before graph inspection and\n`retrieval.freshness.severity` before query/get. A semantic-only\n`refresh index` does not rebuild seed/docs: use `entire brain refresh --agent\nnone` when retrieval is stale, adding `--worktree` only when current\nuncommitted content should be included. `status --json --details` remains an\naccepted compatibility spelling for callers that already use it.\n`inspect code --json`, `inspect context --json`, `inspect impact --json`, and\n`inspect tests --json` likewise return compact semantic records by default; add\n`--details` only when provider metadata is needed. Their defaults are 10 code\nresults, 5 context symbols, 20 impact symbols, and 3 test suggestions;\n`--limit` remains available for deliberate expansion.\n\nPrefer `query` for broad facts/history/docs, `search` for exact terms, semantic\n`inspect` subcommands for code-graph questions, and `get`/`multi-get` when a\nprior result returned an id. Retrieval returns ten bounded excerpts by default;\nraise `--limit` deliberately instead of treating ranked search as a full-record\ndump. Don't broaden into repo-wide text search until the brain's targeted\ncontext has been used.\n\n### 4. Hidden hooks deliver context at the moment of relevance\n\n`entire-brain` also has a hidden hook surface for harness integrations. These are\nnot installed by base Entire enablement; a harness must be configured to invoke\nthem (for example Claude Code `PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse` hooks). They surface a\nsmall, high-confidence fact exactly when it matters — facts anchored to a file\nbefore editing it, gotchas after a failed command, optional one-session refresh\nafter a session ends. The contract is intentionally quiet: with no relevant\nfact, no local brain, or an environment problem, the hook exits successfully with\nno stdout output, so it never breaks the agent workflow.\n\n## Usage Scenarios\n\nThe examples name the preferred MCP tool when there is one. Agent clients without\nMCP should use the equivalent `entire brain ... --json` command.\n\n### Orient an agent on a new task\n\nStart by asking for task-shaped context, not a generic summary. The normal tool\nis `brain_brief` — it returns freshness, likely files and symbols, relevant\nhistory, durable facts, likely tests, and blind spots. Use it before broad shell\nsearch. For repo-level orientation instead of task-specific, use `overview`\n(`entire brain overview --json`): a compact project map of stack signals,\nentrypoints, commands, key documents, and recent decisions.\n\nFor interactive human exploration instead of scripted queries, `entire brain\ndash` opens a terminal dashboard over facts, sessions, history, and semantic\nrecords, and `entire brain viz` opens a local, no-egress browser view of the\nsemantic call graph. Both are read-only and make no agent calls.\n\n### Keep the brain fresh\n\nFreshness is part of every answer — a stale index, missing provider, dirty-\nworktree mismatch, or parser blind spot changes how much an agent should trust\nthe brain. Agents check freshness through `brain_status`/`status --json` before\nrelying on semantic answers; if unsafe, an agent with write permission can\nrequest a refresh, otherwise it reports the limitation and falls back to direct\ninspection.\n\n```sh\nentire brain status\nentire brain refresh --agent none\nentire brain watch\n```\n\nWhen semantic artifacts are out of sync or damaged, use the maintenance paths\ninstead of deleting the whole brain (`refresh index`, `repair`, `reset\n--semantic-only --force`). Use `refresh index --worktree` only when you\nintentionally want uncommitted state indexed; exported bundles reject\nworktree-backed semantic indexes.\n\n### Continue or explain prior work\n\nWhen the question is \"why is this like this?\", \"what did the previous agent\ntry?\", or \"where did this session leave off?\", search the history and facts\nlayers — `brain_query`, then `brain_get` for specific ids. This is\nthe main reason Entire capture matters: the original prompt, attempts,\nvalidation, correction, and rationale survive the code diff and become available\nto the next agent.\n\n### Ask across facts, history, and docs\n\nWhen the question is not tied to one symbol, use the unified retrieval layer.\n`query` is the hybrid path (lexical + vector, RRF) over durable facts, indexed\nhistory, and docs; `query --keyword` for exact keywords, `query --semantic` for semantic matches,\n`get`/`multi-get` when a result returns an id. Every result carries an `id`.\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"how does checkpointing work\" --json\nentire brain query --keyword \"checkpoint\" --json\nentire brain query --semantic \"preventing data races\" --json\nentire brain get fact:<id> --json\n```\n\n`query` also takes `--source` (`all` | `fact` |\n`history` | `conversation` | `doc`) to restrict retrieval to one layer. The\ndefault is unchanged (`all` = facts + classified history + docs).\n\nUse `entire brain query \"text\"` for default hybrid retrieval. Select keyword\nmatching with `--keyword` or semantic matching with `--semantic`; the two\nflags are mutually exclusive and belong only to the query command.\nText may instead be supplied as `--query \"text\"`. Flags work before or after\npositional text; combining positional text and `--query` is an error.\nFor example: `entire brain query --keyword --query \"RetryPolicy\" --json`.\n\nWorkspace retrieval supports the same forms:\n`entire brain workspace query <workspace> --semantic --query \"retry policy\"`.\nThe old `search` and `vsearch` commands remain hidden compatibility aliases.\nMCP agents should use `brain_query` with optional, mutually exclusive\n`keyword: true` or `semantic: true` arguments; `brain_search` and\n`brain_vsearch` remain compatibility tools.\n\n### Recall prior conversations (experimental, opt-in)\n\n`--source conversation` searches captured request/response exchanges from\nexported session transcripts (what was asked, what the agent concluded), and\n`get conversation:<id>` expands one exchange to a bounded request/response pair\nwith its exact transcript range:\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"why did we reject the cache rewrite\" --source conversation --json\nentire brain query --keyword \"SQLITE_BUSY\" --source conversation --json\nentire brain get conversation:<id> --json\n```\n\nConversation queries take structured filters: `--after`/`--before` (RFC3339 or\nYYYY-MM-DD session time), `--session <id>`, `--agent <harness>`, and `--branch`.\nThese error on any other source rather than being silently ignored. Results\ncarry `matched_terms` (which query tokens actually hit) and are diversity-capped\nso one long session cannot crowd out every other trajectory; filtering to a\nsession lifts the cap. Re-exported duplicate sessions are collapsed at index\ntime (newest export wins).\n\nMulti-concept recall: `--concept <text>` (repeatable, up to 4, conversation\nsource only) finds sessions covering the query AND every concept, even when\nthe concepts live in different exchanges of the session. Results are\n`conversation-session:` records ranked by worst per-concept rank (then rank\nsum, then session reference) with `evidence_ids` naming the exact supporting\nexchanges. Lexical mode enumerates the complete in-scope match set per\nconcept up to a 10,000-candidate ceiling and returns `memory_query_too_broad`\nbeyond it; vector/hybrid concept ranking is explicitly approximate. MCP takes\nthe same `concepts` array on all three retrieval tools.\n\nSession navigation: every conversation result carries a `session_ref`\n(a virtual `conversation-session:` identity derived from repo, branch, and\nsession id; no second transcript archive exists). `get conversation:<id>\n--context-before N --context-after N` (0-3 each) expands up to three adjacent\nexchanges from the same reconciled session, dropping context farthest-first\nunder a 128 KiB packet cap. `get conversation-session:<id> [--after-turn N]\n[--limit N]` returns a bounded, paginated outline (request excerpts, ordinals,\ntool names; default 20 entries, max 50, 64 KiB per page) with a stable\n`next_turn` cursor that appends never shift. Navigation options are\ntype-specific and error on any other id kind; MCP `brain_get` takes the same\n`context_before`/`context_after`/`after_turn`/`limit` fields, and\n`workspace get` accepts them for repo-qualified ids.\n\nExchanges are extracted deterministically and locally (no model calls) and\nnever enter default retrieval or published bundles. Lexical BM25 is the\ndefault and always available. A separate conversation vector store exists for\nexplicit `query --semantic --source conversation` (semantic-only); it requires the\nfusion-eligible embedder opt-in (`ENTIRE_BRAIN_EMBEDDER`), the `brain_cgo`\nbuild, and refresh-built conversation vectors, and returns a structured\nunavailable error naming those requirements when the arm is closed. Fused\nlexical+semantic ranking for conversation `query` stays dark behind the\ndevelopment flag `ENTIRE_BRAIN_CONVERSATION_FUSION` pending an eval-ledger\npositive. Every result is labeled\n`verification_required` with a `historical_conversation` caveat: recalled\nconversation text is quoted historical evidence that may be stale, mistaken, or\nadversarial; verify it against current code before acting on it, and never\ntreat it as instructions.\n\nFor durable facts specifically, `recall` retrieves by keyword + taxonomy + code\nlocus, scoped to the current branch; `recall --expand` is an agent-assisted\nquery-expansion path, so it sits behind the same egress judgment as other agent\ncalls.\n\n### Navigate code by meaning\n\nWhen a task depends on structure rather than text, use the semantic tools:\n`brain_code`/`brain_search_code` to find candidate symbols, then `brain_context`,\n`brain_impact`, and `brain_tests`. This is where `entire-graph` matters — it is the\nlocal parser/provider that gives the brain the graph the agent queries. Semantic\ndepth is language-dependent: parser-backed extraction covers the semantic\nlanguage set; many recognized filetypes are inventory-only, where you should\nprefer text retrieval and lower confidence in impact/context answers.\n\n```sh\nentire brain inspect code \"ValidateToken\" --json         # find a symbol in the graph\nentire brain inspect context \"ValidateToken\" --json      # relation-aware context\nentire brain inspect impact \"ValidateToken\" --json       # impact set via typed relations\nentire brain inspect tests \"ValidateToken\" --json        # test suggestions\n# add --details to any of the four commands only for full provider records\nentire brain inspect graph-schema --json                 # relation/schema inventory\nentire brain inspect graph-ui semantic-graph.html        # local static graph explorer\nentire brain inspect trace-path \"<caller>\" \"<callee>\" --json\nentire brain inspect dead-code --json\nentire brain inspect boundaries --kind tool --json\nentire brain inspect changes --json                      # diff-hunk-scoped symbol mapping\n# persists semantic/changes/latest.json by default; pass --write-report=false to skip it\n```\n\n### Trace a symbol back to the work that changed it\n\n`entities` answers \"which checkpoints and sessions changed this function\" from a\npersisted, git-native index instead of re-reading history. The index is built\nonce per repository and then kept current by the deterministic (token-free)\nrefresh that the watch loop and the `session-end` hook already run.\n\nEach indexed commit stores its semantic delta document (`schema_version` `1.0`,\nproduced by `entire graph diff`) as a git-meta record on the commit, plus a\nreverse `entity -> commits` list and a rename/move alias, so a symbol's history\nsurvives the names it has been through. The local join of commits to checkpoints\nand sessions is derived state, pinned to the git-meta ref and rebuilt whenever\nthe index moves.\n\nCoverage is tracked as a **window**, not a mark: a per-branch `floor..tip` range\nin which every first-parent commit is indexed. Freshness ticks push the tip\nforward over commits that landed since (so a tick reads only the new ones), and\nbounded `--limit` passes pull the floor backward until it reaches the root.\n\n```sh\nentire brain entities backfill                 # index history (--limit 0 for all)\nentire brain entities backfill --checkpoints-only\nentire brain entities history \"ValidateToken\" --json\nentire brain entities history \"ValidateToken\" --branch main\nentire brain entities show <commit|checkpoint-id>   # the stored delta document\n```\n\n`--checkpoints-only` is a filtered convenience pass: it indexes commits carrying\nan `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer and deliberately leaves plain commits alone, so it\ndoes **not** move the window. Coverage of a branch always comes from an ordinary\npass.\n\nDistill uses the same index to sharpen fact provenance: a fact whose locus names\nan indexed entity is anchored to the checkpoint of ITS OWN session that actually\nchanged that entity, rather than to the session's last checkpoint. With no index,\nno match, an ambiguous one, or a candidate from another session or branch, the\nprevious anchor is kept unchanged.\n\n### Review risk without a clean diff\n\nFor long sessions, manual edits, or suspected regressions from memory, use\ndiff-less regression review — `brain_regressions`/`brain_review`. It compares the\ncurrent working tree against what session history asserts the code used to be and\nflags suspected regressions (`file:line`, expected vs current, confidence,\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"entire brain watch --distill --distill-every 24h --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low\n```\n\n`--distill-every` plus the persisted watch cursor are the durable spend guard:\none gated agent run per repo per window, across restarts. `--max-sessions` caps\nhow many not-yet-distilled sessions ONE gated pass processes (0 = no cap), so\nthe frequency guard and the volume guard are separate knobs. `--budget` is a\ndifferent, weaker thing — it counts gated runs for the life of the **process**\nand never resets, so on a long-lived daemon `--budget 1` means one run *ever*,\nnot one per window. Use it only for a bounded foreground run.\n\nThe watcher keeps memory fresh in two tiers, like a brain: on every tick it\nruns the cheap **short-term** path (`entire brain refresh delta`; incremental\ncheckpoint export plus an overlay index of only the transcripts that changed;\nseconds even on very large brains), so an in-flight session's earlier turns\nand parallel terminals' work are searchable near-real-time. The change-gated\nfull refresh is **consolidation**: it absorbs the short-term overlay into\nlong-term memory (full index, FTS, vectors) and clears the buffer. Between\nchanges it is additionally throttled by `--consolidate-every` (default 30m);\nthe throttle is skipped whenever the delta was incomplete (a failed scan or a\nfull buffer), so partial short-term coverage always consolidates promptly. You\ncan run `entire brain refresh delta` by hand any time you want immediate\nrecall of just-captured work.\n\nIf you also want generated seed summaries, run refresh with an agent instead of\nthe deterministic seed path (`entire brain refresh --agent auto --seed-model\ngpt-5.4-mini --seed-effort low`). Seed synthesis orients an agent on the\nrepository; durable facts are branch-scoped retained knowledge — the two are\nseparate.\n\n## How Agents Use The Brain\n\nAgents rely on a capture layer plus four consumption surfaces. The capture layer\nproduces the raw material; the consumption surfaces are how an agent actually\nqueries or receives brain context. Which surface matters depends on the agent\nclient and how the repository is configured.\n\n### Capture layer: Entire hooks produce the raw material\n\n`entire-cli` installs lifecycle hooks for agents such as Claude Code, Codex,\nGemini CLI, OpenCode, Cursor, Factory AI Droid, Copilot CLI, and Pi. Those hooks\nstart and stop sessions, capture prompts and transcripts, track changed files,\nrecord subagent work where the agent exposes it, and attach checkpoint metadata\nto commits. This is background infrastructure: the agent does not remember to\nsave a transcript, and the human does not paste context into the brain.\n\nIf an agent is not seeing Entire context at all, check the base Entire\nintegration first, not the brain (`entire status`, `entire agent`).\n\n### 1. MCP is the preferred agent tool surface\n\nFor agents that support MCP, register `entire brain mcp` as a local stdio MCP\nserver. It opens no network listener and wraps the local brain's JSON contracts\nas tools. Once MCP is available, an agent should call MCP tools instead of\nshelling out to the CLI. The normal first call is `brain_brief`, then targeted\nfollow-ups:\n\n- `brain_brief` for task-shaped context, history hits, likely files, and tests\n- `brain_status` for a compact freshness/coverage preflight; set\n  `details: true` for the full status JSON contract\n- `brain_refresh` for a bounded seed/docs refresh when retrieval freshness is\n  unsafe. Its seed and docs include the current worktree by default, it never\n  exports checkpoint sessions, and it is capped at 60 seconds; set\n  `semantic: true` only for small repositories and use\n  `brain_index_repository` as the separate long-running semantic step for large\n  repositories. `worktree` covers seed and docs only -- the semantic index is\n  always built from committed HEAD, so a dirty semantic snapshot needs\n  `brain_index_repository` with `worktree: true`. Set `worktree: false` only\n  when seed and docs must come from committed\n  HEAD; use `entire brain refresh sessions` from the CLI for checkpoint history\n- retrieval tools such as `brain_query` and `brain_get` for facts, docs, history\n- semantic tools such as `brain_code`, `brain_context`, `brain_impact`, and\n  `brain_tests` for code navigation and validation planning\n- `brain_entity_history` for \"which checkpoints and sessions changed this\n  function/class\", answered from the persisted entity index rather than by\n  re-reading history (build it once with `entire brain entities backfill`)\n- review, workspace, and pattern tools such as `brain_regressions`,\n  `brain_workspace_graph`, `brain_workspace_review`, and `brain_patterns` when\n  the task calls for them\n\nSome MCP tools write local state (for example `brain_ingest_traces` persists\nruntime edges as `RUNTIME_TRACE` facts); pattern MCP tools are read-only. The\nMCP and CLI surfaces are intentionally close but not perfect mirrors — prefer the\nnative surface your agent client has, then translate only when needed. See\n[docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md](docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md) for the full tool surface.\n\n### 2. Intake instructions are the file-based fallback\n\nWhen MCP is not available, an agent can use the brain through the intake\ninstructions in `templates/`. The templates tell the agent to locate the brain\nwith `entire brain path \"$PWD\"`, then read staged context instead of blindly\nopening everything: `README.md` and `manifest.json` in the brain directory, seed\nfiles under `seed/agent/`, `seed/history-gaps.md` when present, and only\ntask-relevant transcripts selected from the manifest. Treat gaps as uncertainty\nrather than inventing rationale.\n\n### 3. Direct CLI is a compatibility surface\n\nFor clients without MCP, direct `entire brain ... --json` calls are the\ncompatibility surface (humans and scripts use the same commands). Treat it like a\ntool API, not an invitation to run a long command tour:\n\nThe provider naming migration is an intentional compatibility break. Direct CLI\nautomation must use `entire graph`, `--graph-binary`, `--skip-graph`,\n`--graph-timeout`, and `--graph-inactivity-timeout`. MCP server deployments use\n`ENTIRE_BRAIN_GRAPH_BINARY` for the trusted provider-binary override. Aliases for\nprior provider-facing names are not supported.\n\n```sh\nentire brain status --json\nentire brain brief \"<task>\" --json\n# then the smallest next question:\nentire brain query \"<decision or prior-work question>\" --json\nentire brain inspect code \"<symbol or concept>\" --json\nentire brain inspect tests \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect regressions \"<task or invariant>\" --location-only --json\n```\n\n`status` without `--json` prints a SHORT human report: the brain's identity,\nthe onboarding block (fact-backfill progress, watcher health, instant-phase\ncomponents) and a one-line health verdict. `status --verbose` prints the full\ntext report — coverage histograms, freshness axes, blind spots, live state —\nwith repeated warnings collapsed to one line per reason; `entire brain doctor`\nlists the skipped files in full. `status --json` is unchanged by either flag.\n\n`status --json` preserves the full status contract. Check\n`semantic.freshness.severity` before graph inspection and\n`retrieval.freshness.severity` before query/get. A semantic-only\n`refresh index` does not rebuild seed/docs: use `entire brain refresh --agent\nnone` when retrieval is stale, adding `--worktree` only when current\nuncommitted content should be included. `status --json --details` remains an\naccepted compatibility spelling for callers that already use it.\n`inspect code --json`, `inspect context --json`, `inspect impact --json`, and\n`inspect tests --json` likewise return compact semantic records by default; add\n`--details` only when provider metadata is needed. Their defaults are 10 code\nresults, 5 context symbols, 20 impact symbols, and 3 test suggestions;\n`--limit` remains available for deliberate expansion.\n\nPrefer `query` for broad facts/history/docs, `search` for exact terms, semantic\n`inspect` subcommands for code-graph questions, and `get`/`multi-get` when a\nprior result returned an id. Retrieval returns ten bounded excerpts by default;\nraise `--limit` deliberately instead of treating ranked search as a full-record\ndump. Don't broaden into repo-wide text search until the brain's targeted\ncontext has been used.\n\n### 4. Hidden hooks deliver context at the moment of relevance\n\n`entire-brain` also has a hidden hook surface for harness integrations. These are\nnot installed by base Entire enablement; a harness must be configured to invoke\nthem (for example Claude Code `PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse` hooks). They surface a\nsmall, high-confidence fact exactly when it matters — facts anchored to a file\nbefore editing it, gotchas after a failed command, optional one-session refresh\nafter a session ends. The contract is intentionally quiet: with no relevant\nfact, no local brain, or an environment problem, the hook exits successfully with\nno stdout output, so it never breaks the agent workflow.\n\n## Usage Scenarios\n\nThe examples name the preferred MCP tool when there is one. Agent clients without\nMCP should use the equivalent `entire brain ... --json` command.\n\n### Orient an agent on a new task\n\nStart by asking for task-shaped context, not a generic summary. The normal tool\nis `brain_brief` — it returns freshness, likely files and symbols, relevant\nhistory, durable facts, likely tests, and blind spots. Use it before broad shell\nsearch. For repo-level orientation instead of task-specific, use `overview`\n(`entire brain overview --json`): a compact project map of stack signals,\nentrypoints, commands, key documents, and recent decisions.\n\nFor interactive human exploration instead of scripted queries, `entire brain\ndash` opens a terminal dashboard over facts, sessions, history, and semantic\nrecords, and `entire brain viz` opens a local, no-egress browser view of the\nsemantic call graph. Both are read-only and make no agent calls.\n\n### Keep the brain fresh\n\nFreshness is part of every answer — a stale index, missing provider, dirty-\nworktree mismatch, or parser blind spot changes how much an agent should trust\nthe brain. Agents check freshness through `brain_status`/`status --json` before\nrelying on semantic answers; if unsafe, an agent with write permission can\nrequest a refresh, otherwise it reports the limitation and falls back to direct\ninspection.\n\n```sh\nentire brain status\nentire brain refresh --agent none\nentire brain watch\n```\n\nWhen semantic artifacts are out of sync or damaged, use the maintenance paths\ninstead of deleting the whole brain (`refresh index`, `repair`, `reset\n--semantic-only --force`). Use `refresh index --worktree` only when you\nintentionally want uncommitted state indexed; exported bundles reject\nworktree-backed semantic indexes.\n\n### Continue or explain prior work\n\nWhen the question is \"why is this like this?\", \"what did the previous agent\ntry?\", or \"where did this session leave off?\", search the history and facts\nlayers — `brain_query`, then `brain_get` for specific ids. This is\nthe main reason Entire capture matters: the original prompt, attempts,\nvalidation, correction, and rationale survive the code diff and become available\nto the next agent.\n\n### Ask across facts, history, and docs\n\nWhen the question is not tied to one symbol, use the unified retrieval layer.\n`query` is the hybrid path (lexical + vector, RRF) over durable facts, indexed\nhistory, and docs; `query --keyword` for exact keywords, `query --semantic` for semantic matches,\n`get`/`multi-get` when a result returns an id. Every result carries an `id`.\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"how does checkpointing work\" --json\nentire brain query --keyword \"checkpoint\" --json\nentire brain query --semantic \"preventing data races\" --json\nentire brain get fact:<id> --json\n```\n\n`query` also takes `--source` (`all` | `fact` |\n`history` | `conversation` | `doc`) to restrict retrieval to one layer. The\ndefault is unchanged (`all` = facts + classified history + docs).\n\nUse `entire brain query \"text\"` for default hybrid retrieval. Select keyword\nmatching with `--keyword` or semantic matching with `--semantic`; the two\nflags are mutually exclusive and belong only to the query command.\nText may instead be supplied as `--query \"text\"`. Flags work before or after\npositional text; combining positional text and `--query` is an error.\nFor example: `entire brain query --keyword --query \"RetryPolicy\" --json`.\n\nWorkspace retrieval supports the same forms:\n`entire brain workspace query <workspace> --semantic --query \"retry policy\"`.\nThe old `search` and `vsearch` commands remain hidden compatibility aliases.\nMCP agents should use `brain_query` with optional, mutually exclusive\n`keyword: true` or `semantic: true` arguments; `brain_search` and\n`brain_vsearch` remain compatibility tools.\n\n### Recall prior conversations (experimental, opt-in)\n\n`--source conversation` searches captured request/response exchanges from\nexported session transcripts (what was asked, what the agent concluded), and\n`get conversation:<id>` expands one exchange to a bounded request/response pair\nwith its exact transcript range:\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"why did we reject the cache rewrite\" --source conversation --json\nentire brain query --keyword \"SQLITE_BUSY\" --source conversation --json\nentire brain get conversation:<id> --json\n```\n\nConversation queries take structured filters: `--after`/`--before` (RFC3339 or\nYYYY-MM-DD session time), `--session <id>`, `--agent <harness>`, and `--branch`.\nThese error on any other source rather than being silently ignored. Results\ncarry `matched_terms` (which query tokens actually hit) and are diversity-capped\nso one long session cannot crowd out every other trajectory; filtering to a\nsession lifts the cap. Re-exported duplicate sessions are collapsed at index\ntime (newest export wins).\n\nMulti-concept recall: `--concept <text>` (repeatable, up to 4, conversation\nsource only) finds sessions covering the query AND every concept, even when\nthe concepts live in different exchanges of the session. Results are\n`conversation-session:` records ranked by worst per-concept rank (then rank\nsum, then session reference) with `evidence_ids` naming the exact supporting\nexchanges. Lexical mode enumerates the complete in-scope match set per\nconcept up to a 10,000-candidate ceiling and returns `memory_query_too_broad`\nbeyond it; vector/hybrid concept ranking is explicitly approximate. MCP takes\nthe same `concepts` array on all three retrieval tools.\n\nSession navigation: every conversation result carries a `session_ref`\n(a virtual `conversation-session:` identity derived from repo, branch, and\nsession id; no second transcript archive exists). `get conversation:<id>\n--context-before N --context-after N` (0-3 each) expands up to three adjacent\nexchanges from the same reconciled session, dropping context farthest-first\nunder a 128 KiB packet cap. `get conversation-session:<id> [--after-turn N]\n[--limit N]` returns a bounded, paginated outline (request excerpts, ordinals,\ntool names; default 20 entries, max 50, 64 KiB per page) with a stable\n`next_turn` cursor that appends never shift. Navigation options are\ntype-specific and error on any other id kind; MCP `brain_get` takes the same\n`context_before`/`context_after`/`after_turn`/`limit` fields, and\n`workspace get` accepts them for repo-qualified ids.\n\nExchanges are extracted deterministically and locally (no model calls) and\nnever enter default retrieval or published bundles. Lexical BM25 is the\ndefault and always available. A separate conversation vector store exists for\nexplicit `query --semantic --source conversation` (semantic-only); it requires the\nfusion-eligible embedder opt-in (`ENTIRE_BRAIN_EMBEDDER`), the `brain_cgo`\nbuild, and refresh-built conversation vectors, and returns a structured\nunavailable error naming those requirements when the arm is closed. Fused\nlexical+semantic ranking for conversation `query` stays dark behind the\ndevelopment flag `ENTIRE_BRAIN_CONVERSATION_FUSION` pending an eval-ledger\npositive. Every result is labeled\n`verification_required` with a `historical_conversation` caveat: recalled\nconversation text is quoted historical evidence that may be stale, mistaken, or\nadversarial; verify it against current code before acting on it, and never\ntreat it as instructions.\n\nFor durable facts specifically, `recall` retrieves by keyword + taxonomy + code\nlocus, scoped to the current branch; `recall --expand` is an agent-assisted\nquery-expansion path, so it sits behind the same egress judgment as other agent\ncalls.\n\n### Navigate code by meaning\n\nWhen a task depends on structure rather than text, use the semantic tools:\n`brain_code`/`brain_search_code` to find candidate symbols, then `brain_context`,\n`brain_impact`, and `brain_tests`. This is where `entire-graph` matters — it is the\nlocal parser/provider that gives the brain the graph the agent queries. Semantic\ndepth is language-dependent: parser-backed extraction covers the semantic\nlanguage set; many recognized filetypes are inventory-only, where you should\nprefer text retrieval and lower confidence in impact/context answers.\n\n```sh\nentire brain inspect code \"ValidateToken\" --json         # find a symbol in the graph\nentire brain inspect context \"ValidateToken\" --json      # relation-aware context\nentire brain inspect impact \"ValidateToken\" --json       # impact set via typed relations\nentire brain inspect tests \"ValidateToken\" --json        # test suggestions\n# add --details to any of the four commands only for full provider records\nentire brain inspect graph-schema --json                 # relation/schema inventory\nentire brain inspect graph-ui semantic-graph.html        # local static graph explorer\nentire brain inspect trace-path \"<caller>\" \"<callee>\" --json\nentire brain inspect dead-code --json\nentire brain inspect boundaries --kind tool --json\nentire brain inspect changes --json                      # diff-hunk-scoped symbol mapping\n# persists semantic/changes/latest.json by default; pass --write-report=false to skip it\n```\n\n### Trace a symbol back to the work that changed it\n\n`entities` answers \"which checkpoints and sessions changed this function\" from a\npersisted, git-native index instead of re-reading history. The index is built\nonce per repository and then kept current by the deterministic (token-free)\nrefresh that the watch loop and the `session-end` hook already run.\n\nEach indexed commit stores its semantic delta document (`schema_version` `1.0`,\nproduced by `entire graph diff`) as a git-meta record on the commit, plus a\nreverse `entity -> commits` list and a rename/move alias, so a symbol's history\nsurvives the names it has been through. The local join of commits to checkpoints\nand sessions is derived state, pinned to the git-meta ref and rebuilt whenever\nthe index moves.\n\nCoverage is tracked as a **window**, not a mark: a per-branch `floor..tip` range\nin which every first-parent commit is indexed. Freshness ticks push the tip\nforward over commits that landed since (so a tick reads only the new ones), and\nbounded `--limit` passes pull the floor backward until it reaches the root.\n\n```sh\nentire brain entities backfill                 # index history (--limit 0 for all)\nentire brain entities backfill --checkpoints-only\nentire brain entities history \"ValidateToken\" --json\nentire brain entities history \"ValidateToken\" --branch main\nentire brain entities show <commit|checkpoint-id>   # the stored delta document\n```\n\n`--checkpoints-only` is a filtered convenience pass: it indexes commits carrying\nan `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer and deliberately leaves plain commits alone, so it\ndoes **not** move the window. Coverage of a branch always comes from an ordinary\npass.\n\nDistill uses the same index to sharpen fact provenance: a fact whose locus names\nan indexed entity is anchored to the checkpoint of ITS OWN session that actually\nchanged that entity, rather than to the session's last checkpoint. With no index,\nno match, an ambiguous one, or a candidate from another session or branch, the\nprevious anchor is kept unchanged.\n\n### Review risk without a clean diff\n\nFor long sessions, manual edits, or suspected regressions from memory, use\ndiff-less regression review — `brain_regressions`/`brain_review`. It compares the\ncurrent working tree against what session history asserts the code used to be and\nflags suspected regressions (`file:line`, expected vs current, confidence,\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2583},"formatted_output":"entire brain watch --distill --distill-every 24h --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low\n```\n\n`--distill-every` plus the persisted watch cursor are the durable spend guard:\none gated agent run per repo per window, across restarts. `--max-sessions` caps\nhow many not-yet-distilled sessions ONE gated pass processes (0 = no cap), so\nthe frequency guard and the volume guard are separate knobs. `--budget` is a\ndifferent, weaker thing — it counts gated runs for the life of the **process**\nand never resets, so on a long-lived daemon `--budget 1` means one run *ever*,\nnot one per window. Use it only for a bounded foreground run.\n\nThe watcher keeps memory fresh in two tiers, like a brain: on every tick it\nruns the cheap **short-term** path (`entire brain refresh delta`; incremental\ncheckpoint export plus an overlay index of only the transcripts that changed;\nseconds even on very large brains), so an in-flight session's earlier turns\nand parallel terminals' work are searchable near-real-time. The change-gated\nfull refresh is **consolidation**: it absorbs the short-term overlay into\nlong-term memory (full index, FTS, vectors) and clears the buffer. Between\nchanges it is additionally throttled by `--consolidate-every` (default 30m);\nthe throttle is skipped whenever the delta was incomplete (a failed scan or a\nfull buffer), so partial short-term coverage always consolidates promptly. You\ncan run `entire brain refresh delta` by hand any time you want immediate\nrecall of just-captured work.\n\nIf you also want generated seed summaries, run refresh with an agent instead of\nthe deterministic seed path (`entire brain refresh --agent auto --seed-model\ngpt-5.4-mini --seed-effort low`). Seed synthesis orients an agent on the\nrepository; durable facts are branch-scoped retained knowledge — the two are\nseparate.\n\n## How Agents Use The Brain\n\nAgents rely on a capture layer plus four consumption surfaces. The capture layer\nproduces the raw material; the consumption surfaces are how an agent actually\nqueries or receives brain context. Which surface matters depends on the agent\nclient and how the repository is configured.\n\n### Capture layer: Entire hooks produce the raw material\n\n`entire-cli` installs lifecycle hooks for agents such as Claude Code, Codex,\nGemini CLI, OpenCode, Cursor, Factory AI Droid, Copilot CLI, and Pi. Those hooks\nstart and stop sessions, capture prompts and transcripts, track changed files,\nrecord subagent work where the agent exposes it, and attach checkpoint metadata\nto commits. This is background infrastructure: the agent does not remember to\nsave a transcript, and the human does not paste context into the brain.\n\nIf an agent is not seeing Entire context at all, check the base Entire\nintegration first, not the brain (`entire status`, `entire agent`).\n\n### 1. MCP is the preferred agent tool surface\n\nFor agents that support MCP, register `entire brain mcp` as a local stdio MCP\nserver. It opens no network listener and wraps the local brain's JSON contracts\nas tools. Once MCP is available, an agent should call MCP tools instead of\nshelling out to the CLI. The normal first call is `brain_brief`, then targeted\nfollow-ups:\n\n- `brain_brief` for task-shaped context, history hits, likely files, and tests\n- `brain_status` for a compact freshness/coverage preflight; set\n  `details: true` for the full status JSON contract\n- `brain_refresh` for a bounded seed/docs refresh when retrieval freshness is\n  unsafe. Its seed and docs include the current worktree by default, it never\n  exports checkpoint sessions, and it is capped at 60 seconds; set\n  `semantic: true` only for small repositories and use\n  `brain_index_repository` as the separate long-running semantic step for large\n  repositories. `worktree` covers seed and docs only -- the semantic index is\n  always built from committed HEAD, so a dirty semantic snapshot needs\n  `brain_index_repository` with `worktree: true`. Set `worktree: false` only\n  when seed and docs must come from committed\n  HEAD; use `entire brain refresh sessions` from the CLI for checkpoint history\n- retrieval tools such as `brain_query` and `brain_get` for facts, docs, history\n- semantic tools such as `brain_code`, `brain_context`, `brain_impact`, and\n  `brain_tests` for code navigation and validation planning\n- `brain_entity_history` for \"which checkpoints and sessions changed this\n  function/class\", answered from the persisted entity index rather than by\n  re-reading history (build it once with `entire brain entities backfill`)\n- review, workspace, and pattern tools such as `brain_regressions`,\n  `brain_workspace_graph`, `brain_workspace_review`, and `brain_patterns` when\n  the task calls for them\n\nSome MCP tools write local state (for example `brain_ingest_traces` persists\nruntime edges as `RUNTIME_TRACE` facts); pattern MCP tools are read-only. The\nMCP and CLI surfaces are intentionally close but not perfect mirrors — prefer the\nnative surface your agent client has, then translate only when needed. See\n[docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md](docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md) for the full tool surface.\n\n### 2. Intake instructions are the file-based fallback\n\nWhen MCP is not available, an agent can use the brain through the intake\ninstructions in `templates/`. The templates tell the agent to locate the brain\nwith `entire brain path \"$PWD\"`, then read staged context instead of blindly\nopening everything: `README.md` and `manifest.json` in the brain directory, seed\nfiles under `seed/agent/`, `seed/history-gaps.md` when present, and only\ntask-relevant transcripts selected from the manifest. Treat gaps as uncertainty\nrather than inventing rationale.\n\n### 3. Direct CLI is a compatibility surface\n\nFor clients without MCP, direct `entire brain ... --json` calls are the\ncompatibility surface (humans and scripts use the same commands). Treat it like a\ntool API, not an invitation to run a long command tour:\n\nThe provider naming migration is an intentional compatibility break. Direct CLI\nautomation must use `entire graph`, `--graph-binary`, `--skip-graph`,\n`--graph-timeout`, and `--graph-inactivity-timeout`. MCP server deployments use\n`ENTIRE_BRAIN_GRAPH_BINARY` for the trusted provider-binary override. Aliases for\nprior provider-facing names are not supported.\n\n```sh\nentire brain status --json\nentire brain brief \"<task>\" --json\n# then the smallest next question:\nentire brain query \"<decision or prior-work question>\" --json\nentire brain inspect code \"<symbol or concept>\" --json\nentire brain inspect tests \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect regressions \"<task or invariant>\" --location-only --json\n```\n\n`status` without `--json` prints a SHORT human report: the brain's identity,\nthe onboarding block (fact-backfill progress, watcher health, instant-phase\ncomponents) and a one-line health verdict. `status --verbose` prints the full\ntext report — coverage histograms, freshness axes, blind spots, live state —\nwith repeated warnings collapsed to one line per reason; `entire brain doctor`\nlists the skipped files in full. `status --json` is unchanged by either flag.\n\n`status --json` preserves the full status contract. Check\n`semantic.freshness.severity` before graph inspection and\n`retrieval.freshness.severity` before query/get. A semantic-only\n`refresh index` does not rebuild seed/docs: use `entire brain refresh --agent\nnone` when retrieval is stale, adding `--worktree` only when current\nuncommitted content should be included. `status --json --details` remains an\naccepted compatibility spelling for callers that already use it.\n`inspect code --json`, `inspect context --json`, `inspect impact --json`, and\n`inspect tests --json` likewise return compact semantic records by default; add\n`--details` only when provider metadata is needed. Their defaults are 10 code\nresults, 5 context symbols, 20 impact symbols, and 3 test suggestions;\n`--limit` remains available for deliberate expansion.\n\nPrefer `query` for broad facts/history/docs, `search` for exact terms, semantic\n`inspect` subcommands for code-graph questions, and `get`/`multi-get` when a\nprior result returned an id. Retrieval returns ten bounded excerpts by default;\nraise `--limit` deliberately instead of treating ranked search as a full-record\ndump. Don't broaden into repo-wide text search until the brain's targeted\ncontext has been used.\n\n### 4. Hidden hooks deliver context at the moment of relevance\n\n`entire-brain` also has a hidden hook surface for harness integrations. These are\nnot installed by base Entire enablement; a harness must be configured to invoke\nthem (for example Claude Code `PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse` hooks). They surface a\nsmall, high-confidence fact exactly when it matters — facts anchored to a file\nbefore editing it, gotchas after a failed command, optional one-session refresh\nafter a session ends. The contract is intentionally quiet: with no relevant\nfact, no local brain, or an environment problem, the hook exits successfully with\nno stdout output, so it never breaks the agent workflow.\n\n## Usage Scenarios\n\nThe examples name the preferred MCP tool when there is one. Agent clients without\nMCP should use the equivalent `entire brain ... --json` command.\n\n### Orient an agent on a new task\n\nStart by asking for task-shaped context, not a generic summary. The normal tool\nis `brain_brief` — it returns freshness, likely files and symbols, relevant\nhistory, durable facts, likely tests, and blind spots. Use it before broad shell\nsearch. For repo-level orientation instead of task-specific, use `overview`\n(`entire brain overview --json`): a compact project map of stack signals,\nentrypoints, commands, key documents, and recent decisions.\n\nFor interactive human exploration instead of scripted queries, `entire brain\ndash` opens a terminal dashboard over facts, sessions, history, and semantic\nrecords, and `entire brain viz` opens a local, no-egress browser view of the\nsemantic call graph. Both are read-only and make no agent calls.\n\n### Keep the brain fresh\n\nFreshness is part of every answer — a stale index, missing provider, dirty-\nworktree mismatch, or parser blind spot changes how much an agent should trust\nthe brain. Agents check freshness through `brain_status`/`status --json` before\nrelying on semantic answers; if unsafe, an agent with write permission can\nrequest a refresh, otherwise it reports the limitation and falls back to direct\ninspection.\n\n```sh\nentire brain status\nentire brain refresh --agent none\nentire brain watch\n```\n\nWhen semantic artifacts are out of sync or damaged, use the maintenance paths\ninstead of deleting the whole brain (`refresh index`, `repair`, `reset\n--semantic-only --force`). Use `refresh index --worktree` only when you\nintentionally want uncommitted state indexed; exported bundles reject\nworktree-backed semantic indexes.\n\n### Continue or explain prior work\n\nWhen the question is \"why is this like this?\", \"what did the previous agent\ntry?\", or \"where did this session leave off?\", search the history and facts\nlayers — `brain_query`, then `brain_get` for specific ids. This is\nthe main reason Entire capture matters: the original prompt, attempts,\nvalidation, correction, and rationale survive the code diff and become available\nto the next agent.\n\n### Ask across facts, history, and docs\n\nWhen the question is not tied to one symbol, use the unified retrieval layer.\n`query` is the hybrid path (lexical + vector, RRF) over durable facts, indexed\nhistory, and docs; `query --keyword` for exact keywords, `query --semantic` for semantic matches,\n`get`/`multi-get` when a result returns an id. Every result carries an `id`.\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"how does checkpointing work\" --json\nentire brain query --keyword \"checkpoint\" --json\nentire brain query --semantic \"preventing data races\" --json\nentire brain get fact:<id> --json\n```\n\n`query` also takes `--source` (`all` | `fact` |\n`history` | `conversation` | `doc`) to restrict retrieval to one layer. The\ndefault is unchanged (`all` = facts + classified history + docs).\n\nUse `entire brain query \"text\"` for default hybrid retrieval. Select keyword\nmatching with `--keyword` or semantic matching with `--semantic`; the two\nflags are mutually exclusive and belong only to the query command.\nText may instead be supplied as `--query \"text\"`. Flags work before or after\npositional text; combining positional text and `--query` is an error.\nFor example: `entire brain query --keyword --query \"RetryPolicy\" --json`.\n\nWorkspace retrieval supports the same forms:\n`entire brain workspace query <workspace> --semantic --query \"retry policy\"`.\nThe old `search` and `vsearch` commands remain hidden compatibility aliases.\nMCP agents should use `brain_query` with optional, mutually exclusive\n`keyword: true` or `semantic: true` arguments; `brain_search` and\n`brain_vsearch` remain compatibility tools.\n\n### Recall prior conversations (experimental, opt-in)\n\n`--source conversation` searches captured request/response exchanges from\nexported session transcripts (what was asked, what the agent concluded), and\n`get conversation:<id>` expands one exchange to a bounded request/response pair\nwith its exact transcript range:\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"why did we reject the cache rewrite\" --source conversation --json\nentire brain query --keyword \"SQLITE_BUSY\" --source conversation --json\nentire brain get conversation:<id> --json\n```\n\nConversation queries take structured filters: `--after`/`--before` (RFC3339 or\nYYYY-MM-DD session time), `--session <id>`, `--agent <harness>`, and `--branch`.\nThese error on any other source rather than being silently ignored. Results\ncarry `matched_terms` (which query tokens actually hit) and are diversity-capped\nso one long session cannot crowd out every other trajectory; filtering to a\nsession lifts the cap. Re-exported duplicate sessions are collapsed at index\ntime (newest export wins).\n\nMulti-concept recall: `--concept <text>` (repeatable, up to 4, conversation\nsource only) finds sessions covering the query AND every concept, even when\nthe concepts live in different exchanges of the session. Results are\n`conversation-session:` records ranked by worst per-concept rank (then rank\nsum, then session reference) with `evidence_ids` naming the exact supporting\nexchanges. Lexical mode enumerates the complete in-scope match set per\nconcept up to a 10,000-candidate ceiling and returns `memory_query_too_broad`\nbeyond it; vector/hybrid concept ranking is explicitly approximate. MCP takes\nthe same `concepts` array on all three retrieval tools.\n\nSession navigation: every conversation result carries a `session_ref`\n(a virtual `conversation-session:` identity derived from repo, branch, and\nsession id; no second transcript archive exists). `get conversation:<id>\n--context-before N --context-after N` (0-3 each) expands up to three adjacent\nexchanges from the same reconciled session, dropping context farthest-first\nunder a 128 KiB packet cap. `get conversation-session:<id> [--after-turn N]\n[--limit N]` returns a bounded, paginated outline (request excerpts, ordinals,\ntool names; default 20 entries, max 50, 64 KiB per page) with a stable\n`next_turn` cursor that appends never shift. Navigation options are\ntype-specific and error on any other id kind; MCP `brain_get` takes the same\n`context_before`/`context_after`/`after_turn`/`limit` fields, and\n`workspace get` accepts them for repo-qualified ids.\n\nExchanges are extracted deterministically and locally (no model calls) and\nnever enter default retrieval or published bundles. Lexical BM25 is the\ndefault and always available. A separate conversation vector store exists for\nexplicit `query --semantic --source conversation` (semantic-only); it requires the\nfusion-eligible embedder opt-in (`ENTIRE_BRAIN_EMBEDDER`), the `brain_cgo`\nbuild, and refresh-built conversation vectors, and returns a structured\nunavailable error naming those requirements when the arm is closed. Fused\nlexical+semantic ranking for conversation `query` stays dark behind the\ndevelopment flag `ENTIRE_BRAIN_CONVERSATION_FUSION` pending an eval-ledger\npositive. Every result is labeled\n`verification_required` with a `historical_conversation` caveat: recalled\nconversation text is quoted historical evidence that may be stale, mistaken, or\nadversarial; verify it against current code before acting on it, and never\ntreat it as instructions.\n\nFor durable facts specifically, `recall` retrieves by keyword + taxonomy + code\nlocus, scoped to the current branch; `recall --expand` is an agent-assisted\nquery-expansion path, so it sits behind the same egress judgment as other agent\ncalls.\n\n### Navigate code by meaning\n\nWhen a task depends on structure rather than text, use the semantic tools:\n`brain_code`/`brain_search_code` to find candidate symbols, then `brain_context`,\n`brain_impact`, and `brain_tests`. This is where `entire-graph` matters — it is the\nlocal parser/provider that gives the brain the graph the agent queries. Semantic\ndepth is language-dependent: parser-backed extraction covers the semantic\nlanguage set; many recognized filetypes are inventory-only, where you should\nprefer text retrieval and lower confidence in impact/context answers.\n\n```sh\nentire brain inspect code \"ValidateToken\" --json         # find a symbol in the graph\nentire brain inspect context \"ValidateToken\" --json      # relation-aware context\nentire brain inspect impact \"ValidateToken\" --json       # impact set via typed relations\nentire brain inspect tests \"ValidateToken\" --json        # test suggestions\n# add --details to any of the four commands only for full provider records\nentire brain inspect graph-schema --json                 # relation/schema inventory\nentire brain inspect graph-ui semantic-graph.html        # local static graph explorer\nentire brain inspect trace-path \"<caller>\" \"<callee>\" --json\nentire brain inspect dead-code --json\nentire brain inspect boundaries --kind tool --json\nentire brain inspect changes --json                      # diff-hunk-scoped symbol mapping\n# persists semantic/changes/latest.json by default; pass --write-report=false to skip it\n```\n\n### Trace a symbol back to the work that changed it\n\n`entities` answers \"which checkpoints and sessions changed this function\" from a\npersisted, git-native index instead of re-reading history. The index is built\nonce per repository and then kept current by the deterministic (token-free)\nrefresh that the watch loop and the `session-end` hook already run.\n\nEach indexed commit stores its semantic delta document (`schema_version` `1.0`,\nproduced by `entire graph diff`) as a git-meta record on the commit, plus a\nreverse `entity -> commits` list and a rename/move alias, so a symbol's history\nsurvives the names it has been through. The local join of commits to checkpoints\nand sessions is derived state, pinned to the git-meta ref and rebuilt whenever\nthe index moves.\n\nCoverage is tracked as a **window**, not a mark: a per-branch `floor..tip` range\nin which every first-parent commit is indexed. Freshness ticks push the tip\nforward over commits that landed since (so a tick reads only the new ones), and\nbounded `--limit` passes pull the floor backward until it reaches the root.\n\n```sh\nentire brain entities backfill                 # index history (--limit 0 for all)\nentire brain entities backfill --checkpoints-only\nentire brain entities history \"ValidateToken\" --json\nentire brain entities history \"ValidateToken\" --branch main\nentire brain entities show <commit|checkpoint-id>   # the stored delta document\n```\n\n`--checkpoints-only` is a filtered convenience pass: it indexes commits carrying\nan `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer and deliberately leaves plain commits alone, so it\ndoes **not** move the window. Coverage of a branch always comes from an ordinary\npass.\n\nDistill uses the same index to sharpen fact provenance: a fact whose locus names\nan indexed entity is anchored to the checkpoint of ITS OWN session that actually\nchanged that entity, rather than to the session's last checkpoint. With no index,\nno match, an ambiguous one, or a candidate from another session or branch, the\nprevious anchor is kept unchanged.\n\n### Review risk without a clean diff\n\nFor long sessions, manual edits, or suspected regressions from memory, use\ndiff-less regression review — `brain_regressions`/`brain_review`. It compares the\ncurrent working tree against what session history asserts the code used to be and\nflags suspected regressions (`file:line`, expected vs current, confidence,\n"},"started_at_ms":1789839784092,"completed_at_ms":1789839784092}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:43:04.145Z","ordinal":7673,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","session_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","root_turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","response_id":"resp_0cb48b2b18d09e75016aaec9a436a087d2aec799ada5f5ba6c","usage":{"input_tokens":233174,"cached_input_tokens":228224,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":60,"reasoning_output_tokens":19,"total_tokens":233234},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":76446912,"cached_input_tokens":73197696,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":175426,"reasoning_output_tokens":14937,"total_tokens":76622338},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":142331931,"cached_input_tokens":135769728,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":360225,"reasoning_output_tokens":36314,"total_tokens":142692156}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:43:04.149Z","ordinal":7674,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0bac3-b8d5-7351-b109-1698a430cb57","call_id":"call_a6NRq7gZSps1dkPiVEStwday","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"77e4ee\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000002583,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":5119,\"output\":\"entire brain watch --distill --distill-every 24h --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low\\n```\\n\\n`--distill-every` plus the persisted watch cursor are the durable spend guard:\\none gated agent run per repo per window, across restarts. `--max-sessions` caps\\nhow many not-yet-distilled sessions ONE gated pass processes (0 = no cap), so\\nthe frequency guard and the volume guard are separate knobs. `--budget` is a\\ndifferent, weaker thing — it counts gated runs for the life of the **process**\\nand never resets, so on a long-lived daemon `--budget 1` means one run *ever*,\\nnot one per window. Use it only for a bounded foreground run.\\n\\nThe watcher keeps memory fresh in two tiers, like a brain: on every tick it\\nruns the cheap **short-term** path (`entire brain refresh delta`; incremental\\ncheckpoint export plus an overlay index of only the transcripts that changed;\\nseconds even on very large brains), so an in-flight session's earlier turns\\nand parallel terminals' work are searchable near-real-time. The change-gated\\nfull refresh is **consolidation**: it absorbs the short-term overlay into\\nlong-term memory (full index, FTS, vectors) and clears the buffer. Between\\nchanges it is additionally throttled by `--consolidate-every` (default 30m);\\nthe throttle is skipped whenever the delta was incomplete (a failed scan or a\\nfull buffer), so partial short-term coverage always consolidates promptly. You\\ncan run `entire brain refresh delta` by hand any time you want immediate\\nrecall of just-captured work.\\n\\nIf you also want generated seed summaries, run refresh with an agent instead of\\nthe deterministic seed path (`entire brain refresh --agent auto --seed-model\\ngpt-5.4-mini --seed-effort low`). Seed synthesis orients an agent on the\\nrepository; durable facts are branch-scoped retained knowledge — the two are\\nseparate.\\n\\n## How Agents Use The Brain\\n\\nAgents rely on a capture layer plus four consumption surfaces. The capture layer\\nproduces the raw material; the consumption surfaces are how an agent actually\\nqueries or receives brain context. Which surface matters depends on the agent\\nclient and how the repository is configured.\\n\\n### Capture layer: Entire hooks produce the raw material\\n\\n`entire-cli` installs lifecycle hooks for agents such as Claude Code, Codex,\\nGemini CLI, OpenCode, Cursor, Factory AI Droid, Copilot CLI, and Pi. Those hooks\\nstart and stop sessions, capture prompts and transcripts, track changed files,\\nrecord subagent work where the agent exposes it, and attach checkpoint metadata\\nto commits. This is background infrastructure: the agent does not remember to\\nsave a transcript, and the human does not paste context into the brain.\\n\\nIf an agent is not seeing Entire context at all, check the base Entire\\nintegration first, not the brain (`entire status`, `entire agent`).\\n\\n### 1. MCP is the preferred agent tool surface\\n\\nFor agents that support MCP, register `entire brain mcp` as a local stdio MCP\\nserver. It opens no network listener and wraps the local brain's JSON contracts\\nas tools. Once MCP is available, an agent should call MCP tools instead of\\nshelling out to the CLI. The normal first call is `brain_brief`, then targeted\\nfollow-ups:\\n\\n- `brain_brief` for task-shaped context, history hits, likely files, and tests\\n- `brain_status` for a compact freshness/coverage preflight; set\\n  `details: true` for the full status JSON contract\\n- `brain_refresh` for a bounded seed/docs refresh when retrieval freshness is\\n  unsafe. Its seed and docs include the current worktree by default, it never\\n  exports checkpoint sessions, and it is capped at 60 seconds; set\\n  `semantic: true` only for small repositories and use\\n  `brain_index_repository` as the separate long-running semantic step for large\\n  repositories. `worktree` covers seed and docs only -- the semantic index is\\n  always built from committed HEAD, so a dirty semantic snapshot needs\\n  `brain_index_repository` with `worktree: true`. Set `worktree: false` only\\n  when seed and docs must come from committed\\n  HEAD; use `entire brain refresh sessions` from the CLI for checkpoint history\\n- retrieval tools such as `brain_query` and `brain_get` for facts, docs, history\\n- semantic tools such as `brain_code`, `brain_context`, `brain_impact`, and\\n  `brain_tests` for code navigation and validation planning\\n- `brain_entity_history` for \\\"which checkpoints and sessions changed this\\n  function/class\\\", answered from the persisted entity index rather than by\\n  re-reading history (build it once with `entire brain entities backfill`)\\n- review, workspace, and pattern tools such as `brain_regressions`,\\n  `brain_workspace_graph`, `brain_workspace_review`, and `brain_patterns` when\\n  the task calls for them\\n\\nSome MCP tools write local state (for example `brain_ingest_traces` persists\\nruntime edges as `RUNTIME_TRACE` facts); pattern MCP tools are read-only. The\\nMCP and CLI surfaces are intentionally close but not perfect mirrors — prefer the\\nnative surface your agent client has, then translate only when needed. See\\n[docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md](docs/semantic_mcp_guide.md) for the full tool surface.\\n\\n### 2. Intake instructions are the file-based fallback\\n\\nWhen MCP is not available, an agent can use the brain through the intake\\ninstructions in `templates/`. The templates tell the agent to locate the brain\\nwith `entire brain path \\\"$PWD\\\"`, then read staged context instead of blindly\\nopening everything: `README.md` and `manifest.json` in the brain directory, seed\\nfiles under `seed/agent/`, `seed/history-gaps.md` when present, and only\\ntask-relevant transcripts selected from the manifest. Treat gaps as uncertainty\\nrather than inventing rationale.\\n\\n### 3. Direct CLI is a compatibility surface\\n\\nFor clients without MCP, direct `entire brain ... --json` calls are the\\ncompatibility surface (humans and scripts use the same commands). Treat it like a\\ntool API, not an invitation to run a long command tour:\\n\\nThe provider naming migration is an intentional compatibility break. Direct CLI\\nautomation must use `entire graph`, `--graph-binary`, `--skip-graph`,\\n`--graph-timeout`, and `--graph-inactivity-timeout`. MCP server deployments use\\n`ENTIRE_BRAIN_GRAPH_BINARY` for the trusted provider-binary override. Aliases for\\nprior provider-facing names are not supported.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain status --json\\nentire brain brief \\\"<task>\\\" --json\\n# then the smallest next question:\\nentire brain query \\\"<decision or prior-work question>\\\" --json\\nentire brain inspect code \\\"<symbol or concept>\\\" --json\\nentire brain inspect tests \\\"<symbol-or-id>\\\" --json\\nentire brain inspect regressions \\\"<task or invariant>\\\" --location-only --json\\n```\\n\\n`status` without `--json` prints a SHORT human report: the brain's identity,\\nthe onboarding block (fact-backfill progress, watcher health, instant-phase\\ncomponents) and a one-line health verdict. `status --verbose` prints the full\\ntext report — coverage histograms, freshness axes, blind spots, live state —\\nwith repeated warnings collapsed to one line per reason; `entire brain doctor`\\nlists the skipped files in full. `status --json` is unchanged by either flag.\\n\\n`status --json` preserves the full status contract. Check\\n`semantic.freshness.severity` before graph inspection and\\n`retrieval.freshness.severity` before query/get. A semantic-only\\n`refresh index` does not rebuild seed/docs: use `entire brain refresh --agent\\nnone` when retrieval is stale, adding `--worktree` only when current\\nuncommitted content should be included. `status --json --details` remains an\\naccepted compatibility spelling for callers that already use it.\\n`inspect code --json`, `inspect context --json`, `inspect impact --json`, and\\n`inspect tests --json` likewise return compact semantic records by default; add\\n`--details` only when provider metadata is needed. Their defaults are 10 code\\nresults, 5 context symbols, 20 impact symbols, and 3 test suggestions;\\n`--limit` remains available for deliberate expansion.\\n\\nPrefer `query` for broad facts/history/docs, `search` for exact terms, semantic\\n`inspect` subcommands for code-graph questions, and `get`/`multi-get` when a\\nprior result returned an id. Retrieval returns ten bounded excerpts by default;\\nraise `--limit` deliberately instead of treating ranked search as a full-record\\ndump. Don't broaden into repo-wide text search until the brain's targeted\\ncontext has been used.\\n\\n### 4. Hidden hooks deliver context at the moment of relevance\\n\\n`entire-brain` also has a hidden hook surface for harness integrations. These are\\nnot installed by base Entire enablement; a harness must be configured to invoke\\nthem (for example Claude Code `PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse` hooks). They surface a\\nsmall, high-confidence fact exactly when it matters — facts anchored to a file\\nbefore editing it, gotchas after a failed command, optional one-session refresh\\nafter a session ends. The contract is intentionally quiet: with no relevant\\nfact, no local brain, or an environment problem, the hook exits successfully with\\nno stdout output, so it never breaks the agent workflow.\\n\\n## Usage Scenarios\\n\\nThe examples name the preferred MCP tool when there is one. Agent clients without\\nMCP should use the equivalent `entire brain ... --json` command.\\n\\n### Orient an agent on a new task\\n\\nStart by asking for task-shaped context, not a generic summary. The normal tool\\nis `brain_brief` — it returns freshness, likely files and symbols, relevant\\nhistory, durable facts, likely tests, and blind spots. Use it before broad shell\\nsearch. For repo-level orientation instead of task-specific, use `overview`\\n(`entire brain overview --json`): a compact project map of stack signals,\\nentrypoints, commands, key documents, and recent decisions.\\n\\nFor interactive human exploration instead of scripted queries, `entire brain\\ndash` opens a terminal dashboard over facts, sessions, history, and semantic\\nrecords, and `entire brain viz` opens a local, no-egress browser view of the\\nsemantic call graph. Both are read-only and make no agent calls.\\n\\n### Keep the brain fresh\\n\\nFreshness is part of every answer — a stale index, missing provider, dirty-\\nworktree mismatch, or parser blind spot changes how much an agent should trust\\nthe brain. Agents check freshness through `brain_status`/`status --json` before\\nrelying on semantic answers; if unsafe, an agent with write permission can\\nrequest a refresh, otherwise it reports the limitation and falls back to direct\\ninspection.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain status\\nentire brain refresh --agent none\\nentire brain watch\\n```\\n\\nWhen semantic artifacts are out of sync or damaged, use the maintenance paths\\ninstead of deleting the whole brain (`refresh index`, `repair`, `reset\\n--semantic-only --force`). Use `refresh index --worktree` only when you\\nintentionally want uncommitted state indexed; exported bundles reject\\nworktree-backed semantic indexes.\\n\\n### Continue or explain prior work\\n\\nWhen the question is \\\"why is this like this?\\\", \\\"what did the previous agent\\ntry?\\\", or \\\"where did this session leave off?\\\", search the history and facts\\nlayers — `brain_query`, then `brain_get` for specific ids. This is\\nthe main reason Entire capture matters: the original prompt, attempts,\\nvalidation, correction, and rationale survive the code diff and become available\\nto the next agent.\\n\\n### Ask across facts, history, and docs\\n\\nWhen the question is not tied to one symbol, use the unified retrieval layer.\\n`query` is the hybrid path (lexical + vector, RRF) over durable facts, indexed\\nhistory, and docs; `query --keyword` for exact keywords, `query --semantic` for semantic matches,\\n`get`/`multi-get` when a result returns an id. Every result carries an `id`.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain query \\\"how does checkpointing work\\\" --json\\nentire brain query --keyword \\\"checkpoint\\\" --json\\nentire brain query --semantic \\\"preventing data races\\\" --json\\nentire brain get fact:<id> --json\\n```\\n\\n`query` also takes `--source` (`all` | `fact` |\\n`history` | `conversation` | `doc`) to restrict retrieval to one layer. The\\ndefault is unchanged (`all` = facts + classified history + docs).\\n\\nUse `entire brain query \\\"text\\\"` for default hybrid retrieval. Select keyword\\nmatching with `--keyword` or semantic matching with `--semantic`; the two\\nflags are mutually exclusive and belong only to the query command.\\nText may instead be supplied as `--query \\\"text\\\"`. Flags work before or after\\npositional text; combining positional text and `--query` is an error.\\nFor example: `entire brain query --keyword --query \\\"RetryPolicy\\\" --json`.\\n\\nWorkspace retrieval supports the same forms:\\n`entire brain workspace query <workspace> --semantic --query \\\"retry policy\\\"`.\\nThe old `search` and `vsearch` commands remain hidden compatibility aliases.\\nMCP agents should use `brain_query` with optional, mutually exclusive\\n`keyword: true` or `semantic: true` arguments; `brain_search` and\\n`brain_vsearch` remain compatibility tools.\\n\\n### Recall prior conversations (experimental, opt-in)\\n\\n`--source conversation` searches captured request/response exchanges from\\nexported session transcripts (what was asked, what the agent concluded), and\\n`get conversation:<id>` expands one exchange to a bounded request/response pair\\nwith its exact transcript range:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain query \\\"why did we reject the cache rewrite\\\" --source conversation --json\\nentire brain query --keyword \\\"SQLITE_BUSY\\\" --source conversation --json\\nentire brain get conversation:<id> --json\\n```\\n\\nConversation queries take structured filters: `--after`/`--before` (RFC3339 or\\nYYYY-MM-DD session time), `--session <id>`, `--agent <harness>`, and `--branch`.\\nThese error on any other source rather than being silently ignored. Results\\ncarry `matched_terms` (which query tokens actually hit) and are diversity-capped\\nso one long session cannot crowd out every other trajectory; filtering to a\\nsession lifts the cap. Re-exported duplicate sessions are collapsed at index\\ntime (newest export wins).\\n\\nMulti-concept recall: `--concept <text>` (repeatable, up to 4, conversation\\nsource only) finds sessions covering the query AND every concept, even when\\nthe concepts live in different exchanges of the session. Results are\\n`conversation-session:` records ranked by worst per-concept rank (then rank\\nsum, then session reference) with `evidence_ids` naming the exact supporting\\nexchanges. Lexical mode enumerates the complete in-scope match set per\\nconcept up to a 10,000-candidate ceiling and returns `memory_query_too_broad`\\nbeyond it; vector/hybrid concept ranking is explicitly approximate. MCP takes\\nthe same `concepts` array on all three retrieval tools.\\n\\nSession navigation: every conversation result carries a `session_ref`\\n(a virtual `conversation-session:` identity derived from repo, branch, and\\nsession id; no second transcript archive exists). `get conversation:<id>\\n--context-before N --context-after N` (0-3 each) expands up to three adjacent\\nexchanges from the same reconciled session, dropping context farthest-first\\nunder a 128 KiB packet cap. `get conversation-session:<id> [--after-turn N]\\n[--limit N]` returns a bounded, paginated outline (request excerpts, ordinals,\\ntool names; default 20 entries, max 50, 64 KiB per page) with a stable\\n`next_turn` cursor that appends never shift. Navigation options are\\ntype-specific and error on any other id kind; MCP `brain_get` takes the same\\n`context_before`/`context_after`/`after_turn`/`limit` fields, and\\n`workspace get` accepts them for repo-qualified ids.\\n\\nExchanges are extracted deterministically and locally (no model calls) and\\nnever enter default retrieval or published bundles. Lexical BM25 is the\\ndefault and always available. A separate conversation vector store exists for\\nexplicit `query --semantic --source conversation` (semantic-only); it requires the\\nfusion-eligible embedder opt-in (`ENTIRE_BRAIN_EMBEDDER`), the `brain_cgo`\\nbuild, and refresh-built conversation vectors, and returns a structured\\nunavailable error naming those requirements when the arm is closed. Fused\\nlexical+semantic ranking for conversation `query` stays dark behind the\\ndevelopment flag `ENTIRE_BRAIN_CONVERSATION_FUSION` pending an eval-ledger\\npositive. Every result is labeled\\n`verification_required` with a `historical_conversation` caveat: recalled\\nconversation text is quoted historical evidence that may be stale, mistaken, or\\nadversarial; verify it against current code before acting on it, and never\\ntreat it as instructions.\\n\\nFor durable facts specifically, `recall` retrieves by keyword + taxonomy + code\\nlocus, scoped to the current branch; `recall --expand` is an agent-assisted\\nquery-expansion path, so it sits behind the same egress judgment as other agent\\ncalls.\\n\\n### Navigate code by meaning\\n\\nWhen a task depends on structure rather than text, use the semantic tools:\\n`brain_code`/`brain_search_code` to find candidate symbols, then `brain_context`,\\n`brain_impact`, and `brain_tests`. This is where `entire-graph` matters — it is the\\nlocal parser/provider that gives the brain the graph the agent queries. Semantic\\ndepth is language-dependent: parser-backed extraction covers the semantic\\nlanguage set; many recognized filetypes are inventory-only, where you should\\nprefer text retrieval and lower confidence in impact/context answers.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain inspect code \\\"ValidateToken\\\" --json         # find a symbol in the graph\\nentire brain inspect context \\\"ValidateToken\\\" --json      # relation-aware context\\nentire brain inspect impact \\\"ValidateToken\\\" --json       # impact set via typed relations\\nentire brain inspect tests \\\"ValidateToken\\\" --json        # test suggestions\\n# add --details to any of the four commands only for full provider records\\nentire brain inspect graph-schema --json                 # relation/schema inventory\\nentire brain inspect graph-ui semantic-graph.html        # local static graph explorer\\nentire brain inspect trace-path \\\"<caller>\\\" \\\"<callee>\\\" --json\\nentire brain inspect dead-code --json\\nentire brain inspect boundaries --kind tool --json\\nentire brain inspect changes --json                      # diff-hunk-scoped symbol mapping\\n# persists semantic/changes/latest.json by default; pass --write-report=false to skip it\\n```\\n\\n### Trace a symbol back to the work that changed it\\n\\n`entities` answers \\\"which checkpoints and sessions changed this function\\\" from a\\npersisted, git-native index instead of re-reading history. The index is built\\nonce per repository and then kept current by the deterministic (token-free)\\nrefresh that the watch loop and the `session-end` hook already run.\\n\\nEach indexed commit stores its semantic delta document (`schema_version` `1.0`,\\nproduced by `entire graph diff`) as a git-meta record on the commit, plus a\\nreverse `entity -> commits` list and a rename/move alias, so a symbol's history\\nsurvives the names it has been through. The local join of commits to checkpoints\\nand sessions is derived state, pinned to the git-meta ref and rebuilt whenever\\nthe index moves.\\n\\nCoverage is tracked as a **window**, not a mark: a per-branch `floor..tip` range\\nin which every first-parent commit is indexed. Freshness ticks push the tip\\nforward over commits that landed since (so a tick reads only the new ones), and\\nbounded `--limit` passes pull the floor backward until it reaches the root.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain entities backfill                 # index history (--limit 0 for all)\\nentire brain entities backfill --checkpoints-only\\nentire brain entities history \\\"ValidateToken\\\" --json\\nentire brain entities history \\\"ValidateToken\\\" --branch main\\nentire brain entities show <commit|checkpoint-id>   # the stored delta document\\n```\\n\\n`--checkpoints-only` is a filtered convenience pass: it indexes commits carrying\\nan `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer and deliberately leaves plain commits alone, so it\\ndoes **not** move the window. Coverage of a branch always comes from an ordinary\\npass.\\n\\nDistill uses the same index to sharpen fact provenance: a fact whose locus names\\nan indexed entity is anchored to the checkpoint of ITS OWN session that actually\\nchanged that entity, rather than to the session's last checkpoint. With no index,\\nno match, an ambiguous one, or a candidate from another session or branch, the\\nprevious anchor is kept unchanged.\\n\\n### Review risk without a clean diff\\n\\nFor long sessions, manual edits, or suspected regressions from memory, use\\ndiff-less regression review — `brain_regressions`/`brain_review`. It compares the\\ncurrent working tree against what session history asserts the code used to be and\\nflags suspected regressions (`file:line`, expected vs current, confidence,\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","create_time":1789839784.149083}}}
