{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:43:58.072Z","ordinal":7700,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-94f97c1e-1bf0-4b5b-938a-c37e7139502f","process_id":"92284","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,400p' docs/getting-started.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/thomi/.codex/worktrees/5d33/entire-brain","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,400p' docs/getting-started.md","name":"getting-started.md","path":"docs/getting-started.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"# Getting Started\n\n## Install and discover agent instructions\n\nRun `entire brain init-agents` in the consuming project to install\n`.entire/agent-guide.md` plus managed pointers in `AGENTS.md` and\n`CLAUDE.md`. Reruns reconcile Graph and Brain blocks into one shared workflow and preserve your own text.\nUse `--repo <path>` for another project. Installation does not build indexes,\ncall an agent, or start services.\n\n`entire brain agent-guide` prints the same guide; `guide` remains an alias.\nUse `entire brain capabilities --json` to discover compiled features and their\nrequirements without a repository. Use `status --details --json` for the\ncurrent repository's readiness. Graph's language/relation inventory remains\navailable through `entire graph capabilities --json`.\n\n\nThis guide takes you from nothing to querying a local repository brain. No team\ncontext is assumed.\n\nIt is two commands. The first installs the plugins on this machine; the second\nonboards a repository. Do not stop after the first — installing gives no\nrepository a brain.\n\n```sh\n# once per machine\ngit clone https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\nentire-brain/scripts/install.sh\n\n# once per repository you want a brain for\ncd /path/to/your/repo\nentire brain setup\n```\n\n`entire-brain` is an external-command plugin for the Entire CLI. Once installed\nit is invoked as `entire brain ...`. It builds a local, inspectable \"brain\" for a\nrepository from retained Entire sessions, seed context, docs, decision history,\na semantic code graph, and durable facts, then exposes retrieval, review, and MCP\nsurfaces that agents and humans query. Everything it builds stays on your machine.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- The Entire CLI, available on your `PATH` as `entire`. It is the host that\n  dispatches `entire brain`, and it is what captures the sessions the brain\n  learns from.\n- Git.\n- A Go toolchain (1.27 or newer). It is not optional: every install path here\n  builds from source. There is no prebuilt archive to download -- the tagged\n  releases carry release notes and no binaries -- and there is no `go install`\n  path yet; see \"Versioned install status\" below.\n- The `entire-graph` semantic provider, invoked as `entire graph`. The brain shells\n  out to it (`entire graph snapshot`, `entire graph doctor`) to build the semantic\n  code graph. Building `entire-graph` from source needs a cgo-capable C compiler,\n  because it uses tree-sitter native parser bindings. The brain itself is a\n  pure-Go build and does not need cgo.\n\nYou can build a brain from history, docs, and facts without the provider (see\n\"If you do not have the semantic provider yet\" below), but the semantic code\ngraph needs `entire graph`.\n\nThe provider is `entire-graph` (the public `entireio/entire-graph` repo),\ninvoked as `entire graph`. The brain shells out to `entire graph snapshot` and\n`entire graph doctor` to build and verify the semantic layer. Install the latest\nprovider as shown below. If you do not need the semantic code graph, you can skip\nthe provider and run the brain with `--semantic=false` (see\n\"If you do not have the semantic provider yet\").\n\n## Install\n\nThere are two components: the brain plugin and the `entire-graph` provider it\ncalls. Both are registered with the Entire CLI the same way, using\n`entire plugin install <path>`, which links a local `entire-<name>` executable\ninto Entire's managed plugin directory. You do not have to fetch or arrange the\nprovider yourself -- `scripts/install.sh` does both components in one pass.\n\n### One command\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\nentire-brain/scripts/install.sh\n```\n\nThis is the whole install: it checks the prerequisites listed above and reports\nevery missing one at once, resolves `entire-graph` (using `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR`,\nthen any checkout already on this machine, then a shallow clone of the public\nrepository into `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`), builds\nand registers both plugins, writes the default plugin configuration\n(`entire brain config init`), and runs `entire brain doctor`. It prints which of\nthe three provider routes it took. Neither checkout has to have a particular\nname, and they do not have to be siblings.\n\nThe prerequisites do not go away: Go 1.27 or newer and a cgo-capable C compiler\nare still required, because both components are built from source here. What\ngoes away is having to arrange directories before you start.\n\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` is the same flow for a machine with nothing on it: it\nclones `entire-brain` when it is not already present and then runs\n`scripts/install.sh`. There is no `curl | sh` form, because\n`entireio/entire-brain` is a private repository -- `raw.githubusercontent.com`\nreturns 404 for it without a token -- and because installing means building from\nsource, so the clone has to happen either way.\n\n### Versioned install status\n\nThere is no versioned install today. `entire-brain` is tagged -- `v0.3.0` is the\ncurrent release -- but a tag here is release notes and nothing else: the releases\ncarry **no binaries and no source archive**, so there is nothing to download and\nrun. The `v0.1.0` tags additionally predate the Graph rename (the provider tag\nbuilds `entire-sem` and the brain tag invokes `entire sem`), so do not combine\nthem with the `entire graph` commands in this guide.\n\nA versioned `go install` path is not available either, and will not be until\nmatching Graph-based release tags exist in both repositories. This section will\ngain copy-paste commands when they do. Until then the source install above --\n`scripts/install.sh` against a checkout -- is the only way to install this, which\nis why the Go toolchain is a hard requirement rather than a conditional one.\n\n`entire plugin install` currently supports local executable paths only. It does\nnot fetch from a git URL or a GitHub release, and it does not auto-install the\nprovider dependency.\n\n### Managing the provider checkout yourself\n\nThe one command above is the source install: until matching Graph-based tags are\navailable, both components are built from their current `main` branches, which\ntracks development and is not a reproducible versioned install. Use matching\nrelease tags once they are published.\n\nIf you would rather own the `entire-graph` checkout -- to pin a commit, or to\nwork on the provider -- clone it too and run the same installer:\n\n```sh\ngit clone --branch main https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph.git\ngit clone --branch main https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\ncd entire-brain\nscripts/install.sh\n```\n\nA side-by-side checkout is discovered with no configuration, and so are the usual\nsource directories under `$HOME`. Set\n`ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR=/path/to/entire-graph scripts/install.sh` only when your\ncheckout is somewhere the installer would not look, or when you want to pin one\nspecific checkout; an override that does not hold an `entire-graph` checkout is\nan error rather than a silent fallback. `ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` stops the\ninstaller reaching the network at all, so a machine without egress fails with an\nexplanation instead of hanging on a clone.\n\nTo build and install only the brain from a checkout, run `scripts/install-local.sh`\n(equivalently `mise run install`). You still need `entire graph` installed\nseparately for the semantic layer.\n\n### Release archive (forthcoming)\n\nPrebuilt per-OS/arch archives are the planned packaged distribution channel, but\nthey have not been published yet. Until they appear on the\n[GitHub Releases](https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/releases) page, use\nthe pre-release source installer above.\n\nEach published archive will contain the plugin binary plus `README.md`, `LICENSE`,\nand `entire-plugin.yml`, alongside a `SHA256SUMS` file. After downloading and\nverifying the checksum, extract and install the binary:\n\n```sh\nVERSION=vX.Y.Z # use the matching version published by both repositories\ntar -xzf \"entire-brain-${VERSION}-darwin-arm64.tar.gz\"\nentire plugin install \"./entire-brain-${VERSION}-darwin-arm64/entire-brain\" --force\n```\n\nWhen provider archives are published, install the matching `entire-graph` archive\nthe same way so `entire graph` is available.\n\n## Verify\n\n```sh\nentire brain version          # plugin version\nentire brain doctor           # checks the Entire CLI plugin environment\nentire brain status           # summarizes the brain (empty until you run `entire brain setup` in a repo)\n\nentire graph version            # provider version\nentire graph doctor --json      # provider diagnostics; expect \"no_egress\": true\n```\n\n`entire brain doctor` reports whether the plugin directories and the semantic\nprovider are wired up. If it says the provider is missing, confirm `entire graph`\nresolves and that the installed binary is named `entire-graph` rather than the\nretired `entire-sem`. For a versioned release, also confirm the brain and provider\nreport the same published release version.\n\nThat is the machine set up. No repository has a brain yet — the next section is\nthe command that gives one to a repository you actually work in.\n\n## Onboard a repository: `entire brain setup`\n\nInstalling put two plugins on this machine. It gave no repository a brain. This\nis the step that does, and it is where a first-time reader should go next:\n\n```sh\ncd /path/to/your/repo\nentire brain setup\n```\n\nOne command, three phases:\n\n| phase | blocking? | spends tokens? | installed by default? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. **instant core** — sessions, semantic index, seed, docs, history, entities | yes, seconds | **no**, deterministic | — |\n| 2. **fact backfill** — distill past sessions into durable facts, newest first, detached | no | **yes** | yes, unless `--no-backfill` |\n| 3. **watcher daemon** — one machine-wide launchd agent / systemd user unit | no | **yes**, per window | **yes, with no prompt**, unless `--no-daemon` |\n\nThree things are worth being explicit about before you run it:\n\n- **Phase 2 spends tokens.** It distills your past sessions with an agent CLI,\n  capped at 25 sessions per pass (`--backfill-budget`). `--no-backfill` skips it.\n- **Phase 3 installs a persistent background service, by default, without\n  asking.** launchd with `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive` on macOS, a systemd user unit\n  with `Restart=always` on Linux. It survives logout and reboot, and its gated\n  distill step calls an agent, so it is a recurring spend. `--no-daemon` skips\n  it; `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon` removes one you already have.\n\n  You are told before it happens rather than after. A run that will register a\n  watcher opens with the pre-flight line, ahead of any work:\n\n  ```\n  setup: background watcher: this run will install io.entire.brain-watch.<id> at\n  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.entire.brain-watch.<id>.plist — a persistent launchd\n  service that starts again at every login; pass --no-daemon to skip it, or\n  remove it later with `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`\n  ```\n\n  A re-run that finds the watcher already installed says it *keeps* it instead,\n  and `--no-daemon` prints nothing about a service it will not install.\n- **`entire brain setup --no-backfill --no-daemon` spends nothing at all** and\n  changes no service — it is phase 1 only.\n\n```sh\nentire brain setup --no-backfill --no-daemon   # deterministic build only, zero spend\nentire brain status                            # what was built, backfill progress, daemon health\nentire brain setup --uninstall-daemon          # stop and remove the watcher\n```\n\n### What `setup` needs first, and what it does not\n\n`setup` hard-fails on one thing only: the target must be a **local path that is a\ngit repository** with at least one commit. It checks that before it registers a\nworkspace, writes state, or installs anything.\n\nIt does **not** require, and will not do for you:\n\n- **`entire enable` in the repository.** Hook registration belongs to the Entire\n  CLI's repository enablement, which owns the whole lifecycle hook set;\n  duplicating it in `setup` would leave two hooks racing to distill the same\n  session. `setup` reports the session-end hook as an advisory line, never a\n  failure.\n- **Captured Entire sessions.** A repository Entire has never captured a\n  session in still builds, and the `sessions` component is green: the export\n  returns an empty inventory, which is a true answer. You get a brain from\n  code, docs and git history rather than from your past agent work — which is\n  the one source it is most interesting for, so this is worth fixing, just not\n  by a failure. The export only *fails* when Entire cannot prove the inventory\n  is empty rather than merely unreadable — a configured checkpoint remote it\n  cannot enumerate, offline or unauthenticated — and the hint then says to work\n  a session and re-run, and points at `entire checkpoint list` for what Entire\n  itself can see.\n- **A working `entire graph` provider**, a supported service manager, or an\n  agent CLI — each missing piece degrades exactly its own phase.\n\nSo the order that produces the best brain, rather than merely a successful\ncommand, is:\n\n```sh\nentire enable                              # capture sessions + wire the session-end hook\ngit add .entire .claude && git commit      # enable wrote these and did not commit them\nentire brain setup                         # then onboard: build, backfill, watch\n```\n\nThat order is a quality decision, not a correctness one — running `entire enable`\nafter `setup` works too, and the next backfill pass picks the new sessions up.\n\n**The commit in the middle is not optional.** `entire enable` writes\n`.entire/settings.json` and your agent's hook settings (`.claude/settings.json`\nfor Claude Code) and leaves them uncommitted, so a `setup` run immediately\nafterwards finds a dirty worktree and refuses to seed or index it — two red `x`\non a first run, caused by the command directly above it:\n\n```\n  instant     + sessions  x seed  + history  + docs  x semantic  + patterns  + entities  + memory\n              x seed baseline: dirty_worktree: refusing to seed uncommitted content without --worktree\n                hint: commit or stash the working tree, then re-run `entire brain setup`; `entire enable` writes .entire/ and .claude/ without committing them, which is what a first run usually trips over\n```\n\nBoth files are project configuration that belongs in the repository anyway, so\ncommitting them is the real fix rather than a workaround. (`--worktree`, which\nthe refusal names, is a `refresh` flag — `setup` does not accept it.)\n\n**A repository new to Entire reaches an all-green `setup` with no user action.**\nOn a committed `git init` repository with no remote at all, every component\nbuilds:\n\n```\nBrain ready in 3.6s\n  repo        /path/to/your/repo  (local/your-repo-686eadf04b0f)\n  brain       ~/.local/share/entire/plugins/data/brain/repos/local/your-repo-686eadf04b0f\n  instant     + sessions  + seed  + history  + docs  + semantic  + patterns  + entities  + memory  (3.6s)\n  facts       .............. 0/0 sessions distilled\n  backfill    - skipped (--no-backfill)  (0s)\n  workspace   + default  (20ms)\n  daemon      - skipped: --no-daemon  (0s)\n```\n\n`x semantic index: repo key mismatch` used to be the guaranteed second line\nhere, blamed on the repository having **no git remote**. It was wrong in both\ndirections — it also fired on every non-GitHub origin, because `entire-brain`\nand `entire-graph` spelled the same repository differently — and it is fixed:\nthe brain now accepts the provider's own spelling of the repository it just\nasked the provider to index. A mismatch that still appears means the snapshot\nreally does describe another repository, in practice one the provider cached\nbefore this repo's origin changed, and the hint says to rebuild it with\n`entire brain refresh index --force`.\n\n`setup` still exits 0 whenever at least one component built, and still names the\nones that did not, so `Brain ready (degraded)` is information rather than\nfailure when you do see it. The things that legitimately produce it:\n\n- **an uncommitted worktree** — `x seed` and `x semantic`, almost always the\n  `entire enable` output above, fixed by committing it;\n- **no `entire graph` provider** — `x semantic index:\n  provider_doctor_failed: ...`, fixed by `scripts/install.sh`;\n- **an unreadable checkpoint inventory** — `x session export`, when Entire\n  cannot reach the checkpoint remote to prove there is nothing to export.\n\n### Or build the brain by hand: `refresh`\n\n`refresh` is the same deterministic build on its own — no backfill, no workspace,\nno watcher. Use it when you want the brain and nothing else, or when scripting:\n\n```sh\ncd /path/to/your/repo\n\nentire brain refresh --agent none\nentire brain status\n```\n\n`refresh` exports captured sessions, builds the local history and doc indexes,\nasks `entire graph` for a semantic snapshot, and stores the derived brain under\nEntire's plugin data directory. `--agent none` keeps this build deterministic and\ntoken-free, with no hosted-model calls.\n\nThe semantic snapshot is built from committed state, so run `refresh` on a clean\ncheckout. On a dirty worktree the semantic step can be skipped or refused; index\nuncommitted state deliberately with `entire brain refresh index --worktree`.\nBundle export rejects worktree-backed semantic indexes.\n\n`entire brain status` then reports the brain's sources, durable-fact readiness,\nand semantic coverage, freshness, and blind spots. Add `--json` for a\nmachine-readable report.\n\n### If you do not have the semantic provider yet\n\n`refresh` runs the semantic step by default, and that step fails if `entire graph`\ncannot be verified. To build a brain from sessions, history, docs, and facts\nwithout the provider, disable the semantic step:\n\n```sh\nentire brain refresh --agent none --semantic=false\n```\n\nInstall the Graph-based `entire-graph` from source (or from a matching release tag\nonce available), then re-run `entire brain refresh --agent none` to add the\nsemantic code graph.\n\n### Exploring a repository you don't have locally\n\n`entire brain add <repo-url>` clones the repository, fetches its Entire\ncheckpoint history, and runs the build above in one step; afterward explore\nthe clone with `entire brain dash`, `status`, or `search`.\n\n### Query the brain\n\nEvery result carries an `id` you can fetch in full with `get`.\n\n```sh\nentire brain overview                          # what the project is: stack, commands, recent decisions\nentire brain brief \"add rate limiting to the API\"   # a bounded, task-shaped context packet\nentire brain query \"how does checkpointing work\"    # hybrid lexical + vector search across the brain\nentire brain query --keyword \"checkpoint\"               # exact keyword search\nentire brain recall \"why did we pick this default\"  # durable facts for the current branch\nentire brain get fact:<id>                     # fetch one item in full\n```\n\nAdd `--json` to any of these for machine-readable output. `entire brain agent-guide`\nprints the recommended command set for a coding agent.\n\n### Optional: distill durable facts\n\nDistillation is an opt-in agent step that turns captured sessions into durable\nproject knowledge (decisions, constraints, preferences, gotchas, conventions).\nIt sends redacted transcript chunks to the selected agent, so it spends tokens\nand performs network egress unless you point it at a local loopback agent. Always\nestimate first.\n\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Getting Started\n\n## Install and discover agent instructions\n\nRun `entire brain init-agents` in the consuming project to install\n`.entire/agent-guide.md` plus managed pointers in `AGENTS.md` and\n`CLAUDE.md`. Reruns reconcile Graph and Brain blocks into one shared workflow and preserve your own text.\nUse `--repo <path>` for another project. Installation does not build indexes,\ncall an agent, or start services.\n\n`entire brain agent-guide` prints the same guide; `guide` remains an alias.\nUse `entire brain capabilities --json` to discover compiled features and their\nrequirements without a repository. Use `status --details --json` for the\ncurrent repository's readiness. Graph's language/relation inventory remains\navailable through `entire graph capabilities --json`.\n\n\nThis guide takes you from nothing to querying a local repository brain. No team\ncontext is assumed.\n\nIt is two commands. The first installs the plugins on this machine; the second\nonboards a repository. Do not stop after the first — installing gives no\nrepository a brain.\n\n```sh\n# once per machine\ngit clone https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\nentire-brain/scripts/install.sh\n\n# once per repository you want a brain for\ncd /path/to/your/repo\nentire brain setup\n```\n\n`entire-brain` is an external-command plugin for the Entire CLI. Once installed\nit is invoked as `entire brain ...`. It builds a local, inspectable \"brain\" for a\nrepository from retained Entire sessions, seed context, docs, decision history,\na semantic code graph, and durable facts, then exposes retrieval, review, and MCP\nsurfaces that agents and humans query. Everything it builds stays on your machine.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- The Entire CLI, available on your `PATH` as `entire`. It is the host that\n  dispatches `entire brain`, and it is what captures the sessions the brain\n  learns from.\n- Git.\n- A Go toolchain (1.27 or newer). It is not optional: every install path here\n  builds from source. There is no prebuilt archive to download -- the tagged\n  releases carry release notes and no binaries -- and there is no `go install`\n  path yet; see \"Versioned install status\" below.\n- The `entire-graph` semantic provider, invoked as `entire graph`. The brain shells\n  out to it (`entire graph snapshot`, `entire graph doctor`) to build the semantic\n  code graph. Building `entire-graph` from source needs a cgo-capable C compiler,\n  because it uses tree-sitter native parser bindings. The brain itself is a\n  pure-Go build and does not need cgo.\n\nYou can build a brain from history, docs, and facts without the provider (see\n\"If you do not have the semantic provider yet\" below), but the semantic code\ngraph needs `entire graph`.\n\nThe provider is `entire-graph` (the public `entireio/entire-graph` repo),\ninvoked as `entire graph`. The brain shells out to `entire graph snapshot` and\n`entire graph doctor` to build and verify the semantic layer. Install the latest\nprovider as shown below. If you do not need the semantic code graph, you can skip\nthe provider and run the brain with `--semantic=false` (see\n\"If you do not have the semantic provider yet\").\n\n## Install\n\nThere are two components: the brain plugin and the `entire-graph` provider it\ncalls. Both are registered with the Entire CLI the same way, using\n`entire plugin install <path>`, which links a local `entire-<name>` executable\ninto Entire's managed plugin directory. You do not have to fetch or arrange the\nprovider yourself -- `scripts/install.sh` does both components in one pass.\n\n### One command\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\nentire-brain/scripts/install.sh\n```\n\nThis is the whole install: it checks the prerequisites listed above and reports\nevery missing one at once, resolves `entire-graph` (using `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR`,\nthen any checkout already on this machine, then a shallow clone of the public\nrepository into `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`), builds\nand registers both plugins, writes the default plugin configuration\n(`entire brain config init`), and runs `entire brain doctor`. It prints which of\nthe three provider routes it took. Neither checkout has to have a particular\nname, and they do not have to be siblings.\n\nThe prerequisites do not go away: Go 1.27 or newer and a cgo-capable C compiler\nare still required, because both components are built from source here. What\ngoes away is having to arrange directories before you start.\n\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` is the same flow for a machine with nothing on it: it\nclones `entire-brain` when it is not already present and then runs\n`scripts/install.sh`. There is no `curl | sh` form, because\n`entireio/entire-brain` is a private repository -- `raw.githubusercontent.com`\nreturns 404 for it without a token -- and because installing means building from\nsource, so the clone has to happen either way.\n\n### Versioned install status\n\nThere is no versioned install today. `entire-brain` is tagged -- `v0.3.0` is the\ncurrent release -- but a tag here is release notes and nothing else: the releases\ncarry **no binaries and no source archive**, so there is nothing to download and\nrun. The `v0.1.0` tags additionally predate the Graph rename (the provider tag\nbuilds `entire-sem` and the brain tag invokes `entire sem`), so do not combine\nthem with the `entire graph` commands in this guide.\n\nA versioned `go install` path is not available either, and will not be until\nmatching Graph-based release tags exist in both repositories. This section will\ngain copy-paste commands when they do. Until then the source install above --\n`scripts/install.sh` against a checkout -- is the only way to install this, which\nis why the Go toolchain is a hard requirement rather than a conditional one.\n\n`entire plugin install` currently supports local executable paths only. It does\nnot fetch from a git URL or a GitHub release, and it does not auto-install the\nprovider dependency.\n\n### Managing the provider checkout yourself\n\nThe one command above is the source install: until matching Graph-based tags are\navailable, both components are built from their current `main` branches, which\ntracks development and is not a reproducible versioned install. Use matching\nrelease tags once they are published.\n\nIf you would rather own the `entire-graph` checkout -- to pin a commit, or to\nwork on the provider -- clone it too and run the same installer:\n\n```sh\ngit clone --branch main https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph.git\ngit clone --branch main https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\ncd entire-brain\nscripts/install.sh\n```\n\nA side-by-side checkout is discovered with no configuration, and so are the usual\nsource directories under `$HOME`. Set\n`ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR=/path/to/entire-graph scripts/install.sh` only when your\ncheckout is somewhere the installer would not look, or when you want to pin one\nspecific checkout; an override that does not hold an `entire-graph` checkout is\nan error rather than a silent fallback. `ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` stops the\ninstaller reaching the network at all, so a machine without egress fails with an\nexplanation instead of hanging on a clone.\n\nTo build and install only the brain from a checkout, run `scripts/install-local.sh`\n(equivalently `mise run install`). You still need `entire graph` installed\nseparately for the semantic layer.\n\n### Release archive (forthcoming)\n\nPrebuilt per-OS/arch archives are the planned packaged distribution channel, but\nthey have not been published yet. Until they appear on the\n[GitHub Releases](https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/releases) page, use\nthe pre-release source installer above.\n\nEach published archive will contain the plugin binary plus `README.md`, `LICENSE`,\nand `entire-plugin.yml`, alongside a `SHA256SUMS` file. After downloading and\nverifying the checksum, extract and install the binary:\n\n```sh\nVERSION=vX.Y.Z # use the matching version published by both repositories\ntar -xzf \"entire-brain-${VERSION}-darwin-arm64.tar.gz\"\nentire plugin install \"./entire-brain-${VERSION}-darwin-arm64/entire-brain\" --force\n```\n\nWhen provider archives are published, install the matching `entire-graph` archive\nthe same way so `entire graph` is available.\n\n## Verify\n\n```sh\nentire brain version          # plugin version\nentire brain doctor           # checks the Entire CLI plugin environment\nentire brain status           # summarizes the brain (empty until you run `entire brain setup` in a repo)\n\nentire graph version            # provider version\nentire graph doctor --json      # provider diagnostics; expect \"no_egress\": true\n```\n\n`entire brain doctor` reports whether the plugin directories and the semantic\nprovider are wired up. If it says the provider is missing, confirm `entire graph`\nresolves and that the installed binary is named `entire-graph` rather than the\nretired `entire-sem`. For a versioned release, also confirm the brain and provider\nreport the same published release version.\n\nThat is the machine set up. No repository has a brain yet — the next section is\nthe command that gives one to a repository you actually work in.\n\n## Onboard a repository: `entire brain setup`\n\nInstalling put two plugins on this machine. It gave no repository a brain. This\nis the step that does, and it is where a first-time reader should go next:\n\n```sh\ncd /path/to/your/repo\nentire brain setup\n```\n\nOne command, three phases:\n\n| phase | blocking? | spends tokens? | installed by default? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. **instant core** — sessions, semantic index, seed, docs, history, entities | yes, seconds | **no**, deterministic | — |\n| 2. **fact backfill** — distill past sessions into durable facts, newest first, detached | no | **yes** | yes, unless `--no-backfill` |\n| 3. **watcher daemon** — one machine-wide launchd agent / systemd user unit | no | **yes**, per window | **yes, with no prompt**, unless `--no-daemon` |\n\nThree things are worth being explicit about before you run it:\n\n- **Phase 2 spends tokens.** It distills your past sessions with an agent CLI,\n  capped at 25 sessions per pass (`--backfill-budget`). `--no-backfill` skips it.\n- **Phase 3 installs a persistent background service, by default, without\n  asking.** launchd with `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive` on macOS, a systemd user unit\n  with `Restart=always` on Linux. It survives logout and reboot, and its gated\n  distill step calls an agent, so it is a recurring spend. `--no-daemon` skips\n  it; `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon` removes one you already have.\n\n  You are told before it happens rather than after. A run that will register a\n  watcher opens with the pre-flight line, ahead of any work:\n\n  ```\n  setup: background watcher: this run will install io.entire.brain-watch.<id> at\n  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.entire.brain-watch.<id>.plist — a persistent launchd\n  service that starts again at every login; pass --no-daemon to skip it, or\n  remove it later with `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`\n  ```\n\n  A re-run that finds the watcher already installed says it *keeps* it instead,\n  and `--no-daemon` prints nothing about a service it will not install.\n- **`entire brain setup --no-backfill --no-daemon` spends nothing at all** and\n  changes no service — it is phase 1 only.\n\n```sh\nentire brain setup --no-backfill --no-daemon   # deterministic build only, zero spend\nentire brain status                            # what was built, backfill progress, daemon health\nentire brain setup --uninstall-daemon          # stop and remove the watcher\n```\n\n### What `setup` needs first, and what it does not\n\n`setup` hard-fails on one thing only: the target must be a **local path that is a\ngit repository** with at least one commit. It checks that before it registers a\nworkspace, writes state, or installs anything.\n\nIt does **not** require, and will not do for you:\n\n- **`entire enable` in the repository.** Hook registration belongs to the Entire\n  CLI's repository enablement, which owns the whole lifecycle hook set;\n  duplicating it in `setup` would leave two hooks racing to distill the same\n  session. `setup` reports the session-end hook as an advisory line, never a\n  failure.\n- **Captured Entire sessions.** A repository Entire has never captured a\n  session in still builds, and the `sessions` component is green: the export\n  returns an empty inventory, which is a true answer. You get a brain from\n  code, docs and git history rather than from your past agent work — which is\n  the one source it is most interesting for, so this is worth fixing, just not\n  by a failure. The export only *fails* when Entire cannot prove the inventory\n  is empty rather than merely unreadable — a configured checkpoint remote it\n  cannot enumerate, offline or unauthenticated — and the hint then says to work\n  a session and re-run, and points at `entire checkpoint list` for what Entire\n  itself can see.\n- **A working `entire graph` provider**, a supported service manager, or an\n  agent CLI — each missing piece degrades exactly its own phase.\n\nSo the order that produces the best brain, rather than merely a successful\ncommand, is:\n\n```sh\nentire enable                              # capture sessions + wire the session-end hook\ngit add .entire .claude && git commit      # enable wrote these and did not commit them\nentire brain setup                         # then onboard: build, backfill, watch\n```\n\nThat order is a quality decision, not a correctness one — running `entire enable`\nafter `setup` works too, and the next backfill pass picks the new sessions up.\n\n**The commit in the middle is not optional.** `entire enable` writes\n`.entire/settings.json` and your agent's hook settings (`.claude/settings.json`\nfor Claude Code) and leaves them uncommitted, so a `setup` run immediately\nafterwards finds a dirty worktree and refuses to seed or index it — two red `x`\non a first run, caused by the command directly above it:\n\n```\n  instant     + sessions  x seed  + history  + docs  x semantic  + patterns  + entities  + memory\n              x seed baseline: dirty_worktree: refusing to seed uncommitted content without --worktree\n                hint: commit or stash the working tree, then re-run `entire brain setup`; `entire enable` writes .entire/ and .claude/ without committing them, which is what a first run usually trips over\n```\n\nBoth files are project configuration that belongs in the repository anyway, so\ncommitting them is the real fix rather than a workaround. (`--worktree`, which\nthe refusal names, is a `refresh` flag — `setup` does not accept it.)\n\n**A repository new to Entire reaches an all-green `setup` with no user action.**\nOn a committed `git init` repository with no remote at all, every component\nbuilds:\n\n```\nBrain ready in 3.6s\n  repo        /path/to/your/repo  (local/your-repo-686eadf04b0f)\n  brain       ~/.local/share/entire/plugins/data/brain/repos/local/your-repo-686eadf04b0f\n  instant     + sessions  + seed  + history  + docs  + semantic  + patterns  + entities  + memory  (3.6s)\n  facts       .............. 0/0 sessions distilled\n  backfill    - skipped (--no-backfill)  (0s)\n  workspace   + default  (20ms)\n  daemon      - skipped: --no-daemon  (0s)\n```\n\n`x semantic index: repo key mismatch` used to be the guaranteed second line\nhere, blamed on the repository having **no git remote**. It was wrong in both\ndirections — it also fired on every non-GitHub origin, because `entire-brain`\nand `entire-graph` spelled the same repository differently — and it is fixed:\nthe brain now accepts the provider's own spelling of the repository it just\nasked the provider to index. A mismatch that still appears means the snapshot\nreally does describe another repository, in practice one the provider cached\nbefore this repo's origin changed, and the hint says to rebuild it with\n`entire brain refresh index --force`.\n\n`setup` still exits 0 whenever at least one component built, and still names the\nones that did not, so `Brain ready (degraded)` is information rather than\nfailure when you do see it. The things that legitimately produce it:\n\n- **an uncommitted worktree** — `x seed` and `x semantic`, almost always the\n  `entire enable` output above, fixed by committing it;\n- **no `entire graph` provider** — `x semantic index:\n  provider_doctor_failed: ...`, fixed by `scripts/install.sh`;\n- **an unreadable checkpoint inventory** — `x session export`, when Entire\n  cannot reach the checkpoint remote to prove there is nothing to export.\n\n### Or build the brain by hand: `refresh`\n\n`refresh` is the same deterministic build on its own — no backfill, no workspace,\nno watcher. Use it when you want the brain and nothing else, or when scripting:\n\n```sh\ncd /path/to/your/repo\n\nentire brain refresh --agent none\nentire brain status\n```\n\n`refresh` exports captured sessions, builds the local history and doc indexes,\nasks `entire graph` for a semantic snapshot, and stores the derived brain under\nEntire's plugin data directory. `--agent none` keeps this build deterministic and\ntoken-free, with no hosted-model calls.\n\nThe semantic snapshot is built from committed state, so run `refresh` on a clean\ncheckout. On a dirty worktree the semantic step can be skipped or refused; index\nuncommitted state deliberately with `entire brain refresh index --worktree`.\nBundle export rejects worktree-backed semantic indexes.\n\n`entire brain status` then reports the brain's sources, durable-fact readiness,\nand semantic coverage, freshness, and blind spots. Add `--json` for a\nmachine-readable report.\n\n### If you do not have the semantic provider yet\n\n`refresh` runs the semantic step by default, and that step fails if `entire graph`\ncannot be verified. To build a brain from sessions, history, docs, and facts\nwithout the provider, disable the semantic step:\n\n```sh\nentire brain refresh --agent none --semantic=false\n```\n\nInstall the Graph-based `entire-graph` from source (or from a matching release tag\nonce available), then re-run `entire brain refresh --agent none` to add the\nsemantic code graph.\n\n### Exploring a repository you don't have locally\n\n`entire brain add <repo-url>` clones the repository, fetches its Entire\ncheckpoint history, and runs the build above in one step; afterward explore\nthe clone with `entire brain dash`, `status`, or `search`.\n\n### Query the brain\n\nEvery result carries an `id` you can fetch in full with `get`.\n\n```sh\nentire brain overview                          # what the project is: stack, commands, recent decisions\nentire brain brief \"add rate limiting to the API\"   # a bounded, task-shaped context packet\nentire brain query \"how does checkpointing work\"    # hybrid lexical + vector search across the brain\nentire brain query --keyword \"checkpoint\"               # exact keyword search\nentire brain recall \"why did we pick this default\"  # durable facts for the current branch\nentire brain get fact:<id>                     # fetch one item in full\n```\n\nAdd `--json` to any of these for machine-readable output. `entire brain agent-guide`\nprints the recommended command set for a coding agent.\n\n### Optional: distill durable facts\n\nDistillation is an opt-in agent step that turns captured sessions into durable\nproject knowledge (decisions, constraints, preferences, gotchas, conventions).\nIt sends redacted transcript chunks to the selected agent, so it spends tokens\nand performs network egress unless you point it at a local loopback agent. Always\nestimate first.\n\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2625},"formatted_output":"# Getting Started\n\n## Install and discover agent instructions\n\nRun `entire brain init-agents` in the consuming project to install\n`.entire/agent-guide.md` plus managed pointers in `AGENTS.md` and\n`CLAUDE.md`. Reruns reconcile Graph and Brain blocks into one shared workflow and preserve your own text.\nUse `--repo <path>` for another project. Installation does not build indexes,\ncall an agent, or start services.\n\n`entire brain agent-guide` prints the same guide; `guide` remains an alias.\nUse `entire brain capabilities --json` to discover compiled features and their\nrequirements without a repository. Use `status --details --json` for the\ncurrent repository's readiness. Graph's language/relation inventory remains\navailable through `entire graph capabilities --json`.\n\n\nThis guide takes you from nothing to querying a local repository brain. No team\ncontext is assumed.\n\nIt is two commands. The first installs the plugins on this machine; the second\nonboards a repository. Do not stop after the first — installing gives no\nrepository a brain.\n\n```sh\n# once per machine\ngit clone https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\nentire-brain/scripts/install.sh\n\n# once per repository you want a brain for\ncd /path/to/your/repo\nentire brain setup\n```\n\n`entire-brain` is an external-command plugin for the Entire CLI. Once installed\nit is invoked as `entire brain ...`. It builds a local, inspectable \"brain\" for a\nrepository from retained Entire sessions, seed context, docs, decision history,\na semantic code graph, and durable facts, then exposes retrieval, review, and MCP\nsurfaces that agents and humans query. Everything it builds stays on your machine.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- The Entire CLI, available on your `PATH` as `entire`. It is the host that\n  dispatches `entire brain`, and it is what captures the sessions the brain\n  learns from.\n- Git.\n- A Go toolchain (1.27 or newer). It is not optional: every install path here\n  builds from source. There is no prebuilt archive to download -- the tagged\n  releases carry release notes and no binaries -- and there is no `go install`\n  path yet; see \"Versioned install status\" below.\n- The `entire-graph` semantic provider, invoked as `entire graph`. The brain shells\n  out to it (`entire graph snapshot`, `entire graph doctor`) to build the semantic\n  code graph. Building `entire-graph` from source needs a cgo-capable C compiler,\n  because it uses tree-sitter native parser bindings. The brain itself is a\n  pure-Go build and does not need cgo.\n\nYou can build a brain from history, docs, and facts without the provider (see\n\"If you do not have the semantic provider yet\" below), but the semantic code\ngraph needs `entire graph`.\n\nThe provider is `entire-graph` (the public `entireio/entire-graph` repo),\ninvoked as `entire graph`. The brain shells out to `entire graph snapshot` and\n`entire graph doctor` to build and verify the semantic layer. Install the latest\nprovider as shown below. If you do not need the semantic code graph, you can skip\nthe provider and run the brain with `--semantic=false` (see\n\"If you do not have the semantic provider yet\").\n\n## Install\n\nThere are two components: the brain plugin and the `entire-graph` provider it\ncalls. Both are registered with the Entire CLI the same way, using\n`entire plugin install <path>`, which links a local `entire-<name>` executable\ninto Entire's managed plugin directory. You do not have to fetch or arrange the\nprovider yourself -- `scripts/install.sh` does both components in one pass.\n\n### One command\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\nentire-brain/scripts/install.sh\n```\n\nThis is the whole install: it checks the prerequisites listed above and reports\nevery missing one at once, resolves `entire-graph` (using `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR`,\nthen any checkout already on this machine, then a shallow clone of the public\nrepository into `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`), builds\nand registers both plugins, writes the default plugin configuration\n(`entire brain config init`), and runs `entire brain doctor`. It prints which of\nthe three provider routes it took. Neither checkout has to have a particular\nname, and they do not have to be siblings.\n\nThe prerequisites do not go away: Go 1.27 or newer and a cgo-capable C compiler\nare still required, because both components are built from source here. What\ngoes away is having to arrange directories before you start.\n\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` is the same flow for a machine with nothing on it: it\nclones `entire-brain` when it is not already present and then runs\n`scripts/install.sh`. There is no `curl | sh` form, because\n`entireio/entire-brain` is a private repository -- `raw.githubusercontent.com`\nreturns 404 for it without a token -- and because installing means building from\nsource, so the clone has to happen either way.\n\n### Versioned install status\n\nThere is no versioned install today. `entire-brain` is tagged -- `v0.3.0` is the\ncurrent release -- but a tag here is release notes and nothing else: the releases\ncarry **no binaries and no source archive**, so there is nothing to download and\nrun. The `v0.1.0` tags additionally predate the Graph rename (the provider tag\nbuilds `entire-sem` and the brain tag invokes `entire sem`), so do not combine\nthem with the `entire graph` commands in this guide.\n\nA versioned `go install` path is not available either, and will not be until\nmatching Graph-based release tags exist in both repositories. This section will\ngain copy-paste commands when they do. Until then the source install above --\n`scripts/install.sh` against a checkout -- is the only way to install this, which\nis why the Go toolchain is a hard requirement rather than a conditional one.\n\n`entire plugin install` currently supports local executable paths only. It does\nnot fetch from a git URL or a GitHub release, and it does not auto-install the\nprovider dependency.\n\n### Managing the provider checkout yourself\n\nThe one command above is the source install: until matching Graph-based tags are\navailable, both components are built from their current `main` branches, which\ntracks development and is not a reproducible versioned install. Use matching\nrelease tags once they are published.\n\nIf you would rather own the `entire-graph` checkout -- to pin a commit, or to\nwork on the provider -- clone it too and run the same installer:\n\n```sh\ngit clone --branch main https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph.git\ngit clone --branch main https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\ncd entire-brain\nscripts/install.sh\n```\n\nA side-by-side checkout is discovered with no configuration, and so are the usual\nsource directories under `$HOME`. Set\n`ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR=/path/to/entire-graph scripts/install.sh` only when your\ncheckout is somewhere the installer would not look, or when you want to pin one\nspecific checkout; an override that does not hold an `entire-graph` checkout is\nan error rather than a silent fallback. `ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` stops the\ninstaller reaching the network at all, so a machine without egress fails with an\nexplanation instead of hanging on a clone.\n\nTo build and install only the brain from a checkout, run `scripts/install-local.sh`\n(equivalently `mise run install`). You still need `entire graph` installed\nseparately for the semantic layer.\n\n### Release archive (forthcoming)\n\nPrebuilt per-OS/arch archives are the planned packaged distribution channel, but\nthey have not been published yet. Until they appear on the\n[GitHub Releases](https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/releases) page, use\nthe pre-release source installer above.\n\nEach published archive will contain the plugin binary plus `README.md`, `LICENSE`,\nand `entire-plugin.yml`, alongside a `SHA256SUMS` file. After downloading and\nverifying the checksum, extract and install the binary:\n\n```sh\nVERSION=vX.Y.Z # use the matching version published by both repositories\ntar -xzf \"entire-brain-${VERSION}-darwin-arm64.tar.gz\"\nentire plugin install \"./entire-brain-${VERSION}-darwin-arm64/entire-brain\" --force\n```\n\nWhen provider archives are published, install the matching `entire-graph` archive\nthe same way so `entire graph` is available.\n\n## Verify\n\n```sh\nentire brain version          # plugin version\nentire brain doctor           # checks the Entire CLI plugin environment\nentire brain status           # summarizes the brain (empty until you run `entire brain setup` in a repo)\n\nentire graph version            # provider version\nentire graph doctor --json      # provider diagnostics; expect \"no_egress\": true\n```\n\n`entire brain doctor` reports whether the plugin directories and the semantic\nprovider are wired up. If it says the provider is missing, confirm `entire graph`\nresolves and that the installed binary is named `entire-graph` rather than the\nretired `entire-sem`. For a versioned release, also confirm the brain and provider\nreport the same published release version.\n\nThat is the machine set up. No repository has a brain yet — the next section is\nthe command that gives one to a repository you actually work in.\n\n## Onboard a repository: `entire brain setup`\n\nInstalling put two plugins on this machine. It gave no repository a brain. This\nis the step that does, and it is where a first-time reader should go next:\n\n```sh\ncd /path/to/your/repo\nentire brain setup\n```\n\nOne command, three phases:\n\n| phase | blocking? | spends tokens? | installed by default? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. **instant core** — sessions, semantic index, seed, docs, history, entities | yes, seconds | **no**, deterministic | — |\n| 2. **fact backfill** — distill past sessions into durable facts, newest first, detached | no | **yes** | yes, unless `--no-backfill` |\n| 3. **watcher daemon** — one machine-wide launchd agent / systemd user unit | no | **yes**, per window | **yes, with no prompt**, unless `--no-daemon` |\n\nThree things are worth being explicit about before you run it:\n\n- **Phase 2 spends tokens.** It distills your past sessions with an agent CLI,\n  capped at 25 sessions per pass (`--backfill-budget`). `--no-backfill` skips it.\n- **Phase 3 installs a persistent background service, by default, without\n  asking.** launchd with `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive` on macOS, a systemd user unit\n  with `Restart=always` on Linux. It survives logout and reboot, and its gated\n  distill step calls an agent, so it is a recurring spend. `--no-daemon` skips\n  it; `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon` removes one you already have.\n\n  You are told before it happens rather than after. A run that will register a\n  watcher opens with the pre-flight line, ahead of any work:\n\n  ```\n  setup: background watcher: this run will install io.entire.brain-watch.<id> at\n  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.entire.brain-watch.<id>.plist — a persistent launchd\n  service that starts again at every login; pass --no-daemon to skip it, or\n  remove it later with `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`\n  ```\n\n  A re-run that finds the watcher already installed says it *keeps* it instead,\n  and `--no-daemon` prints nothing about a service it will not install.\n- **`entire brain setup --no-backfill --no-daemon` spends nothing at all** and\n  changes no service — it is phase 1 only.\n\n```sh\nentire brain setup --no-backfill --no-daemon   # deterministic build only, zero spend\nentire brain status                            # what was built, backfill progress, daemon health\nentire brain setup --uninstall-daemon          # stop and remove the watcher\n```\n\n### What `setup` needs first, and what it does not\n\n`setup` hard-fails on one thing only: the target must be a **local path that is a\ngit repository** with at least one commit. It checks that before it registers a\nworkspace, writes state, or installs anything.\n\nIt does **not** require, and will not do for you:\n\n- **`entire enable` in the repository.** Hook registration belongs to the Entire\n  CLI's repository enablement, which owns the whole lifecycle hook set;\n  duplicating it in `setup` would leave two hooks racing to distill the same\n  session. `setup` reports the session-end hook as an advisory line, never a\n  failure.\n- **Captured Entire sessions.** A repository Entire has never captured a\n  session in still builds, and the `sessions` component is green: the export\n  returns an empty inventory, which is a true answer. You get a brain from\n  code, docs and git history rather than from your past agent work — which is\n  the one source it is most interesting for, so this is worth fixing, just not\n  by a failure. The export only *fails* when Entire cannot prove the inventory\n  is empty rather than merely unreadable — a configured checkpoint remote it\n  cannot enumerate, offline or unauthenticated — and the hint then says to work\n  a session and re-run, and points at `entire checkpoint list` for what Entire\n  itself can see.\n- **A working `entire graph` provider**, a supported service manager, or an\n  agent CLI — each missing piece degrades exactly its own phase.\n\nSo the order that produces the best brain, rather than merely a successful\ncommand, is:\n\n```sh\nentire enable                              # capture sessions + wire the session-end hook\ngit add .entire .claude && git commit      # enable wrote these and did not commit them\nentire brain setup                         # then onboard: build, backfill, watch\n```\n\nThat order is a quality decision, not a correctness one — running `entire enable`\nafter `setup` works too, and the next backfill pass picks the new sessions up.\n\n**The commit in the middle is not optional.** `entire enable` writes\n`.entire/settings.json` and your agent's hook settings (`.claude/settings.json`\nfor Claude Code) and leaves them uncommitted, so a `setup` run immediately\nafterwards finds a dirty worktree and refuses to seed or index it — two red `x`\non a first run, caused by the command directly above it:\n\n```\n  instant     + sessions  x seed  + history  + docs  x semantic  + patterns  + entities  + memory\n              x seed baseline: dirty_worktree: refusing to seed uncommitted content without --worktree\n                hint: commit or stash the working tree, then re-run `entire brain setup`; `entire enable` writes .entire/ and .claude/ without committing them, which is what a first run usually trips over\n```\n\nBoth files are project configuration that belongs in the repository anyway, so\ncommitting them is the real fix rather than a workaround. (`--worktree`, which\nthe refusal names, is a `refresh` flag — `setup` does not accept it.)\n\n**A repository new to Entire reaches an all-green `setup` with no user action.**\nOn a committed `git init` repository with no remote at all, every component\nbuilds:\n\n```\nBrain ready in 3.6s\n  repo        /path/to/your/repo  (local/your-repo-686eadf04b0f)\n  brain       ~/.local/share/entire/plugins/data/brain/repos/local/your-repo-686eadf04b0f\n  instant     + sessions  + seed  + history  + docs  + semantic  + patterns  + entities  + memory  (3.6s)\n  facts       .............. 0/0 sessions distilled\n  backfill    - skipped (--no-backfill)  (0s)\n  workspace   + default  (20ms)\n  daemon      - skipped: --no-daemon  (0s)\n```\n\n`x semantic index: repo key mismatch` used to be the guaranteed second line\nhere, blamed on the repository having **no git remote**. It was wrong in both\ndirections — it also fired on every non-GitHub origin, because `entire-brain`\nand `entire-graph` spelled the same repository differently — and it is fixed:\nthe brain now accepts the provider's own spelling of the repository it just\nasked the provider to index. A mismatch that still appears means the snapshot\nreally does describe another repository, in practice one the provider cached\nbefore this repo's origin changed, and the hint says to rebuild it with\n`entire brain refresh index --force`.\n\n`setup` still exits 0 whenever at least one component built, and still names the\nones that did not, so `Brain ready (degraded)` is information rather than\nfailure when you do see it. The things that legitimately produce it:\n\n- **an uncommitted worktree** — `x seed` and `x semantic`, almost always the\n  `entire enable` output above, fixed by committing it;\n- **no `entire graph` provider** — `x semantic index:\n  provider_doctor_failed: ...`, fixed by `scripts/install.sh`;\n- **an unreadable checkpoint inventory** — `x session export`, when Entire\n  cannot reach the checkpoint remote to prove there is nothing to export.\n\n### Or build the brain by hand: `refresh`\n\n`refresh` is the same deterministic build on its own — no backfill, no workspace,\nno watcher. Use it when you want the brain and nothing else, or when scripting:\n\n```sh\ncd /path/to/your/repo\n\nentire brain refresh --agent none\nentire brain status\n```\n\n`refresh` exports captured sessions, builds the local history and doc indexes,\nasks `entire graph` for a semantic snapshot, and stores the derived brain under\nEntire's plugin data directory. `--agent none` keeps this build deterministic and\ntoken-free, with no hosted-model calls.\n\nThe semantic snapshot is built from committed state, so run `refresh` on a clean\ncheckout. On a dirty worktree the semantic step can be skipped or refused; index\nuncommitted state deliberately with `entire brain refresh index --worktree`.\nBundle export rejects worktree-backed semantic indexes.\n\n`entire brain status` then reports the brain's sources, durable-fact readiness,\nand semantic coverage, freshness, and blind spots. Add `--json` for a\nmachine-readable report.\n\n### If you do not have the semantic provider yet\n\n`refresh` runs the semantic step by default, and that step fails if `entire graph`\ncannot be verified. To build a brain from sessions, history, docs, and facts\nwithout the provider, disable the semantic step:\n\n```sh\nentire brain refresh --agent none --semantic=false\n```\n\nInstall the Graph-based `entire-graph` from source (or from a matching release tag\nonce available), then re-run `entire brain refresh --agent none` to add the\nsemantic code graph.\n\n### Exploring a repository you don't have locally\n\n`entire brain add <repo-url>` clones the repository, fetches its Entire\ncheckpoint history, and runs the build above in one step; afterward explore\nthe clone with `entire brain dash`, `status`, or `search`.\n\n### Query the brain\n\nEvery result carries an `id` you can fetch in full with `get`.\n\n```sh\nentire brain overview                          # what the project is: stack, commands, recent decisions\nentire brain brief \"add rate limiting to the API\"   # a bounded, task-shaped context packet\nentire brain query \"how does checkpointing work\"    # hybrid lexical + vector search across the brain\nentire brain query --keyword \"checkpoint\"               # exact keyword search\nentire brain recall \"why did we pick this default\"  # durable facts for the current branch\nentire brain get fact:<id>                     # fetch one item in full\n```\n\nAdd `--json` to any of these for machine-readable output. `entire brain agent-guide`\nprints the recommended command set for a coding agent.\n\n### Optional: distill durable facts\n\nDistillation is an opt-in agent step that turns captured sessions into durable\nproject knowledge (decisions, constraints, preferences, gotchas, conventions).\nIt sends redacted transcript chunks to the selected agent, so it spends tokens\nand performs network egress unless you point it at a local loopback agent. Always\nestimate first.\n\n"},"started_at_ms":1789839838072,"completed_at_ms":1789839838072}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:43:58.112Z","ordinal":7701,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","session_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","root_turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","response_id":"resp_0cb48b2b18d09e75016aaec9d7749887d2ab1d6d22f0a66ed4","usage":{"input_tokens":243878,"cached_input_tokens":242944,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":133,"reasoning_output_tokens":28,"total_tokens":244011},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":77413668,"cached_input_tokens":74153344,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":176833,"reasoning_output_tokens":15035,"total_tokens":77590501},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":143298687,"cached_input_tokens":136725376,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":361632,"reasoning_output_tokens":36412,"total_tokens":143660319}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:43:58.128Z","ordinal":7702,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0bac4-8bb0-7092-93a3-048d6a98b050","call_id":"call_pzDgfxjTiDbjxJiSZrtGgzQA","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"f279a1\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000002625,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":4846,\"output\":\"# Getting Started\\n\\n## Install and discover agent instructions\\n\\nRun `entire brain init-agents` in the consuming project to install\\n`.entire/agent-guide.md` plus managed pointers in `AGENTS.md` and\\n`CLAUDE.md`. Reruns reconcile Graph and Brain blocks into one shared workflow and preserve your own text.\\nUse `--repo <path>` for another project. Installation does not build indexes,\\ncall an agent, or start services.\\n\\n`entire brain agent-guide` prints the same guide; `guide` remains an alias.\\nUse `entire brain capabilities --json` to discover compiled features and their\\nrequirements without a repository. Use `status --details --json` for the\\ncurrent repository's readiness. Graph's language/relation inventory remains\\navailable through `entire graph capabilities --json`.\\n\\n\\nThis guide takes you from nothing to querying a local repository brain. No team\\ncontext is assumed.\\n\\nIt is two commands. The first installs the plugins on this machine; the second\\nonboards a repository. Do not stop after the first — installing gives no\\nrepository a brain.\\n\\n```sh\\n# once per machine\\ngit clone https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\\nentire-brain/scripts/install.sh\\n\\n# once per repository you want a brain for\\ncd /path/to/your/repo\\nentire brain setup\\n```\\n\\n`entire-brain` is an external-command plugin for the Entire CLI. Once installed\\nit is invoked as `entire brain ...`. It builds a local, inspectable \\\"brain\\\" for a\\nrepository from retained Entire sessions, seed context, docs, decision history,\\na semantic code graph, and durable facts, then exposes retrieval, review, and MCP\\nsurfaces that agents and humans query. Everything it builds stays on your machine.\\n\\n## Requirements\\n\\n- The Entire CLI, available on your `PATH` as `entire`. It is the host that\\n  dispatches `entire brain`, and it is what captures the sessions the brain\\n  learns from.\\n- Git.\\n- A Go toolchain (1.27 or newer). It is not optional: every install path here\\n  builds from source. There is no prebuilt archive to download -- the tagged\\n  releases carry release notes and no binaries -- and there is no `go install`\\n  path yet; see \\\"Versioned install status\\\" below.\\n- The `entire-graph` semantic provider, invoked as `entire graph`. The brain shells\\n  out to it (`entire graph snapshot`, `entire graph doctor`) to build the semantic\\n  code graph. Building `entire-graph` from source needs a cgo-capable C compiler,\\n  because it uses tree-sitter native parser bindings. The brain itself is a\\n  pure-Go build and does not need cgo.\\n\\nYou can build a brain from history, docs, and facts without the provider (see\\n\\\"If you do not have the semantic provider yet\\\" below), but the semantic code\\ngraph needs `entire graph`.\\n\\nThe provider is `entire-graph` (the public `entireio/entire-graph` repo),\\ninvoked as `entire graph`. The brain shells out to `entire graph snapshot` and\\n`entire graph doctor` to build and verify the semantic layer. Install the latest\\nprovider as shown below. If you do not need the semantic code graph, you can skip\\nthe provider and run the brain with `--semantic=false` (see\\n\\\"If you do not have the semantic provider yet\\\").\\n\\n## Install\\n\\nThere are two components: the brain plugin and the `entire-graph` provider it\\ncalls. Both are registered with the Entire CLI the same way, using\\n`entire plugin install <path>`, which links a local `entire-<name>` executable\\ninto Entire's managed plugin directory. You do not have to fetch or arrange the\\nprovider yourself -- `scripts/install.sh` does both components in one pass.\\n\\n### One command\\n\\n```sh\\ngit clone https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\\nentire-brain/scripts/install.sh\\n```\\n\\nThis is the whole install: it checks the prerequisites listed above and reports\\nevery missing one at once, resolves `entire-graph` (using `$ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR`,\\nthen any checkout already on this machine, then a shallow clone of the public\\nrepository into `${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/entire-brain/entire-graph`), builds\\nand registers both plugins, writes the default plugin configuration\\n(`entire brain config init`), and runs `entire brain doctor`. It prints which of\\nthe three provider routes it took. Neither checkout has to have a particular\\nname, and they do not have to be siblings.\\n\\nThe prerequisites do not go away: Go 1.27 or newer and a cgo-capable C compiler\\nare still required, because both components are built from source here. What\\ngoes away is having to arrange directories before you start.\\n\\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` is the same flow for a machine with nothing on it: it\\nclones `entire-brain` when it is not already present and then runs\\n`scripts/install.sh`. There is no `curl | sh` form, because\\n`entireio/entire-brain` is a private repository -- `raw.githubusercontent.com`\\nreturns 404 for it without a token -- and because installing means building from\\nsource, so the clone has to happen either way.\\n\\n### Versioned install status\\n\\nThere is no versioned install today. `entire-brain` is tagged -- `v0.3.0` is the\\ncurrent release -- but a tag here is release notes and nothing else: the releases\\ncarry **no binaries and no source archive**, so there is nothing to download and\\nrun. The `v0.1.0` tags additionally predate the Graph rename (the provider tag\\nbuilds `entire-sem` and the brain tag invokes `entire sem`), so do not combine\\nthem with the `entire graph` commands in this guide.\\n\\nA versioned `go install` path is not available either, and will not be until\\nmatching Graph-based release tags exist in both repositories. This section will\\ngain copy-paste commands when they do. Until then the source install above --\\n`scripts/install.sh` against a checkout -- is the only way to install this, which\\nis why the Go toolchain is a hard requirement rather than a conditional one.\\n\\n`entire plugin install` currently supports local executable paths only. It does\\nnot fetch from a git URL or a GitHub release, and it does not auto-install the\\nprovider dependency.\\n\\n### Managing the provider checkout yourself\\n\\nThe one command above is the source install: until matching Graph-based tags are\\navailable, both components are built from their current `main` branches, which\\ntracks development and is not a reproducible versioned install. Use matching\\nrelease tags once they are published.\\n\\nIf you would rather own the `entire-graph` checkout -- to pin a commit, or to\\nwork on the provider -- clone it too and run the same installer:\\n\\n```sh\\ngit clone --branch main https://github.com/entireio/entire-graph.git\\ngit clone --branch main https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git\\ncd entire-brain\\nscripts/install.sh\\n```\\n\\nA side-by-side checkout is discovered with no configuration, and so are the usual\\nsource directories under `$HOME`. Set\\n`ENTIRE_GRAPH_DIR=/path/to/entire-graph scripts/install.sh` only when your\\ncheckout is somewhere the installer would not look, or when you want to pin one\\nspecific checkout; an override that does not hold an `entire-graph` checkout is\\nan error rather than a silent fallback. `ENTIRE_INSTALL_OFFLINE=1` stops the\\ninstaller reaching the network at all, so a machine without egress fails with an\\nexplanation instead of hanging on a clone.\\n\\nTo build and install only the brain from a checkout, run `scripts/install-local.sh`\\n(equivalently `mise run install`). You still need `entire graph` installed\\nseparately for the semantic layer.\\n\\n### Release archive (forthcoming)\\n\\nPrebuilt per-OS/arch archives are the planned packaged distribution channel, but\\nthey have not been published yet. Until they appear on the\\n[GitHub Releases](https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/releases) page, use\\nthe pre-release source installer above.\\n\\nEach published archive will contain the plugin binary plus `README.md`, `LICENSE`,\\nand `entire-plugin.yml`, alongside a `SHA256SUMS` file. After downloading and\\nverifying the checksum, extract and install the binary:\\n\\n```sh\\nVERSION=vX.Y.Z # use the matching version published by both repositories\\ntar -xzf \\\"entire-brain-${VERSION}-darwin-arm64.tar.gz\\\"\\nentire plugin install \\\"./entire-brain-${VERSION}-darwin-arm64/entire-brain\\\" --force\\n```\\n\\nWhen provider archives are published, install the matching `entire-graph` archive\\nthe same way so `entire graph` is available.\\n\\n## Verify\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain version          # plugin version\\nentire brain doctor           # checks the Entire CLI plugin environment\\nentire brain status           # summarizes the brain (empty until you run `entire brain setup` in a repo)\\n\\nentire graph version            # provider version\\nentire graph doctor --json      # provider diagnostics; expect \\\"no_egress\\\": true\\n```\\n\\n`entire brain doctor` reports whether the plugin directories and the semantic\\nprovider are wired up. If it says the provider is missing, confirm `entire graph`\\nresolves and that the installed binary is named `entire-graph` rather than the\\nretired `entire-sem`. For a versioned release, also confirm the brain and provider\\nreport the same published release version.\\n\\nThat is the machine set up. No repository has a brain yet — the next section is\\nthe command that gives one to a repository you actually work in.\\n\\n## Onboard a repository: `entire brain setup`\\n\\nInstalling put two plugins on this machine. It gave no repository a brain. This\\nis the step that does, and it is where a first-time reader should go next:\\n\\n```sh\\ncd /path/to/your/repo\\nentire brain setup\\n```\\n\\nOne command, three phases:\\n\\n| phase | blocking? | spends tokens? | installed by default? |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 1. **instant core** — sessions, semantic index, seed, docs, history, entities | yes, seconds | **no**, deterministic | — |\\n| 2. **fact backfill** — distill past sessions into durable facts, newest first, detached | no | **yes** | yes, unless `--no-backfill` |\\n| 3. **watcher daemon** — one machine-wide launchd agent / systemd user unit | no | **yes**, per window | **yes, with no prompt**, unless `--no-daemon` |\\n\\nThree things are worth being explicit about before you run it:\\n\\n- **Phase 2 spends tokens.** It distills your past sessions with an agent CLI,\\n  capped at 25 sessions per pass (`--backfill-budget`). `--no-backfill` skips it.\\n- **Phase 3 installs a persistent background service, by default, without\\n  asking.** launchd with `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive` on macOS, a systemd user unit\\n  with `Restart=always` on Linux. It survives logout and reboot, and its gated\\n  distill step calls an agent, so it is a recurring spend. `--no-daemon` skips\\n  it; `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon` removes one you already have.\\n\\n  You are told before it happens rather than after. A run that will register a\\n  watcher opens with the pre-flight line, ahead of any work:\\n\\n  ```\\n  setup: background watcher: this run will install io.entire.brain-watch.<id> at\\n  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.entire.brain-watch.<id>.plist — a persistent launchd\\n  service that starts again at every login; pass --no-daemon to skip it, or\\n  remove it later with `entire brain setup --uninstall-daemon`\\n  ```\\n\\n  A re-run that finds the watcher already installed says it *keeps* it instead,\\n  and `--no-daemon` prints nothing about a service it will not install.\\n- **`entire brain setup --no-backfill --no-daemon` spends nothing at all** and\\n  changes no service — it is phase 1 only.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain setup --no-backfill --no-daemon   # deterministic build only, zero spend\\nentire brain status                            # what was built, backfill progress, daemon health\\nentire brain setup --uninstall-daemon          # stop and remove the watcher\\n```\\n\\n### What `setup` needs first, and what it does not\\n\\n`setup` hard-fails on one thing only: the target must be a **local path that is a\\ngit repository** with at least one commit. It checks that before it registers a\\nworkspace, writes state, or installs anything.\\n\\nIt does **not** require, and will not do for you:\\n\\n- **`entire enable` in the repository.** Hook registration belongs to the Entire\\n  CLI's repository enablement, which owns the whole lifecycle hook set;\\n  duplicating it in `setup` would leave two hooks racing to distill the same\\n  session. `setup` reports the session-end hook as an advisory line, never a\\n  failure.\\n- **Captured Entire sessions.** A repository Entire has never captured a\\n  session in still builds, and the `sessions` component is green: the export\\n  returns an empty inventory, which is a true answer. You get a brain from\\n  code, docs and git history rather than from your past agent work — which is\\n  the one source it is most interesting for, so this is worth fixing, just not\\n  by a failure. The export only *fails* when Entire cannot prove the inventory\\n  is empty rather than merely unreadable — a configured checkpoint remote it\\n  cannot enumerate, offline or unauthenticated — and the hint then says to work\\n  a session and re-run, and points at `entire checkpoint list` for what Entire\\n  itself can see.\\n- **A working `entire graph` provider**, a supported service manager, or an\\n  agent CLI — each missing piece degrades exactly its own phase.\\n\\nSo the order that produces the best brain, rather than merely a successful\\ncommand, is:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire enable                              # capture sessions + wire the session-end hook\\ngit add .entire .claude && git commit      # enable wrote these and did not commit them\\nentire brain setup                         # then onboard: build, backfill, watch\\n```\\n\\nThat order is a quality decision, not a correctness one — running `entire enable`\\nafter `setup` works too, and the next backfill pass picks the new sessions up.\\n\\n**The commit in the middle is not optional.** `entire enable` writes\\n`.entire/settings.json` and your agent's hook settings (`.claude/settings.json`\\nfor Claude Code) and leaves them uncommitted, so a `setup` run immediately\\nafterwards finds a dirty worktree and refuses to seed or index it — two red `x`\\non a first run, caused by the command directly above it:\\n\\n```\\n  instant     + sessions  x seed  + history  + docs  x semantic  + patterns  + entities  + memory\\n              x seed baseline: dirty_worktree: refusing to seed uncommitted content without --worktree\\n                hint: commit or stash the working tree, then re-run `entire brain setup`; `entire enable` writes .entire/ and .claude/ without committing them, which is what a first run usually trips over\\n```\\n\\nBoth files are project configuration that belongs in the repository anyway, so\\ncommitting them is the real fix rather than a workaround. (`--worktree`, which\\nthe refusal names, is a `refresh` flag — `setup` does not accept it.)\\n\\n**A repository new to Entire reaches an all-green `setup` with no user action.**\\nOn a committed `git init` repository with no remote at all, every component\\nbuilds:\\n\\n```\\nBrain ready in 3.6s\\n  repo        /path/to/your/repo  (local/your-repo-686eadf04b0f)\\n  brain       ~/.local/share/entire/plugins/data/brain/repos/local/your-repo-686eadf04b0f\\n  instant     + sessions  + seed  + history  + docs  + semantic  + patterns  + entities  + memory  (3.6s)\\n  facts       .............. 0/0 sessions distilled\\n  backfill    - skipped (--no-backfill)  (0s)\\n  workspace   + default  (20ms)\\n  daemon      - skipped: --no-daemon  (0s)\\n```\\n\\n`x semantic index: repo key mismatch` used to be the guaranteed second line\\nhere, blamed on the repository having **no git remote**. It was wrong in both\\ndirections — it also fired on every non-GitHub origin, because `entire-brain`\\nand `entire-graph` spelled the same repository differently — and it is fixed:\\nthe brain now accepts the provider's own spelling of the repository it just\\nasked the provider to index. A mismatch that still appears means the snapshot\\nreally does describe another repository, in practice one the provider cached\\nbefore this repo's origin changed, and the hint says to rebuild it with\\n`entire brain refresh index --force`.\\n\\n`setup` still exits 0 whenever at least one component built, and still names the\\nones that did not, so `Brain ready (degraded)` is information rather than\\nfailure when you do see it. The things that legitimately produce it:\\n\\n- **an uncommitted worktree** — `x seed` and `x semantic`, almost always the\\n  `entire enable` output above, fixed by committing it;\\n- **no `entire graph` provider** — `x semantic index:\\n  provider_doctor_failed: ...`, fixed by `scripts/install.sh`;\\n- **an unreadable checkpoint inventory** — `x session export`, when Entire\\n  cannot reach the checkpoint remote to prove there is nothing to export.\\n\\n### Or build the brain by hand: `refresh`\\n\\n`refresh` is the same deterministic build on its own — no backfill, no workspace,\\nno watcher. Use it when you want the brain and nothing else, or when scripting:\\n\\n```sh\\ncd /path/to/your/repo\\n\\nentire brain refresh --agent none\\nentire brain status\\n```\\n\\n`refresh` exports captured sessions, builds the local history and doc indexes,\\nasks `entire graph` for a semantic snapshot, and stores the derived brain under\\nEntire's plugin data directory. `--agent none` keeps this build deterministic and\\ntoken-free, with no hosted-model calls.\\n\\nThe semantic snapshot is built from committed state, so run `refresh` on a clean\\ncheckout. On a dirty worktree the semantic step can be skipped or refused; index\\nuncommitted state deliberately with `entire brain refresh index --worktree`.\\nBundle export rejects worktree-backed semantic indexes.\\n\\n`entire brain status` then reports the brain's sources, durable-fact readiness,\\nand semantic coverage, freshness, and blind spots. Add `--json` for a\\nmachine-readable report.\\n\\n### If you do not have the semantic provider yet\\n\\n`refresh` runs the semantic step by default, and that step fails if `entire graph`\\ncannot be verified. To build a brain from sessions, history, docs, and facts\\nwithout the provider, disable the semantic step:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain refresh --agent none --semantic=false\\n```\\n\\nInstall the Graph-based `entire-graph` from source (or from a matching release tag\\nonce available), then re-run `entire brain refresh --agent none` to add the\\nsemantic code graph.\\n\\n### Exploring a repository you don't have locally\\n\\n`entire brain add <repo-url>` clones the repository, fetches its Entire\\ncheckpoint history, and runs the build above in one step; afterward explore\\nthe clone with `entire brain dash`, `status`, or `search`.\\n\\n### Query the brain\\n\\nEvery result carries an `id` you can fetch in full with `get`.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain overview                          # what the project is: stack, commands, recent decisions\\nentire brain brief \\\"add rate limiting to the API\\\"   # a bounded, task-shaped context packet\\nentire brain query \\\"how does checkpointing work\\\"    # hybrid lexical + vector search across the brain\\nentire brain query --keyword \\\"checkpoint\\\"               # exact keyword search\\nentire brain recall \\\"why did we pick this default\\\"  # durable facts for the current branch\\nentire brain get fact:<id>                     # fetch one item in full\\n```\\n\\nAdd `--json` to any of these for machine-readable output. `entire brain agent-guide`\\nprints the recommended command set for a coding agent.\\n\\n### Optional: distill durable facts\\n\\nDistillation is an opt-in agent step that turns captured sessions into durable\\nproject knowledge (decisions, constraints, preferences, gotchas, conventions).\\nIt sends redacted transcript chunks to the selected agent, so it spends tokens\\nand performs network egress unless you point it at a local loopback agent. Always\\nestimate first.\\n\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","create_time":1789839838.128299}}}
