{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:46:38.174Z","ordinal":7725,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0cb48b2b18d09e75016aaeca7d455c87d29f15b49cff36d21d","status":"completed","call_id":"call_bubRYwXIZpQGBnXmrCB1lv3b","name":"exec","input":"text(await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '401,972p' docs/getting-started.md\",\"max_output_tokens\":11000}));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","create_time":1789839993.407218}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:46:38.261Z","ordinal":7726,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-6abeea5a-a68b-4d83-ac8a-3575fc83c1fc","process_id":"6595","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '401,972p' docs/getting-started.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/thomi/.codex/worktrees/5d33/entire-brain","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '401,972p' docs/getting-started.md","name":"getting-started.md","path":"docs/getting-started.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"```sh\nentire brain distill --dry-run --json                                  # estimate work; no agent call\nentire brain distill --agent codex --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low   # extract facts\nentire brain facts status                                              # review readiness\n```\n\nDistillation is incremental and cached: unchanged sessions are skipped, and\nlow-confidence merges are queued for `entire brain facts review`.\n\n### Use the brain from an agent\n\nFor agents that speak MCP, register the brain as a local stdio MCP server. It\nopens no network listener.\n\n```sh\nentire brain mcp\n```\n\nIf you ran `entire brain setup` without `--no-daemon`, a watcher is already\ninstalled and keeping this repository fresh; the commands below are the manual\nequivalents, for a brain built with `refresh` or a repository set up with\n`--no-daemon`.\n\nThe default watcher performs deterministic refreshes only. Token-spending work,\nsuch as fact distillation or seed synthesis, is opt-in and separately gated.\n\nTo keep the durable-facts layer fresh as new sessions land, enable distillation\nexplicitly:\n\n```sh\nentire brain watch --distill --distill-every 24h --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low --budget 1\n```\n\nIf you also want generated seed summaries, run refresh with an agent instead of\nthe deterministic seed path:\n\n```sh\nentire brain refresh --agent auto --seed-model gpt-5.4-mini --seed-effort low\n```\n\nSeed synthesis is separate from fact distillation: seed files orient an agent on\nthe repository, while durable facts are branch-scoped retained knowledge.\n\n## How Agents Use The Brain\n\nAgents rely on a capture layer plus four consumption surfaces. The capture\nlayer produces the raw material; the consumption surfaces are how an agent\nactually queries or receives brain context. Which surface matters depends on the\nagent client and how the repository is configured.\n\n### Capture Layer: Entire Hooks Produce The Raw Material\n\n`entire-cli` installs lifecycle hooks for agents such as Claude Code, Codex,\nGemini CLI, OpenCode, Cursor, Factory AI Droid, Copilot CLI, and Pi. Those\nhooks start and stop sessions, capture prompts and transcripts, track changed\nfiles, record subagent work where the agent exposes it, and attach checkpoint\nmetadata to commits.\n\nThis capture path is background infrastructure. The coding agent does not need\nto remember to save a transcript, and the human does not need to paste context\ninto the brain. `entire brain refresh` later ingests the retained sessions and\ncheckpoint history that the hooks produced.\n\nIf an agent is not seeing Entire context at all, first check the base Entire\nintegration, not the brain:\n\n```sh\nentire status\nentire agent\n```\n\n### 1. MCP Is The Preferred Agent Tool Surface\n\nFor agents that support MCP, register `entire brain mcp` as a local stdio MCP\nserver. The server opens no network listener and wraps the local brain's JSON\ncontracts as tools.\n\nOnce MCP is available, an agent should call MCP tools instead of shelling out to\nthe `entire brain` CLI. The normal first call is `brain_brief` for the task.\nThen the agent should make only targeted follow-up calls:\n\n- `brain_brief` for task-shaped context, history hits, likely files, and tests\n- `brain_status` to check freshness, coverage, and blind spots\n- retrieval tools such as `brain_query` and `brain_get` for facts, docs, and\n  history\n- semantic tools such as `brain_code`, `brain_context`, `brain_impact`, and\n  `brain_tests` for code navigation and validation planning\n- `brain_entity_history` for \"which checkpoints and sessions changed this\n  function/class\", answered from the persisted entity index rather than by\n  re-reading history (build it once with `entire brain entities backfill`)\n- review, workspace, and pattern tools such as `brain_regressions`,\n  `brain_workspace_graph`, `brain_workspace_review`, and `brain_patterns` when\n  the task calls for them\n\n`brain_brief` keeps its existing JSON-in-text response by default. Clients\nexperimenting with a smaller agent packet can pass\n`packet_format: \"compact_v1\"`, `packet_format: \"compact_v2\"`, or\n`packet_format: \"compact_v3\"`; these are opt-in serialization changes over the\nsame ranked report, not different retrieval treatments. In v2 and v3's hashed\nin-band legend, `~` means absent and `^` means the previous value in the same\nopcode and column, across interleaved records. V2 limits `^` to metadata\ncolumns; v3 permits it for every exact repeated value. V3 also uses the compact\n`entire.brain_brief c3` marker and an unpadded base64url SHA-256 footer. Both\nformats use positional rows for a repeated family only when its complete\ncanonical encoding is strictly smaller than keyed rows; ties remain keyed. Use\n`legacy_json` (or omit the argument) when a consumer depends on the existing\nJSON contract.\n\nSome MCP tools write local state. For example, `brain_ingest_traces` persists\nruntime edges as `RUNTIME_TRACE` facts. Pattern MCP tools are read-only; skill\nformation remains a CLI-only write path.\n\nThe benchmark harness treats this as the cleanest agent contract: MCP-backed\nagents call named `brain_*` tools, and in MCP conditions they are explicitly\nkept away from direct `entire brain` shell commands or prewritten history\nexcerpt files. See [Semantic MCP Guide](semantic_mcp_guide.md) for the full tool\nsurface.\n\nThe MCP and CLI surfaces are intentionally close, but they are not perfect\nmirrors. Some CLI-only flows, such as `recall --expand`, have no dedicated MCP\ntool. Some MCP tools, such as `brain_review`, wrap machine contracts that are\nhidden or discouraged as direct human CLI verbs. Prefer the native surface your\nagent client has, then translate only when needed.\n\n### 2. Intake Instructions Are The File-Based Fallback\n\nWhen MCP is not available, an agent can still use the brain through the intake\ninstructions in `templates/`. The Codex and Claude templates tell the agent to\nlocate the brain with:\n\n```sh\nentire brain path \"$PWD\"\n```\n\nThen the agent reads staged context instead of blindly opening everything:\n\n- `README.md` and `manifest.json` in the brain directory\n- seed files such as `seed/agent/quick-overview.md`,\n  `seed/agent/overview.md`, `seed/agent/architecture.md`,\n  `seed/agent/risks.md`, `seed/agent/maintenance-guide.md`, and\n  `seed/agent/open-questions.md`\n- `seed/history-gaps.md`, when present\n- only task-relevant transcripts or records selected from the manifest\n\nThis is how an agent like Codex can use `entire-brain` without a dedicated MCP\nconnection: resolve the local brain, read the manifest and seed context, inspect\nonly the relevant retained history, and treat gaps as uncertainty rather than\ninventing rationale.\n\n### 3. Direct CLI Is A Compatibility Surface\n\nFor agent clients without MCP, direct `entire brain ... --json` calls are the\ncompatibility surface. Humans and scripts can use the same commands. Agents\nusing this surface should treat it like a tool API, not as an invitation to run\na long command tour.\n\nA disciplined direct-CLI intake is:\n\n```sh\nentire brain status --json\nentire brain brief \"<task>\" --json\n```\n\nThen ask the smallest next question. Examples:\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"<decision or prior-work question>\" --json\nentire brain inspect code \"<symbol or concept>\" --json\nentire brain inspect context \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect tests \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect regressions \"<task or invariant>\" --location-only --json\n```\n\nAgents should prefer `query` for broad facts/history/docs, `search` for exact\nterms, semantic `inspect` subcommands for code graph questions, and `get` or\n`multi-get` when a prior result returned an id. They should not broaden into\nrepo-wide text search until the brain's targeted context has been used.\n\n### 4. Hidden Hooks Deliver Context At The Moment Of Relevance\n\n`entire-brain` also has a hidden hook surface for harness integrations. These\nhooks are not installed by the base Entire repository enablement flow; a harness\nmust be configured to invoke them, for example from Claude Code\n`PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse` hooks or Entire CLI lifecycle hooks. Until then they\nhave no effect. They are not intended as human-facing commands, although they\ncan be hand-tested when\ndebugging harness wiring. They are designed to surface a small, high-confidence\nfact exactly when it matters:\n\n- before editing a file, facts anchored to that file\n- after a failed command, gotchas and known dead ends that match the failure\n- after a session ends, optional one-session refresh and distillation\n\nThe contract is intentionally quiet: if there is no relevant fact, no local\nbrain, or an environment problem, the hook exits successfully with no output on\nstdout. Diagnostic one-liners may still go to stderr. That keeps hooks from\nbreaking the agent workflow or flooding the transcript. Session-end distillation\nis still an explicit token-spending path: it skips when no agent is available\nand must respect no-egress/local-only policy.\n\n## Usage Scenarios\n\nThe examples below name the preferred MCP tool when there is one. Agent clients\nwithout MCP should use the equivalent `entire brain ... --json` command from\nthe direct-CLI surface above.\n\n### Orient An Agent On A New Task\n\nThe agent should start by asking for task-shaped context, not a generic project\nsummary. The normal tool is `brain_brief`. The useful output is the freshness\nstate, likely files and symbols, relevant history records, durable facts, likely\ntests, and any blind spots.\n\nBegin substantive orientation with a brief unless equivalent context is already\navailable. Reuse useful locations without a redundant Graph query. Inspect source\ndirectly when locations are sufficient, including small edits and follow-ups. Do\nnot automatically refresh or repair tools to answer an ordinary query. See\n[coordinated agent instructions](agent-coordination.md).\n\nFor repo-level orientation rather than task-specific orientation, use\n`overview`. It returns a compact project map: stack signals, entrypoints,\ncommands, key documents, and recent decisions.\n\n```sh\nentire brain overview --json\n```\n\nFor interactive human exploration instead of scripted queries, `entire brain\ndash` opens a terminal dashboard over facts, sessions, history, and semantic\nrecords, and `entire brain viz` opens a local, no-egress browser view of the\nsemantic call graph. Both are read-only and make no agent calls.\n\n### Keep The Brain Fresh\n\nFreshness is part of every answer. A stale semantic index, missing provider,\ndirty-worktree mismatch, or parser blind spot changes how much an agent should\ntrust the brain.\n\nAgents should check freshness through `brain_status` or `status --json` before\nrelying on semantic answers. If freshness is unsafe, an agent with write\npermission can request a refresh; otherwise it should report the limitation and\nfall back to direct repository inspection.\n\nHumans or automation should keep the brain fresh:\n\n```sh\nentire brain status\nentire brain refresh --agent none\nentire brain watch\n```\n\nWhen semantic artifacts are out of sync or damaged, use the semantic\nmaintenance paths instead of deleting the whole brain:\n\n```sh\nentire brain refresh index\nentire brain repair\nentire brain reset --semantic-only --force\n```\n\nUse `refresh index --worktree` only when you intentionally want uncommitted\nstate in the semantic index. Exported bundles reject worktree-backed semantic\nindexes.\n\n### Continue Or Explain Prior Work\n\nWhen the question is \"why is this like this?\", \"what did the previous agent\ntry?\", or \"where did this session leave off?\", the agent should search the\nhistory and facts layers. The normal retrieval tool is `brain_query`,\nfollowed by `brain_get` for specific ids.\n\nThis is the main reason Entire capture matters: the original prompt, attempts,\nvalidation, correction, and rationale can survive the code diff and become\navailable to the next agent.\n\n### Ask Across Facts, History, And Docs\n\nWhen the question is not tied to one symbol, use the unified retrieval layer.\n`query` is the normal hybrid path over durable facts, indexed history, and docs.\nUse `query --keyword` for exact keywords, `query --semantic` for semantic matches, and\n`get`/`multi-get` when a result returns an id worth reading in full.\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"how does checkpointing work\" --json\nentire brain query --keyword \"checkpoint\" --json\nentire brain query --semantic \"preventing data races\" --json\nentire brain get fact:<id> --json\n```\n\nUse `entire brain query \"text\"` for default hybrid retrieval. Select keyword\nmatching with `--keyword` or semantic matching with `--semantic`; the two\nflags are mutually exclusive and belong only to the query command.\nText may instead be supplied as `--query \"text\"`. Flags work before or after\npositional text; combining positional text and `--query` is an error.\nFor example: `entire brain query --keyword --query \"RetryPolicy\" --json`.\n\nWorkspace retrieval supports the same forms:\n`entire brain workspace query <workspace> --semantic --query \"retry policy\"`.\nThe old `search` and `vsearch` commands remain hidden compatibility aliases.\nMCP agents should use `brain_query` with optional, mutually exclusive\n`keyword: true` or `semantic: true` arguments; `brain_search` and\n`brain_vsearch` remain compatibility tools.\n\nFor durable facts specifically, use `recall`. `recall --expand` is an\nagent-assisted query expansion path, so it belongs behind the same egress\njudgment as other agent calls.\n\n```sh\nentire brain recall \"account deletion\" --k 5\nentire brain recall \"MirrorCommittedMetadataRef\" --expand\n```\n\n### Navigate Code By Meaning\n\nWhen a task depends on structure rather than text, the agent should use semantic\ntools. The normal path is: find candidate symbols, inspect relation-aware\ncontext, inspect likely impact, then ask for tests. The normal tools are\n`brain_code` or `brain_search_code`, followed by `brain_context`,\n`brain_impact`, and `brain_tests`.\n\nThis is where `entire-graph` matters. It is not an agent memory layer; it is the\nlocal parser/provider that gives `entire-brain` the graph the agent queries.\nSemantic depth is language-dependent: parser-backed extraction covers the\nsemantic language set, while many recognized filetypes are inventory-only. For\ninventory-only files, prefer text retrieval and lower confidence in\nimpact/context answers.\n\nFor deeper graph work, use the graph specialists: schema inventory, local graph\nUI, source snippets, directed trace paths, dead-code candidates, boundary\nenumeration, and working-tree change mapping.\n\n```sh\nentire brain inspect graph-schema --json\nentire brain inspect graph-ui semantic-graph.html\nentire brain inspect snippet \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect trace-path \"<caller>\" \"<callee>\" --json\nentire brain inspect dead-code --json\nentire brain inspect boundaries --kind tool --json\nentire brain inspect changes --json\n```\n\n### Review Risk Without A Clean Diff\n\nFor long-running sessions, manual edits, or suspected regressions from memory,\nthe agent should use diff-less regression review. The normal tools are\n`brain_regressions` or `brain_review`.\n\nWhen the goal is a fair investigation, prefer location-only output first. It\npoints the agent to suspected files and lines without handing it the expected\nanswer text.\n\n### Connect Runtime Evidence To Source\n\nWhen static structure is not enough, import runtime trace edges with\n`brain_ingest_traces` or `inspect ingest-traces`. The brain compares the runtime\nedges with known static relations, persists them as `RUNTIME_TRACE` graph facts,\nand makes them available to graph schema, trace-path, and brief/context flows.\n\nThis is useful for incidents and performance/debugging tasks where \"what\nactually happened?\" matters more than \"what could call this?\"\n\n### Work Across Multiple Repositories\n\nUse workspaces when the behavior crosses repository boundaries: a frontend calls\nan API, a service emits an event another service consumes, one repo imports a\npackage from another, or deployment/config resources connect separate projects.\n\nHumans create and refresh the workspace. Agents consume workspace context\nthrough MCP workspace tools where available, or through direct workspace CLI\ncommands when MCP does not expose the required traversal. The workspace brain\nkeeps member repos local and builds cross-repo contracts and graph edges from\ntheir existing local brains.\n\n```sh\nentire brain workspace create platform\nentire brain workspace add platform ../api --name api\nentire brain workspace add platform ../web --name web\nentire brain workspace refresh platform --full\n```\n\nAfter the workspace exists, use workspace context, graph, impact, retrieval,\nand review flows when the question crosses repos:\n\n```sh\nentire brain workspace inspect context platform \"checkout\" --json\nentire brain workspace inspect impact platform \"checkout\" --json\nentire brain workspace inspect graph platform --json\nentire brain workspace query platform \"checkout\" --json\nentire brain workspace review platform \"checkout regression\" --json\nentire brain workspace watch platform --once\n```\n\n### Preserve Durable Project Knowledge\n\nDurable facts are short, provenance-anchored statements about decisions,\nconstraints, preferences, pitfalls, and standing rules. Humans can write them\ndirectly when they know the rule. Agents can distill candidate facts from\ncaptured sessions when explicitly asked or when a session-end integration is\nconfigured to do so.\n\n```sh\nentire brain remember \"Prefer table-driven tests for parser edge cases\" --path preferences.tests\nentire brain distill --dry-run --json\nentire brain distill --agent codex --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low\nentire brain facts tree --depth 1\n```\n\nDistillation is the token-spending path. It is incremental and cached, but it\nstill sends redacted transcript chunks to the selected agent unless you choose a\nlocal loopback agent such as Ollama or use dry-run/no-egress mode.\n\nDurable facts also have a lifecycle. Review queued merge/supersede proposals,\npromote branch facts when they should carry forward, retract facts that are no\nlonger true, and garbage-collect stale retractions.\n\n```sh\nentire brain facts review\nentire brain facts promote --from <branch> --strategy keep-both\nentire brain facts retract <fact-id>\nentire brain facts gc --force\nentire brain inspect blame <fact-id> --json\n```\n\n### Extract Reusable Agent Skills\n\nSkill extraction is not \"turn repeated commands into a checklist.\" The current\npipeline forms skills only from accepted deep dossiers: verified evidence that a\npattern is non-obvious and useful to future agents.\n\nThere are several evidence paths:\n\n- recurring task patterns that pass deep verification\n- verified themes that capture latent practices by meaning\n- corrected or failed episodes grouped into lessons\n- durable gotchas, conventions, and invariants grouped into capabilities\n- workspace families where equivalent procedures differ across repos\n\nA human or explicitly authorized automation runs the egress-gated verification\nand formation commands. The agent performs the verification and `SKILL.md`\nsynthesis under that explicit request. The verifier is cached. Refresh, watch,\nbrief, query, MCP reads, and workspace reads do not silently call it.\n\n```sh\nentire brain patterns\nentire brain patterns verify --deep\nentire brain patterns verify --themes\nentire brain patterns verify --lessons\nentire brain patterns verify --conventions\nentire brain patterns skills\nentire brain patterns skills form <id>\nentire brain patterns skills form <id> --yes\n```\n\n`patterns skills` lists formable proposals, not raw pattern rows. `form <id>`\npreviews the evidence, generated `SKILL.md`, and destination paths without\nwriting. If no accepted deep dossier exists yet, `form` refuses and tells you to\nrun verification first; it never falls back to shallow evidence. `--yes` writes\nthe skill; existing files require `--force`.\n\nBy default, generated skills target the cross-agent `standard` destination\n(`.agents/skills/`). Use `--target claude-code|codex|factoryai-droid|all` only\nwhen an agent-specific destination is needed.\n\nWorkspaces mirror the same flow for cross-repo families:\n\n```sh\nentire brain workspace patterns verify <workspace>\nentire brain workspace patterns skills <workspace>\nentire brain workspace patterns skills form <workspace> <id>\n```\n\nPattern inspection is useful even when you are not forming skills. Use it to see\nrecurring tasks, practices, risks, themes, verifier state, and whether accepted\nskills are stale.\n\n```sh\nentire brain patterns\nentire brain patterns status\nentire brain workspace patterns <workspace>\nentire brain workspace patterns status <workspace>\n```\n\n### Measure Brain Quality\n\nThe brain includes evaluation harnesses for maintainers who need evidence that a\nretriever or fact layer is actually helping. These are not required for everyday\nuse, but they are the right surface before claiming quality improvements.\n\n```sh\nentire brain facts eval-gen > facts-tasks.json\nentire brain facts eval --tasks facts-tasks.json --retriever facts --json > facts-eval.json\nentire brain facts eval-compare --a <before.json> --b <after.json>\nentire brain history-eval-gen > history-tasks.json\nentire brain history-eval --tasks history-tasks.json --json\n```\n\nFor semantic performance and provider output, use the semantic benchmark and\nstatus audit instead of relying on anecdotes:\n\n```sh\nentire brain bench semantic .\nentire brain status --json\n```\n\n### Tune Retrieval\n\nThe default vector arm uses the bundled local Model2Vec embedder. For higher\nsemantic recall, opt into a local transformer embedder served by Ollama or a\ncompatible loopback endpoint:\n\n```sh\nollama pull embeddinggemma\nENTIRE_BRAIN_EMBEDDER=ollama entire brain query \"preventing data races\" --json\n```\n\nKeep this distinction clear: changing the embedder changes retrieval behavior,\nnot the underlying source of truth. Facts, history, docs, and semantic records\nstill come from the local brain.\n\n## Privacy And Egress\n\nThe default brain artifacts are local and inspectable. Deterministic refresh,\nsemantic indexing, local history indexing, and MCP tool calls do not require a\nhosted model. The MCP adapter is stdio-only.\n\nSome operations perform network egress, either by sending selected context to a\nmodel or by fetching over the network:\n\n- a configured `checkpoint_remote` lets `refresh` and its `sessions` export\n  stage fetch checkpoint history from that remote into a throwaway temp repo;\n  this is network egress, not a hosted-model call, and is skipped under\n  no-egress mode\n- seed synthesis during `refresh --agent auto`\n- fact distillation with `distill --agent ...`\n- query expansion with `recall --expand`\n- pattern verification and skill synthesis\n- judged evaluation commands\n- `publish` uploads the serialized local brain to hosted Entire; opt-in and off\n  by default, requiring both the command and `ENTIRE_BRAIN_ALLOW_HOSTED=1`\n\nUse `--agent none`, `--dry-run`, local loopback Ollama, or\n`ENTIRE_BRAIN_NO_EGRESS=1` / `ENTIRE_BRAIN_LOCAL_ONLY=1` when the repo must stay\nstrictly local. No-egress mode enforces locality for no-agent, dry-run, and\nloopback-Ollama paths by validating URLs, redirects, and resolved dial targets.\nA custom `--agent command` runner is treated as a trusted local command and is\nnot enforceably loopback-only; no-egress mode cannot stop that runner from\nmaking its own network calls.\n\nRemember that base Entire session capture stores transcripts and metadata on\nthe repository's `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch. Anyone with access to that\nbranch can read captured prompts, tool activity, and retained transcript data.\nEntire redacts detected secrets before writing checkpoint metadata, but\nredaction is best-effort. Redaction covers transcript and checkpoint metadata,\nnot every local working artifact. Entire also writes temporary shadow branches\nsuch as `entire/<short-hash>` whose code-file snapshots are raw, unredacted\nblobs of the working tree. Gitignored files are filtered out only as a partial\ndefense. Entire does not push shadow branches; do not push them manually, or\nunredacted source could reach the remote. Review the Entire CLI security and\nprivacy guide before enabling Entire on sensitive or public repositories.\n\n## Storage And Configuration\n\nEntire CLI supplies the plugin directories that make the brain durable:\n\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_CONFIG_DIR` for `brain.json`\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` for generated repo brains and workspaces\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_STATE_DIR` for regenerable cursors such as watcher state\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR` for caches\n- `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` for the current checkout when invoked inside a repo\n\nRepo keys are derived from the repository origin. For example,\n`github.com/entireio/cli` becomes `gh/entireio/cli`.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"```sh\nentire brain distill --dry-run --json                                  # estimate work; no agent call\nentire brain distill --agent codex --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low   # extract facts\nentire brain facts status                                              # review readiness\n```\n\nDistillation is incremental and cached: unchanged sessions are skipped, and\nlow-confidence merges are queued for `entire brain facts review`.\n\n### Use the brain from an agent\n\nFor agents that speak MCP, register the brain as a local stdio MCP server. It\nopens no network listener.\n\n```sh\nentire brain mcp\n```\n\nIf you ran `entire brain setup` without `--no-daemon`, a watcher is already\ninstalled and keeping this repository fresh; the commands below are the manual\nequivalents, for a brain built with `refresh` or a repository set up with\n`--no-daemon`.\n\nThe default watcher performs deterministic refreshes only. Token-spending work,\nsuch as fact distillation or seed synthesis, is opt-in and separately gated.\n\nTo keep the durable-facts layer fresh as new sessions land, enable distillation\nexplicitly:\n\n```sh\nentire brain watch --distill --distill-every 24h --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low --budget 1\n```\n\nIf you also want generated seed summaries, run refresh with an agent instead of\nthe deterministic seed path:\n\n```sh\nentire brain refresh --agent auto --seed-model gpt-5.4-mini --seed-effort low\n```\n\nSeed synthesis is separate from fact distillation: seed files orient an agent on\nthe repository, while durable facts are branch-scoped retained knowledge.\n\n## How Agents Use The Brain\n\nAgents rely on a capture layer plus four consumption surfaces. The capture\nlayer produces the raw material; the consumption surfaces are how an agent\nactually queries or receives brain context. Which surface matters depends on the\nagent client and how the repository is configured.\n\n### Capture Layer: Entire Hooks Produce The Raw Material\n\n`entire-cli` installs lifecycle hooks for agents such as Claude Code, Codex,\nGemini CLI, OpenCode, Cursor, Factory AI Droid, Copilot CLI, and Pi. Those\nhooks start and stop sessions, capture prompts and transcripts, track changed\nfiles, record subagent work where the agent exposes it, and attach checkpoint\nmetadata to commits.\n\nThis capture path is background infrastructure. The coding agent does not need\nto remember to save a transcript, and the human does not need to paste context\ninto the brain. `entire brain refresh` later ingests the retained sessions and\ncheckpoint history that the hooks produced.\n\nIf an agent is not seeing Entire context at all, first check the base Entire\nintegration, not the brain:\n\n```sh\nentire status\nentire agent\n```\n\n### 1. MCP Is The Preferred Agent Tool Surface\n\nFor agents that support MCP, register `entire brain mcp` as a local stdio MCP\nserver. The server opens no network listener and wraps the local brain's JSON\ncontracts as tools.\n\nOnce MCP is available, an agent should call MCP tools instead of shelling out to\nthe `entire brain` CLI. The normal first call is `brain_brief` for the task.\nThen the agent should make only targeted follow-up calls:\n\n- `brain_brief` for task-shaped context, history hits, likely files, and tests\n- `brain_status` to check freshness, coverage, and blind spots\n- retrieval tools such as `brain_query` and `brain_get` for facts, docs, and\n  history\n- semantic tools such as `brain_code`, `brain_context`, `brain_impact`, and\n  `brain_tests` for code navigation and validation planning\n- `brain_entity_history` for \"which checkpoints and sessions changed this\n  function/class\", answered from the persisted entity index rather than by\n  re-reading history (build it once with `entire brain entities backfill`)\n- review, workspace, and pattern tools such as `brain_regressions`,\n  `brain_workspace_graph`, `brain_workspace_review`, and `brain_patterns` when\n  the task calls for them\n\n`brain_brief` keeps its existing JSON-in-text response by default. Clients\nexperimenting with a smaller agent packet can pass\n`packet_format: \"compact_v1\"`, `packet_format: \"compact_v2\"`, or\n`packet_format: \"compact_v3\"`; these are opt-in serialization changes over the\nsame ranked report, not different retrieval treatments. In v2 and v3's hashed\nin-band legend, `~` means absent and `^` means the previous value in the same\nopcode and column, across interleaved records. V2 limits `^` to metadata\ncolumns; v3 permits it for every exact repeated value. V3 also uses the compact\n`entire.brain_brief c3` marker and an unpadded base64url SHA-256 footer. Both\nformats use positional rows for a repeated family only when its complete\ncanonical encoding is strictly smaller than keyed rows; ties remain keyed. Use\n`legacy_json` (or omit the argument) when a consumer depends on the existing\nJSON contract.\n\nSome MCP tools write local state. For example, `brain_ingest_traces` persists\nruntime edges as `RUNTIME_TRACE` facts. Pattern MCP tools are read-only; skill\nformation remains a CLI-only write path.\n\nThe benchmark harness treats this as the cleanest agent contract: MCP-backed\nagents call named `brain_*` tools, and in MCP conditions they are explicitly\nkept away from direct `entire brain` shell commands or prewritten history\nexcerpt files. See [Semantic MCP Guide](semantic_mcp_guide.md) for the full tool\nsurface.\n\nThe MCP and CLI surfaces are intentionally close, but they are not perfect\nmirrors. Some CLI-only flows, such as `recall --expand`, have no dedicated MCP\ntool. Some MCP tools, such as `brain_review`, wrap machine contracts that are\nhidden or discouraged as direct human CLI verbs. Prefer the native surface your\nagent client has, then translate only when needed.\n\n### 2. Intake Instructions Are The File-Based Fallback\n\nWhen MCP is not available, an agent can still use the brain through the intake\ninstructions in `templates/`. The Codex and Claude templates tell the agent to\nlocate the brain with:\n\n```sh\nentire brain path \"$PWD\"\n```\n\nThen the agent reads staged context instead of blindly opening everything:\n\n- `README.md` and `manifest.json` in the brain directory\n- seed files such as `seed/agent/quick-overview.md`,\n  `seed/agent/overview.md`, `seed/agent/architecture.md`,\n  `seed/agent/risks.md`, `seed/agent/maintenance-guide.md`, and\n  `seed/agent/open-questions.md`\n- `seed/history-gaps.md`, when present\n- only task-relevant transcripts or records selected from the manifest\n\nThis is how an agent like Codex can use `entire-brain` without a dedicated MCP\nconnection: resolve the local brain, read the manifest and seed context, inspect\nonly the relevant retained history, and treat gaps as uncertainty rather than\ninventing rationale.\n\n### 3. Direct CLI Is A Compatibility Surface\n\nFor agent clients without MCP, direct `entire brain ... --json` calls are the\ncompatibility surface. Humans and scripts can use the same commands. Agents\nusing this surface should treat it like a tool API, not as an invitation to run\na long command tour.\n\nA disciplined direct-CLI intake is:\n\n```sh\nentire brain status --json\nentire brain brief \"<task>\" --json\n```\n\nThen ask the smallest next question. Examples:\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"<decision or prior-work question>\" --json\nentire brain inspect code \"<symbol or concept>\" --json\nentire brain inspect context \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect tests \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect regressions \"<task or invariant>\" --location-only --json\n```\n\nAgents should prefer `query` for broad facts/history/docs, `search` for exact\nterms, semantic `inspect` subcommands for code graph questions, and `get` or\n`multi-get` when a prior result returned an id. They should not broaden into\nrepo-wide text search until the brain's targeted context has been used.\n\n### 4. Hidden Hooks Deliver Context At The Moment Of Relevance\n\n`entire-brain` also has a hidden hook surface for harness integrations. These\nhooks are not installed by the base Entire repository enablement flow; a harness\nmust be configured to invoke them, for example from Claude Code\n`PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse` hooks or Entire CLI lifecycle hooks. Until then they\nhave no effect. They are not intended as human-facing commands, although they\ncan be hand-tested when\ndebugging harness wiring. They are designed to surface a small, high-confidence\nfact exactly when it matters:\n\n- before editing a file, facts anchored to that file\n- after a failed command, gotchas and known dead ends that match the failure\n- after a session ends, optional one-session refresh and distillation\n\nThe contract is intentionally quiet: if there is no relevant fact, no local\nbrain, or an environment problem, the hook exits successfully with no output on\nstdout. Diagnostic one-liners may still go to stderr. That keeps hooks from\nbreaking the agent workflow or flooding the transcript. Session-end distillation\nis still an explicit token-spending path: it skips when no agent is available\nand must respect no-egress/local-only policy.\n\n## Usage Scenarios\n\nThe examples below name the preferred MCP tool when there is one. Agent clients\nwithout MCP should use the equivalent `entire brain ... --json` command from\nthe direct-CLI surface above.\n\n### Orient An Agent On A New Task\n\nThe agent should start by asking for task-shaped context, not a generic project\nsummary. The normal tool is `brain_brief`. The useful output is the freshness\nstate, likely files and symbols, relevant history records, durable facts, likely\ntests, and any blind spots.\n\nBegin substantive orientation with a brief unless equivalent context is already\navailable. Reuse useful locations without a redundant Graph query. Inspect source\ndirectly when locations are sufficient, including small edits and follow-ups. Do\nnot automatically refresh or repair tools to answer an ordinary query. See\n[coordinated agent instructions](agent-coordination.md).\n\nFor repo-level orientation rather than task-specific orientation, use\n`overview`. It returns a compact project map: stack signals, entrypoints,\ncommands, key documents, and recent decisions.\n\n```sh\nentire brain overview --json\n```\n\nFor interactive human exploration instead of scripted queries, `entire brain\ndash` opens a terminal dashboard over facts, sessions, history, and semantic\nrecords, and `entire brain viz` opens a local, no-egress browser view of the\nsemantic call graph. Both are read-only and make no agent calls.\n\n### Keep The Brain Fresh\n\nFreshness is part of every answer. A stale semantic index, missing provider,\ndirty-worktree mismatch, or parser blind spot changes how much an agent should\ntrust the brain.\n\nAgents should check freshness through `brain_status` or `status --json` before\nrelying on semantic answers. If freshness is unsafe, an agent with write\npermission can request a refresh; otherwise it should report the limitation and\nfall back to direct repository inspection.\n\nHumans or automation should keep the brain fresh:\n\n```sh\nentire brain status\nentire brain refresh --agent none\nentire brain watch\n```\n\nWhen semantic artifacts are out of sync or damaged, use the semantic\nmaintenance paths instead of deleting the whole brain:\n\n```sh\nentire brain refresh index\nentire brain repair\nentire brain reset --semantic-only --force\n```\n\nUse `refresh index --worktree` only when you intentionally want uncommitted\nstate in the semantic index. Exported bundles reject worktree-backed semantic\nindexes.\n\n### Continue Or Explain Prior Work\n\nWhen the question is \"why is this like this?\", \"what did the previous agent\ntry?\", or \"where did this session leave off?\", the agent should search the\nhistory and facts layers. The normal retrieval tool is `brain_query`,\nfollowed by `brain_get` for specific ids.\n\nThis is the main reason Entire capture matters: the original prompt, attempts,\nvalidation, correction, and rationale can survive the code diff and become\navailable to the next agent.\n\n### Ask Across Facts, History, And Docs\n\nWhen the question is not tied to one symbol, use the unified retrieval layer.\n`query` is the normal hybrid path over durable facts, indexed history, and docs.\nUse `query --keyword` for exact keywords, `query --semantic` for semantic matches, and\n`get`/`multi-get` when a result returns an id worth reading in full.\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"how does checkpointing work\" --json\nentire brain query --keyword \"checkpoint\" --json\nentire brain query --semantic \"preventing data races\" --json\nentire brain get fact:<id> --json\n```\n\nUse `entire brain query \"text\"` for default hybrid retrieval. Select keyword\nmatching with `--keyword` or semantic matching with `--semantic`; the two\nflags are mutually exclusive and belong only to the query command.\nText may instead be supplied as `--query \"text\"`. Flags work before or after\npositional text; combining positional text and `--query` is an error.\nFor example: `entire brain query --keyword --query \"RetryPolicy\" --json`.\n\nWorkspace retrieval supports the same forms:\n`entire brain workspace query <workspace> --semantic --query \"retry policy\"`.\nThe old `search` and `vsearch` commands remain hidden compatibility aliases.\nMCP agents should use `brain_query` with optional, mutually exclusive\n`keyword: true` or `semantic: true` arguments; `brain_search` and\n`brain_vsearch` remain compatibility tools.\n\nFor durable facts specifically, use `recall`. `recall --expand` is an\nagent-assisted query expansion path, so it belongs behind the same egress\njudgment as other agent calls.\n\n```sh\nentire brain recall \"account deletion\" --k 5\nentire brain recall \"MirrorCommittedMetadataRef\" --expand\n```\n\n### Navigate Code By Meaning\n\nWhen a task depends on structure rather than text, the agent should use semantic\ntools. The normal path is: find candidate symbols, inspect relation-aware\ncontext, inspect likely impact, then ask for tests. The normal tools are\n`brain_code` or `brain_search_code`, followed by `brain_context`,\n`brain_impact`, and `brain_tests`.\n\nThis is where `entire-graph` matters. It is not an agent memory layer; it is the\nlocal parser/provider that gives `entire-brain` the graph the agent queries.\nSemantic depth is language-dependent: parser-backed extraction covers the\nsemantic language set, while many recognized filetypes are inventory-only. For\ninventory-only files, prefer text retrieval and lower confidence in\nimpact/context answers.\n\nFor deeper graph work, use the graph specialists: schema inventory, local graph\nUI, source snippets, directed trace paths, dead-code candidates, boundary\nenumeration, and working-tree change mapping.\n\n```sh\nentire brain inspect graph-schema --json\nentire brain inspect graph-ui semantic-graph.html\nentire brain inspect snippet \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect trace-path \"<caller>\" \"<callee>\" --json\nentire brain inspect dead-code --json\nentire brain inspect boundaries --kind tool --json\nentire brain inspect changes --json\n```\n\n### Review Risk Without A Clean Diff\n\nFor long-running sessions, manual edits, or suspected regressions from memory,\nthe agent should use diff-less regression review. The normal tools are\n`brain_regressions` or `brain_review`.\n\nWhen the goal is a fair investigation, prefer location-only output first. It\npoints the agent to suspected files and lines without handing it the expected\nanswer text.\n\n### Connect Runtime Evidence To Source\n\nWhen static structure is not enough, import runtime trace edges with\n`brain_ingest_traces` or `inspect ingest-traces`. The brain compares the runtime\nedges with known static relations, persists them as `RUNTIME_TRACE` graph facts,\nand makes them available to graph schema, trace-path, and brief/context flows.\n\nThis is useful for incidents and performance/debugging tasks where \"what\nactually happened?\" matters more than \"what could call this?\"\n\n### Work Across Multiple Repositories\n\nUse workspaces when the behavior crosses repository boundaries: a frontend calls\nan API, a service emits an event another service consumes, one repo imports a\npackage from another, or deployment/config resources connect separate projects.\n\nHumans create and refresh the workspace. Agents consume workspace context\nthrough MCP workspace tools where available, or through direct workspace CLI\ncommands when MCP does not expose the required traversal. The workspace brain\nkeeps member repos local and builds cross-repo contracts and graph edges from\ntheir existing local brains.\n\n```sh\nentire brain workspace create platform\nentire brain workspace add platform ../api --name api\nentire brain workspace add platform ../web --name web\nentire brain workspace refresh platform --full\n```\n\nAfter the workspace exists, use workspace context, graph, impact, retrieval,\nand review flows when the question crosses repos:\n\n```sh\nentire brain workspace inspect context platform \"checkout\" --json\nentire brain workspace inspect impact platform \"checkout\" --json\nentire brain workspace inspect graph platform --json\nentire brain workspace query platform \"checkout\" --json\nentire brain workspace review platform \"checkout regression\" --json\nentire brain workspace watch platform --once\n```\n\n### Preserve Durable Project Knowledge\n\nDurable facts are short, provenance-anchored statements about decisions,\nconstraints, preferences, pitfalls, and standing rules. Humans can write them\ndirectly when they know the rule. Agents can distill candidate facts from\ncaptured sessions when explicitly asked or when a session-end integration is\nconfigured to do so.\n\n```sh\nentire brain remember \"Prefer table-driven tests for parser edge cases\" --path preferences.tests\nentire brain distill --dry-run --json\nentire brain distill --agent codex --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low\nentire brain facts tree --depth 1\n```\n\nDistillation is the token-spending path. It is incremental and cached, but it\nstill sends redacted transcript chunks to the selected agent unless you choose a\nlocal loopback agent such as Ollama or use dry-run/no-egress mode.\n\nDurable facts also have a lifecycle. Review queued merge/supersede proposals,\npromote branch facts when they should carry forward, retract facts that are no\nlonger true, and garbage-collect stale retractions.\n\n```sh\nentire brain facts review\nentire brain facts promote --from <branch> --strategy keep-both\nentire brain facts retract <fact-id>\nentire brain facts gc --force\nentire brain inspect blame <fact-id> --json\n```\n\n### Extract Reusable Agent Skills\n\nSkill extraction is not \"turn repeated commands into a checklist.\" The current\npipeline forms skills only from accepted deep dossiers: verified evidence that a\npattern is non-obvious and useful to future agents.\n\nThere are several evidence paths:\n\n- recurring task patterns that pass deep verification\n- verified themes that capture latent practices by meaning\n- corrected or failed episodes grouped into lessons\n- durable gotchas, conventions, and invariants grouped into capabilities\n- workspace families where equivalent procedures differ across repos\n\nA human or explicitly authorized automation runs the egress-gated verification\nand formation commands. The agent performs the verification and `SKILL.md`\nsynthesis under that explicit request. The verifier is cached. Refresh, watch,\nbrief, query, MCP reads, and workspace reads do not silently call it.\n\n```sh\nentire brain patterns\nentire brain patterns verify --deep\nentire brain patterns verify --themes\nentire brain patterns verify --lessons\nentire brain patterns verify --conventions\nentire brain patterns skills\nentire brain patterns skills form <id>\nentire brain patterns skills form <id> --yes\n```\n\n`patterns skills` lists formable proposals, not raw pattern rows. `form <id>`\npreviews the evidence, generated `SKILL.md`, and destination paths without\nwriting. If no accepted deep dossier exists yet, `form` refuses and tells you to\nrun verification first; it never falls back to shallow evidence. `--yes` writes\nthe skill; existing files require `--force`.\n\nBy default, generated skills target the cross-agent `standard` destination\n(`.agents/skills/`). Use `--target claude-code|codex|factoryai-droid|all` only\nwhen an agent-specific destination is needed.\n\nWorkspaces mirror the same flow for cross-repo families:\n\n```sh\nentire brain workspace patterns verify <workspace>\nentire brain workspace patterns skills <workspace>\nentire brain workspace patterns skills form <workspace> <id>\n```\n\nPattern inspection is useful even when you are not forming skills. Use it to see\nrecurring tasks, practices, risks, themes, verifier state, and whether accepted\nskills are stale.\n\n```sh\nentire brain patterns\nentire brain patterns status\nentire brain workspace patterns <workspace>\nentire brain workspace patterns status <workspace>\n```\n\n### Measure Brain Quality\n\nThe brain includes evaluation harnesses for maintainers who need evidence that a\nretriever or fact layer is actually helping. These are not required for everyday\nuse, but they are the right surface before claiming quality improvements.\n\n```sh\nentire brain facts eval-gen > facts-tasks.json\nentire brain facts eval --tasks facts-tasks.json --retriever facts --json > facts-eval.json\nentire brain facts eval-compare --a <before.json> --b <after.json>\nentire brain history-eval-gen > history-tasks.json\nentire brain history-eval --tasks history-tasks.json --json\n```\n\nFor semantic performance and provider output, use the semantic benchmark and\nstatus audit instead of relying on anecdotes:\n\n```sh\nentire brain bench semantic .\nentire brain status --json\n```\n\n### Tune Retrieval\n\nThe default vector arm uses the bundled local Model2Vec embedder. For higher\nsemantic recall, opt into a local transformer embedder served by Ollama or a\ncompatible loopback endpoint:\n\n```sh\nollama pull embeddinggemma\nENTIRE_BRAIN_EMBEDDER=ollama entire brain query \"preventing data races\" --json\n```\n\nKeep this distinction clear: changing the embedder changes retrieval behavior,\nnot the underlying source of truth. Facts, history, docs, and semantic records\nstill come from the local brain.\n\n## Privacy And Egress\n\nThe default brain artifacts are local and inspectable. Deterministic refresh,\nsemantic indexing, local history indexing, and MCP tool calls do not require a\nhosted model. The MCP adapter is stdio-only.\n\nSome operations perform network egress, either by sending selected context to a\nmodel or by fetching over the network:\n\n- a configured `checkpoint_remote` lets `refresh` and its `sessions` export\n  stage fetch checkpoint history from that remote into a throwaway temp repo;\n  this is network egress, not a hosted-model call, and is skipped under\n  no-egress mode\n- seed synthesis during `refresh --agent auto`\n- fact distillation with `distill --agent ...`\n- query expansion with `recall --expand`\n- pattern verification and skill synthesis\n- judged evaluation commands\n- `publish` uploads the serialized local brain to hosted Entire; opt-in and off\n  by default, requiring both the command and `ENTIRE_BRAIN_ALLOW_HOSTED=1`\n\nUse `--agent none`, `--dry-run`, local loopback Ollama, or\n`ENTIRE_BRAIN_NO_EGRESS=1` / `ENTIRE_BRAIN_LOCAL_ONLY=1` when the repo must stay\nstrictly local. No-egress mode enforces locality for no-agent, dry-run, and\nloopback-Ollama paths by validating URLs, redirects, and resolved dial targets.\nA custom `--agent command` runner is treated as a trusted local command and is\nnot enforceably loopback-only; no-egress mode cannot stop that runner from\nmaking its own network calls.\n\nRemember that base Entire session capture stores transcripts and metadata on\nthe repository's `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch. Anyone with access to that\nbranch can read captured prompts, tool activity, and retained transcript data.\nEntire redacts detected secrets before writing checkpoint metadata, but\nredaction is best-effort. Redaction covers transcript and checkpoint metadata,\nnot every local working artifact. Entire also writes temporary shadow branches\nsuch as `entire/<short-hash>` whose code-file snapshots are raw, unredacted\nblobs of the working tree. Gitignored files are filtered out only as a partial\ndefense. Entire does not push shadow branches; do not push them manually, or\nunredacted source could reach the remote. Review the Entire CLI security and\nprivacy guide before enabling Entire on sensitive or public repositories.\n\n## Storage And Configuration\n\nEntire CLI supplies the plugin directories that make the brain durable:\n\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_CONFIG_DIR` for `brain.json`\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` for generated repo brains and workspaces\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_STATE_DIR` for regenerable cursors such as watcher state\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR` for caches\n- `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` for the current checkout when invoked inside a repo\n\nRepo keys are derived from the repository origin. For example,\n`github.com/entireio/cli` becomes `gh/entireio/cli`.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":1625},"formatted_output":"```sh\nentire brain distill --dry-run --json                                  # estimate work; no agent call\nentire brain distill --agent codex --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low   # extract facts\nentire brain facts status                                              # review readiness\n```\n\nDistillation is incremental and cached: unchanged sessions are skipped, and\nlow-confidence merges are queued for `entire brain facts review`.\n\n### Use the brain from an agent\n\nFor agents that speak MCP, register the brain as a local stdio MCP server. It\nopens no network listener.\n\n```sh\nentire brain mcp\n```\n\nIf you ran `entire brain setup` without `--no-daemon`, a watcher is already\ninstalled and keeping this repository fresh; the commands below are the manual\nequivalents, for a brain built with `refresh` or a repository set up with\n`--no-daemon`.\n\nThe default watcher performs deterministic refreshes only. Token-spending work,\nsuch as fact distillation or seed synthesis, is opt-in and separately gated.\n\nTo keep the durable-facts layer fresh as new sessions land, enable distillation\nexplicitly:\n\n```sh\nentire brain watch --distill --distill-every 24h --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low --budget 1\n```\n\nIf you also want generated seed summaries, run refresh with an agent instead of\nthe deterministic seed path:\n\n```sh\nentire brain refresh --agent auto --seed-model gpt-5.4-mini --seed-effort low\n```\n\nSeed synthesis is separate from fact distillation: seed files orient an agent on\nthe repository, while durable facts are branch-scoped retained knowledge.\n\n## How Agents Use The Brain\n\nAgents rely on a capture layer plus four consumption surfaces. The capture\nlayer produces the raw material; the consumption surfaces are how an agent\nactually queries or receives brain context. Which surface matters depends on the\nagent client and how the repository is configured.\n\n### Capture Layer: Entire Hooks Produce The Raw Material\n\n`entire-cli` installs lifecycle hooks for agents such as Claude Code, Codex,\nGemini CLI, OpenCode, Cursor, Factory AI Droid, Copilot CLI, and Pi. Those\nhooks start and stop sessions, capture prompts and transcripts, track changed\nfiles, record subagent work where the agent exposes it, and attach checkpoint\nmetadata to commits.\n\nThis capture path is background infrastructure. The coding agent does not need\nto remember to save a transcript, and the human does not need to paste context\ninto the brain. `entire brain refresh` later ingests the retained sessions and\ncheckpoint history that the hooks produced.\n\nIf an agent is not seeing Entire context at all, first check the base Entire\nintegration, not the brain:\n\n```sh\nentire status\nentire agent\n```\n\n### 1. MCP Is The Preferred Agent Tool Surface\n\nFor agents that support MCP, register `entire brain mcp` as a local stdio MCP\nserver. The server opens no network listener and wraps the local brain's JSON\ncontracts as tools.\n\nOnce MCP is available, an agent should call MCP tools instead of shelling out to\nthe `entire brain` CLI. The normal first call is `brain_brief` for the task.\nThen the agent should make only targeted follow-up calls:\n\n- `brain_brief` for task-shaped context, history hits, likely files, and tests\n- `brain_status` to check freshness, coverage, and blind spots\n- retrieval tools such as `brain_query` and `brain_get` for facts, docs, and\n  history\n- semantic tools such as `brain_code`, `brain_context`, `brain_impact`, and\n  `brain_tests` for code navigation and validation planning\n- `brain_entity_history` for \"which checkpoints and sessions changed this\n  function/class\", answered from the persisted entity index rather than by\n  re-reading history (build it once with `entire brain entities backfill`)\n- review, workspace, and pattern tools such as `brain_regressions`,\n  `brain_workspace_graph`, `brain_workspace_review`, and `brain_patterns` when\n  the task calls for them\n\n`brain_brief` keeps its existing JSON-in-text response by default. Clients\nexperimenting with a smaller agent packet can pass\n`packet_format: \"compact_v1\"`, `packet_format: \"compact_v2\"`, or\n`packet_format: \"compact_v3\"`; these are opt-in serialization changes over the\nsame ranked report, not different retrieval treatments. In v2 and v3's hashed\nin-band legend, `~` means absent and `^` means the previous value in the same\nopcode and column, across interleaved records. V2 limits `^` to metadata\ncolumns; v3 permits it for every exact repeated value. V3 also uses the compact\n`entire.brain_brief c3` marker and an unpadded base64url SHA-256 footer. Both\nformats use positional rows for a repeated family only when its complete\ncanonical encoding is strictly smaller than keyed rows; ties remain keyed. Use\n`legacy_json` (or omit the argument) when a consumer depends on the existing\nJSON contract.\n\nSome MCP tools write local state. For example, `brain_ingest_traces` persists\nruntime edges as `RUNTIME_TRACE` facts. Pattern MCP tools are read-only; skill\nformation remains a CLI-only write path.\n\nThe benchmark harness treats this as the cleanest agent contract: MCP-backed\nagents call named `brain_*` tools, and in MCP conditions they are explicitly\nkept away from direct `entire brain` shell commands or prewritten history\nexcerpt files. See [Semantic MCP Guide](semantic_mcp_guide.md) for the full tool\nsurface.\n\nThe MCP and CLI surfaces are intentionally close, but they are not perfect\nmirrors. Some CLI-only flows, such as `recall --expand`, have no dedicated MCP\ntool. Some MCP tools, such as `brain_review`, wrap machine contracts that are\nhidden or discouraged as direct human CLI verbs. Prefer the native surface your\nagent client has, then translate only when needed.\n\n### 2. Intake Instructions Are The File-Based Fallback\n\nWhen MCP is not available, an agent can still use the brain through the intake\ninstructions in `templates/`. The Codex and Claude templates tell the agent to\nlocate the brain with:\n\n```sh\nentire brain path \"$PWD\"\n```\n\nThen the agent reads staged context instead of blindly opening everything:\n\n- `README.md` and `manifest.json` in the brain directory\n- seed files such as `seed/agent/quick-overview.md`,\n  `seed/agent/overview.md`, `seed/agent/architecture.md`,\n  `seed/agent/risks.md`, `seed/agent/maintenance-guide.md`, and\n  `seed/agent/open-questions.md`\n- `seed/history-gaps.md`, when present\n- only task-relevant transcripts or records selected from the manifest\n\nThis is how an agent like Codex can use `entire-brain` without a dedicated MCP\nconnection: resolve the local brain, read the manifest and seed context, inspect\nonly the relevant retained history, and treat gaps as uncertainty rather than\ninventing rationale.\n\n### 3. Direct CLI Is A Compatibility Surface\n\nFor agent clients without MCP, direct `entire brain ... --json` calls are the\ncompatibility surface. Humans and scripts can use the same commands. Agents\nusing this surface should treat it like a tool API, not as an invitation to run\na long command tour.\n\nA disciplined direct-CLI intake is:\n\n```sh\nentire brain status --json\nentire brain brief \"<task>\" --json\n```\n\nThen ask the smallest next question. Examples:\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"<decision or prior-work question>\" --json\nentire brain inspect code \"<symbol or concept>\" --json\nentire brain inspect context \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect tests \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect regressions \"<task or invariant>\" --location-only --json\n```\n\nAgents should prefer `query` for broad facts/history/docs, `search` for exact\nterms, semantic `inspect` subcommands for code graph questions, and `get` or\n`multi-get` when a prior result returned an id. They should not broaden into\nrepo-wide text search until the brain's targeted context has been used.\n\n### 4. Hidden Hooks Deliver Context At The Moment Of Relevance\n\n`entire-brain` also has a hidden hook surface for harness integrations. These\nhooks are not installed by the base Entire repository enablement flow; a harness\nmust be configured to invoke them, for example from Claude Code\n`PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse` hooks or Entire CLI lifecycle hooks. Until then they\nhave no effect. They are not intended as human-facing commands, although they\ncan be hand-tested when\ndebugging harness wiring. They are designed to surface a small, high-confidence\nfact exactly when it matters:\n\n- before editing a file, facts anchored to that file\n- after a failed command, gotchas and known dead ends that match the failure\n- after a session ends, optional one-session refresh and distillation\n\nThe contract is intentionally quiet: if there is no relevant fact, no local\nbrain, or an environment problem, the hook exits successfully with no output on\nstdout. Diagnostic one-liners may still go to stderr. That keeps hooks from\nbreaking the agent workflow or flooding the transcript. Session-end distillation\nis still an explicit token-spending path: it skips when no agent is available\nand must respect no-egress/local-only policy.\n\n## Usage Scenarios\n\nThe examples below name the preferred MCP tool when there is one. Agent clients\nwithout MCP should use the equivalent `entire brain ... --json` command from\nthe direct-CLI surface above.\n\n### Orient An Agent On A New Task\n\nThe agent should start by asking for task-shaped context, not a generic project\nsummary. The normal tool is `brain_brief`. The useful output is the freshness\nstate, likely files and symbols, relevant history records, durable facts, likely\ntests, and any blind spots.\n\nBegin substantive orientation with a brief unless equivalent context is already\navailable. Reuse useful locations without a redundant Graph query. Inspect source\ndirectly when locations are sufficient, including small edits and follow-ups. Do\nnot automatically refresh or repair tools to answer an ordinary query. See\n[coordinated agent instructions](agent-coordination.md).\n\nFor repo-level orientation rather than task-specific orientation, use\n`overview`. It returns a compact project map: stack signals, entrypoints,\ncommands, key documents, and recent decisions.\n\n```sh\nentire brain overview --json\n```\n\nFor interactive human exploration instead of scripted queries, `entire brain\ndash` opens a terminal dashboard over facts, sessions, history, and semantic\nrecords, and `entire brain viz` opens a local, no-egress browser view of the\nsemantic call graph. Both are read-only and make no agent calls.\n\n### Keep The Brain Fresh\n\nFreshness is part of every answer. A stale semantic index, missing provider,\ndirty-worktree mismatch, or parser blind spot changes how much an agent should\ntrust the brain.\n\nAgents should check freshness through `brain_status` or `status --json` before\nrelying on semantic answers. If freshness is unsafe, an agent with write\npermission can request a refresh; otherwise it should report the limitation and\nfall back to direct repository inspection.\n\nHumans or automation should keep the brain fresh:\n\n```sh\nentire brain status\nentire brain refresh --agent none\nentire brain watch\n```\n\nWhen semantic artifacts are out of sync or damaged, use the semantic\nmaintenance paths instead of deleting the whole brain:\n\n```sh\nentire brain refresh index\nentire brain repair\nentire brain reset --semantic-only --force\n```\n\nUse `refresh index --worktree` only when you intentionally want uncommitted\nstate in the semantic index. Exported bundles reject worktree-backed semantic\nindexes.\n\n### Continue Or Explain Prior Work\n\nWhen the question is \"why is this like this?\", \"what did the previous agent\ntry?\", or \"where did this session leave off?\", the agent should search the\nhistory and facts layers. The normal retrieval tool is `brain_query`,\nfollowed by `brain_get` for specific ids.\n\nThis is the main reason Entire capture matters: the original prompt, attempts,\nvalidation, correction, and rationale can survive the code diff and become\navailable to the next agent.\n\n### Ask Across Facts, History, And Docs\n\nWhen the question is not tied to one symbol, use the unified retrieval layer.\n`query` is the normal hybrid path over durable facts, indexed history, and docs.\nUse `query --keyword` for exact keywords, `query --semantic` for semantic matches, and\n`get`/`multi-get` when a result returns an id worth reading in full.\n\n```sh\nentire brain query \"how does checkpointing work\" --json\nentire brain query --keyword \"checkpoint\" --json\nentire brain query --semantic \"preventing data races\" --json\nentire brain get fact:<id> --json\n```\n\nUse `entire brain query \"text\"` for default hybrid retrieval. Select keyword\nmatching with `--keyword` or semantic matching with `--semantic`; the two\nflags are mutually exclusive and belong only to the query command.\nText may instead be supplied as `--query \"text\"`. Flags work before or after\npositional text; combining positional text and `--query` is an error.\nFor example: `entire brain query --keyword --query \"RetryPolicy\" --json`.\n\nWorkspace retrieval supports the same forms:\n`entire brain workspace query <workspace> --semantic --query \"retry policy\"`.\nThe old `search` and `vsearch` commands remain hidden compatibility aliases.\nMCP agents should use `brain_query` with optional, mutually exclusive\n`keyword: true` or `semantic: true` arguments; `brain_search` and\n`brain_vsearch` remain compatibility tools.\n\nFor durable facts specifically, use `recall`. `recall --expand` is an\nagent-assisted query expansion path, so it belongs behind the same egress\njudgment as other agent calls.\n\n```sh\nentire brain recall \"account deletion\" --k 5\nentire brain recall \"MirrorCommittedMetadataRef\" --expand\n```\n\n### Navigate Code By Meaning\n\nWhen a task depends on structure rather than text, the agent should use semantic\ntools. The normal path is: find candidate symbols, inspect relation-aware\ncontext, inspect likely impact, then ask for tests. The normal tools are\n`brain_code` or `brain_search_code`, followed by `brain_context`,\n`brain_impact`, and `brain_tests`.\n\nThis is where `entire-graph` matters. It is not an agent memory layer; it is the\nlocal parser/provider that gives `entire-brain` the graph the agent queries.\nSemantic depth is language-dependent: parser-backed extraction covers the\nsemantic language set, while many recognized filetypes are inventory-only. For\ninventory-only files, prefer text retrieval and lower confidence in\nimpact/context answers.\n\nFor deeper graph work, use the graph specialists: schema inventory, local graph\nUI, source snippets, directed trace paths, dead-code candidates, boundary\nenumeration, and working-tree change mapping.\n\n```sh\nentire brain inspect graph-schema --json\nentire brain inspect graph-ui semantic-graph.html\nentire brain inspect snippet \"<symbol-or-id>\" --json\nentire brain inspect trace-path \"<caller>\" \"<callee>\" --json\nentire brain inspect dead-code --json\nentire brain inspect boundaries --kind tool --json\nentire brain inspect changes --json\n```\n\n### Review Risk Without A Clean Diff\n\nFor long-running sessions, manual edits, or suspected regressions from memory,\nthe agent should use diff-less regression review. The normal tools are\n`brain_regressions` or `brain_review`.\n\nWhen the goal is a fair investigation, prefer location-only output first. It\npoints the agent to suspected files and lines without handing it the expected\nanswer text.\n\n### Connect Runtime Evidence To Source\n\nWhen static structure is not enough, import runtime trace edges with\n`brain_ingest_traces` or `inspect ingest-traces`. The brain compares the runtime\nedges with known static relations, persists them as `RUNTIME_TRACE` graph facts,\nand makes them available to graph schema, trace-path, and brief/context flows.\n\nThis is useful for incidents and performance/debugging tasks where \"what\nactually happened?\" matters more than \"what could call this?\"\n\n### Work Across Multiple Repositories\n\nUse workspaces when the behavior crosses repository boundaries: a frontend calls\nan API, a service emits an event another service consumes, one repo imports a\npackage from another, or deployment/config resources connect separate projects.\n\nHumans create and refresh the workspace. Agents consume workspace context\nthrough MCP workspace tools where available, or through direct workspace CLI\ncommands when MCP does not expose the required traversal. The workspace brain\nkeeps member repos local and builds cross-repo contracts and graph edges from\ntheir existing local brains.\n\n```sh\nentire brain workspace create platform\nentire brain workspace add platform ../api --name api\nentire brain workspace add platform ../web --name web\nentire brain workspace refresh platform --full\n```\n\nAfter the workspace exists, use workspace context, graph, impact, retrieval,\nand review flows when the question crosses repos:\n\n```sh\nentire brain workspace inspect context platform \"checkout\" --json\nentire brain workspace inspect impact platform \"checkout\" --json\nentire brain workspace inspect graph platform --json\nentire brain workspace query platform \"checkout\" --json\nentire brain workspace review platform \"checkout regression\" --json\nentire brain workspace watch platform --once\n```\n\n### Preserve Durable Project Knowledge\n\nDurable facts are short, provenance-anchored statements about decisions,\nconstraints, preferences, pitfalls, and standing rules. Humans can write them\ndirectly when they know the rule. Agents can distill candidate facts from\ncaptured sessions when explicitly asked or when a session-end integration is\nconfigured to do so.\n\n```sh\nentire brain remember \"Prefer table-driven tests for parser edge cases\" --path preferences.tests\nentire brain distill --dry-run --json\nentire brain distill --agent codex --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low\nentire brain facts tree --depth 1\n```\n\nDistillation is the token-spending path. It is incremental and cached, but it\nstill sends redacted transcript chunks to the selected agent unless you choose a\nlocal loopback agent such as Ollama or use dry-run/no-egress mode.\n\nDurable facts also have a lifecycle. Review queued merge/supersede proposals,\npromote branch facts when they should carry forward, retract facts that are no\nlonger true, and garbage-collect stale retractions.\n\n```sh\nentire brain facts review\nentire brain facts promote --from <branch> --strategy keep-both\nentire brain facts retract <fact-id>\nentire brain facts gc --force\nentire brain inspect blame <fact-id> --json\n```\n\n### Extract Reusable Agent Skills\n\nSkill extraction is not \"turn repeated commands into a checklist.\" The current\npipeline forms skills only from accepted deep dossiers: verified evidence that a\npattern is non-obvious and useful to future agents.\n\nThere are several evidence paths:\n\n- recurring task patterns that pass deep verification\n- verified themes that capture latent practices by meaning\n- corrected or failed episodes grouped into lessons\n- durable gotchas, conventions, and invariants grouped into capabilities\n- workspace families where equivalent procedures differ across repos\n\nA human or explicitly authorized automation runs the egress-gated verification\nand formation commands. The agent performs the verification and `SKILL.md`\nsynthesis under that explicit request. The verifier is cached. Refresh, watch,\nbrief, query, MCP reads, and workspace reads do not silently call it.\n\n```sh\nentire brain patterns\nentire brain patterns verify --deep\nentire brain patterns verify --themes\nentire brain patterns verify --lessons\nentire brain patterns verify --conventions\nentire brain patterns skills\nentire brain patterns skills form <id>\nentire brain patterns skills form <id> --yes\n```\n\n`patterns skills` lists formable proposals, not raw pattern rows. `form <id>`\npreviews the evidence, generated `SKILL.md`, and destination paths without\nwriting. If no accepted deep dossier exists yet, `form` refuses and tells you to\nrun verification first; it never falls back to shallow evidence. `--yes` writes\nthe skill; existing files require `--force`.\n\nBy default, generated skills target the cross-agent `standard` destination\n(`.agents/skills/`). Use `--target claude-code|codex|factoryai-droid|all` only\nwhen an agent-specific destination is needed.\n\nWorkspaces mirror the same flow for cross-repo families:\n\n```sh\nentire brain workspace patterns verify <workspace>\nentire brain workspace patterns skills <workspace>\nentire brain workspace patterns skills form <workspace> <id>\n```\n\nPattern inspection is useful even when you are not forming skills. Use it to see\nrecurring tasks, practices, risks, themes, verifier state, and whether accepted\nskills are stale.\n\n```sh\nentire brain patterns\nentire brain patterns status\nentire brain workspace patterns <workspace>\nentire brain workspace patterns status <workspace>\n```\n\n### Measure Brain Quality\n\nThe brain includes evaluation harnesses for maintainers who need evidence that a\nretriever or fact layer is actually helping. These are not required for everyday\nuse, but they are the right surface before claiming quality improvements.\n\n```sh\nentire brain facts eval-gen > facts-tasks.json\nentire brain facts eval --tasks facts-tasks.json --retriever facts --json > facts-eval.json\nentire brain facts eval-compare --a <before.json> --b <after.json>\nentire brain history-eval-gen > history-tasks.json\nentire brain history-eval --tasks history-tasks.json --json\n```\n\nFor semantic performance and provider output, use the semantic benchmark and\nstatus audit instead of relying on anecdotes:\n\n```sh\nentire brain bench semantic .\nentire brain status --json\n```\n\n### Tune Retrieval\n\nThe default vector arm uses the bundled local Model2Vec embedder. For higher\nsemantic recall, opt into a local transformer embedder served by Ollama or a\ncompatible loopback endpoint:\n\n```sh\nollama pull embeddinggemma\nENTIRE_BRAIN_EMBEDDER=ollama entire brain query \"preventing data races\" --json\n```\n\nKeep this distinction clear: changing the embedder changes retrieval behavior,\nnot the underlying source of truth. Facts, history, docs, and semantic records\nstill come from the local brain.\n\n## Privacy And Egress\n\nThe default brain artifacts are local and inspectable. Deterministic refresh,\nsemantic indexing, local history indexing, and MCP tool calls do not require a\nhosted model. The MCP adapter is stdio-only.\n\nSome operations perform network egress, either by sending selected context to a\nmodel or by fetching over the network:\n\n- a configured `checkpoint_remote` lets `refresh` and its `sessions` export\n  stage fetch checkpoint history from that remote into a throwaway temp repo;\n  this is network egress, not a hosted-model call, and is skipped under\n  no-egress mode\n- seed synthesis during `refresh --agent auto`\n- fact distillation with `distill --agent ...`\n- query expansion with `recall --expand`\n- pattern verification and skill synthesis\n- judged evaluation commands\n- `publish` uploads the serialized local brain to hosted Entire; opt-in and off\n  by default, requiring both the command and `ENTIRE_BRAIN_ALLOW_HOSTED=1`\n\nUse `--agent none`, `--dry-run`, local loopback Ollama, or\n`ENTIRE_BRAIN_NO_EGRESS=1` / `ENTIRE_BRAIN_LOCAL_ONLY=1` when the repo must stay\nstrictly local. No-egress mode enforces locality for no-agent, dry-run, and\nloopback-Ollama paths by validating URLs, redirects, and resolved dial targets.\nA custom `--agent command` runner is treated as a trusted local command and is\nnot enforceably loopback-only; no-egress mode cannot stop that runner from\nmaking its own network calls.\n\nRemember that base Entire session capture stores transcripts and metadata on\nthe repository's `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch. Anyone with access to that\nbranch can read captured prompts, tool activity, and retained transcript data.\nEntire redacts detected secrets before writing checkpoint metadata, but\nredaction is best-effort. Redaction covers transcript and checkpoint metadata,\nnot every local working artifact. Entire also writes temporary shadow branches\nsuch as `entire/<short-hash>` whose code-file snapshots are raw, unredacted\nblobs of the working tree. Gitignored files are filtered out only as a partial\ndefense. Entire does not push shadow branches; do not push them manually, or\nunredacted source could reach the remote. Review the Entire CLI security and\nprivacy guide before enabling Entire on sensitive or public repositories.\n\n## Storage And Configuration\n\nEntire CLI supplies the plugin directories that make the brain durable:\n\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_CONFIG_DIR` for `brain.json`\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` for generated repo brains and workspaces\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_STATE_DIR` for regenerable cursors such as watcher state\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR` for caches\n- `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` for the current checkout when invoked inside a repo\n\nRepo keys are derived from the repository origin. For example,\n`github.com/entireio/cli` becomes `gh/entireio/cli`.\n"},"started_at_ms":1789839998260,"completed_at_ms":1789839998260}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:46:38.270Z","ordinal":7727,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","session_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","root_turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","response_id":"resp_0cb48b2b18d09e75016aaeca79355487d286f8ea6bf85451d4","usage":{"input_tokens":40303,"cached_input_tokens":33664,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":96,"reasoning_output_tokens":53,"total_tokens":40399},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":77764966,"cached_input_tokens":74492544,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":181706,"reasoning_output_tokens":15088,"total_tokens":77946672},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":143649985,"cached_input_tokens":137064576,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":366505,"reasoning_output_tokens":36465,"total_tokens":144016490}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T17:46:38.316Z","ordinal":7728,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a0bac6-fd6c-7210-bada-9c8aeaad6ce7","call_id":"call_bubRYwXIZpQGBnXmrCB1lv3b","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"12c252\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000001625,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":6180,\"output\":\"```sh\\nentire brain distill --dry-run --json                                  # estimate work; no agent call\\nentire brain distill --agent codex --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low   # extract facts\\nentire brain facts status                                              # review readiness\\n```\\n\\nDistillation is incremental and cached: unchanged sessions are skipped, and\\nlow-confidence merges are queued for `entire brain facts review`.\\n\\n### Use the brain from an agent\\n\\nFor agents that speak MCP, register the brain as a local stdio MCP server. It\\nopens no network listener.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain mcp\\n```\\n\\nIf you ran `entire brain setup` without `--no-daemon`, a watcher is already\\ninstalled and keeping this repository fresh; the commands below are the manual\\nequivalents, for a brain built with `refresh` or a repository set up with\\n`--no-daemon`.\\n\\nThe default watcher performs deterministic refreshes only. Token-spending work,\\nsuch as fact distillation or seed synthesis, is opt-in and separately gated.\\n\\nTo keep the durable-facts layer fresh as new sessions land, enable distillation\\nexplicitly:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain watch --distill --distill-every 24h --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low --budget 1\\n```\\n\\nIf you also want generated seed summaries, run refresh with an agent instead of\\nthe deterministic seed path:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain refresh --agent auto --seed-model gpt-5.4-mini --seed-effort low\\n```\\n\\nSeed synthesis is separate from fact distillation: seed files orient an agent on\\nthe repository, while durable facts are branch-scoped retained knowledge.\\n\\n## How Agents Use The Brain\\n\\nAgents rely on a capture layer plus four consumption surfaces. The capture\\nlayer produces the raw material; the consumption surfaces are how an agent\\nactually queries or receives brain context. Which surface matters depends on the\\nagent client and how the repository is configured.\\n\\n### Capture Layer: Entire Hooks Produce The Raw Material\\n\\n`entire-cli` installs lifecycle hooks for agents such as Claude Code, Codex,\\nGemini CLI, OpenCode, Cursor, Factory AI Droid, Copilot CLI, and Pi. Those\\nhooks start and stop sessions, capture prompts and transcripts, track changed\\nfiles, record subagent work where the agent exposes it, and attach checkpoint\\nmetadata to commits.\\n\\nThis capture path is background infrastructure. The coding agent does not need\\nto remember to save a transcript, and the human does not need to paste context\\ninto the brain. `entire brain refresh` later ingests the retained sessions and\\ncheckpoint history that the hooks produced.\\n\\nIf an agent is not seeing Entire context at all, first check the base Entire\\nintegration, not the brain:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire status\\nentire agent\\n```\\n\\n### 1. MCP Is The Preferred Agent Tool Surface\\n\\nFor agents that support MCP, register `entire brain mcp` as a local stdio MCP\\nserver. The server opens no network listener and wraps the local brain's JSON\\ncontracts as tools.\\n\\nOnce MCP is available, an agent should call MCP tools instead of shelling out to\\nthe `entire brain` CLI. The normal first call is `brain_brief` for the task.\\nThen the agent should make only targeted follow-up calls:\\n\\n- `brain_brief` for task-shaped context, history hits, likely files, and tests\\n- `brain_status` to check freshness, coverage, and blind spots\\n- retrieval tools such as `brain_query` and `brain_get` for facts, docs, and\\n  history\\n- semantic tools such as `brain_code`, `brain_context`, `brain_impact`, and\\n  `brain_tests` for code navigation and validation planning\\n- `brain_entity_history` for \\\"which checkpoints and sessions changed this\\n  function/class\\\", answered from the persisted entity index rather than by\\n  re-reading history (build it once with `entire brain entities backfill`)\\n- review, workspace, and pattern tools such as `brain_regressions`,\\n  `brain_workspace_graph`, `brain_workspace_review`, and `brain_patterns` when\\n  the task calls for them\\n\\n`brain_brief` keeps its existing JSON-in-text response by default. Clients\\nexperimenting with a smaller agent packet can pass\\n`packet_format: \\\"compact_v1\\\"`, `packet_format: \\\"compact_v2\\\"`, or\\n`packet_format: \\\"compact_v3\\\"`; these are opt-in serialization changes over the\\nsame ranked report, not different retrieval treatments. In v2 and v3's hashed\\nin-band legend, `~` means absent and `^` means the previous value in the same\\nopcode and column, across interleaved records. V2 limits `^` to metadata\\ncolumns; v3 permits it for every exact repeated value. V3 also uses the compact\\n`entire.brain_brief c3` marker and an unpadded base64url SHA-256 footer. Both\\nformats use positional rows for a repeated family only when its complete\\ncanonical encoding is strictly smaller than keyed rows; ties remain keyed. Use\\n`legacy_json` (or omit the argument) when a consumer depends on the existing\\nJSON contract.\\n\\nSome MCP tools write local state. For example, `brain_ingest_traces` persists\\nruntime edges as `RUNTIME_TRACE` facts. Pattern MCP tools are read-only; skill\\nformation remains a CLI-only write path.\\n\\nThe benchmark harness treats this as the cleanest agent contract: MCP-backed\\nagents call named `brain_*` tools, and in MCP conditions they are explicitly\\nkept away from direct `entire brain` shell commands or prewritten history\\nexcerpt files. See [Semantic MCP Guide](semantic_mcp_guide.md) for the full tool\\nsurface.\\n\\nThe MCP and CLI surfaces are intentionally close, but they are not perfect\\nmirrors. Some CLI-only flows, such as `recall --expand`, have no dedicated MCP\\ntool. Some MCP tools, such as `brain_review`, wrap machine contracts that are\\nhidden or discouraged as direct human CLI verbs. Prefer the native surface your\\nagent client has, then translate only when needed.\\n\\n### 2. Intake Instructions Are The File-Based Fallback\\n\\nWhen MCP is not available, an agent can still use the brain through the intake\\ninstructions in `templates/`. The Codex and Claude templates tell the agent to\\nlocate the brain with:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain path \\\"$PWD\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThen the agent reads staged context instead of blindly opening everything:\\n\\n- `README.md` and `manifest.json` in the brain directory\\n- seed files such as `seed/agent/quick-overview.md`,\\n  `seed/agent/overview.md`, `seed/agent/architecture.md`,\\n  `seed/agent/risks.md`, `seed/agent/maintenance-guide.md`, and\\n  `seed/agent/open-questions.md`\\n- `seed/history-gaps.md`, when present\\n- only task-relevant transcripts or records selected from the manifest\\n\\nThis is how an agent like Codex can use `entire-brain` without a dedicated MCP\\nconnection: resolve the local brain, read the manifest and seed context, inspect\\nonly the relevant retained history, and treat gaps as uncertainty rather than\\ninventing rationale.\\n\\n### 3. Direct CLI Is A Compatibility Surface\\n\\nFor agent clients without MCP, direct `entire brain ... --json` calls are the\\ncompatibility surface. Humans and scripts can use the same commands. Agents\\nusing this surface should treat it like a tool API, not as an invitation to run\\na long command tour.\\n\\nA disciplined direct-CLI intake is:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain status --json\\nentire brain brief \\\"<task>\\\" --json\\n```\\n\\nThen ask the smallest next question. Examples:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain query \\\"<decision or prior-work question>\\\" --json\\nentire brain inspect code \\\"<symbol or concept>\\\" --json\\nentire brain inspect context \\\"<symbol-or-id>\\\" --json\\nentire brain inspect tests \\\"<symbol-or-id>\\\" --json\\nentire brain inspect regressions \\\"<task or invariant>\\\" --location-only --json\\n```\\n\\nAgents should prefer `query` for broad facts/history/docs, `search` for exact\\nterms, semantic `inspect` subcommands for code graph questions, and `get` or\\n`multi-get` when a prior result returned an id. They should not broaden into\\nrepo-wide text search until the brain's targeted context has been used.\\n\\n### 4. Hidden Hooks Deliver Context At The Moment Of Relevance\\n\\n`entire-brain` also has a hidden hook surface for harness integrations. These\\nhooks are not installed by the base Entire repository enablement flow; a harness\\nmust be configured to invoke them, for example from Claude Code\\n`PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse` hooks or Entire CLI lifecycle hooks. Until then they\\nhave no effect. They are not intended as human-facing commands, although they\\ncan be hand-tested when\\ndebugging harness wiring. They are designed to surface a small, high-confidence\\nfact exactly when it matters:\\n\\n- before editing a file, facts anchored to that file\\n- after a failed command, gotchas and known dead ends that match the failure\\n- after a session ends, optional one-session refresh and distillation\\n\\nThe contract is intentionally quiet: if there is no relevant fact, no local\\nbrain, or an environment problem, the hook exits successfully with no output on\\nstdout. Diagnostic one-liners may still go to stderr. That keeps hooks from\\nbreaking the agent workflow or flooding the transcript. Session-end distillation\\nis still an explicit token-spending path: it skips when no agent is available\\nand must respect no-egress/local-only policy.\\n\\n## Usage Scenarios\\n\\nThe examples below name the preferred MCP tool when there is one. Agent clients\\nwithout MCP should use the equivalent `entire brain ... --json` command from\\nthe direct-CLI surface above.\\n\\n### Orient An Agent On A New Task\\n\\nThe agent should start by asking for task-shaped context, not a generic project\\nsummary. The normal tool is `brain_brief`. The useful output is the freshness\\nstate, likely files and symbols, relevant history records, durable facts, likely\\ntests, and any blind spots.\\n\\nBegin substantive orientation with a brief unless equivalent context is already\\navailable. Reuse useful locations without a redundant Graph query. Inspect source\\ndirectly when locations are sufficient, including small edits and follow-ups. Do\\nnot automatically refresh or repair tools to answer an ordinary query. See\\n[coordinated agent instructions](agent-coordination.md).\\n\\nFor repo-level orientation rather than task-specific orientation, use\\n`overview`. It returns a compact project map: stack signals, entrypoints,\\ncommands, key documents, and recent decisions.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain overview --json\\n```\\n\\nFor interactive human exploration instead of scripted queries, `entire brain\\ndash` opens a terminal dashboard over facts, sessions, history, and semantic\\nrecords, and `entire brain viz` opens a local, no-egress browser view of the\\nsemantic call graph. Both are read-only and make no agent calls.\\n\\n### Keep The Brain Fresh\\n\\nFreshness is part of every answer. A stale semantic index, missing provider,\\ndirty-worktree mismatch, or parser blind spot changes how much an agent should\\ntrust the brain.\\n\\nAgents should check freshness through `brain_status` or `status --json` before\\nrelying on semantic answers. If freshness is unsafe, an agent with write\\npermission can request a refresh; otherwise it should report the limitation and\\nfall back to direct repository inspection.\\n\\nHumans or automation should keep the brain fresh:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain status\\nentire brain refresh --agent none\\nentire brain watch\\n```\\n\\nWhen semantic artifacts are out of sync or damaged, use the semantic\\nmaintenance paths instead of deleting the whole brain:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain refresh index\\nentire brain repair\\nentire brain reset --semantic-only --force\\n```\\n\\nUse `refresh index --worktree` only when you intentionally want uncommitted\\nstate in the semantic index. Exported bundles reject worktree-backed semantic\\nindexes.\\n\\n### Continue Or Explain Prior Work\\n\\nWhen the question is \\\"why is this like this?\\\", \\\"what did the previous agent\\ntry?\\\", or \\\"where did this session leave off?\\\", the agent should search the\\nhistory and facts layers. The normal retrieval tool is `brain_query`,\\nfollowed by `brain_get` for specific ids.\\n\\nThis is the main reason Entire capture matters: the original prompt, attempts,\\nvalidation, correction, and rationale can survive the code diff and become\\navailable to the next agent.\\n\\n### Ask Across Facts, History, And Docs\\n\\nWhen the question is not tied to one symbol, use the unified retrieval layer.\\n`query` is the normal hybrid path over durable facts, indexed history, and docs.\\nUse `query --keyword` for exact keywords, `query --semantic` for semantic matches, and\\n`get`/`multi-get` when a result returns an id worth reading in full.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain query \\\"how does checkpointing work\\\" --json\\nentire brain query --keyword \\\"checkpoint\\\" --json\\nentire brain query --semantic \\\"preventing data races\\\" --json\\nentire brain get fact:<id> --json\\n```\\n\\nUse `entire brain query \\\"text\\\"` for default hybrid retrieval. Select keyword\\nmatching with `--keyword` or semantic matching with `--semantic`; the two\\nflags are mutually exclusive and belong only to the query command.\\nText may instead be supplied as `--query \\\"text\\\"`. Flags work before or after\\npositional text; combining positional text and `--query` is an error.\\nFor example: `entire brain query --keyword --query \\\"RetryPolicy\\\" --json`.\\n\\nWorkspace retrieval supports the same forms:\\n`entire brain workspace query <workspace> --semantic --query \\\"retry policy\\\"`.\\nThe old `search` and `vsearch` commands remain hidden compatibility aliases.\\nMCP agents should use `brain_query` with optional, mutually exclusive\\n`keyword: true` or `semantic: true` arguments; `brain_search` and\\n`brain_vsearch` remain compatibility tools.\\n\\nFor durable facts specifically, use `recall`. `recall --expand` is an\\nagent-assisted query expansion path, so it belongs behind the same egress\\njudgment as other agent calls.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain recall \\\"account deletion\\\" --k 5\\nentire brain recall \\\"MirrorCommittedMetadataRef\\\" --expand\\n```\\n\\n### Navigate Code By Meaning\\n\\nWhen a task depends on structure rather than text, the agent should use semantic\\ntools. The normal path is: find candidate symbols, inspect relation-aware\\ncontext, inspect likely impact, then ask for tests. The normal tools are\\n`brain_code` or `brain_search_code`, followed by `brain_context`,\\n`brain_impact`, and `brain_tests`.\\n\\nThis is where `entire-graph` matters. It is not an agent memory layer; it is the\\nlocal parser/provider that gives `entire-brain` the graph the agent queries.\\nSemantic depth is language-dependent: parser-backed extraction covers the\\nsemantic language set, while many recognized filetypes are inventory-only. For\\ninventory-only files, prefer text retrieval and lower confidence in\\nimpact/context answers.\\n\\nFor deeper graph work, use the graph specialists: schema inventory, local graph\\nUI, source snippets, directed trace paths, dead-code candidates, boundary\\nenumeration, and working-tree change mapping.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain inspect graph-schema --json\\nentire brain inspect graph-ui semantic-graph.html\\nentire brain inspect snippet \\\"<symbol-or-id>\\\" --json\\nentire brain inspect trace-path \\\"<caller>\\\" \\\"<callee>\\\" --json\\nentire brain inspect dead-code --json\\nentire brain inspect boundaries --kind tool --json\\nentire brain inspect changes --json\\n```\\n\\n### Review Risk Without A Clean Diff\\n\\nFor long-running sessions, manual edits, or suspected regressions from memory,\\nthe agent should use diff-less regression review. The normal tools are\\n`brain_regressions` or `brain_review`.\\n\\nWhen the goal is a fair investigation, prefer location-only output first. It\\npoints the agent to suspected files and lines without handing it the expected\\nanswer text.\\n\\n### Connect Runtime Evidence To Source\\n\\nWhen static structure is not enough, import runtime trace edges with\\n`brain_ingest_traces` or `inspect ingest-traces`. The brain compares the runtime\\nedges with known static relations, persists them as `RUNTIME_TRACE` graph facts,\\nand makes them available to graph schema, trace-path, and brief/context flows.\\n\\nThis is useful for incidents and performance/debugging tasks where \\\"what\\nactually happened?\\\" matters more than \\\"what could call this?\\\"\\n\\n### Work Across Multiple Repositories\\n\\nUse workspaces when the behavior crosses repository boundaries: a frontend calls\\nan API, a service emits an event another service consumes, one repo imports a\\npackage from another, or deployment/config resources connect separate projects.\\n\\nHumans create and refresh the workspace. Agents consume workspace context\\nthrough MCP workspace tools where available, or through direct workspace CLI\\ncommands when MCP does not expose the required traversal. The workspace brain\\nkeeps member repos local and builds cross-repo contracts and graph edges from\\ntheir existing local brains.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain workspace create platform\\nentire brain workspace add platform ../api --name api\\nentire brain workspace add platform ../web --name web\\nentire brain workspace refresh platform --full\\n```\\n\\nAfter the workspace exists, use workspace context, graph, impact, retrieval,\\nand review flows when the question crosses repos:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain workspace inspect context platform \\\"checkout\\\" --json\\nentire brain workspace inspect impact platform \\\"checkout\\\" --json\\nentire brain workspace inspect graph platform --json\\nentire brain workspace query platform \\\"checkout\\\" --json\\nentire brain workspace review platform \\\"checkout regression\\\" --json\\nentire brain workspace watch platform --once\\n```\\n\\n### Preserve Durable Project Knowledge\\n\\nDurable facts are short, provenance-anchored statements about decisions,\\nconstraints, preferences, pitfalls, and standing rules. Humans can write them\\ndirectly when they know the rule. Agents can distill candidate facts from\\ncaptured sessions when explicitly asked or when a session-end integration is\\nconfigured to do so.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain remember \\\"Prefer table-driven tests for parser edge cases\\\" --path preferences.tests\\nentire brain distill --dry-run --json\\nentire brain distill --agent codex --model gpt-5.4-mini --effort low\\nentire brain facts tree --depth 1\\n```\\n\\nDistillation is the token-spending path. It is incremental and cached, but it\\nstill sends redacted transcript chunks to the selected agent unless you choose a\\nlocal loopback agent such as Ollama or use dry-run/no-egress mode.\\n\\nDurable facts also have a lifecycle. Review queued merge/supersede proposals,\\npromote branch facts when they should carry forward, retract facts that are no\\nlonger true, and garbage-collect stale retractions.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain facts review\\nentire brain facts promote --from <branch> --strategy keep-both\\nentire brain facts retract <fact-id>\\nentire brain facts gc --force\\nentire brain inspect blame <fact-id> --json\\n```\\n\\n### Extract Reusable Agent Skills\\n\\nSkill extraction is not \\\"turn repeated commands into a checklist.\\\" The current\\npipeline forms skills only from accepted deep dossiers: verified evidence that a\\npattern is non-obvious and useful to future agents.\\n\\nThere are several evidence paths:\\n\\n- recurring task patterns that pass deep verification\\n- verified themes that capture latent practices by meaning\\n- corrected or failed episodes grouped into lessons\\n- durable gotchas, conventions, and invariants grouped into capabilities\\n- workspace families where equivalent procedures differ across repos\\n\\nA human or explicitly authorized automation runs the egress-gated verification\\nand formation commands. The agent performs the verification and `SKILL.md`\\nsynthesis under that explicit request. The verifier is cached. Refresh, watch,\\nbrief, query, MCP reads, and workspace reads do not silently call it.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain patterns\\nentire brain patterns verify --deep\\nentire brain patterns verify --themes\\nentire brain patterns verify --lessons\\nentire brain patterns verify --conventions\\nentire brain patterns skills\\nentire brain patterns skills form <id>\\nentire brain patterns skills form <id> --yes\\n```\\n\\n`patterns skills` lists formable proposals, not raw pattern rows. `form <id>`\\npreviews the evidence, generated `SKILL.md`, and destination paths without\\nwriting. If no accepted deep dossier exists yet, `form` refuses and tells you to\\nrun verification first; it never falls back to shallow evidence. `--yes` writes\\nthe skill; existing files require `--force`.\\n\\nBy default, generated skills target the cross-agent `standard` destination\\n(`.agents/skills/`). Use `--target claude-code|codex|factoryai-droid|all` only\\nwhen an agent-specific destination is needed.\\n\\nWorkspaces mirror the same flow for cross-repo families:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain workspace patterns verify <workspace>\\nentire brain workspace patterns skills <workspace>\\nentire brain workspace patterns skills form <workspace> <id>\\n```\\n\\nPattern inspection is useful even when you are not forming skills. Use it to see\\nrecurring tasks, practices, risks, themes, verifier state, and whether accepted\\nskills are stale.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain patterns\\nentire brain patterns status\\nentire brain workspace patterns <workspace>\\nentire brain workspace patterns status <workspace>\\n```\\n\\n### Measure Brain Quality\\n\\nThe brain includes evaluation harnesses for maintainers who need evidence that a\\nretriever or fact layer is actually helping. These are not required for everyday\\nuse, but they are the right surface before claiming quality improvements.\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain facts eval-gen > facts-tasks.json\\nentire brain facts eval --tasks facts-tasks.json --retriever facts --json > facts-eval.json\\nentire brain facts eval-compare --a <before.json> --b <after.json>\\nentire brain history-eval-gen > history-tasks.json\\nentire brain history-eval --tasks history-tasks.json --json\\n```\\n\\nFor semantic performance and provider output, use the semantic benchmark and\\nstatus audit instead of relying on anecdotes:\\n\\n```sh\\nentire brain bench semantic .\\nentire brain status --json\\n```\\n\\n### Tune Retrieval\\n\\nThe default vector arm uses the bundled local Model2Vec embedder. For higher\\nsemantic recall, opt into a local transformer embedder served by Ollama or a\\ncompatible loopback endpoint:\\n\\n```sh\\nollama pull embeddinggemma\\nENTIRE_BRAIN_EMBEDDER=ollama entire brain query \\\"preventing data races\\\" --json\\n```\\n\\nKeep this distinction clear: changing the embedder changes retrieval behavior,\\nnot the underlying source of truth. Facts, history, docs, and semantic records\\nstill come from the local brain.\\n\\n## Privacy And Egress\\n\\nThe default brain artifacts are local and inspectable. Deterministic refresh,\\nsemantic indexing, local history indexing, and MCP tool calls do not require a\\nhosted model. The MCP adapter is stdio-only.\\n\\nSome operations perform network egress, either by sending selected context to a\\nmodel or by fetching over the network:\\n\\n- a configured `checkpoint_remote` lets `refresh` and its `sessions` export\\n  stage fetch checkpoint history from that remote into a throwaway temp repo;\\n  this is network egress, not a hosted-model call, and is skipped under\\n  no-egress mode\\n- seed synthesis during `refresh --agent auto`\\n- fact distillation with `distill --agent ...`\\n- query expansion with `recall --expand`\\n- pattern verification and skill synthesis\\n- judged evaluation commands\\n- `publish` uploads the serialized local brain to hosted Entire; opt-in and off\\n  by default, requiring both the command and `ENTIRE_BRAIN_ALLOW_HOSTED=1`\\n\\nUse `--agent none`, `--dry-run`, local loopback Ollama, or\\n`ENTIRE_BRAIN_NO_EGRESS=1` / `ENTIRE_BRAIN_LOCAL_ONLY=1` when the repo must stay\\nstrictly local. No-egress mode enforces locality for no-agent, dry-run, and\\nloopback-Ollama paths by validating URLs, redirects, and resolved dial targets.\\nA custom `--agent command` runner is treated as a trusted local command and is\\nnot enforceably loopback-only; no-egress mode cannot stop that runner from\\nmaking its own network calls.\\n\\nRemember that base Entire session capture stores transcripts and metadata on\\nthe repository's `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch. Anyone with access to that\\nbranch can read captured prompts, tool activity, and retained transcript data.\\nEntire redacts detected secrets before writing checkpoint metadata, but\\nredaction is best-effort. Redaction covers transcript and checkpoint metadata,\\nnot every local working artifact. Entire also writes temporary shadow branches\\nsuch as `entire/<short-hash>` whose code-file snapshots are raw, unredacted\\nblobs of the working tree. Gitignored files are filtered out only as a partial\\ndefense. Entire does not push shadow branches; do not push them manually, or\\nunredacted source could reach the remote. Review the Entire CLI security and\\nprivacy guide before enabling Entire on sensitive or public repositories.\\n\\n## Storage And Configuration\\n\\nEntire CLI supplies the plugin directories that make the brain durable:\\n\\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_CONFIG_DIR` for `brain.json`\\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_DATA_DIR` for generated repo brains and workspaces\\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_STATE_DIR` for regenerable cursors such as watcher state\\n- `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR` for caches\\n- `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` for the current checkout when invoked inside a repo\\n\\nRepo keys are derived from the repository origin. For example,\\n`github.com/entireio/cli` becomes `gh/entireio/cli`.\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","create_time":1789839998.31687}}}
