{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T18:06:14.503Z","ordinal":8320,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0cb48b2b18d09e75016aaecea62bb487d2ad0e834925f7fdc7","status":"completed","call_id":"call_RKuYCPbtkE6VqX98AUUpfdaC","name":"exec","input":"text(await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"cat > /tmp/assemble-quality-audit.py <<'PY'\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nimport re,json,collections\\nB=Path('/Users/thomi/.codex/visualizations/2026/09/18/01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9/code-quality-audit');R=Path('/Users/thomi/.codex/worktrees/5d33/entire-brain')\\nold=(B/'audit.md').read_text();expanded=(B/'expanded-findings.md').read_text();initial=old.split('## Findings\\\\n',1)[1].split('## Verification\\\\n',1)[0]\\ninitial=re.sub(r'^### (\\\\d+)\\\\. (P\\\\d) —',r'## F\\\\1 — \\\\2:',initial,flags=re.M)\\nentries=[]\\nfor text in [initial,expanded]:\\n for m in re.finditer(r'^#{2,3} F(\\\\d+) — (P[123])(?: —|:) (.+)$',text,re.M):entries.append((int(m[1]),m[2],m[3]))\\nassert len(entries)==101 and {i for i,_,_ in entries}==set(range(1,102))\\ncounts=collections.Counter(p for _,p,_ in entries)\\nextra='''\\n## Final documentation and retained-data observations\\nThese are editorial, design or evidence-readiness observations, not additional numbered runtime findings.\\n\\n- `docs/durable_facts_plan.md` says capture-time redaction means secrets can never reach facts and no prose scanner is needed. That absolute assurance is unsound and conflicts with the later defensive redaction/privacy contracts. F49, F58, F61 and F76 supply concrete reasons to avoid promising universal protection.\\n- The facts plan/handover mixes shipped and in-progress labels: hierarchy summaries and default semantic recall are both described as shipped and still open. It also labels a useful/1k improvement a “validated win” while admitting its primary comparison misses the stated Holm threshold. Claims that every lexical lever is exhausted or only one architecture can scale exceed the finite experiments described.\\n- Historical benchmark plans intentionally select positive discovery scenarios. Those retained scenarios cannot establish population-wide benefit without an independent held-out sample. This is a design limitation, not evidence of falsified results. Preserve the documents' historical labels.\\n- The temporal powered-pilot memo retains a validity paragraph with the earlier roughly .04 result while its corrected headline gives p=.43 and excludes invalid rows. Reconcile the narrative to the corrected analysis. Draft power assumptions from a one-task pilot need independent statistical validation before confirmatory use.\\n- The history-delivery report calls extra reachable context necessarily conservative because it adds cost. Extra useful information may reduce later search cost; that directional guarantee is not established.\\n- BrainMark review artifacts are unsealed and not reproducible as a complete checked-in corpus: only 15 of 1,806 main candidates and none of 79 fresh candidates are retained; all 350 review pairs are blank; current config and slate/index bindings differ. Missing candidates are a disclosed materialization requirement, not automatically a defect. Do not mistake these artifacts for a completed, reviewed seal.\\n- All 998 retained fresh PR records have unique repo/number identities, valid commit-shaped fields, post-cutoff merge dates, bounded file inventories and safe relative file paths. The snapshot hash matches its index. This validates recorded structure, not remote ancestry or the truth of each issue body.\\n- All 73 mined-scale tasks and patches match their 146 manifest hash bindings. All 189 task definitions have validation commands and resolvable declared patches. Every panel task reference resolves. The current task-review ledger has one stale prompt hash; prompt-lint triage reports 103 oracle-assisted and 86 needs-human-review, with no confirmatory eligibility inferred from a clean parse.\\n- All 67 JSON schemas pass an independent Draft 2020-12 meta-schema and full local-reference check. All 29 unambiguously mapped retained instances pass their schemas. The repository's smaller validator intentionally supports only a subset of keywords and cannot validate this entire schema catalog; the independent check used jsonschema 4.26.0 installed under /tmp only.\\n- All 119 patch fixtures parse without applying them and have no detected absolute/traversal target paths. The npm lock has 139 package entries, agrees with the root manifest and retains integrity hashes for registry packages. All generated report record counts checked against retained NDJSON agree.\\n\\nGenerated payloads were checked by full parsing, per-record/field traversal, contract checks, hashes, retained-evidence auditors and the previously reviewed/executed consumer tests. They were not all manually read byte by byte. External task repositories, live agents, upstream model training and historical benchmark executions were not rerun.\\n'''\\n# Keep original detailed findings intact, including explicitly labelled candidates and evidence caveats.\\n(B/'findings.md').write_text('# Detailed code-quality findings\\\\n\\\\nRead-only audit, completed 19 September 2026. F1–F101 are the numbered findings; unnumbered coverage notes and candidates are not additional confirmed defects. Severity expresses repair priority, not measured incident frequency.\\\\n\\\\n'+initial+'\\\\n'+expanded.split('\\\\n',1)[1]+extra)\\n# Line-addressable index for every numbered finding.\\nlines=(B/'findings.md').read_text().splitlines();anchors={}\\nfor i,line in enumerate(lines,1):\\n m=re.match(r'^#{2,3} F(\\\\d+) — P[123](?: —|:)',line)\\n if m:anchors[int(m[1])]=i\\nindex='\\\\n'.join(f'| [F{n}]({B}/findings.md:{anchors[n]}) | {sev} | {title} |' for n,sev,title in sorted(entries))\\nreport=f'''# Entire Brain code-quality audit\\n\\nCompleted 19 September 2026. Read-only review of the current codebase. **All 1,656 tracked files are accounted for; no repository files changed.** Final hashes match the initial inventory and Git status is clean. No fixes, commits, PRs, paid agent runs or external uploads were made.\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\nThe codebase has substantial behavioral tests and deliberate storage, compatibility and privacy safeguards. Nevertheless, the review found **101 actionable issues: {counts['P1']} P1, {counts['P2']} P2 and {counts['P3']} P3**. These include confirmed behavioral defects and explicitly identified maintainability/documentation findings; they are not 101 security vulnerabilities. P1 means high-priority data-integrity/privacy or destructive-state risk, P2 means a concrete correctness/reliability/maintainability issue, and P3 means lower-priority clarity or test-quality work. No P0 issue was established.\\n\\nThe recurring problem is uneven enforcement of contracts at alternate entry points, cache fast paths, partial failures and retries. A strong happy-path suite does not establish safe failure behavior. The most serious cases can erase retained transcripts or human review work, retain supposedly purged text, or include excluded/out-of-root data in publication.\\n\\n“AI slop” here means observable quality defects, not an attribution of authorship. The review found excessive repair narratives in comments, misleading guarantees, vague names, unnecessary indirection, dead compatibility machinery, repeated corpus scans, quadratic work despite output limits, duplicated test logic and assertions that never run. Some complicated code protects real invariants and should remain; length alone was not used as a finding.\\n\\n## Highest-priority findings\\n\\n| ID | Problem | Concrete consequence |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| F80 | Partial checkpoint metadata discovery is treated as complete | A successful refresh deletes previously retained transcripts for temporarily unreadable sessions. |\\n| F58 | Purge retry drops the original transcript inventory | Purge and verification report success while an inventoried transcript survives. |\\n| F76 | Regression review bypasses session exclusion | Successfully excluded transcript text returns in review findings. |\\n| F61 | Publishing omits the privacy guard/serialization | Facts sourced from excluded sessions enter the upload payload. |\\n| F62 | Publishing checks leaf paths but follows symlinked parents | Files outside the intended brain enter the publication artifact. |\\n| F23 | Failed Git filter enumeration is cached as safe | A later protected operation runs a configured filter after a cancelled probe. |\\n| F88 | Prescreen regeneration overwrites human review files | Completed reviewer verdicts and notes are replaced with blank templates. |\\n\\nThese were demonstrated with temporary synthetic fixtures or overlays. Publication reproductions inspected locally constructed payloads; no real content was uploaded. [Details and evidence]({B}/findings.md) explain triggers, affected paths, recommendations and limits.\\n\\n## Quality and complexity findings\\n\\n- **Comments and documentation:** F7 and its supplements record multi-paragraph change narratives around small helpers, stale claims and comments contradicted by implementation. F19 identifies an O(1) claim despite whole-map scans. F101 identifies ten broken reference links. Retain concise current invariants and move incident history to historical records.\\n- **Names and indirection:** F8 covers nearly synonymous status methods and a callback that ignores its input. F6 shows how `stage` hides both progress reporting and abort policy inside a 494-line refresh orchestrator. Clearer outcomes and cohesive phase functions would make these policies reviewable without introducing another framework.\\n- **Unnecessary work:** F5 repeats fact-review grouping in one request; F19 scans a growing map per mutation; F72 performs repeated receipt lookups under the write lock; F75 allocates a full cross-product before applying a small output limit. Runtime cost is not claimed where only algorithmic shape was established.\\n- **Dead or weakly justified machinery:** F20 retains obsolete activation detection and expensive tests. F38 embeds topic-specific rules in a generic ranker; this is a design-quality finding, with overfitting risk rather than a measured relevance loss.\\n- **Tests that provide false confidence:** F16 and supplements cover fuzzers stopped before decoding, duplicated implementations, tautologies and missing behavioral assertions. F99 collects prohibited calls without asserting the collection. F3 quarantines arbitrary failures from entire modules; F4 makes synthetic tests depend on a particular host binary.\\n- **Measurement integrity:** F84 loses Claude token usage, F87 accepts missing evidence, F90 scores detector errors as no false alarms, F93 promotes failed proof, F94 misses contrary server evidence, F95 omits staged edits and F97 drops token-accounting provenance. These affect whether benchmark results can support claims.\\n\\n## Coverage\\n\\n| Category | Files | Review method |\\n|---|---:|---|\\n| Go implementation | 255 | Full source inspection and cross-file contract review |\\n| Go tests | 390 | Full source inspection, behavioral-strength review and relevant suite execution |\\n| Python implementation | 109 | Full source inspection and bounded reproductions |\\n| Python tests | 81 | Full source inspection and benchmark suite execution |\\n| Tooling/configuration/web source | 55 | Full source inspection and applicable checks |\\n| Documentation and retained text logs | 136 | Full text review; historical assertions separated from current evidence |\\n| Data, schemas and fixtures | 629 | Full structural processing plus artifact/consumer contract review; 3 also manually source-inspected |\\n| Embedded model binary | 1 | Complete binary-layout/EOF validation, token/scale checks, hash and existing golden parity coverage |\\n| **Total** | **1,656** | **No pending inventory entries** |\\n\\nThe [per-file ledger]({B}/coverage.json) records the method, notes and original hash for every file. Full source inspection is distinct from generated-data contract review. The latter does not imply manual reading of every byte of a 17.6 MB upstream PR snapshot. All 629 data/fixture files were processed; 470 JSON documents, 26 JSONL/NDJSON files, 119 patches, 11 other text fixtures and 3 XML assets passed structural parsing. Semantic checks include all 67 schemas and their local references, 29 explicitly matched instances, all task/panel references, mined manifest bindings, complete snapshot identities and retained-report counts. Details are in the [data report]({B}/evidence/data-contract-validation.json) and [independent schema validation]({B}/evidence/schema-validation-independent.json).\\n\\nThe embedded model has 63,091 vocabulary entries, 512 dimensions, positive finite scales, no duplicate/empty tokens and exact file termination. This establishes local format integrity, not provenance or model quality beyond the golden tests.\\n\\n## Entire Graph and Brain\\n\\nBoth were used throughout, with recorded capability checks, task briefs, targeted rationale retrieval, caller/consumer inspection and impact queries. Graph provider `entire-graph` was available at `main-2c24bb80`; Brain at `main-dca7e1d8-dirty`. Current source and executed checks resolved present behavior; retrieved history explained intent.\\n\\nGraph prewarming indexed 1,565 files, 49,814 symbols and 163,359 relations. It skipped 47 oversized/minified JSON inputs; symbol reports covered 625 Go files rather than every build-tagged/platform-specific file. Dynamic dispatch and heuristic edges were checked in source where relevant. Broad discovery queries that stalled were stopped; focused impact calls and known-file inspection supplied the affected evidence. Brain's stored semantic state was stale, so it was not treated as proof of current source behavior. Empty tool results were not used as proof of dead code. These limitations did not exclude files from the manual inventory review.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n| Check | Observed result |\\n|---|---|\\n| Go vet and formatting | Passed / clean |\\n| Default full Go race suite | Passed; CLI package 620.867 seconds |\\n| CGO/FTS5 full Go race suite | Passed; CLI package 524.322 seconds |\\n| Linux and Windows amd64 builds, CGO disabled | Passed; compilation only |\\n| Focused existing contract tests | Passed |\\n| Python 3.14.4 discovery suite | 72 modules passed, 2 failed, 3 quarantined |\\n| Owner-approval rerun | Same host binary hash failure |\\n| Isolation rerun | All 14 tests passed; initial cleanup flake remains recorded |\\n| Targeted diagnostic reproductions | Behaviors retained beside their findings; deliberately failing regression assertions are evidence, not repaired tests |\\n| Clean retained history audit | Passed: 20 records, zero hard flags, one proof-ready comparison |\\n| Historical release audit | No-claim policy passes with 16 explicitly allowed historical task-hash flags; zero proof-ready comparisons |\\n| Both retained distill audits | Failed current-source binding for `distill_cmd.go` |\\n| Retained Radar tool audit | Failed current-source binding for `retrieve_cmd.go`; historical test events remain retained |\\n| Facts no-claim / proof fixture / workspace no-claim audits | Passed their limited contracts; do not establish production benefit |\\n| Protocol preparation | Failed stale source/analyzer bindings and a missing calibration artifact |\\n| Task prompt lint | Failed one review-ledger hash; 103 oracle-assisted / 86 needing review, all triage |\\n| Final file/hash/status verification | 1,656/1,656 matched; no additions, deletions, changed bytes or Git changes |\\n\\n[Go race log]({B}/evidence/entire-brain-audit-tests.log), [CGO log]({B}/evidence/entire-brain-audit-cgo.log), [Python log]({B}/evidence/entire-brain-audit-python.log), [final integrity record]({B}/evidence/final-integrity-check.json). Tests ran on macOS/arm64 with Go 1.27.1; Python checks used 3.14.4. Audit scripts and a schema-validation dependency were placed outside the repository.\\n\\n## Scope limits\\n\\nThis completes the repository audit, not a certification that no defects remain. Native Windows execution, live hosted providers, paid benchmark reruns, external task-repository validation, upstream model provenance and independent human relevance labels were not performed. Historical benchmark claims retain their original limitations and cannot become current-source proof solely because their JSON parses. Unnumbered candidates in the detailed notes remain candidates; they are excluded from the 101 finding count. Source-only performance observations are not timings. No evidence or source hashes were rewritten to make failed gates pass.\\n\\n## Finding index\\n\\nEvery row links to its detailed evidence and repair direction. Recommendations are audit output only; no implementation work was performed.\\n\\n| Finding | Priority | Issue |\\n|---|---|---|\\n{index}\\n'''\\n(B/'audit.md').write_text(report)\\n(B/'continuation.md').write_text('# Audit complete\\\\n\\\\nAll 1,656 tracked files covered. 101 findings: '+str(dict(counts))+'. No pending coverage. audit.md is the final report; findings.md contains all numbered findings and coverage notes. final-integrity-check.json confirms all original file hashes and clean Git status. No repository changes.\\\\n')\\nprint('findings',len(entries),'severity',dict(counts),'report_bytes',len(report.encode()),'details_bytes',(B/'findings.md').stat().st_size)\\nPY\\n/opt/homebrew/bin/python3.14 -B /tmp/assemble-quality-audit.py\",\"max_output_tokens\":1500}));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","create_time":1789841057.986691}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T18:06:14.529Z","ordinal":8321,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","session_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","root_turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","response_id":"resp_0cb48b2b18d09e75016aaecea1dc1087d2b7006f8c7cc37a92","usage":{"input_tokens":209466,"cached_input_tokens":208768,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":3774,"reasoning_output_tokens":55,"total_tokens":213240},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":90036341,"cached_input_tokens":86354944,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":210776,"reasoning_output_tokens":18685,"total_tokens":90247117},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":155921360,"cached_input_tokens":148926976,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":395575,"reasoning_output_tokens":40062,"total_tokens":156316935}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-09-19T18:06:14.619Z","ordinal":8322,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9","turn_id":"01a0ba4f-3944-7ce3-88ac-4c7975e84b1b","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-776b2c75-304f-40cd-884f-72538189f2ae","process_id":"60879","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","cat > /tmp/assemble-quality-audit.py <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re,json,collections\nB=Path('/Users/thomi/.codex/visualizations/2026/09/18/01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9/code-quality-audit');R=Path('/Users/thomi/.codex/worktrees/5d33/entire-brain')\nold=(B/'audit.md').read_text();expanded=(B/'expanded-findings.md').read_text();initial=old.split('## Findings\\n',1)[1].split('## Verification\\n',1)[0]\ninitial=re.sub(r'^### (\\d+)\\. (P\\d) —',r'## F\\1 — \\2:',initial,flags=re.M)\nentries=[]\nfor text in [initial,expanded]:\n for m in re.finditer(r'^#{2,3} F(\\d+) — (P[123])(?: —|:) (.+)$',text,re.M):entries.append((int(m[1]),m[2],m[3]))\nassert len(entries)==101 and {i for i,_,_ in entries}==set(range(1,102))\ncounts=collections.Counter(p for _,p,_ in entries)\nextra='''\n## Final documentation and retained-data observations\nThese are editorial, design or evidence-readiness observations, not additional numbered runtime findings.\n\n- `docs/durable_facts_plan.md` says capture-time redaction means secrets can never reach facts and no prose scanner is needed. That absolute assurance is unsound and conflicts with the later defensive redaction/privacy contracts. F49, F58, F61 and F76 supply concrete reasons to avoid promising universal protection.\n- The facts plan/handover mixes shipped and in-progress labels: hierarchy summaries and default semantic recall are both described as shipped and still open. It also labels a useful/1k improvement a “validated win” while admitting its primary comparison misses the stated Holm threshold. Claims that every lexical lever is exhausted or only one architecture can scale exceed the finite experiments described.\n- Historical benchmark plans intentionally select positive discovery scenarios. Those retained scenarios cannot establish population-wide benefit without an independent held-out sample. This is a design limitation, not evidence of falsified results. Preserve the documents' historical labels.\n- The temporal powered-pilot memo retains a validity paragraph with the earlier roughly .04 result while its corrected headline gives p=.43 and excludes invalid rows. Reconcile the narrative to the corrected analysis. Draft power assumptions from a one-task pilot need independent statistical validation before confirmatory use.\n- The history-delivery report calls extra reachable context necessarily conservative because it adds cost. Extra useful information may reduce later search cost; that directional guarantee is not established.\n- BrainMark review artifacts are unsealed and not reproducible as a complete checked-in corpus: only 15 of 1,806 main candidates and none of 79 fresh candidates are retained; all 350 review pairs are blank; current config and slate/index bindings differ. Missing candidates are a disclosed materialization requirement, not automatically a defect. Do not mistake these artifacts for a completed, reviewed seal.\n- All 998 retained fresh PR records have unique repo/number identities, valid commit-shaped fields, post-cutoff merge dates, bounded file inventories and safe relative file paths. The snapshot hash matches its index. This validates recorded structure, not remote ancestry or the truth of each issue body.\n- All 73 mined-scale tasks and patches match their 146 manifest hash bindings. All 189 task definitions have validation commands and resolvable declared patches. Every panel task reference resolves. The current task-review ledger has one stale prompt hash; prompt-lint triage reports 103 oracle-assisted and 86 needs-human-review, with no confirmatory eligibility inferred from a clean parse.\n- All 67 JSON schemas pass an independent Draft 2020-12 meta-schema and full local-reference check. All 29 unambiguously mapped retained instances pass their schemas. The repository's smaller validator intentionally supports only a subset of keywords and cannot validate this entire schema catalog; the independent check used jsonschema 4.26.0 installed under /tmp only.\n- All 119 patch fixtures parse without applying them and have no detected absolute/traversal target paths. The npm lock has 139 package entries, agrees with the root manifest and retains integrity hashes for registry packages. All generated report record counts checked against retained NDJSON agree.\n\nGenerated payloads were checked by full parsing, per-record/field traversal, contract checks, hashes, retained-evidence auditors and the previously reviewed/executed consumer tests. They were not all manually read byte by byte. External task repositories, live agents, upstream model training and historical benchmark executions were not rerun.\n'''\n# Keep original detailed findings intact, including explicitly labelled candidates and evidence caveats.\n(B/'findings.md').write_text('# Detailed code-quality findings\\n\\nRead-only audit, completed 19 September 2026. F1–F101 are the numbered findings; unnumbered coverage notes and candidates are not additional confirmed defects. Severity expresses repair priority, not measured incident frequency.\\n\\n'+initial+'\\n'+expanded.split('\\n',1)[1]+extra)\n# Line-addressable index for every numbered finding.\nlines=(B/'findings.md').read_text().splitlines();anchors={}\nfor i,line in enumerate(lines,1):\n m=re.match(r'^#{2,3} F(\\d+) — P[123](?: —|:)',line)\n if m:anchors[int(m[1])]=i\nindex='\\n'.join(f'| [F{n}]({B}/findings.md:{anchors[n]}) | {sev} | {title} |' for n,sev,title in sorted(entries))\nreport=f'''# Entire Brain code-quality audit\n\nCompleted 19 September 2026. Read-only review of the current codebase. **All 1,656 tracked files are accounted for; no repository files changed.** Final hashes match the initial inventory and Git status is clean. No fixes, commits, PRs, paid agent runs or external uploads were made.\n\n## Assessment\n\nThe codebase has substantial behavioral tests and deliberate storage, compatibility and privacy safeguards. Nevertheless, the review found **101 actionable issues: {counts['P1']} P1, {counts['P2']} P2 and {counts['P3']} P3**. These include confirmed behavioral defects and explicitly identified maintainability/documentation findings; they are not 101 security vulnerabilities. P1 means high-priority data-integrity/privacy or destructive-state risk, P2 means a concrete correctness/reliability/maintainability issue, and P3 means lower-priority clarity or test-quality work. No P0 issue was established.\n\nThe recurring problem is uneven enforcement of contracts at alternate entry points, cache fast paths, partial failures and retries. A strong happy-path suite does not establish safe failure behavior. The most serious cases can erase retained transcripts or human review work, retain supposedly purged text, or include excluded/out-of-root data in publication.\n\n“AI slop” here means observable quality defects, not an attribution of authorship. The review found excessive repair narratives in comments, misleading guarantees, vague names, unnecessary indirection, dead compatibility machinery, repeated corpus scans, quadratic work despite output limits, duplicated test logic and assertions that never run. Some complicated code protects real invariants and should remain; length alone was not used as a finding.\n\n## Highest-priority findings\n\n| ID | Problem | Concrete consequence |\n|---|---|---|\n| F80 | Partial checkpoint metadata discovery is treated as complete | A successful refresh deletes previously retained transcripts for temporarily unreadable sessions. |\n| F58 | Purge retry drops the original transcript inventory | Purge and verification report success while an inventoried transcript survives. |\n| F76 | Regression review bypasses session exclusion | Successfully excluded transcript text returns in review findings. |\n| F61 | Publishing omits the privacy guard/serialization | Facts sourced from excluded sessions enter the upload payload. |\n| F62 | Publishing checks leaf paths but follows symlinked parents | Files outside the intended brain enter the publication artifact. |\n| F23 | Failed Git filter enumeration is cached as safe | A later protected operation runs a configured filter after a cancelled probe. |\n| F88 | Prescreen regeneration overwrites human review files | Completed reviewer verdicts and notes are replaced with blank templates. |\n\nThese were demonstrated with temporary synthetic fixtures or overlays. Publication reproductions inspected locally constructed payloads; no real content was uploaded. [Details and evidence]({B}/findings.md) explain triggers, affected paths, recommendations and limits.\n\n## Quality and complexity findings\n\n- **Comments and documentation:** F7 and its supplements record multi-paragraph change narratives around small helpers, stale claims and comments contradicted by implementation. F19 identifies an O(1) claim despite whole-map scans. F101 identifies ten broken reference links. Retain concise current invariants and move incident history to historical records.\n- **Names and indirection:** F8 covers nearly synonymous status methods and a callback that ignores its input. F6 shows how `stage` hides both progress reporting and abort policy inside a 494-line refresh orchestrator. Clearer outcomes and cohesive phase functions would make these policies reviewable without introducing another framework.\n- **Unnecessary work:** F5 repeats fact-review grouping in one request; F19 scans a growing map per mutation; F72 performs repeated receipt lookups under the write lock; F75 allocates a full cross-product before applying a small output limit. Runtime cost is not claimed where only algorithmic shape was established.\n- **Dead or weakly justified machinery:** F20 retains obsolete activation detection and expensive tests. F38 embeds topic-specific rules in a generic ranker; this is a design-quality finding, with overfitting risk rather than a measured relevance loss.\n- **Tests that provide false confidence:** F16 and supplements cover fuzzers stopped before decoding, duplicated implementations, tautologies and missing behavioral assertions. F99 collects prohibited calls without asserting the collection. F3 quarantines arbitrary failures from entire modules; F4 makes synthetic tests depend on a particular host binary.\n- **Measurement integrity:** F84 loses Claude token usage, F87 accepts missing evidence, F90 scores detector errors as no false alarms, F93 promotes failed proof, F94 misses contrary server evidence, F95 omits staged edits and F97 drops token-accounting provenance. These affect whether benchmark results can support claims.\n\n## Coverage\n\n| Category | Files | Review method |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Go implementation | 255 | Full source inspection and cross-file contract review |\n| Go tests | 390 | Full source inspection, behavioral-strength review and relevant suite execution |\n| Python implementation | 109 | Full source inspection and bounded reproductions |\n| Python tests | 81 | Full source inspection and benchmark suite execution |\n| Tooling/configuration/web source | 55 | Full source inspection and applicable checks |\n| Documentation and retained text logs | 136 | Full text review; historical assertions separated from current evidence |\n| Data, schemas and fixtures | 629 | Full structural processing plus artifact/consumer contract review; 3 also manually source-inspected |\n| Embedded model binary | 1 | Complete binary-layout/EOF validation, token/scale checks, hash and existing golden parity coverage |\n| **Total** | **1,656** | **No pending inventory entries** |\n\nThe [per-file ledger]({B}/coverage.json) records the method, notes and original hash for every file. Full source inspection is distinct from generated-data contract review. The latter does not imply manual reading of every byte of a 17.6 MB upstream PR snapshot. All 629 data/fixture files were processed; 470 JSON documents, 26 JSONL/NDJSON files, 119 patches, 11 other text fixtures and 3 XML assets passed structural parsing. Semantic checks include all 67 schemas and their local references, 29 explicitly matched instances, all task/panel references, mined manifest bindings, complete snapshot identities and retained-report counts. Details are in the [data report]({B}/evidence/data-contract-validation.json) and [independent schema validation]({B}/evidence/schema-validation-independent.json).\n\nThe embedded model has 63,091 vocabulary entries, 512 dimensions, positive finite scales, no duplicate/empty tokens and exact file termination. This establishes local format integrity, not provenance or model quality beyond the golden tests.\n\n## Entire Graph and Brain\n\nBoth were used throughout, with recorded capability checks, task briefs, targeted rationale retrieval, caller/consumer inspection and impact queries. Graph provider `entire-graph` was available at `main-2c24bb80`; Brain at `main-dca7e1d8-dirty`. Current source and executed checks resolved present behavior; retrieved history explained intent.\n\nGraph prewarming indexed 1,565 files, 49,814 symbols and 163,359 relations. It skipped 47 oversized/minified JSON inputs; symbol reports covered 625 Go files rather than every build-tagged/platform-specific file. Dynamic dispatch and heuristic edges were checked in source where relevant. Broad discovery queries that stalled were stopped; focused impact calls and known-file inspection supplied the affected evidence. Brain's stored semantic state was stale, so it was not treated as proof of current source behavior. Empty tool results were not used as proof of dead code. These limitations did not exclude files from the manual inventory review.\n\n## Verification\n\n| Check | Observed result |\n|---|---|\n| Go vet and formatting | Passed / clean |\n| Default full Go race suite | Passed; CLI package 620.867 seconds |\n| CGO/FTS5 full Go race suite | Passed; CLI package 524.322 seconds |\n| Linux and Windows amd64 builds, CGO disabled | Passed; compilation only |\n| Focused existing contract tests | Passed |\n| Python 3.14.4 discovery suite | 72 modules passed, 2 failed, 3 quarantined |\n| Owner-approval rerun | Same host binary hash failure |\n| Isolation rerun | All 14 tests passed; initial cleanup flake remains recorded |\n| Targeted diagnostic reproductions | Behaviors retained beside their findings; deliberately failing regression assertions are evidence, not repaired tests |\n| Clean retained history audit | Passed: 20 records, zero hard flags, one proof-ready comparison |\n| Historical release audit | No-claim policy passes with 16 explicitly allowed historical task-hash flags; zero proof-ready comparisons |\n| Both retained distill audits | Failed current-source binding for `distill_cmd.go` |\n| Retained Radar tool audit | Failed current-source binding for `retrieve_cmd.go`; historical test events remain retained |\n| Facts no-claim / proof fixture / workspace no-claim audits | Passed their limited contracts; do not establish production benefit |\n| Protocol preparation | Failed stale source/analyzer bindings and a missing calibration artifact |\n| Task prompt lint | Failed one review-ledger hash; 103 oracle-assisted / 86 needing review, all triage |\n| Final file/hash/status verification | 1,656/1,656 matched; no additions, deletions, changed bytes or Git changes |\n\n[Go race log]({B}/evidence/entire-brain-audit-tests.log), [CGO log]({B}/evidence/entire-brain-audit-cgo.log), [Python log]({B}/evidence/entire-brain-audit-python.log), [final integrity record]({B}/evidence/final-integrity-check.json). Tests ran on macOS/arm64 with Go 1.27.1; Python checks used 3.14.4. Audit scripts and a schema-validation dependency were placed outside the repository.\n\n## Scope limits\n\nThis completes the repository audit, not a certification that no defects remain. Native Windows execution, live hosted providers, paid benchmark reruns, external task-repository validation, upstream model provenance and independent human relevance labels were not performed. Historical benchmark claims retain their original limitations and cannot become current-source proof solely because their JSON parses. Unnumbered candidates in the detailed notes remain candidates; they are excluded from the 101 finding count. Source-only performance observations are not timings. No evidence or source hashes were rewritten to make failed gates pass.\n\n## Finding index\n\nEvery row links to its detailed evidence and repair direction. Recommendations are audit output only; no implementation work was performed.\n\n| Finding | Priority | Issue |\n|---|---|---|\n{index}\n'''\n(B/'audit.md').write_text(report)\n(B/'continuation.md').write_text('# Audit complete\\n\\nAll 1,656 tracked files covered. 101 findings: '+str(dict(counts))+'. No pending coverage. audit.md is the final report; findings.md contains all numbered findings and coverage notes. final-integrity-check.json confirms all original file hashes and clean Git status. No repository changes.\\n')\nprint('findings',len(entries),'severity',dict(counts),'report_bytes',len(report.encode()),'details_bytes',(B/'findings.md').stat().st_size)\nPY\n/opt/homebrew/bin/python3.14 -B /tmp/assemble-quality-audit.py"],"cwd":"file:///Users/thomi/.codex/worktrees/5d33/entire-brain","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"cat > /tmp/assemble-quality-audit.py <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re,json,collections\nB=Path('/Users/thomi/.codex/visualizations/2026/09/18/01a0b6ce-e2ad-7853-bb2f-19731062a2c9/code-quality-audit');R=Path('/Users/thomi/.codex/worktrees/5d33/entire-brain')\nold=(B/'audit.md').read_text();expanded=(B/'expanded-findings.md').read_text();initial=old.split('## Findings\